package com.darkshamanism.lotcscholar.services

@Suppress("LongMethod", "MagicNumber")
internal object StaticBooksData_AXIOS_TAHN {

    private fun _AXIOS_TAHN_0(): List<StaticBook> = listOf(
        StaticBook("'The Balrog'", "Arinislia", "'The Taint'\n\n7th of The Grand Harvest, 1475\nThe day was calm and gentle. The sun was high, shining its rays down upon the Father Tree as the druids went about their day. One of the druid children suddenly runs up, gasping for breath as she appeared to have been running.\nShe looked panicked, saying that she ran into a Dread Lord summoning something in the nearby woods. Suddenly a loud shriek was heard. . All look up the natural carved stairs of the Father Tree to see the Dread Lord stand before them.\n. A ghoul standing next to him along with some followers, the Dread Lord unleashed the ghoul down upon the druids, commanded to kill them all. During this, the Dread Lord then turned and advanced up the tree,\n, telling his followers to block all that dare move up. The ghoul was quickly defeated, but not before spreading its taint on all that it bit along with ones that were near when it exploded.\nIt appeared that if something wasn’t done quickly to cleanse, they perished.\n\n       The druids now advancing further up the tree, the followers were quickly removed before advancing further up.\n. Again the druids were blocked and met with a horrible abomination. The Dread Lord cackled down at them, before unleashing a balrog upon the druids, charging down full force.\nSeveral that were in the way were easily knocked off the tree to their deaths.  The druids seemed unorganized, yet with some coordination from a few within the order, they quickly rooted the balrog and threw it off the tree.\nThe Dread Lord far gone by then yet the top of the tree tainted. The druids advance upward, circling around the taint and focused, using their healing magic to remove the corruption of the tree.\nBelow where the balrog  fell off the tree, several came from different nations and organizations and struck down the abomination where it stood.  With the Father Tree healed once more, the druids descended down and helped cleanse and heal the wounded.", ""),
        StaticBook("((Piano numbers)", "Geedin Jayamen", "OOC:\n\nnoteblock numbers on a 'piano' for songs.\n\n7 8 9\n4 5 6\n1 2 3", ""),
        StaticBook("*A note*", "[Cosmos Druid] Indra", "For keeping you company at night and chasing away those night terrors. You'll always be my friend no matter what.\n\nLove,\nSister Cosmos", ""),
        StaticBook("-- #13", "Fox", "[!] A majority of the notes were burnt beyond repair. Though some remained legible.\nExper-ment #--\nSta-us: Unkn-w-\nLoc-tio-: Un--o--\nSynop-is: [!] Most of it was scratched out.\n\n...\n\nDecei-in-. Ho-ror. D- no- trust --.\n\nI was wrong.\n\n[!] The rest is gone.", ""),
        StaticBook("-=Tale of Two=-", "Locke Lamora", "It is a power I let course through my veins, and seep into my thoughts. A hindering presence floating amongst my thoughts, dripping poison all the while.. memories crumbling away at its touch, my life growing distant and numb. This poison had a potency,\nnot to the effects of death.. but to erase your very memory what made you.. you. Its touch frosty as the tops of the dwarven mountains, with a chill apparant to that of steels touch. I dare not speak further lest it haunt my soul, and damned I be to see\nit walk the shadows of my form. So to we shall stroll hand and hand into the night of blackest hue to succumb to the desires of our hearts allowing our minds to be free in their spirits. A tale of two forgotten amongst the pages let you know now that this\nbook is the only living remnant of their once bountiful love.. now to you bear the curse to forever be lonesome in your state of mind befitted with murderous rage, and inciteful ideals. *The rest of the pages would appear to be torn away.. droplets of\nblood adorning the pages that still clung to the binding..!", ""),
        StaticBook("-Fire Evocation-", "Garsto 'Spark' Leaf", "{-----------------}         Fire Evocation\n{-----------------}This book is about Fire evocation.\n\nThe Fire Evocationists conjure appears to be real. But it is fake. One does not have power over the elements. For an Evocationist to conjure fire from the\nvoid they have to understand the element in the real/physical world first. The easiest way of bringing Fire into the physical world would be to light a torch by using Evocation or by creating a fire which then lights a fire. As a mage becomes more\nexperienced they can eventually form balls of Fire which they could shape into objects of Fire.\n\n((The Next part of this book is OOC. Its mainly for ones who already know Magic but want to Emote it. It is OOC. Remember that...\nGarsto is focused on a spot on a hay bale in front of him.\n\nis twitching a tad with his eyes locked on the position of the hay bale. He is muttering something. \n\nA tiny faint orange flame appears on the hay bale.\nThe flame gets smaller and blows itself out.\n\nGarsto falls back into a chair in fatigue.\n\nThat was an example of a Tier 1.\n\nThe next is Tier two.\nGarsto is concentrating on an unlit torch in front of him.\n\nHis forehead is sweating and he is breathing heavily.\n\nA Spark flickers on the coal in the torch.\nThe Spark lights the coal which results in the torch being lighted.\n\nGarsto's kitchen is now lit by the torch.\n\nStumbles in Fatigue.\n\nThat was a Tier Two. Next is a Tier Four.\nGarsto is concentrating with his hand outstretched towards the cow.\n\nHe begins to breath heavily and his forehead is sweating.\n\nHe mutters something. Flames erupt from his hand spinning into a shape.\nThe Shape looks something like a small dagger. Its not clear though.\n\nOpens his eyes and mutters something sending the fiery dagger towards the cow. \n\nThat was all OOC remember.))\nIts suggest that you meditate to help your self connect to the void. This can help you improve and go through tier 1.  At Tier two the user is able to hit a target most of the time. At the end of tier two the mage will be experimenting with conjuring Fire\nballs. Its said by Tier three the mage can conjure small fire balls successfully which they can conjure as Explosive or just normal. They can also vary the temperature of their fire. \n\n((Ooc: If you dont emote Fatigue you aren't doing it right.))\nUnless you are tier five and you are conjuring a small Spark. Even if you are Tier five you can still have Fatigue depending on how big the flame. Attempting to summon white hot flames. eah, not a great idea. You would die of heat. Or lack of Mana.\nSummoning fire from a distance takes time. ((Ooc: Summoning fire ON or BENEATH someone isn't allowed.)) You cant mold flames until you start to reach the end of Tier two. Even then you can only just about mold it into a ball of fire. With difficulty.\n((Ooc: Controlling a flame that is in the Physical world and was made in the Physical world, not drawn from the void would be considered power gaming.)) You will have increased heart rate, breathing heavily and will still get sweaty at levels above tier 3\nFrom personal experiences I learnt that when pulling from the void it feels like 'tugging a rope to pull something from nothingness.'\n\nI hope this book helped you with what you were looking for about Fire Evocation.\n/Signed Garsto Leaf./\n((Ooc: If I find you use this for some description wether its for the lore of your magic application or anything else. I will make a comment on your post saying its a copy of what I wrote origionally for when I tried to apply for magic.))", ""),
        StaticBook("-Fire Studies-", "Mars Hoppus", "FIRE EVOCATION\n\nMy name is Marcus Hoppus. I will be using this book to record my studies of fire, as the first part of my studies to become a Fire Evocationist.\n{\"text\":\"\"}\nDAY 2\n\nI woke up fairly late today. Around 2 PM. After putting on some rather warm clothes, I entered my cold little stone room where I began my studies by lighting a torch. The rather small flame stayed very still. It very slowly swayed\nside to side, but nothing much besides that, because of the enviornment: A dark basement room. I assume that it will be a lot more active and swaying outside than inside. It emitted a very light orange to white. It made a very light crackling sound, like\nthe sound of someone stepping on autumn leaves, or cracking very thin twigs. Holding the wood that the flame emitted from warmed my chilled hands in the cold room. I put it very close to my face. Now that I was paying so much attention to such a small and\nweak looking flame, it surprised me how powerful and warm it was. Just by putting it up to my face, my cheeks were instantly warmed. I very lightly put it against a cheek.\nThe first very light touch stung. It left a small slightly miscolored patch of skin\non my cheek. I focused on the light it emitted. Probably the weakest part about it, the small flame didn't give my much illumination. It was enough to see around 4 feet around me, but not enough to get a good look at the whole room. The flame seemed\nvery... Spunky. It was jittery and delicate. It wasn't large and strong and dark, but it was powerful, and zapped you. Anyways, I'm going to go patch up this burn now. I plan on studying a larger fire tomorrow.\nDAY 3\n\nToday, I will be studying a much larger fire. I will be making a campfire in my basement, and be studying it's attributes. When I started the fire, I immediately noticed how similar it was to the torch that I had studied. It was small\nand crackily. It's sound was similar to the torch, except it sounded stronger. Even in the beginning stages of the flame, it sounded a lot deeper. More hearty. Sort of like the sound of a halfling letting out a large, pleasant laugh. As it grew and\nexpanded, it stuck to this sort of sound. Much deeper. Hearty is probably the best word to describe it. The campfire flame was a lot brighter. I could see the whole room clearly. It was hypnotizing, yet very difficult to look at. I had to take off my\nglasses, because the magnification was paining my eyes. I also ditched my warm winter clothes that I had brought in for the cold room. The fire provided more than enough heat. By the end of my session, I was soaking in sweat. The trickle of the flames\nfelt hot. Hotter than any other flames I've ever been around, giving that I was in such a small room. The smoke was intoxicating. It hurt my eyes and my nose, but felt so comforting. I brushed my hands against the very top of the fire, feeling the same\nsparky feeling, then lowered my hand into the fire for a brief moment. I immediately regretted this action. My hand felt engulfed in burning heat, as if the campfire had consumed my hand as a sacrifice. Although the damage probably wasn't as bad as it\ncould've been, my nerves and skin shreiked at the touch of the dangerous dark orange monster. I immediately ripped my hand from it's grasp, and slapped it against the cool stone wall. I was left with a twitching grey hand. I ended my session here,\nattempting to cool my hand in a bucket of ice before any damage was done. The sound of the fire going out when I patted it down sounded swift and merciless. I could hear the fire howl at it's demise. Somehow, at the same time though, it sounded like it\nwas what the fire wanted. Like it was now one with the air and wind again. Anyways, my hand's pretty fucking screwed up now. It'll probably heal. I think.", ""),
        StaticBook("...", "Joe", "Ah, Conjuration; the rarest Arcane Art known... well, it won't be rare anytime. Of course, once you begin to learn the art, you will be reffered to as a \"Conjurer\". A conjurer is an individual who access the Void and construct living organism which are\nfueled wholly by the mana of their creator. Through the depths of the void, a Conjuration user will be able to summon mighty creatures or placid flowers into reality. Alas, as a conjurer you will be constantly delivering your mana for virtually every\nsecond your ersatz summon exists. You will soon come to a realisation that Conjuration uses the most of your mana unlike any other Arcane Science. In order to uphold the existence of your faux creature in reality, you must feed it precisely a great\nportion of mana to flow within it. As the creature you summon will be downright complex, the process of building it within the Void will be rather tiresome and there is a good chance you may be drained completely of your mana until you reach\nunconsciousness, as that happens your beast will be removed from the physical realm and be sent back to the Void. Yes, you can decide to desist the flow of mana beforehand, in which case the fauna will vanish to no consequence. Now, for you to construct\nan organism within the Void, you must first have a sharp and great understanding of both the interior and exterior of the living you wish to formulate. This can be achieved by oftenly killing and dissecting the creature you wish to summon. However, it is\nalso best to keep very detailed anatomical diagrams of the beast you wish to conjure. Remember to have notes on the animal's exterior, interior and bone structure if you want to have an accurate understanding of it. In the opaque Void, the fabrics which\ngenerate the entity are summoned bone by bone, layer by layer, muscle by muscle and organ by organ the Conjurer crafts and sculpts their being into an exact copy of their previously existing self. As a result of poor research, the Conjurer may fail at\nthis point, producing a being unlike that of its true and real form will result in an uneven and revolting entity which would certainly turn to a sludge once conjured. After a while of practice, the Conjurer would be able to summon the creature exactly\nhow it was before it was slain and have it formed with sheer stability. As it will behave akin to the life form it represents, it will follow its creator's commands. As a Conjurer myself, I would highly recommend you begin to study roses, then petite\ninsects until you grow stronger and confident enough to study anything larger. Think of Conjuration as art. You are the painter and the Void is the frame. What you paint in that frame will need to look beautiful and make sense in the judge's eyes. Bear in\nmind that the \"judge\" is the physicla realm. If the judge thinks your work of art is good, it will be stamped on and crushed and it will appear as an abomination in the mortal plane, you will be smacked in the face for your poot efforts, that smack will\ntire you. Keep practicing and your artistic skills will improve and the judge will begin to continue to accept your art. Overtime, your frame will expand and leave you open to produce which the judge can view. Be warned though, the larger the harder. And\nyes, you can fabricate hybrid fauna. I'll leave that for you to grasp the concept of that alone.\n\n\n-READ THIS NEWBIE-\n\nIf this tome becomes the next Illusion Arcane Art... I swear, as one of the most\npowerful conjurers in this realm, I will stalk and kill these conjurers I was not informed of beforehand with an army of flying honeybadgers... Or a colossal elemental... You better watch yourself, reader.", ""),
        StaticBook("18", "March Ash", "I have experienced an Epiphany. I have seen time spread out before me like a ribbon, and I could see the end of it. Not the end of me; I know only of my time in the Far Glade, and I know that the end of it will not be the end of me.\nNor of us; as far as I can see, we are as one. But I never needed prophecy to know that.\n \n~March Ash", ""),
        StaticBook("2nd Explanation", "Douglas", "Learnings on \"The\nMundane Book of the\nLibrary\" by Douglas\nHolger, Dedicant of\nZa'Kabar, written on\nthe 10th of the\nAmber Cold, 1571.\n\n*A note was\nscribbled, the words\nclearly woven and\nlooped with worry\nand nervousness. *\nNo references\nbut brief mentions. Don't\nsue me please. \n\nThey say there is\nmore than meets the\neye. I say that all the\ntime to the ladies, but\nwith this book, it is\nadmittedly true. The\ntome describes a\nteaching that is unworthy\nof its cover; it has a\ndaring approach.\nIt doesn't sound like\nthe facts I read from\na book, but the words\nof a seasoned sage.\nIt seems as if it was\nthe whole truth *a\ndark patch of ink, no\nlarger than a mole,\nrested on the last\npoint of the 'h',\npresumably from a\nhesitant pause*; but\nhere are my thoughts\nand learnings.\n\nFirstly, the book\ndenounces the\nexistence of the\nCreator, and with\neverything that we\ncan hypothesize, the\nreasoning is logical.\nInstead of a deity\ncreating the realm,\nthey focus on before\nthe \"all\". \"Many say\nthat in the beginning,\nall came from\none...This is wrong.\nBefore the All, there\nwas the Void\".\nThrough this\nstatement they also\ndeny the deity's\nactions of shaping\nthe Chaos to an\nOrder of his \"will\".\n\nNow my guess is that\nthe book points out\non the illogical fact\nthat a \"will\" cannot\nexist without a\nsentient being. The\nCreator, and his/her\nmany aliases, are\ndepicted as beings to\nhave a sense of\nfamiliarity with the\naudience. But the text\nthrows that out and\ninserts a abstract\nconcept known as the\nVoid.\n\nThe Void is explained\nwith resemblance to\nthe remnants of my\nremaining\nnot-debunked\nknowledge; it is\ninfinite, beyond our\nrealm. However, 'an\nevent' happened. The\nbook explains the\nparadox; how\ncan something infinite\nhave an anomaly\nwithin it at a point?\nThink of it my way,\npretend the Void is a\npack of Orcs. They\nwill cause disorder,\nbreed, and cause\nmore disorder,\nexpanding their\nnumbers\nexponentially. But\nthink, maybe, just\nmaybe there was one\nOrc who thought twice\nbefore crushing a\nflower beneath his\nlog of a foot. The\nbottom line is, Order\nwithin Disorder.\nThen the explanation\nof what this \"Order\"\nwas derived from.\nThe book theorized\nthat it was 'pure,\nuntainted energy'\nwhich became\nEverything for a\nbrief moment; its\nnature was so\nchaotic and\nfluctuating to cause\nthis. This is good\nreasoning, but I\nwanted to take this\nfurther to\ncomprehend it\nmore...rationally. The\nantithesis of 'there\nshall be life with\ndeath' is fine and all,\nbut I would rather\nspeculate something\nit being more complex\nrather than accept\nthe razor of\nsimplicity. So I\nlearned from this\nparagraph like this:\n'The Void from its\nperfect sequence of\ndisorder, at a split\nsecond, had an\nanomaly that\nreleased some of its\nelement Chaos, and\nthat element\nregenerated itself\nand pieced and\nrealigned itself into\nthe realm.\" It's\nsimilar to the\nstatement in the book,\nwhich is the point, but\nallows me to compile\nthe vague\ndescriptions of the\nbook and research\nthem on my own in the\nfuture.\nThe book continues\nwith the description\nof our mere selves,\ntrying to play God. \"It\nwas in our nature.\nAnd who could blame\nus?\". Like an orphan\nasking where her\nparents were, we\nwere the same. We\nwanted answers for\nour selfish desires\nand questions about\nour origin, and we\nopened the box of no\nbehind. As Tay the\nArchdruid of the\nPuma puts it, we\nopened the door to\nour otherworldly\ngifts, understood our\nplace in the universe,\nour creation as a\nform of sequenced\nChaos and\nrandomness within the\ninfinite space.\n\nAnd like dumb power\nhungry individuals, we\ngot full of ourselves\nand tried commandeering\nit. \n\nIt is understood that\nChaos is neither good\nor evil. Every\nsituation could use a\nbit of Chaos to help it\nrectify, as well as\nChaos can destroy\nmindlessly. Chaos is\nnot neutral however,\nit is a representation\nof pure random\nchance. It has no\nmind, no conscious, no\nsympathy, no hate, no\nregrets. If the\nsequence was aligned\nfor Chaos to just\ndestroy us as it\ncreated us, it would\ndo so immediately\nwithout warning.\n\nAnd that's what\nmakes this book\nterrifying. \"A single\ncrack, that's all it\ntakes, and then...\"\nquoted the book. If\nyou take time to\ncomprehend the\nsheer insignificance\nyou hold currently\nwith your existence,\nplease take a seat\nbefore your legs\ngive out. You, your\nfamily, your loved\nones, all born from\nchance and can\ndisappear at once.\nThe book illustrates\nwe thought ourselves\nas something greater,\nbeing able to see a\nmethod in the Chaos\nand perform magic in\nour world; but that\ndoes not mean that\nthe variables of\nChaos won't\nfluctuate slightly at\nthe disturbance. Like\nhow the book\nreferences to our\ncreator as \"The Void.\nThe All\", we took our\ngood luck of a realm,\n\"The All\", being\ncreated from a\npossibility of infinity\nfor us, and used it\nup immediately by\ntaking advantage of\nits connection to the\nVoid and started\nusing it for wars and\nother selfish\nreasons. \n\nThat's why I try to\ndeny this. Emphasize\non 'try to'. No one\nwants to accept the\nfact that your\nstepping on thin ice\ninstead of stone; but\nuntil we can prove it\nfor ourselves,\nthere's no use in\ndenying what has\nhappened over the\nages due to the Void.\nNo one knows what's\ntrue or not, but the\nVoid is something that\nshouldn't be taken\nlightly; that's the\nsimple summary of\nthis book.\n\n*a small scripted\nsentence at the\ncorner of the book\ncaught your eye, and\nwith prolonged\nsquinting you make\nout the sentence,\nclearly scratched on\nthe parchment with\nfrustration and\nforce* What the fuck\ndoes \"Tuva'seth\"\nmean?", ""),
        StaticBook("30 Strictures", "Ypres Signus", ";-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;\n        The Holy\n     Strictures of\n      Yeu Rthulu\n\n    As according to\nthe cannon which has been confirmed and past down by all servants of Yeu Rthulu. Blessings upon you.\nStricture 1: One must never find joy in the death of any man, woman, or child. \n\nStricture 2: It is right for one to be bound in matrimony as to create lifemates, but one must never force such a sacred act on another.\nStricture 3: One must never make love outside of marriage.\n\nStricture 4: One must restrict themselves from gluttony and drunkenness.\nStricture 5: One must restrict themselves from un-marital lust and intercourse.\n\nStricture 6: One must restrict themselves from taking what is not theirs.\nStricture 7: One must find peace and tolerance to deal with those whom're not enlightened, for they follow their own path.\n\nStricture 8: One must restrict themselves from needlessly striking out against someone.\nStricture 9: One must not allow one's lust for revenge overtake them. Just retribution may be needed, but vengeance is reserved for the Gods.\nStricture 10: One must not deceive or harm one another with words.\n\n \nStricture 11: One must not abandon those whom trust one for support.\nStricture 12: One must allow their hearts to not be apathetic to their brother's burdens.\n \nStricture 13: One must respect their family, for they're of same blood.\nStricture 14: One must not show hatred to anyone due to their race, their age, or gender.\n \nStricture 15: One must restrict from the use of substances which muffle and cloud the emotions as to use to deal with their pain.\nStricture 16: One must support all whom are widowed, lame, or otherwise unable to support themselves. \n\n \nStricture 16: One must support all whom are widowed, lame, or otherwise unable to support themselves.\nStricture 18: One must be submit under the discernment of others as to have a understanding of what is brought by justice, and what is brought by anger.\nStricture 19: One must keep themselves accountable to their spouses. And likewise hold their spouses accountable.\n\nStricture 20: One must not allow one's anger to provoke trouble, and pain.\nStricture 21: One must allow themselves to be counted as equal unless proven otherwise by content of character.\n\nStricture 22: One must not challenge or disobey those whom are appointed to authority.\nStricture 23: One must find mercy within themselves to be patient with the mistakes of others.\n\nStricture 24: One must find joy in whatever he has, whether little, or great.\nStricture 25: One must find the purest of joys from their spouse.\nStricture 26: It is in due right, and in way of nature that those bound in marriage should enjoy the privileges of sexual intercourse and relations. Yet, it is to be kept between the 2 lifemates,-\nnot extending to others and creating such things as orgies or brothels, for they are detestable and disgusting.\n\nStricture 27: One must support and nurture their spouse as they would themselves.\nStricture 28: One must love their spouse more than any worldly thing, or person.\n\nStricture 29: One must not hide their true nature from their spouse. For it is the true nature of lifemates that must be bound together.\nStricture 30: One must honor and follow the strictures if they wish to live a pure life, contented, and compassionately.\n\n\n-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;-;", ""),
        StaticBook("30 Strictures", "Quavinir", "Stricture 1: One must never find joy in the death of any man, woman, or child. \n\nStricture 2: It is right for one to be bound in matrimony as to create lifemates, but one must never force such a sacred act on another.\nStricture 3: One must never make love outside of marriage.\n\nStricture 4: One must restrict themselves from gluttony and drunkenness.\nStricture 5: One must restrict themselves from un-marital lust and intercourse.\n\nStricture 6: One must restrict themselves from taking what is not theirs.\nStricture 7: One must restrict their tounge from lashing words, for words are made to build up, not harm.\n\nStricture 8: One must restrict themselves from needlessly striking out against someone.\nStricture 9: One must not allow one's lust for revenge overtake them. Just retribution may be needed, but vengeance is reserved for the Gods.\n\nStricture 10: One must not deceive or harm one another with words.\nStricture 11: One must not abandon those whom trust one for support.\n\nStricture 12: One must allow their hearts to not be apathetic to their brother's burdens.\nStricture 13: One must respect their family, for they're of same blood.\n\nStricture 14: One must not show hatred to anyone due to their race, their age, or gender.\nStricture 15: One must restrict from the use of substances which muffle and cloud the emotions as to use to deal with their pain.\n\nStricture 16: One must support all whom are widowed, lame, or otherwise unable to support themselves.\nStricture 17: One must not harm any man, or woman without the cause of just retribution.\n\nStricture 18: One must be submit under the discernment of others as to have a understanding of what is brought by justice, and what is brought by anger.\nStricture 19: One must keep themselves accountable to their spouses. And likewise hold their spouses accountable.\n\nStricture 20: One must not allow one's anger to provoke trouble, and pain.\nStricture 21: One must allow themselves to be counted as equal unless proven otherwise by content of character.\n\nStricture 22: One must not challenge or disobey those whom are appointed to authority.\nStricture 23: One must find mercy within themselves to be patient with the mistakes of others.\n\nStricture 24: One must find joy in whatever he has, whether little, or great.\nStricture 25: One must find the purest of joys from their spouse.\n\nStricture 26: It is in due right, and in way of nature that those bound in marriage should enjoy the privileges of sexual intercourse and relations.-\n-Yet, it is to be kept between the 2 lifemates, not extending to others and creating such things as orgies or brothels, for they are detestable and disgusting.\nStricture 27: One must support and nurture their spouse as they would themselves.\n\nStricture 28: One must love their spouse more than any worldly thing, or person.\nStricture 29: One must not hide their true nature from their spouse. For it is the true nature of lifemates that must be bound together.\nStricture 30: One must honor and follow the strictures if they wish to live a pure life, contented, and compassionately.", ""),
        StaticBook("8G-71", "Bell Sano", "Crime Scene Report\n8 Glover Street\n11th Amber Cold 1571\n\nOfficers Reporting\n\nUlric Aesterwald\nBell Sano\nDavius Croster.\n\nScene found during a patrol for suspicious persons\nScene summary:\n\nFirst floor visibly destroyed, appears to be a search for an item, loose pools of blood lead to the stairs.\n\nSecond floor similarly destroyed, a large wooden bed trashed requiring some large force to do so.\nSecond floor cont. Next to the bed the trail of blood from the first floor leads to a chair covered in............. (the writing here is sloppy)\n\nWhat appears to be human gore where it is assumed the murder took place.\nWritten up by Sergeant Crostermonger.\n\nFinalized by Senior Sergeant Sano.", ""),
