        StaticBook("<3", "Analiana Elverhilin", "{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\nnerd", ""),
        StaticBook("[!] Note", "Ceruberr Asul'Ailer", "Dear Armin,\n\nDue to some unrest in Haelun'or I rode here with my horses, both of which I have left in the stables behind the library. If you could please provide me with keys to the gate and pens that would be much appreciated.\n\nMy thanks. Ceruberr.", ""),
        StaticBook("[AMC] Flyer", "Francois d'Aumont", "Aumont Mercantile\n         Company\n    -=- -=- -=- -=-\n\nHiring Now! Looking for miners, farmers, lumberjacks, chefs, leatherworkers, breeders, and merchants.\n\nWeekly wages!\nLocation: Felsen\n(Msg: Aumont)", ""),
        StaticBook("A Better World", "Orithur, The Archivist", "To Further The World\n\n - - - - - - - - - - -\n\nChapter 1 - Time\n\nWithin our world exists an eternal, unquestionable constant, and it is named 'Time'. This variable is the single most destructive thing any reality may ever\nhope to observe. Time withers trees, its crumbles rock, and it takes lives. But time brings about discovery and new life, it ushers existence itself. And while time will forever establish events as inevitable, it can never entirely control them. This,\nremains up to us. Whether Dwarf, Orc, Human or Elf the path is never fully laid for us, and it is our combined efforts that see the future carved in our image.\n\nIt is no lie to say that the image we carve today, is one of war, distrust and self\ndestruction. We are inherently flawed in that way, but with effort we can overcome this.\n\nOur world holds so much dear potential, and every day I see glimpses of it in the smallest of ways. In how a child's curiousity beams as it learns upon the\nnature of a beast we as mortalkind have seen driven to near extinction - the Dragon.\n\nIn how we theorise our own existence and purpose through science and religion. In how we seek moral absolutes and put value upon justice, honour and sanctity.\nWith our dear friend Time, these morals continue to grow. With each day a small part of our ignorance to the world and its design dies, but this is a slow happening. For over a thousand years the change has been minimal, because we do not as a collective,\nseek change. We find bliss in ignorance, we are content with our limited knowledge, and we are blind to what it means to progress.\n\nIf we do not teach our young of the ways of the world, its cultures and its beliefs, then we continue to allow this\nignorance to fester and grow throughout the generations. Our minds are warped to false realities constructed by our fathers and mothers. We know only what they believe is worth knowing, and from that we can never grow.\nChapter 2 - The Mortal Truth\n\nIt is said that our actions speak louder than words, but this could not be more false in the areas of education and study. What we say to our fellow descendant can sway them from one path to another.\nOur words to each other can validate what we percieve as truths, or shun what we know to be fact. The pressure of our peers can push us into ignorance, even if we are aware it is happening we are seldom able to prevent it. Only by dedicating ourselves to\nconcepts, to study, and to learning can we hope to break free of this ignorance. And only be spreading it can we hope to change the world.\n\nAll races and descendants are drawn to violence and war as a means of solving problems. While it is true that\nthis may apply to some more than others, it is a regular truth regardless. I consider myself both a teacher and a philosopher, and yet I am no exception to this rule. I have seen life taken and taken it myself. But it always means nothing. It can never\nnot be a waste.\n\nIf we were instead to show mercy where we would bring about judgement, then we prove ourselves greater than ever before. This is however, a foolish dream, for if we wish to be realistic our advance into progress must be slow.\nWe must be prepared for small changes at a time, small ways in which our lives alter to bring about the progression of our kind.\n\nInstead of judging, we should be examining. Instead of war, we should have dispute. Instead of light, and dark, we should\nsimply have life. To categorise things as good and evil, is to make a prejudgement upon them, disregarding intention and character for an 'absolute' overpowering archetype based on a system of simplicity.\n\nWe must understand and appreciate that\nour world is filled with greys, nothing can ever be black and white, all things are situational and all things require consideration. There exist wars today between dark and light, but the reasoning is so upsettingly simple. Dark fights light, thus light\nfights dark and vice versa. One side exists to counter the other in and endless, pointless dispute that has gone on for centuries upon centuries.\n\nThe Mortal Truth, is that unless we actively choose to overcome it, is that we are born blind. If we ever\nwish for perfect sight, we must first learn to see. This task alone is a difficult one, but what makes it more difficult is that it is not enough. We must then teach others as we do ourselves, a sense of unity is more than essential.\nChapter 3 - Fear\n\nFear is a primary blockade in our paths of enlightenment. Fear is brother to discrimination and prejudice. Fear is an emotional response to intellectual situations. Fear is the ultimate primal instinct, and while we may rely on it as a\nmeans of survival, to depend on it entirely is to become primal ourselves. We must learn to let go of out instincts and experiment with trust. Trust earns itself in return, much like how violence can only lead to more violence. Trust will eliminate fear,\nand then it will grow. I have mentioned frequently of a unity amongst all things, and how it is necessary if mortalkind is ever to hope to advance intellectually in the days to come. \n\nThis unity is built on trust, but trust will never flourish\nunless we let it. Unless we force ourselves into positions of trust, and be the first to extend the hand, it can never bloom as the flower it has the potential to be.\n\nNow this is not to say that fear is a useless nor negative aspect of the mortal mind.\nTo act in fear is often safe, and has us cautious in times of danger. But one must learn where to draw the line, we must discover at what point does fear no longer help us, but hinder us. There is no specific answer to this question, it is yet another\nthing that we as a species must ask ourselves, and make our own judgement on. It is this ideal of self analysis and philosophy that will drive us toward reason and logic. To believe that a single mind holds all the answers, is a simple act of ignorance.\nChapter 4 - Culture and Belief\n\nAll across our known world, a variety of varying cultures and beliefs flourish in independence. And while some would argue that to own a distinctive belief or culture is positive, I would argue that we allow these views\nto overwhelm all others. We accept what we have been taught to accept as fact, no matter how flawed it may seem. Metztli worship is almost entirely contained to the Kharajyr race, while Cannonism is almost entirely human. The reason that beliefs are so\nisolated to individual races, is that they are part of the culture - they are taught to their children as fact and thus grow knowing nothing else. \n\nI admit that many beliefs hold merit, and some may even accurately describe the state of the world we live\nin (though none may ever hope to prove it). But I do not believe that any particular religion in itself can claim to be factual when each religion is so incredibly biased toward its racial origin.\n\nWe will believe in what benefits us directly.\nWe let faith in taught ideals bring us to war and on occasion, the very brink of destruction. Many would die proudly in the name of what their fathers taught them, but for what reason? As much as we may admire our elders, they are only as flawed as we\nare, they will never find truth where we cannot.\n\nBut regardless of this most obvious fact, we fight on regardless. I consider that it is perhaps because we have no alternative, and that confuses us. If we cannot fight for what we believe in simply\nbecause we cannot believe in it, then what is there that is worth fighting for? If there is no Creator and no Seven Skies, when our lives are spent what would it all mean?\n\nThis returns us to fear. Fear of the unknown. Fear of a race or culture we do not\nunderstand, and fear of a world that we may one day understand far too well.\nChapter 5 - Oblivion\n\nAnd thus we must consider the inevitable, one of the few unquestionable truths of the world - Death.\n\nThe death of an enemy, ensures that he will never speak, nor raise a sword against you again,\nbut the death of a friend or a brother, will bring about spite and acts of vengence. \nWe like to believe that death is a conclusion, but it is only ever another move on the great game of life, one man's death means another man's quest. And when a game of\ndeath is played, there can never be a winner. \nMany believe that death is simply the transition from one life to the next, whereas others believe that death is a simple nothing, an absolute oblivion - and thus we begin to connect the dots between our\nmortal aspects: time, truth, fear and faith. All of them are connected to death, and all of them rely on it.\n\nWe often judge a man by his duration and not by his achievements. We often use death as a means to justify achievement itself. Death is to us,\na restart. It is the start of an infinite cycle that we have been playing out since the very first death upon our world.\n\nWe fear it, we desire it upon others, we do not understand it. Conflict stems from death, and leads to death. But I ask you to think\nnot on oblivion, but on its opposite. We focus so much on the end and forget that we are alive. Yes, an end will come, it always does, but we cannot allow this end to negate the life.\n\nIf we were to accept death as a mysterious inevitability and draw our\nattention to means of bettering our lives without death, then we would find not only a more intelligent world, but a more fruitful one. We could make new discoveries, spend time learning tolerance to each others faiths and cultures, learn of the world we\nlive in instead of the one that may exist after death.\n\nYes, one day we may truly understand what it means to die, and what it means to commit another to the grave. But we will never understand death if we do not first understand life in its fullest.\nChapter 6 - The Conclusion\n\nLearn to use time as a tool, and not to watch it as a clock. Let age not determine proximity to death or naivety of youth, but instead the total sum of your experiences. Where the old are wise and the young are\nuninfluenced and uncorrupted.\n\nLearn to understand the Mortal Truth as a flaw to be overcomed through great effort, and not an unquestionable fact of existence. A truth can change should we allow it, our design does not dictate our destiny, and though\nwe may refuse to believe that we can overcome our nature, know that it is possible, and know that we as a species have the power to redefine ourselves, and redefine truth.\n\nLearn to use fear situationally, and to sacrifice it in an act of risk so that we\nmay leave the boundries of comfort in ignorance, and experience true friendship and alliances that we would otherwise neglect on instinct.\n\nLearn that Faith and Culture are both situational, and can never fully justify an existence as fact,\nnor justify the will of any man or nation. We must discover the many forms that faith takes and learn to appreciate them all, for in each there is a hopeful beauty, but we cannot continue to let it consume us entirely. Once more, faith must be recieved in\nmoderation, and not allow it to embody us.\n\nLearn to neglect death as a means to an end, and understand it to be an end in itself. We must accomplish all we can before death comes, and never seek to hasten it. For to hasten death upon ourselves or others\nis the ultimate form of self destruction, and it is what has torn our world apart for many centuries.\n\nThere is an inherent beauty in all aspects of our being. We are far from perfect, but perfection exists within us. We seek good, but we must learn to\ntell the difference between good for ourselves and good for the world. To achieve this, is no easy task - it will require great communication and great sacrifice, to put the ideals of others before our own, but in return you will recieve the same.\nEven on the path of betterment, many will oppose you. If you allow yourself to buckle under those that deny change, then you exist as one of the influenced. To be outspoken against a world you know could be better, this is true good, this is true life.\nBefriend Time\n     Change Truth\n     Conquer Fear\n     Control Faith\n     Despise Death\n\n\n- Orithur, Founder of the Library of Dragur.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Bloodied Page", "Owl", "[!] The bloodied page seems ripped out from a journal. Or perhaps a note.\n\nIt reads almost as if it were dialogue. How strange.\n\n...\nI.. I have no idea what just occurred. Have they found us? Is these the- the visions?\n\nWhat do you mean? What happened to her experiments?\n\nI'm hearing- No. Brother, you must-\n[!] The rest is unfinished.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Book", "Anonymous", "*On the cover of the book, the name \"Migueleo Brackenankle\" is written in faded gold type.*\n\n[Unreadable mess, obscured further by large, copper stains] -rst tests have begun well,\nwith very little damage I can see. Sadly, it appears that the fly has become lethargic and tired. I expect it to kark it soon. Bloody quitters, these insects... Reminds me of my days in that pisshole academy. I reckon I just\nneed to refine the process with the swamp-lilies, and then replace the r- [The page is too mottled with dried blood to make out]\n\n[Several pages on, faded words can finally be read] -1276, second month of testing.\nKhorvad's arse is less wretched than the stench that's lingering from that bastard fly. Need to bury it further away. Though, at least the smell's keeping those crows away from my pumpkings.\n\n*Upon the next\npage, there is an undated entry in rushed writing*\n\nSuccess at last! I just needed to remove the swamp-lilies entirely from the equation! Don't even remember why I needed them in the first place...\n[The writing appears to become less legible leading to the end of the sentence, but then returns to its original rushed form]\n\nMy old mate from the academy made me one of his pumpking pies\nwhile visiting, and then I see this fat little snail just chomping away during dinner. Hadn't tried one before for the concotion, so I tipped it on the pie for the little bugger to eat! Bastard grew to the size of a dog,\nand he's still energetic! Going to brew up a fresh batch, then me and it are going to those limp-wristed academists and shoving it right in their fat, fu-\n\n*There appears to be a violent, thick pen-stroke,\nbefore the rest of the page is covered in a deep coppery stain. There are no more entries*", ""),
        StaticBook("A Chill", "Alder Tundrak", "It began with a chill.\nThe true reason why the Triumvirate have cut connection from the Monks of the Order of Wilven is not yet known, speculation seems to point to the Triumvirate becoming angered with mortals for the disrespect and misstreatment given\nto those who adhere to their teachings, the Monks. I ask the question, why would the Triumvirate who in that idea wish to spite the mortals remove power from that which has been mistreated. Surely the Triumvirate are not foolish, I know not why our boons\nhave been halted, why our wards lifted, and why our homes have been taken, what I know is that I, Elder Monk Alder Tundrak, after many years in service to Tariel, Lord of the Stars have been left homless, with no explanation. I do not seek fabricated\ntales as to why such has occured, I simply seek awnsers as to what I must do now. \n\nElder Monk Alder Tundrak Cloud Temple Docks of Vailor", ""),
        StaticBook("A Cycle", "Katherine Liverflower", "Rain\n\nIt falls from the heavens\n\nIts descent is a constant battle to keep itself together\n\nIt falls without question of its purpose\nRacing ahead blindly against all the others\n\nIt falls for a life time\n\nReady to explode itself across the fabric of life\n\nIt falls to allow life to continue through its beauty    \n\n           Rain", ""),
        StaticBook("A Diary", "Anonymous", "*The pages appear to have been damaged by time, only fragments are legible*\n\n1283 Sno... Maide...\n\nEzekiel pur… Order of Nine.\n\n*Ten pages are unintelligibly\ndamaged*\n\n1283 18th of …\n\nReforging a soul-ring similar to what which Ezekiel possessed is proving more difficult than anticipated. Only poltergeists remain bound to my will.\nTime is running out.\n*Further pages are damaged*\n\n...8t… 12…\n\nIde… has come to… m… The appar… Which Eze… bound to his will returned lon… ago. Perhaps a diff… arr… m…\n29th of… 1283\n\nMy soul appears just as fragile attached to my mortal body as the souls which cling to one another in the spectral beasts known as apparitions. Perhaps I might bind my soul to\nthat collective entirely to manipulate it. Results will be reported.\n\n*Hundreds of pages are unintelligible*\n\n… 1283\n\nIt is done.\n*There are no more entries in the damaged diary*", ""),
        StaticBook("A Dirty Note", "Orenian Knight", "[!] The page is stained with more mud, and blood alongside it. It smells terrible.\n\nWith hunting beasts that feed on blood, being able to poison the blood is useful. The smell of blood is strong where this potion lies.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Formal Invite", "Charlene Lavoie", "~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~You are formally invited to attend a private soiree at Lavoie Manor in the far Northwestern mountains. Please dress appropriately!\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n((Wagon will pick you up IC. Time will either be Sun 11/20 after the coronation, or Friday 11/25. PM Tehlulu or Cablam. Do not share invites or trade w/o permission. We have a guestlist.))", ""),
        StaticBook("A Get Well Card", "Meliah", "*A drawing of a happy sheep is revealed here.*\n\n\\)_-_-_/)\n  { o  o  }\n  {   v   }-_-_-\\\n   -----           =\n  {_ _ _ _ _ _ _/\n   w- w-  - w-w\n\nF e  l   b E  t    \n     l           t  A\n     -MELIAH", ""),
        StaticBook("A Halfing's Tale", "Timothy McPatt", "~*~Prolouge~*~\n\n1st Deep Cold, 1461\n\nLife as a Halfling is hard. Don't believe me? Well, this is my own personal account of my life and such throughout Anthos and the Fringe.\n\nI suppose I should start with my birth...\n~*~Chapter 1~*~\n\nI don't mean to sound cocky, but you could say my skills were apparent at the first appearance of my naked body!\n\nI was born to a simple home in Lenfarthing, the son of a farmer and clothes maker. Rumors were always circulating around the\nvillage about Momma and Pappy making love so loudly that it made the Dark Elves shiver in their sleep. That made me a proud, little one.\n\nAnyways, on the night I was born, the clouds gave way to a stunning and vibrant light. Some say it was just a full -\nmoon, but I beg to differ! I think it was a sign that I'd be a unique child!\n\nWhen my Momma gave her final push, I slipped from the Doctor's hands and happened to hand atop an anvil. It was then that my destiny was decided! I'd be the best Smith in all of\nAnthos and the lands after! And, as you can tell, so I became!\n\n~*~Chapter 2~*~\n\nGrowing up in Lenfarthing is the best upbringing anyone can ever hope for. Filled with fishing, smithing, eating, music and anything else you could possibly want!\nI myself enjoyed playing the fiddle and fishing on warm, summer days. The winters were filled with smithing practice and educational skills. I was reading and writing at an early age, even writing the name of our family above our Burrow's door! Such fun!\nMy smithing skills were improving, and my father was wanting me to improve and learn to sell what I made. So, he began taking me with him to the nearby city of Kingston to help sell Momma's clothes and his crops.\n\nLearning to be an adequate salesman, I\nbegan searching for instructors in the art of smithing! Thankfully, Kingston saw many travelers, many being dwarves!\n\nLearning day to day with helpful travelers improved my skills and made me aquire a new assortment of useful mental tools.\n~*~Chapter 3~*~\n\nThe day finally came. The day I was both hoping for and dreading. Reaching a mature age, I told my parents I wanted to move to Kingston and help craft armor and weapons for the soldiers.\n\nMy father was strangely upset about this, telling\nme that it was not the best choice. He was a very peaceful man, and was fine with crafting things for show. But he knew that the tools I would make would be used for cleaving heads off and such. I was young and foolish, not even taking this into account.\nI began my life in Kingston by making simple yet beatuifully crafted tools and weapons for citizens.\n\nThe life was good, even taking me to far away places to smith! One time, i was even in the dwarven capital! Learned a lot there, I did!\nBut, like anyone with a sensible mind, I had to return home sometime!\n\n~*~Chapter 4~*~\n\nReturning home was met with warm hugs and kisses! Those of Lenfarthing hardly remembered me, but we are nothing without love!\n\nI noticed my parents were\nabsent from the crowds. I asked where they had gone to discover they had left to sell their items along the roads of Anthos!\n\nHow excited I was by this, yet still a little sad for not having seen them on my return home. But, of course I'll see them again!\nI didn't stay long, leaving back to Kingston to finsih up the orders I had promised. Then, well, Setherien came. And you all know how that ended, so... Fringe!\n\n~*~Chapter 5~*~\n\nWe all know how we came, here, so I won't waste your time there! Ha!\nEntering the Fringe, I did what most did; wander. For years, I wandered the lands and lived day to day on few coins and fruits.\n\nThe life was fun and hard! Even learned much about surviving out there!\n\nBut, eventually I realized that I needed some coin,\nso I seeked work in all of the lands. The only one were I was met with open arms was - strangely enough - Kaldonia!\n\nSer Mizu Silverblade offered me a job as his Houses Blacksmith! A grande opportunity! I was extremely excited for such a job!\nStarting off with mass orders of armor and swords, the Elders of the House were astonished at my quality and care put into making their gear. My first payment was 2,500 coins!\n\nI work there now, but of course the story ends when I want it to! Ha!\n~*~Chapter 6~*~\n\nWorking for the Silverblades has made life... weird. Between getting kidnapped, fighting and saving Orcs, my life has never failed to be interesting there!\n\nBut even with so much excitement, one must never forget their roots! That is\nwhy I returned to my people, currently living in Ivybend. Once more, they accepted me with open arms and even built me a cozy, little Forge!\n\nLiving a simple life is the best, and it is the path of the Halflings. I read, create and steal sheep! Oh, what\na life it is!\n\n~*~Epilogue~*~\n\nLife among our races are all different! But none compare to that of a Halfling. Why, you might ask? One, simple fact:\n\nWe live like we always have.\nNo war or politics get in our way as we live day to day with farming, smithing and other trades that make us a people.\n\nNone are corrupt or angry! We are who we are. And what we are... are Halflings.\n\n- Timothy McPatt,\n  7th, Deep Cold, 1461", ""),
        StaticBook("A Joke!", "Lyulen", "My Life", ""),
        StaticBook("A Journal", "Anonymous", "Day 1: Today I begin my trek to Mount Thahn, the colossal mountain in the centre of the world. It is an intriguing target for scientific study as it is a frozen tundra, yet is surrounded by temperate to hot lands. This is a\nmystery I shall solve, and which will catapult me to recognition in the guild and that fool Havery will finally see me as a proper rival.\n\nDay 3: Arrived at Mount Thahn. Was rudely thrown out of the dwarven\ncity built upon the upper slopes for attempting to enter the Remembrancer's library. Apparently the dwarves take issue with having their doors broken open with axes, but they shouldn't lock their knowledge away like that! Not\nfrom me, anyway. They can lock it away from Havery all they like.\n\nDay 4: A little prospecting soon deduced why: the mountain stone is rich in thanhium, a heat-absorbing ore. The ore has many useful\nmagical properties, including use as a power source for enchantments. I will hire some miners at once!\n\nDay 5: Sadly, setting up a thanhium mining operation could prove difficult. The dwarves have\ndiscovered and claimed the thanhium. All of it. They haven't actually dug it up, but a foul smelling dwarf informed me at swordpoint that it's all theirs. Should I attempt to steal it from under their noses, they'll likely respond by\nby force of arms. I will search for a place where we could potentially mine the ore without them noticing.\n\nDay 8: I discovered a cavern today. It seemed to warm as I went down, as if the thanhium\nwere... missing. Surely there should be more of it as one goes down? It's as if it's already been dug up, but the dwarves live on top of the mountain, not below it. I will venture deeper tomorrow.\n\nDay 9: I found\na huge door at the end of the cavern. It looks dwarven, but different, like it's centuries of architectural development ahead. Could the dwarves have developed time travel and invaded the past? I wouldn't put it past them. That being\nsaid, stealing their time machine and claiming it as my own invention... Forget beating Havery, this could send me right to the top! I must open this door...\n\nDay 13: No luck opening the door.\nDay 17: Still no luck opening the door. This sure is a low technology door for future dwarves, though.\n\nDay 20: It has occurred to me that the future dwarf theory is, to put it as the orcs would, a load of\nskah. The door isn't a future door, it's an ancient one. Have the dwarves devolved? I could believe that, they must have lost their brilliance with their height. Still no luck opening the door, but I suppose we could tunnel around it. Heard\nwhat sounded like people skulking around.\n\nDay 21: This discovery is remarkable! I can't write it down in fear that I will lose these notes and Havery will steal my discovery. We collapsed the\ntunnel we dug and I will hire a full expedition to loot this place. We need to get out quietly though, as the dwarves are onto us. I've heard they can be so quiet that they can sne\n\n*The journal stops abruptly here.*", ""),
        StaticBook("A Letter", "Solaria Soulheart", "To my beloved niece Valeria,\n\nI've missed you, dear. I hope you're doing alright, we haven't spoken in many years. I hope to see you again soon, last time we got together was truly fun. Keep safe.\n\nBest wishes,\nYour dear aunt Solaria\nA few foxglove petals are tacked onto the back of the page.", ""),
        StaticBook("A letter", "Avilia Frostbeard", "Ah'm jst gonna go and watc' t'e ocean fat'er. A'll be back soon\n\n(a small drawing of a waving Avilia could be seen here)\n\n\n-Avilia Frostbeard", ""),
        StaticBook("A Lost Artist", "Lady Steward Arahaelth Aureon", "[!]\n\nA tattered book sits infront of you, the cover damaged and the pages a little torn. Otherwise in perfect condition!\n[!]\n\nThe first page presents a messy and rushed sketch of a singular flower, rested upon the table. The drawing has been made with charcoal and is very smugged. The charcoal would most likely rub onto one's fingers and hands.\n[!]\n\nThe current page seems to be half torn, the intact half revealing a face of a young human male. The person seems perfect with angelical features. Though this can only be made out slightly due to smudging.\n[!]\n\nThe next page portrays the same man, but he looks different from the previous image. Replacing the perfect features are various cuts, scars and a ruthless expression. He is shown to be drinking a pint of ale.\n[!]\n\nThe page has been completely torn out.\n[!]\n\nA forest of trees has been sketched upon the page. Some of the trees are shown to be dead, and some are only just saplings. And in the middle of the opening is a single tree stump.\n[!]\n\nPresented on the page is a family of deer grazing in a field. There are three of them, a doe, a fawn and a stag. The fawn is nuzzled close to his mother.\n[!]\n\nThe next page is of the same stag, though only his head has been drawn. The drawing pays close attention to the male deer's features as it is shown to be looking straight out at the viewer.\n[!]\n\nA different scene is shown, very clearly a tavern. A heavily built man is shown to be throwing a punch at an elf, whilst holding a mug of ale in his hand.\n[!]\n\nA similar background environment is still being shown. Though this one presents a woman with blonde hair and a scar across her face, healing  bruised woman. A light is shown to be emitting from the palm of the cleric's hand.\n[!]\n\nThis page shows a scene of ruins, with a church featured in the middle - the steeple having crumpled off. It seems to have been drawn from a high view point.\n[!]\n\nAn eerie scene featuring two large, glowing mushrooms in a darkly lit cave. Various ores and smaller mushrooms litter the scene.\n[!]\n\nA close up of a cat's head and features is presented upon the page. A heavy amount of black charcoal has been use to re-create the colour of the cat's coat (It would most likely smudge upon one's fingertips.)", ""),
        StaticBook("A lovely poem", "Stefjan Kovacs", "A poem to my mamej, Astrid Kovacs.\n\nGod wished for me to learn the value of family.\nGod wished for me to love without limits, to help those in need, to say thanks and ask forgiveness.\nHe wished for me to live without fear and so he sent me -\nsomeone to teach me all of those things.\nMy mamej!", ""),
        StaticBook("A Lover's Day.", "Kili Al-Deen", "they shine brightly in the moon lit gaze,i have traveled near and far yet seen not such beauty as this\nlike a diamond strong and proud yet pretty and pleasing to the eye.their touch is of a gentil flower on a warm sunny day,their voice is soft and\ngentil that fills the heart with a warm reassurance and a aw that makes you feel as thuo you have been touched and carryed away by a cloud,you get lost in their gaze as one gets lost in a sunset and your mood rises like the sun in the dawn. you yearn\nto be around them with and want to see their smile and happyness you feel as if its your duty to make them smile and joyfull.thy are my warm sunrise and Beautiful jem.to rise before you and make you your favorite dish and bring you roses of compasi\nhonnor and love that goes to no one but you,My dear love.\n___________________\n\n         By:Kili Al-Deen\n      (ibraheemc2000)\n\nnotes from writer:\nI made this for the enjoyment of the read and the warmth of the heart-Kili Al-Deen.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Loving Note", "Aenor Calithil", "I love you, Renn.\nPlease don't be mad at me.\nLook at me, look at this, look at me, look at this, now look at me.\nAren't I amazing?\nLook at how handsome I am.\n\nYou can't possibly stay mad at this face.\nHave I ever mentioned how great you are?\nYou're the utmost adorable wife any individual could ask for.\n\nLove you, mayilu.\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\nI'm cut off, aren't I?", ""),
        StaticBook("A Mans Descent", "Ilkazar", "A \nMans\nDescent\n\"The few people who know the name Coltaine Wick, likely associate it with evil deeds, dark mages, and other such unsavory things, but he wasnt always a nefarious old man. As a young man during the Stormlord Rebellion, he happily\nserved his king and country of Oren, he was like any average man. But in war someone has to lose and in this case it was the side he fought for. As the rebels took over all he had ever cared for, his regiment was disbanded, he himself\nwas outlawed from Petrus, and a 500 mina bounty was placed on his head, things were not well for him, every day he feared would be his last, he never lost faith in his fallen king though, and still wore the honored Crow on his clothing\nearning him many beatings. It was in this low point in his life that he first began to tread the path that would lead him to darkness later in life.\nHe was drinking away his sorrow in a pint of ale at the then famous Standing Tree in when he first came\nacross one of the orginal Wraiths, beings of such horrific power they the could reduce a man to an ashen husk in mere moments. The aforementioned Wraith Vinzakra was making a grand time slaughtering people outside the tavern he sat in.\nAll Coltaine could do was watch in awe, wheels turning in his mind he thought \"There is a man with the power to control his fate, if I had but a portion of such power, oh the things I would do!\" It was then he set off to find that wretched being\nand serve it in what way he could. So he went to Embermoor, long rumored to be a haunt for necromancers and the like, which he soon found out to be very true. He was allowed to join the Adherency there, and serve Vinzakra for some time.\nMurdering and terrorizing the people of Athera became second nature for him in the time he spent in the Adherency, after a time he went off to study the art of earth evocation, being taught the art by a Graven, when he had returned, the Adherency had been\ndestroyed, losing yet another home, Coltaine drifted around from cult to cult for decades, necromancers, dreadknights, shades.\nHe served them all and faithfully, never betraying those we would call friend.\nAnd, after managing to sell his soul, he will continue to serve the dark for all of eternity.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("A Note", "Anonymous", "*The pages seem to be written in a near-illegible scrawl*\n\nBark of iron, deep in the south. The rip in the world that we must embrace! It gives gifts! It gives wonderful gifts to those who serve!\nA blade most perfect! So perfect that it does not belong in our world!\n\nAll it takes is a few drops of blood! A few drips from your wrist, and it shall awaken! And when it does, it will look upon you with\nsuch favour! The sweetest favour! It is an impossible thing, but it is a wonderful thing! Seek it out, brothers and sisters!\n\nSeek it seek it seek it deep in the south, for amongst the Islands of the\nAnkulos Peninsula, it waits for others to appreciate its luminescence!\n\n*A second piece of writing by a different hand is scribbled below the first*\n\nDo not trust it. The tree is evil in a\nway that I cannot comprehend. Take only its first gift, and then never return.\n\n*A third piece of writing by yet another hand is scrawled at the very bottom of the page*\nTake nothing from it! It wants only to consume! It consumes everything! It will consume you! I can't get it off me I can't get it off me I can't get it out of me only flame will end it\nBurN th TrEE\n*The writing trails off to nothing*", ""),
        StaticBook("A note", "Mosue", "Did it really have to come to this? I never wanted to become something… something like this. I’m not a human anymore. No. I’m a monster… I promise people with a home, a place to live, safe away from the chaos of the world… and then…\nFather. I'm sorry.\n[!] The rest of the note is frowny faces and drops of blood.", ""),
        StaticBook("A note to Drudii", "The Prince", "Malo arboris cadit in eos qui fata conteram pace.\n\nRecipis luco, et uri.\n\nReliquit tui animalia cadaver.\n\nScimus enim te feci.\n[!] Upon the back of the note would be a simple mark, three vertical lines.", ""),
        StaticBook("A note, a note!", "Galanthil", "I baked you these cookies as a sign of regret.\nI understand that my actions were not admirable from your perspective, however, I could no longer bear to watch you suffer.\nIf you no longer wish to associate yourself with me, I will not object\n(that much), if indeed you decide to forgive me (which you should) you will always be welcome for tea at my house in the upper district.\nOnce again, you have my sincerest apologies,\nGalanthil Maehr'indor\n\nP.S. I climbed through your window, please\ndon't be mad it was a lot of work.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Note.", "Hiylu Oronar", "[!] The handwriting of the note is messy and childlike, some words mispelled!\n\"Dear maln,\nI'm staying with Eris while you're gone, so don't be worried! I miss you, tell me when you're back please. \n\n~Hiylu.\n\nP.S. Eris lives in Sutica, in a purpul house.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("A Page", "Anonymous", "The 5th of Snows Maiden, 1302\n\nWe, the Order of Nine close upon the sanctuary of the heretic known as Ezekiel Cunningham. He appears to have sealed himself in an underground cave of sorts.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Pirate", "Myrna", "         A Pirate's Tale\n\n\n                    \n\n\n\nWritten by Myrna Reylana\nPart 1\n\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\nI sighed as I wiped the warm sweat off my brow, leaning back on my feet. It was a hot day, and it stank of fish. A giggle echoed from my left, and I quickly crouched down. I hid inside the tall grass, which co\nenveloped my body. My dress was scrunched up around my knees for better mobility as I moved through the shrub on my hands and knees, which would have been quite the sight if someone were to see me. There was a rustle directly in front of me as I crawled\nthrough the dry stalks. I took in an exaggerated breath, as if I was one of the huge cats in the jungles to the far south, stalking their prey by smell. Another giggle erupted, this time right behind one of the thicker grasses in front of me; so I\npounced. I knocked the small child over, much to their delight, \"Caught you!\" I declared, as my little brother lay trapped beneath me.\n\nElliot squealed, a grin on his face, \"How'd you know where to find me?\"\n\nI sat up, letting him scramble to a\nposition as I pretended to ponder his question, \"I smelled you, obviously!\"\n\nHis little nose scrunched up, and he grabbed the collar of his tunic. He sniffed it carefully, then looked at me. \"I don't smell like anything though!\" he huffed.\n\"I guess I just have a better nose, then.\" I shrugged as I stood up.\n\nI brushed the top layer of dirt and dust off my dress, and grabbed my little brother's hand. There were still dirt stains on his trousers and the bits I couldn't brush off my dress\nI tugged him along anyways. It was almost lunchtime.\n\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\nEnd of Part One", ""),
        StaticBook("A Poem", "Anonymous", "The Fabled Forge\n\nDeep within the dwarven mountains cold,\nMiles under ancient earth and caverns untold,\nLies the ancient dwarven smithy of wealth long lost,\nA place of\ndwarven dreams,\nInto where flow rivers of gold,\nThe ancient smithy an awe to behold,\nMany a dwarf lost in the caves,\nThose who found it took the secret to their graves,\nKnow sons of Urguan,\nHeed these words\nand take hold,\nFor one day you might find this wealth foretold.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Quick Summary", "Balduul Exrith", "~The Warhawkes~\n\nThe Warhawkes are\na tribe of mainly dark elf and other races,\nthat live in a society that is lead by two chieftains. They believe that they are in one big family. The Warhawkes live in teepees and longhouses, similiar to\nthat of nomads\nand snow elves. The Warhawkes, despite their name, are based upon peace and democratic values. In the tribe, it is believed that all members have a voice. Inorder to join, one must undergo several trials by doing a task asked of them by the chieftain or\ntaskmaster. After completing said assignment, they could conduct another task, or jump to the question part of the trial. During the trial, the person is asked questions about their history and true name. They are to bound themselves to the Warhawke oath\nad do whatever it takes to fulfill their tribe's needs. The Warhawkes are enemies with the Orenian Empire, The Dwarven Kingdom, The Deciples of Wisdom, the Undead and the Daemons. Currently, they are not in war with any of them.The Warhawkes have allied\nthemselves with the Orcs, some rogue mages and the turtles. They have possibly allied themselves with the snow elves and the cat people. Unfortunately, due to tensions between the Dreadlanders and Oren, the Warhawkes have had to move the camp to an area\nof High Elf land. This agreement has created a small alliance with the High Elven peoples. The Warhawkes have yet to speak with the Wood Elves on this matter. Surviving Dreadlanders have also joined the Warhawkes.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Remembrance", "Crineas", "Recollections of the Wedding between Elswyn Deathsbane\nand Ramza Mantisuku\n-------------------\n\n\n\nTranscribed by Crineas Acosta\n24th of The First Seed, 1566\nThe sun was high in the midday sky. The birds chirp as they flew about, singing their songs within the trees of the Druid Grove. Several rays of sunlight cast off their glow through the leaves of the trees,\n, revealing a ceremony area for the wedding that is about to take place.\n\n Those on the invitation list have already arrived, taking their seats upon several oak roots on either side of the path that led directly to the altar\nThe roots themselves curved and twisted in a delicate way.\n\nCarefully woven thorns of roses with buds yet to be fully bloomed intertwined within the roots as if waiting on a druid’s command\nSome of the druids attending seemingly stood off to the side, quietly observant and making sure none will interrupt. \n\n----\n§P§S\nRamza  stood up at the altar near Elorna. His tall, elven form dressed in a formal fashion with his signature red rose color upon his suit. Even standing tall and joyful, a slight bit of nervousness is seen in his eyes\nHe waited quietly, casting a look over at Elorna where he receives a reassuring smile from her, knowing everything will be alright before his ear flickers.\nHis head quickly turning towards the path as soothing music begins to play. The trees and soft blades of grass begin to sway as if a gentle breeze was blowing through.\n\n-----------\nMirabella and Lemon the fox first appear. Mirabella’s halfling form in a beautiful, white and red flower girl dress with red roses perfectly placed within her hair golden locks.\nWithin her left hand is a handle attached to a woven, wicker basket carrying rose petals while her right hand gently grasps them\n\n Her fingers release the petals as she begins to walk, letting them float gently to the ground.\nThe fox dressed in a little black suit with a tiny handle within its mouth, attached to another small woven, wicker basket. Yet inside this one appears to be a tiny pillow with two rings lying on it\nThe fox carefully walks behind Mirabella. Its tail gently swaying side to side as they both make their way up to the altar. Mirabella moving to the right as Lemon moves to the left, sitting the basket down next to Ramza\nRamza smiling down at the fox before his ear flickers once more, looking back down the path. His eyes widen. A slight blush to his cheeks able to be seen as he stares quietly, admiring his bride to be.\nElly’s figure appears at the beginning of the path. Her eyes staring at Ramza’s form, giving him a soft gentle smile before she begins to walk. Her dress white and red with a large rose placed in the center of her upper body\nSmaller roses delicately woven around her arms with several perfectly placed in her long, branded, light blond hair. Within her hands is a beautiful bouquet of fresh, fully bloomed and vibrant red roses\nEach step she takes is elegant and graceful as she begins to move. Each roll of seats she passes, the buds begin to bloom into fully lush red roses\nNow stepping up to the altar, she moves to the right to stand next to Mirabella while her body faces towards Ramza. A faint blush to her cheeks, staring at his tall, elven form with a gentle, soft smile placed on her lips\nHer eyes show slight nervousness as well as his, yet also joy and compassion. Turning, both Ramza and Elly look at Elorna who smiles and nods in return at them before she begins.\nElswyen looks to Ramza in the eyes, beginning her vows.\n\n\n--------------\nAuthor's note\n\nThe wedding vows will be written in a seperate publishment\nAs Elorna finishes her words, Elly and Ramza would turn to gaze at each other. Ramza’s cheeks blushing a little more before he takes a step forward, moving his hand up to gently carcass Elly’s cheek\nHis head lowering while his eyes slowly close, pressing his lips against hers to softly kiss her. Elly’s cheeks blush a little more as well, leaning her head up slightly with her eyes closing before her lips meet his, softly kissing him back\nEveryone begins to stand and clap. The birds beginning to fly about while squirrels and other critters start to dart around. Breaking the kiss, Ramza bends downward, lifting Elly up bridal style within his arms before he turns and begins to walk\nElly chuckles lightly, signaling him to stop for a moment to let Lemon hop up in Elly’s lap before he continues on.\nIn the main part of the Druid Grove, delicately twisted and woven oak roots have been moved to make tables for the food that had been prepared. Red banners with roses hang from the branches of the trees while soothing music can be heard within the area\nGuests begin to make their way towards the reception area. Some sample the lovely dishes that were made while others move to begin to dance to the songs. Ramza and Elly suddenly appear once more\nRamza’s hand gently holds hers while he guides her to the middle of the floor.\nHis other hand moves to gently place on the side of her waist while she does the same in return to him before they begin to move, dancing along to the melodies of the music\nOthers begin to join in with their partners, making it last as the sun begins to set. The now married couple moving towards the exit\nTheir loved ones and friends congratulating and waving them off as their figures disappear from view, heading towards their home.\n\n----\n\nFIN.", ""),
        StaticBook("A report", "Prodayo", "A Report\n\nBy: Prodayo, storyseeker\n(The following is copied directly from my personal journal, converted to the actual incident and not in rhyme.)\nI came upon the scene a little after it started. A dark elf was accusing a Northman of a crime. (the crime was as follows: the northfolk had barged into a elf tavern and ordered drinks for free. when the owner denied them that, they wounded him)\nAs the two continued to bicker, the north man summoned a lightning ball to frightenthe darkelf. at this point Bjorn showed up and began to calm things. The reason for this report is what the darkelf said in leaving.\nThe darkelf began to say that we are going to regret this. he began to talk of the anger of the dwarves and that a coalition against the empire would snuff it out. whether this threat had any weight to it or not, I feel it important enough to report.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Sealed Book", "a sticky substance seals the book", "I have arrived back from my journeys amongst the frigid lands. Many thoughts have been had, and many things were learned. Our kin is different, and my time with the ice snake taught me even more about their heros.\n\nOur old friends, the\nIrehearts still thrive, and I learned much about them. Their loyalty is still flickering, and is possible to flare up again. Their governing body seems weak and feable, my time with their hero gave sight to this.\n\nWhile with the\nworshippers I saw much. I learned of the new generation that see as we do. It is too bad that our Ice Dragon was corrupted once again, he was one of the stronger ones we had purified.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Simple Story", "Unknown Author", "*The old tome is worn and dusty, and the ink is partially faded.*\n\n=================== A Story\n===================\n\" Two chaste Temple Monks were walking down the road during a heavy downpour of rain. They came across a beautiful young woman who was taking shelter under a tree. She called out to the monks, requesting that they carry her across a small stream.\nThe first monk immediately moved to help the woman while the second silently protested. The first monk scooped the beautiful lady up in his arms, and he carried her across the stream. After crossing it, he promptly set her down and continued on his way.\nThe two began walking down the path once more, and the second monk remained silent for several minutes as the woman disappeared from view. Finally, he spoke to his brother.\n\n'As monks of the Cloud Temple, we are not supposed to have contact with woman!\nWhy did you pick that women up so casually and carry her across the stream?' \n\nThe first monk replied, \n\n'Brother, I set the woman down long ago. Why is it that you have not?' \"", ""),
        StaticBook("A slim grimoire", "Unknown", "ＦＡＬＬＥＭ´ＲＯＣＨ\n       ＢＨＡＫＴＡＬ\n         III\n  III     I     III\n   I   I  I  I   I\n   IIIIII I IIIIII\n     I  II II  I\n     II  III  II\n      IIIIIIIII\n        IIIII\n        IIIII\n        I I I\n          `     '\n[!]\nAn odd grimoire with tattered black leather\ncover sits in your hands. By the looks of it, it probably has something to do with magic, but the entirety of the book is written in an odd language that aches the head of those who can't read it.\nHazk,\nmerin'gnu vegaati zu'nokion. \n\nRe'nokion talsh vokarl\nya'pelosr therthe'pelosr-bhaktalserthek-unkra zu'gathan.\nE\nOdurnur blothr zu'kraemsal\n\nDu\nPelosr'basular koneg\nqsuzph'dvemd\n\nDo'nekaro kuram savurr'zu bhakthal.\n\n[]  []  []  []  []  []  []\nKraemsal lerikah odurnur ubyz'fikt koneg vurrsht-chelion\nil do'jhorvastur \nsavurr'vegaati qsuzph'dvemd\n\naza uvot, do'varsku -\nO'ag'vokarl\nE\nDo'odurnur bhatti'dvemd\n\nDu\nDo'kree haurz qsuzph\nkon kreoz'bhaktal\n\n           [!]\n  Pictures have been\n  drawn alongside the\n  instruction.\n[!]\nThe first picture seems to have an odd lump of rock.\n\nIn the second one, some sort of hollow figure has been molded from the ore.\nVe\nDo'kree thur'hadan\nfhi al'kraemsal\n\n   o  -->  ö\n   O        []/\n\neg zmask, a'kyoni Kha.\n\nCul\npelosr'basular koneg'qsuzph\nZin\nDo'infinst qsuzph egodar burz'fhi yatha\n\n\n     Undere dayn!\n\nqsuzph lerikah ezg saiz undere aldunr lerikah al'kraemsal.\n    ö \n  \\[] -> ,;     ✔\n[!]\nAnother set of pictures are on the side. In the first, the figure is made to resemble a kharajyr.\n\nIn the second one, the kharajyr shrinks considerably in size.\n☬\n\n\n\n\n\n                   -1496\n                   - Z.C.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Spark", "Athe'lor", "The Founding of Electrical Evocation\n\nSalamandra (or Regaki, I should state), founded Electrical Evocation in a year not known, though was approximated to have been sometime during the races stay in Elysium. However, the tale stretches backward into\nthe deep roots of Asulon, where the possibility for this, all began. \n\nRegaki was the violent, and dangerous figure that was a terror in the night for the common man, elf, or dwarf alike. Never was his skin shown, and this caused many to believe he was a\ndemon, manifested, or perhaps the physical embodiment of the tortured soul of a great wizard, returned from the grave to slay all mortals. Nonetheless, a hooded figure left a note for Regaki, knowing it would be found. The directions on the note lead to a\ngrand, isolated tower where the robed figure would wait for him. Atop the tower did the shadow stand as the demon approached, rain and thunder battering the two of them with no effect. Rock, fire, and water beckoned to the command of the ghoul, -\nshattering the defenses of the robed figure; yet, still, the robed figure appeared to refuse to fight back, aside from casting minor defensive magic to protect himself. It was apparent Regaki would triumph, but in the last moment, bolt of lightning\nreigned down from the heavens, attracted to his golden mask. With ear-piercing howls and shrieks the demon squirmed as the gold melded to its face, until Regaki fell silent.\n\nWhen he awoke, he could not feel his face. He touched it, but the gold was hard,\nand no matter how hard he tried, the mask would not yield from his head. \n\n...And so some great time passed, before Regaki could be called Salamandra again. With extensive research into his mask, it was concluded that the lightning's energy had been\nabsorbed into the golden pieces, at least somewhat. Over time the mage found himself able to draw this energy from the mask, and bend it to his will. Before long the mask's power would wane, however, so he made it his obligation to create electricty\nhimself. The Undead had been capable of doing it in very large quantities, so perhaps he too could create lightning, albeit not as efficiently. \n\nThis was the founding of Electrical Evocation.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Stale Tale", "Nym 'Burzbuub' Eildi", "A Story of Felsen\n\n\nOnce there was a city named Felsen, capital of the Orenian Empire, home of Canonism. In this city, there lived a boy- young and innocent in God's sight. He lived in the city with his parents, who loved him very much. He was\nterribly boring. He always did what he was told and never did anything wrong. Everyone hated him. This boy's name was Faris. One day, Faris was walking through the streets, being boring and being made fun of by every boy he ever met- especially by the\nstreet urchins. However, he then did something not too boring. A street urchin walked up to him with a sharp stick seeking to murder him in cold blood. The boy was his archnemesis- the leader of the gang of urchins named Mason. Mason hated Faris, because\nhe had a loving family and good behavior. He found him to be a true scrub and the worst sort of person- worse than gutter filth. As they approached the rest of the boys began to insult Faris. Mason then remarked \"Finally gonna 'each you a lesson.\" Mason\nmoved forward, thrusting the stick towards Faris' abdomen. Faris then did the unexpected and took a step backwards, avoiding the stick. Then pulling out a small dagger from his pocket moving to stab it into Mason's jugular chord. Blood squirting from\nhis neck, the life fading from his eyes. The other boys scattering and screaming for guards. Faris, removing the blade from Mason's neck. His clothes mostly bloodied now. The guards rushed to the scene, one from behind, running the boy through with his\nshining blade, now bloody. Thus ending the perfectly intolerable boy with his perfectly loving family, and finally his perfectly unexpected murder.\n\n\nThe End.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Strange Dream", "Nemir", "I dreamt I was old.\n\nMy hair thinned.\n\nMy skin wrinkled.\n\nMy eyes paled.\n\nI watched as this all slowly happened in my dream.  It started with how I am today.\nI saw me, but without pointed ears.\n\nI was standing in a field littered with daisies and tulips, the sun could've been warm.  I couldn't feel anything, I only saw.  But I bet it was warm.\nIt was odd, in my dream.  The sun was looming over me and the field.  It was noon, but noon only lasted for a mere second.  Suddenly it was dusk, then night.  The moon's chalky form shined fainlty in the black sky.  It dragged\nits self across the void of night just as the sun swam through the blueness of day.  \n\nSuddenly dawn.\n\nI still saw me standing in the field, no emotion showed on my young face.  I felt reminded of a statue, the only\nmovements I saw myself do was breathing.\n\nNight again.\nThen day.\n\nThis cycle repeated.\nEvery second it grew faster, soon the days passed as quick flashes.  Within the\nmiliseconds of day I saw myself slowly changing.  My skin slowly changed, thinning and wrinkling.  My hair remained white,  but it wasn't smooth and soft as it is out of this dream.  It became scraggly.  I looked closer, the flashing of\nday and night still proceeding.  I looked into my blank eyes, they were slowly fading, wisdom filled them.  But I felt as if I knew nothing, I felt as if I was watching someone else, a stranger, but they were still me.\nThe years went by with the seconds.  The field remained unchanged around the aged stranger that is me.  After moments, they were gone.  A grave stood where they had been with my name chisled on it.  I saw the year of when I died, but when my gaze\nshifted to another part of the stone I suddently couldn't remember the number.  I only could remember my name, nothing else.\nI felt frustrated, but I couldn't remember what I was irritated about. I finally looked up from the headstone, it was noon.\nAnd it stayed noon.\n\nI woke up at that, young as I was when I had went to bed.\n\nMy ears were pointed, and I was in my room.  The faint light of the morning sun was barely lighting my cool room.  I sat up.  It was still dawn, I was still young,\nand my ears were still pointed.\n\nI didn't know what to think of the dream.  I'd never be able to grow old, not with this curse of a long life.  Or is it a curse?  That'd be a topic for another book.\n\n§", ""),
        StaticBook("A Study of Air.", "The Scribe.", "A Study of Air.\nAir has always been a curious element. It is said to be the element of the free-minded. Those who wish harm upon others and seek to delve into chaos shall not find themselves working with the air around themselves easily as a manipulated\nelement. Now, as many of you prospective mages are probably jumping up and down, I am going to say now; This is not a book on how to learn Air Evocation. It is merely a study of the properties of air observed over time.\nAir is a gas. Much as when water is heated to a boil it turns into a gas, air is constantly in a gasseous state. However, the boiling temperature (temperature at which liquids turn to gas) of air is far, far lower than any witnessable temperature upon any\nrealm the races have currently visited. I believe that if we visited a realm where air was a liquid, it would be covered with insurmountable oceans, and we would all drown.\nAir moves from high concentration to low concentration. Upon working with a bellow, I was able to pump more air into a box than currently resided with in. After opening the box, a breeze was produced. The opposite was produced upon\nusing a bellow to remove air from a sealed box. A slight breeze was created blowing into the box. \nIf one places a leather bag into a large jar, and seals the bag upon the lid of a jar using thick rope, one shall find that pulling that bag up from the jar\nis a difficult task. This is due to the amount of air within the jar. The air on the outside of the jar is exerting force due to its concentration, while the air on the inside is extering less due to its lack of concentration.\nThis is also an effect of\nsuction. The lesser amount of air tries to pull air to it to equalize the concentration, but it cannot pull the air through the leather.\nAir is finite. There is only a certain amount of air within a certain amount of space, and that air can be exhausted through breathing.\nAir is comprised of multiple elements, though I do not know what they are. This was a rather unfortunate experiment to test. I locked a squirel in a contained box, and then submerged the box underwater. Obviously, the animal died as\nthe air in the box ran out. But, when I opened the box underwater, bubbles were created, leaving me to believe there was still gas located in the box. The question is, if the squirrel inhaled all of the air, what was left?\nI hypothesize that\nthe lungs of creatures only use the majority of air to breath, and other trace elements are left over as they are not needed by our lungs.\nAir can absorb heat just as a solid or liquid can. Upon heating a rod and holding it in a room, the space around the rod remains heated, obviously, but when the rod is removed from the room, the air within the room is still heated.\nThis next section is comprised of recounts of scientific interviews about air.\nThe properties of air are weight, color, density, air mass and pressure.  ~uncited mali'aheral.", ""),
        StaticBook("A tale of death", "Alcetra Enn", "There are many great mysterys in this world today we unravel one of the greatest mysterys of them all. What is death like? I was wandering in the grove until i was a man who was afraid of death, a wood elf assured him that death was nothing to be\nafraid of, he said that he could take the old man and some curious people to experience the afterlife, i accepted the offer as i was most eager to unravel the mystery. He put his fingers on our heads and we were taken by a great slumber.\nWe awoke in sandy shores where he led us to a gateway that entered the afterlife, it was a marvellous experience full of trees, water and animals. He told us that when we die we will have our own afterlife which will be altered to our personality and\nmemories. For example a destructive individual will have fire and blood in his afterlife, whilst a elegant peace lover will have silence and peace. We went into a cave filled with animal statues and vines, this most likely means that this afterlife\nrepresented a peace loving druid. After this marvellous experience we were awoken back at the grove, the old man who was eternally afraid of death immediately longed for peace. Now you know that death is a place that will suit your personality and a\nplace where you will live a happy life full of prosper.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Token", "Salamandra", "Snowy peaks and mountain tops did guide the lad on his way; through wretched cold and face-freezing snow was the price that he had to pay. Although the path was broken and battered, his robes soggy and tattered, the boy continued on, and his spirit never\nshattered.\nLong had he waited, oh yes, to rejoin the Mages Guild. Ambros as arch-mage, missing before, had returned, and he was thrilled. Months did he stay there before the guild came to fall, then the boy packed his things, his miserable rings, and flew\noff on erroneous wings. Abominations took the guild, so the boy had nowhere to go. Nothing to do, no friends to turn to, the boy would wander where he did not know. Darkness and shadow followed him, wherever the lad went, until a being of bone sucked out\nhis soul, and the threads of fate were sown. Regret, sorrow, and anger, filled his narrow frame. He hurt not a soul in his madness, though his death was assured, all the same. A cleric ended the lich's existance, though the bones did not fight back; and\nso is the story of the clerical order's high prophet, braxis's false glory.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Tome", "Anonymous", "The Stonekin\n\nThe Descendants of Urguan were a hearty people, sturdy in form and idealogy. With this, of course, came those who were a tad too zealous for their own good. with a culture and lifestyle idolizing\nthe mountains and rock, there came a group almost religious about their love and adoration for the peaks of stone that were about the lands. With this exaltation for hte strong and grand, this group turned more passionate\nthan expected; the loose collection of arcane artists within the cult gathered with the most devout and ardent to the order of Faith of the Mountain Leaders with an idea that would further their works, believed\nto bring them to divination. They tried to make the perfect being, and after several decades of experimentation and testing, something was made.\n\nThe Faith of the Mountain\nproduced numerous of these creations after their original birth, seen as the most true and correct embodiments of their emotions for the glorius mounds of compact perfection.\n\nThese supposed\n\"supreme beings\" were far from so, but anyone may think what they will. In truth, these men made one of the first collaborations of the natural elements into an entity; an elemental. These beings are the\ntrue works of stone masonry in its most primal and gruttal fashion. Morphs shaped of pebbles to boulders, these things lacking sentience were none the less exalted and glorified, called gods and avatars\nfor their creative powers, able to format all things earthen. Although, they were correct in partial; they represented the mountains, the rocks, the unmoving and forever tough. And it was so, the Stonekin outlasted the children of\nUrguan. It is said that, to this day, they remain in the last shrine of the Faith of the Mountain, home to the last trio of Stonekin, the elementals forever locked away in their own tor, a crag made just for these three men\nof gem and subsurface grain.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Torn Scrap", "Thurak'Yar", "‘... -n Uruk by the name of Ghorash. Stumbling into my blarg, she pleaded for my help. No Feuruk would find a mate as a cripple, and without her arm there was no hope for the youngest of her cubs. Reluctantly, and without promise, I agreed\nto the attempt. Her own limb had been lost in the klomp, so I improvised with that of a dead Feuruk I’d preserved from last moon. Despite my doubts, with the aid of Akezo the torn flesh of the woman stitched and fused back together; accepting\nthe new limb no doubt with thanks to the prompting of the well-meaning spirit. The attachment was simple, however several more precise blessings were required to aid Ghorash in her finer motor controls.\nUnfortunately, I forgot my limits. Nothing may last forever, and to change and warp flesh is to take control of the fate of nature. The blessing Akezo gave was only temporary, and weeks later the limb grew fetid, and the Feuruk died.’", ""),
        StaticBook("A Valiant Soldie", "Kaiser Decimus Rovin, Vandalore", "===Heil Vandaloria===\n\n\n\n\n    The Valiant Soldier\n\n\n\n    Approved by the\n    Reich Ministry of\n    Enlightenment and\n    Truth\nYoung Billy played in his garden the day they came. A soaring Eagle upon their tabards, these were the Kaisereichs finest. \n\nHe watched them parade into the Caliphate, and watched a throng of curious people begin to cheer them into the city, eager to see\nthe fighting men and women of the Kaisereich. The clapping continued for hours as the troops poured in, banners held high, it was if it was a public show of solidarity with the common man. \n\nYoung runners ran beside the parade, handing out small\nflyers to those who would take them. The flyers listed places men and women alike could sign up for the Imperial Army, the fighting force of the Kaisereich. One such places was the Recruitment office in the Caliphate, near the main gate.\nAlongside the runners and the parade, men on horseback and wearing a thick leather coat and a snazzy black cap were the Ministry of Truth Officers. They were helping to distribute flyers to the runners. They handled the media, arts and news within the\nKaisereich. \n\nAnd last but not least, came the officers of the Kaisereichs Imperial Army. The mighty officers, and the Champion of Hallowvale, a Caliphate local! The black children in the crowd roared as they saw him. They shouted \"Praise the Champion!\"\nOne of the officers at the end of the parade brought forth a large rolling float - and people saw a portrait of the Kaiser. As the float came closer and closer, a figure emerged through the portrait - it was only the Kaiser himself! The crowd roared with\npraise and love, the parading soliders could barely contain the crowd for their show of unabashed dedication to the Kaiser. He waved to the crowd, and made a stirring speech about re-vitalization of the core aspects of the Caliphate, and how we as a\npeoples were going to make the Caliphate better for all. \n\n\n            PRAISE\n              THE\n            KAISER", ""),
        StaticBook("A Verse", "Anonymous", "The boarman walked on the mountain high,\nTo the altar they built long ago,\nHe knew in his heart twas his time to die,\nFor the shaman said it was so.\n\nBut his heart did not race in fear,\nHe knew why he must now go,\nUp on the slopes of the mountain high,\nTo the altar they built long ago.\n\nSick we was not nor old in age,\nBarely past his twenty-first year,\nYet he went to the altar to gods of\nrage,\nOf flame, fure, fire and fear.\n\nHe climbed upon the icy peak,\nThe altar lay just ahead,\nIt was time to discard this body weak,\nHe would die but would not be dead.\nHe carved the circle in frozen mud,\nIn the center of a dwarven bone,\nHe marked the ring with elven blood, Intermixed with the thanhic stone.\n\nHe struck the ring with his ancient spear,\nAnd the altar lit in voidflame,\nThe thanhium burned purple and clear,\nAnd thus over the mountains it came.\n\nHuge white teeth and huge black wings,\nHellish thunder in flight,\nArchon of terror, devourer of kings,\nThe dragon flew through the night.\n\nThe boarman stood with spear in hand,\nAs the mighty beast did near,\nHe stood in thanhic ring of flame,\nHis heart devoid of fear.\nThe dragon swooped and in one sweep,\nConsumed the boarman whole,\nBut this was not the sign of boarman weak,\nFor this battle was of the soul.\n\nThe dragon writhed, its mind\naflame,\nTwo minds fought for control,\nThe beast did falter in infinite pain,\nConquered by boarman's soul.\n\nVictorious he roared with fiery breath,\nHis new wings took\nhim to the sky,\nAnd then he tumbled and fell to his death,\nFor boars know not how to fly.", ""),
        StaticBook("A Warning.", "Kairn", "{\"text\":\"\"}", ""),
        StaticBook("A White Tome", "Ashanaak", "[!] The tome is bound in white leather, with only a silver diamond encircling a silver dot embossed on the front cover: the Clerical Eye. Its pages would be marblesque in color, with silver trim, and the writing a flowing font in black ink. [!]\n>>--<<=+=>>--<<\nThe Utterings of Tahariae\n>>--<<=+=>>--<<\n\n\n\nAssembled and Recorded by the Prophet Ashanaak in Vailor\n------=+=------\nThe Five Sacred Tenets of Tahariae\n------=+=------\n\nAs Recorded by the Prophet Braxis in early Anthos:\n\n\n[I] Shelter and protect thy brethren.\n[II] Cannot stray from thy faith.\n\n[III] Shall guide the lost.\n\n[IV] Shall not kill, harm, or spill the blood of the innocent.\n\n[V] Abuse of thine gift and punishment shall fall to you.\nAs Interpreted by the High Priest Heshakomeu Sohae in late Athera:\n\n\n[I] Shelter and protect thy brothers and sisters in Tahariae.\n\n[II] Do not stray in thy dedication to Tahariae.\n[III] Guide and protect the Descendants of the Four against the Impure.\n\n[IV] Do not spill the blood of the innocent.\n\n[V] Do not use Tahariae's blessing for anything other than His will.\n------=+=------\nThe Four Revelations\n------=+=------\n\nAs Recited through the Prophet Ashanaak by the Blessed Aengul of Purity, Tahariae, between the years 1436 and 1442:\n---------------\nThe Revelation of Judgment: The Apokalytiria\n---------------\n\nI am the Glory of the Rising Sun. I am the Bringer of Light. I am the Lord of Purity and the Archon of Justice. I am the Vehement Lord. I am Tahariae. Witness Me, My Chosen\nProphet. Witness Me.\n\nI, the Divine Stag, He Who Walks in Pure Light, the Great Purge, have merely watched My Servants for too long. You have Divided, and Divided you shall fall. You failed to Understand the Mission I had given you, and so you have\ndeteriorated into mere wandering healers and warriors who know only my Blessed Name. Your mockery of Faith and Piety offends Me, and so I return once more. Witness Me, Ashanaak, My Chosen Prophet. Witness Me.\n---------------\nThe Revelation of Wisdom: The Eight Woes\n---------------\n\nAnd now, my Chosen Prophet, recite My Sacred Words to thy Brothers and Sisters, or My Blessing shall be torn from their hearts for their Sins:\n[I] Woe to those that turn against Me and My Mission, for they must be cast out of thy ranks.\n[II] Woe to those who only know Me for My Blessing, and not for My Mission, and do not recognize Me and My Glory in prayer except when My power is needed.\n[III] Woe to those who refuse to heal the Innocent, to return to Pure form what has been rendered Impure.\n[IV] Woe to those who let the Impure, the Beings of Darkness and Evil, and the Descendants who bring harm to the Innocents, walk free when they cross thy path.\n[V] Woe to those who cause harm to the Innocent, for they are those you are sworn to protect.\n[VI] Woe to those who are sexually immoral, who give themselves outside of the holy bed of marriage, who dirty themselves and thus dirty the Name they represent.\n[VII] Woe to those who give themselves over to perpetual drunkenness and intoxication, for they dirty themselves and thus dirty the Name they represent. Such is only acceptable in celebrations and festivities.\n[VIII] Woe to those who steal, cheat, and utter vulgarities, who abuse the Innocent with their tongues and hands.\n---------------\nThe Revelation of Valor: The Eight Commands\n---------------\n\nAnd now, Ashanaak, I give you these Commands. Recite My Sacred Words to thy Brothers and Sisters, that you may live a Pure life:\n[I] Go forth, and spread not just my Light among the Descendants, but My Words and the Truth of My Glory.\n[II] Go forth, and become true Clerics among the Descendants: scholars, protectors, and leaders, to guide the Innocent with words, justice, and wisdom.\n[III] Go forth, and guard yourself against marriage, for service to Me demands complete and total commitment. If you do marry, do not marry those who serve Darkness and Evil, for in doing so you taint the very nature of holy matrimony.\n[IV] Go forth, and kneel before Me in maniael: private prayer, gratitude, and submission before My Glory when you awake and before you sleep.\n[V] Go forth, and kneel before Me in manaakh: prayer, worship, and submission in fellowship with your Brothers and Sisters in Me once each Elven year.\n[VI] Go forth, and respect the fallen clerics who I have sent back to you, to listen and follow what they have returned to say. And when their wisdom has been imparted, that you will have the strength and courage to send them back to My Halls.\n[VII] Go forth, and follow My Chosen Prophet, for they shall be My Light in the darkness of the world for you.\n[VIII] Go forth, and in My Blessed Name, use any means necessary to bring Purity to the Taint and Darkness that has become the world. The realm must return to how it once was, when the Four once ruled in Peace and Taint had not yet manifested. Through the\nsuffering My Mission shall bring you, remember that it is not out of Moral Good that you must do these things, but out of Necessity.\n---------------\nThe Revelation of Hope: The Anachorisi\n---------------\n\nDo all that I have commanded for you to do, and do not do all I have commanded you not to do, and you shall not perish. Nay; once suffering consume you and you leave thy mortal coil,\nI shall welcome you into My Sacred Halls, to forever be with Me in Purity, to forever be witness to My Sacred Light, forever and ever more.\n------=+=------\nThe Anamniseis\n------=+=------\n\nThe Interactions the Blessed Aengul of Purity, Tahariae, has had with His Clerics, as Recorded and Collected by the Prophet Ashanaak:\nIt was in Aegis that the Blessed Aengul of Purity, Tahariae, first came to mortalkind, but He did not reveal His blessed name. In mortal man’s wonder at His eternal and pure radiance, He was worshipped as the Creator, and only the most holy of clerics\ncould serve Him.\n\nIt was in Asulon, Aerial, the Arch-Aengul, lost the ability to grant power to Her servants, and the Aengul Gazardiael withdrew His blessing, thus why He is known as the Abandoner. Tahariae, in His infinite kindness, granted the clerics\nHis Blessing, so the holy work of healing and the purging of evil could be done still, yet He still did not reveal His blessed name, and the clerics believed their patrons to be Aerial and Gazardiael.\n\nIt was in early Anthos that He revealed Himself and\nHis Sacred Mission of Purity to the Prophet Braxis, that the bodies of Mankind be whole and protected from the forces of evil, and that taint be removed from the realm, and that the realm be returned to the Purity that existed when the Four Brothers first\nwalked the Earth.\n\nIt was in late Anthos, when the clerics were few and Setherien and the wraiths threatened the realm, that Tahariae chose four to be His champions, perfect representations of His four aspects: Judgment, Wisdom, Valor, and Hope.\nTogether, they fought the many threats to the purity of the realm.\n\nIt was in late Thales Tahariae summoned forth His clerics to investigate a portal, through which we were to enter. The Abandoner, Gazardiael, confronted Tahariae, and the two did battle\natop the mountain on which the portal was located. After the battle, and Gazardiael briefly pushed back, Tahariae manifested before the gathered clerics and their allies as a radiant white stag. He told us the portal required two keys to open, as well as\nthe Itharel of Wisdom Hosper, who had been captured by forces unknown. “O Lord, can you tell us where Hosper is?” I asked. “Nay,” He responded. “A dark force clouds My vision; I cannot see him.” So began the quest for the keys began, of which I was\nnot\ninvolved any further in.\n\nIt was in Athera that the Order of the White Lance, a union between the servants of Tahariae and Xan, Aengul of Gaurdianship and Order, was formed. Alas, it was not to last, and so began the Schism and the Conflict of Light. In\nlate Athera, Aldrik, Second Itharel of Hope, was given a vision by Tahariae commanding the Clerics and the Paladins to reunite, but such did not come to pass.\n\nIt was in Vailor that Tahariae commanded His followers to leap from high places and He would\nsave us. It was a test of faith to prepare us for His message: the realm was passing through a Great Cycle that must occur, lest the world be consumed by the Destroyer. However, in order for the Realm to be protected, four Harvesters wielding scythes must\ncut down many innocents. “For it is not out of Moral Good or Holiness that we do these things, but out of Necessity,” sayeth Tahariae unto us. So great was His insistence we trust in Him that two of us briefly lost connection to Him for not leaping\nenough, but they were forgiven, and His Blessing returned to their hearts.\n\nIt was many years later, amidst the assault of the clerics by the Being known as the Undying One, that Tahariae revealed His new commands by speaking through the cleric Ashanaak,\nchoosing him to be His next Prophet.\n\nSuch is the history of the actions of the Blessed Aengul of Purity, Tahariae, in our realm. May our actions be a testament to the Glory of Tahariae, Blessed Be His Name.\n[!] An ornate picture of a stag facing the viewer is here. Its antlers are tall and winding, and a radiant halo surrounds his head. [!]", ""),
        StaticBook("A. by A. (VOL1)", "Ashur", "===================Archery by Ashur\nVOL I:\nCreation & Manufacture\n===================\n\n*A magnificent drawing is depicted of a Dark Elf ranger, standing amidst numerous bodies of undead thralls.*\nI am Ashur Uilosson of Laurelin, once Prince of the Holy Princedom of Malinor. My eyes have seen the rise and fall of kingdoms, races, and entire worlds. I can tell you from experience that life as we know it has few constants.\n\nArchery is one of the\nArchery's roots trace beyond the first Holy Princedom, to the bands of Wood Elves that used them for hunting and defense. While each race has adapted the tool to their own devices, the art of the bow and arrow has several uses, to include:\n-- Hunting of small and large game\n\n-- Battle and warfare\n\n-- Competitive sport\n\n-- Exercise of the chest, shoulder, arm, and hand musculature\n\n-- Wooing the attractive sex\nThe new bowman should devote their time to learning the upkeep and components of their tool. Many owners simply purchase a bow from a merchant and leave it to rot in a chest, only to experience failure.\n\nStudy these terms:\nBACK - The side of the bow facing away from you, and towards the target.\n\nBELLY - The side of the bow facing towards you when properly gripped.\n\nBOWSTRING - A woven cord of varying material attached at the limb tips.\nBRACE HEIGHT - The location in the bowstring where you nock (place) your arrow.\n\nCENTER SERVING - A thicker portion of the bowstring, either reinforced material or a sleeve, centered in the middle of the string. Used for reinforcement.\nEND SERVING - Same as the center serving, but on the ends of the bowstring. The loops of the string are typically made from these.\n\nLIMB TIP - The far end of the bow, usually shaped like an arrowhead with a narrow base for the bowstring's loop.\nLOWER LIMB - The bottom half of the bow.\n\nNOCKING POINTS - A pair of beads sometimes placed in the bowstring to mark the center serving. Arrows are nocked between the beads.\nSTRING NOCK - The loop on either end of the bowstring, usually made by \"whipping\" or re-weaving the fibres of the end cord back into the string after tying a loop. Made of the end serving.\n\nUPPER LIMP - The top half of the bow.\nWhen selecting the wood for the bow, you judge the candidate by two factors: type of wood, and amount of imperfections. Woods vary in texture, strength, and lifespan, and some seemingly feeble flaws in the wood only worsen over time.\nConsider oak, lemon tree, hickory, yew, black locust, or teak as a solid hardwood. Some woods are flexible, such as juniper or mulberry. Make sure your wood is completely dry, however; if you must cut it while still green, give it 3-4 weeks to dry out.\n1) CURVE\nAll wood has a natural curve. Place your wood on the ground, holding the top tip with one hand, and press lightly against the middle. It will swivel so that its natural belly faces you, and its back faces out. Mark these appropriately.\n2) SHAPE\nPlace the bottom end of the bow on your foot, one hand atop the bow, and use a knife to shave (on the belly only) ontil the upper and lower limbs curve the same way. You want it strongest and thickest in the middle, at the handhold.\n3) NOTCHES\nCut notches that begin at the sides and curve around toward the belly of the bow. There should be one on each limb, within 2.5 - 5 cm from the tip. They should be just deep enough to hold the string in place without sacrificing integrity.\n4) BOWSTRING\nNot stretchy! Suitable materials for string creation are:\n\n-- rawhide\n-- hemp\n-- fishing line\n-- cotton or silk\n-- twine\n\nBut they must be braided, or they will fail.\nYou can either braid the strands by hand until they are tight, or you can tie them next to each other on a stick, on both ends. Then twirl the two sticks in opposite directions until the fibres bind together into a solid strand. Experiment!\n5) TILLER\nHang the bow upside-down from a limb or rafter and slowly pull the string downwards, watching how your bow reacts. It should stretch comfortably to the length of your arm extended from the shoulder.\n6) ARROWS\n*A beautiful diagram of the common hunting arrow is displayed*\n\nARROWHEAD - an attachment to the arrow's point. Metal, rock, glass, bone - all are effective.\n\nFLETCHING - feathers attached below the nock, for stability.\nINDEX FLETCHING - a differently-colored fletching that will face outward from the bow, towards you, when nocked.\n\nNOCK - The U-shaped end of the arrow that is tucked into the string.\n\nPOINT - The bare end of the arrow.\nSHAFT - The body of the arrow.\n\nArrows should either be half the length of the bow, or the distance between the brace height and middle of the bow when drawn. You can shape arrows by holding them gently above hot coals, then held straight while cooli\nArrowheads can be attached by shaping a U-shaped notch into the point, and shaping the bottom of the arrowhead to fit snugly within. Ensure it's weighted equally on both sides so it doesn't throw off the arrow's flight.\n7) STORAGE\nUnstring your bow when not being used, to increase its lifespan. Keep stored in a cool, dry place - or however you can manage. Working folk like you and I don't have the best options sometimes... and we can bend the rules from time to time.§", ""),
        StaticBook("A. by A. (VOL2)", "Ashur", "===================Archery by Ashur\nVOL II:\nApplied Skills & Training\n===================\n\n*Here lies an illustration of a Dark Elf, instructing four younger elves as they practice on an archery range.*\nI am Ashur Uilosson of Laurelin, once Prince of the Holy Princedom of Malinor. My eyes have seen the rise and fall of kingdoms, races, and entire worlds. I can tell you from experience that life as we know it has few constants. Archery is one of them.\nOnce the aspiring bowman has familiarized themselves with proper creation and upkeep of the bow, as detailed in Volume I, the next obvious step is learning how to be effective with one. This is far simpler to write on paper than to actually practice.\nThese rules are geared towards expanding your knowledge and skills, and not a single one should be skipped. I can say from centuries of experience that these tenants will always hold true, given enough time and training.\n--STANCE\nYou should be standing with your chest sideways, in perspective with your target. Right-handed shooters (the hand you grip the arrow with) should face the right, and the reverse for left-handed archers.\n--STANCE, CONT.\nSpace your feet to shoulder width, rotating them so they point directly in line with your body - facing to the right or left. Stand straight, naturally aligning your spine, and rotate your chin over the shoulder (or as close as you can.\n--STANCE, CONT.\nRotate your hips so they are tucked or rolled under your upper body. This flattens your lower back, which allows you to lower your chest and ribs downward towards your stomach. Also push your shoulders downward.\n--STANCE, CONT.\nWhen practicing, you should go through these steps like a checklist, every single time you fire an arrow. Repetitions develop muscle memory, and muscle memory will separate you from the whelp fighting on the other side.\n--STANCE, CONT.\nStay focused and sharp while practicing. Initially, you should fire 30 arrows in one session before ending for the day. With each successful day, add more arrows, minding your stance with every single one. Remember: muscle memory.\n--NOCKING\nEach archer has their own style when nocking the arrow to the string, and there is no single doctrine for this manuever. This is simply something you must practice until it flows for you, which is uncomfortable at first. Persevere.\n--NOCKING, CONT.\nThe nock of the arrow should /snap/ onto the string the same way and the same amount of force, with every single arrow. This is why handcrafting your arrows is so important! You must build them to your specific bowstring.\n--NOCKING, CONT.\nNocks that fit differently, even by a little, will influence the arrow's flight. Loose nocks might indicate that your center serving is compressed and needs replacement. Adjust your equipment as such, ensuring the best for your gear.\n--GRIP\nThe grip of the bow should sit in the web of your hand, between your thumb and index finger. Position your thumb so that it points at your target when the bow is raised. Then, relax your other fingers as they wrap 'round the grip.\n--GRIP, CONT.\nWhen the bow is raised, the knuckles on the arrow side should create a 45 degree angle. The bow hand must remain absolutely still through the entire shot process.\n\nNow, onto your bowstring hand.\n--GRIP, CONT.\nGently wrap the first 3 fingers of your bowstring hand below the arrow shaft. The string should be at the first groove of the fingers, with the index finger pushed up against the dock of the arrow. Your drawing hand must be relaxed and fl\n--SETUP\nWith a proper grip prepared, extend the bow-arm forward, its elbow rotated vertically. The forearm of your bowstring hand should remain as close to, or parallel to, the arrow during the next action.\n--SETUP, CONT.\nRaise the bow-arm slightly above nose level, pointing towards your target. As you do so, rotate your chest and shoulders so your upper body is parallel to the arrow. The bowstring hand should be raised to nose level as well.\n--SETUP, CONT.\nThe bow-arm's elbow should be rotated to the inside joint at the elbow is straight up and down.\n\nNote that you still haven't pulled the arrow back. This comes next! Be patient and mindful. Every step counts.\n--DRAWING\nDraw the bowstring back towards the side of your face, ending in a position where your index finger rests at the far corner of your mouth. Move your drawing arm back and down as far as possible. Picture your shoulder blade rotating.\n--ANCHOR\nPoint your thumb down into the palm of your hand, and fold your little finger toward the palm as well. Press your hand snug against the side of your face. Note that the bowstring should be located just off the side of your nose.\n--ANCHOR, CONT.\nYour anchor position is when you are primed to fire, holding the whole draw weight of the bow. It must be EXACTLY THE SAME, every single time you do it, if you want consistency in your archery. Practice constantly. Obviously.\n--TRANSFER\nNow you will move the draw weight from your arms and shoulders, into your back. Align the bow arm and shoulder through your chest to be in-line with the drawing arm shoulder. Rotating your upper torso helps here.\n--TRANSFER, CONT.\nMove your drawing arm elbow directly behind the arrow, holding this position with your back muscles. The forearm and the back of your drawing hand should be relaxed.\n\nBy doing this, you free your arms of unneeded agony.\n--AIMING\nLook down the shaft of the arrow, placing the point on the target, also using your thumb as a reference. Quickly check your form: weight evenly distributed? Hips over your knees and feet? Chest down? Lower back flat?\n--AIMING, CONT.\nCarefully sight up the bowstring and line it up with the center of the bow limbs. Remember that by this point, your back muscles should be supporting the bulk of the draw weight.\n--FIRING\nBegin the release process by expanding from the center of the chest, outward, towards your shoulders. At the same time, allow the bowstring to leave your fingers. Pushing it slightly may help you when learning.\n--FIRING, CONT.\nOpen the fingers in a crisp but smooth motion. If you slowly open your fingers and let it roll off, you risk the arrow deviating from the path. Snap the fingers open and let it fly straight.\n--FIRING, CONT.\nContinue the expansion process by letting your chest expand, increasing the intensity of your back muscles at the same time. Using the diaphragm here is a helpful aid when first learning to harness the lower muscles.\n--FOLLOW-THROUGH\nThere is a minute, but very much real, period of time inbetween the arrow's release and when the arrow fully leaves the bow. If you muck about with your stance during this moment, your arrow will fly falsely, and do you discredit.\n--FOLLOW-THROUGH, CONT.\nContinue to engage the back muscles. Your drawing hand should move straight back along the side of your dace, stopping slightly below and behind the ear. The bow arm should keep the bow completely still. Remain until arrow leave\nThese are the steps to success as an archer. There are no hidden secrets here - the path to skill only asks diligence and patience. Even if you are not naturally inclined towards the art of archery, your dedication and practice will supercede\n--natural talents by far.\n\nPractice is the only way. Find a target, prepare your tools, and begin immediately. The best time to practice was yesterday, and the second best time is right now.", ""),
        StaticBook("A. by A. (VOL3)", "Ashur", "===================Archery by Ashur\nVOL III: Hunting \n===================\n\n*The cover page depicts a Dark Elf concealed behind low brush, nocking an arrow while herds of swine graze in the valley before him.*\nI am Ashur Uilosson of Laurelin, once Prince of the Holy Princedom of Malinor. My eyes have seen the rise and fall of kingdoms, races, and entire worlds. I can tell you from experience that life as we know it has few constants. Archery is one of them.\nHunting is archery's most valuable application. It provides food for yourself and others, hides to tan and apply as raw material, it sharpens the senses... and if caught somewhere you ought not be, it provides a decent excuse.\nBefore setting out, you should have solid knowledge of the terrain and local life. Unfamiliar plants and animals alike can spell disaster for the inexperienced hunter, so consult with a local expert beforehand. Maps are a bonus, if available.\nWhether your excursion is 8 hours or 8 days, you should be prepared to handle any malfunction in your equipment, yet light enough with your gear to move nimbly across the landscape. Consider this list as a starting guide:\n-Arrows (10 x day)\n-Boots\n-Bowstring (x2)\n-Cloak\n-Compass\n-Flint & Steel\n-Foods (dried)\n-Gloves\n-Map\n-Pack\n-Quiver\n-Rain protection\n-Skinning knife\n-Sleep system (sack, pad, rain sheet)\n-Socks (x2 pair)\n-Spare arrowheads\n-Spare fletchings\n-Spare twine\n-Tinder supply (fire)\n-Water supply (3 liters x day)\n\nThis is by no means definitive. Change and adapt as you see fit.\nThe first style of hunting I will cover is \"Stationary\", commonly used to survey a single location or plot of land over time. This requires more preparation but arguably yields the most material for the hunter, given practice and training.\nWhen surveying land as possible hunting ground, you should ask yourself questions:\n\n--How far is the nearest town, on foot or by horse?\n--Do others use this land?\n--How difficult is the terrain?\n--Is my prey small enough to manage by myself, or do I need others?\n--Are there any hostile plants or animals I should be aware of?\n--Are there any temples/shrines to unholy gods that will possess me? (I'm serious, by the way.)\nYou should judge the land based on where and how you can position two constructs: the FUNNEL, and the STAND.\n\nFUNNELs are landmarks, natural or manmade, that force wildlife down a certain path, \"funneling\" them a specific direction.\nSTANDs are locations for you to operate out of while hunting. The tried-and-true method is an elevated wooden platform reached via ladder, which gives you an aerial view of the funnels you're observing. Also good against bandits.\nBefore setting out for your trek, however, you must remove the scent from yourself and all other equipment. Unfamiliar scents will doom you to ill results, so you must thoroughly embrace an easily-accessible scent: smoke. Smoke yourself and your gear.\nOnce you've covered your scent, set out for your stand with several hours before your prey is most active. Moving efficiently through the wilderness is a topic worthy of several books, but I'll summarize the key tenants here:\n--When walking, keep your weight on the back foot, stepping with the outer ridge of your feet and slowly rolling inwards.\n--If you make a loud noise, freeze completely for at least 5 minutes. The animals are watching you, more than likely.\n--Take your time. There's no rush, assuming you planned properly.\n-Scramble your steps rather than walking at a steady pace. The normal walking cadence of a hunter will scare off everything in sight. Be creative.\nOnce you've arrived, take the time to get yourself comfortable and prepared. It may be several hours before your prey is out and about. Remember to keep noise to a minimum, and don't do anything to spread unfamiliar scents in the area.\nOnce your prey is in sight, resist the urge to attempt a kill immediately. Take the time to observe its movements - what is it doing? Are there others near? Is it acting odd? The animsl may know something you don't, and observation will reveal it to you.\nIf practicable, a shot to the brain will provide the cleanest and most efficient kill. Otherwise, aim for the \"boiler room\" - the heart and lungs. Merciful kills provide the least suffering for the animal, and the least effort to recover its body.\nBeware, however, the \"instant drop\", when your prey collapses immediately when struck. It may simply be in shock, only to recover and lash out when you approach. If this happens, wait a few minutes and watch the beastie before moving to claim it.\nMore often than not, however, your prey will travel for several meters before meeting its demise. For this reason, mentally mark the exact location where you shot the animal before moving there. Firing another arrow as a mark may be useful for novices.\nOnce you find the location, use all of your senses to find its blood trail. Don't just look on the earth! Low brush may bear signs of passage. Listen for movement or cries. Check for the smell of blood or urine. See if its tracks deteriorate over time.\nOnce you find the corpse, don't succumb to the temptation of waltzing up and poking it to check for life. For example, if the thing's eyes are closed, it is almost certainly still alive. A blade or bolt to the jugular will mercifully end its suffering.\nRecovery of the animal should be planned out in advance. You should strive to harvest as much of the animal as you can, so if you are hunting prey too large to handle by yourself... bring more hunters. A simple solution to a simple problem.\nMy recommendation would be to bring the entire animal with you, all parts included, and dissect it in the comfort of your home. Brains are used for tanning, the organs can be eaten or sold, the meat can be cooked, and so forth. Be meticulous. Use it all.\n-=MOBILE HUNTING=-\n\nThis skill requires FAR more practice and knowledge, covering multiple topics: tracking, animal lore, concealment, and geography among them. Hunting in this manner is used when on the move... or the run, more accurately.\nDisguising yourself and your signs becomes paramount. Interweave local fauna with your clothing and avoid situations where you may stand out - for example, if you stand on a ridge and your prey is below, your body is easily visible on the horizon.\nConsider moving through regions once, and only once, to minimize your scent and impact.\n\nTruly, your goal with mobile hunting is not to discover prey, but SIGNS of prey, and follow them to their makers. This requires discretion and attention to detail.\nAs previously stated, you should find a local expert of the land and ask for his wisdom on local hunting grounds, animal behavior, any local banditry, and so forth. This will set you leagues above the other whelps out hunting for the same herd as you.\nThis is only the briefest of introductions to hunting with the bow and arrow, and while secondhand experiences are helpful for the student, the only true way to learn is practice, constantly and repeatedly. I wish you luck with your excursions.", ""),
        StaticBook("Abstract", "[Sohaer] Kalenz", "The Valleian'Acal of Larihei and the effects on the physical body.\n\nLomanih et al.; Malin's Welcome 198\n\nIn this paper we will attepmt to categorize, without searching for too through an explanation, the effects of enchanted\ngold-infused pools. We will begin with the physical alterations and come to accept that continued exposure to the pools will only lighten one's skin and hair tones while potentially increasing a subject's height. We see that neither of these effects are\nharmful to the physical body.\n\nWe will also conclude that these pools bolster an Elf's magical prowess and their natural talent to channel the Tanya that flows through our earth. We will illustrate why this increased acuity only has\npositive effects, and the suggestion of hallucinations or a clouding of judgement is beyond preposterous.\n\nFInally we shall discuss the benefits of bathing in the pools and encourage all of Malin's pure children to do the same.\nThis shall be done by introducing a concept known as the maehr'sae hiylun'ehya, which will be the driving philosophy behind our arguments.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ace Soulblade II", "Vulkin Frostbeard", "~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~Private Journal of \nAce Soulblade II. \n\n\nPropert of Ace Soulblade head of House Soulblade Noble house of the Kingdom of Oren.\n\n\n\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nDay 1: I travel with my father Ace and our Household under the flag of House Horen the Royal house and current seat of the King. We set forth abandoning asulon not knowing where we will end up but we have no choice.\nDay 2: We have set sail and left Asulon but I noticed something in the distance. I climb the Crowes nest of the ship, pulled out a scope and made out what seem to be dwarves. Not your typical Dwarves either. These were a tribal looking group, almost as if\nbarbaric. They seem familiar to that of a Frostbeard or even Irongut clan. Although these dwarves are different, they give off a fierce and hostile feeling. They been following us from a great distance showing no signs of threat but their presence is un-\nwelcome. I wish they would stop following us already.\n\n\nDay 3: We have finally made land although my memory is fuzzy as of late and unsure why. We currently travel inward with the Oren Goverment and military.\nDay 73: It has been some time since I last used the journal. We have made great progress rebuilding our lives and watching our kingdom grow again. I set out though in a few days to search for a place to build off a land of our own under the Kingdoms watch\nDay 75: I was scouting ahead of my party when I ran into the mysterious dwarves that we spotted at see. I was captured and had to fight for my life leaving me to become a slave after my survival in the fight.\nDay 192: I was caught using my journal, I was beaten and left with hunger and cold for days before they treated me like I was worth  keeping.\n\nDay 195: Since a few days ago I been aloud a little more freedom, I been given time to walk and talk-\namongst their kind learning a few words a day. I slowely feel like I am being accepted among them although I still have a long way.\n\nDay 199: I have managed to understand a bit of their language and in return managed to teach them how to speak a little of\nmine. We have finally managed to get communication to flow between us.\n\nDay 241: I have made great progress with the Dwarves that have captured me. I can now speak their language as well as they can speak mine. To my amazement this-\nclan of dwarves claim to be sons of Urguan under a bloodline of Thalv'ar Beastforge who they claim was the first of their clan.\n\n\nDay 908: I walk among them as a welcome outsider which none of done before. Much of the clan is still unknown for most\nof their history is gone but their history can be learned by translating an old language of their from the etchings in the blades of the weapons once held by their ancestors. They told me they use to tell stories to the younger generatiosn by etching them\ninto their blades.\n\nDay 1142: I am no longer captive and free to go. I leave wit hknowledge of an anciant Dwarven clan that calles themselves the Beastforge. They are a barbaric race that loves combat but most of all are curious.\nThey seek to be the best at combat, I sure hope they dont learn magik.\n\n\n\n\n\nMy final words in my journal after my long life, I just have one thing to say. Time\nwill never stop nor will the rise and fall of Clans and Households, Kingdoms and City states. I just wish mcjshe (the writing scribbles off before ending for reasons unknown. You speculate the passig was at that moment.)", ""),
        StaticBook("Acerbus' Journal", "Koraz", "Amber's Cold, 1476\n\nI've begun this journal as a way to record the tales of the strange events happening with the beginning of the Wars with Iblees. Already the Dwarves have opened a gateway to release the dreaded Daemon, and already many skirmishes have\narised, but none so great as this day. Today the Undead legions have launched their first true assault upon the Descendants by assaulting the Dwarven city of Kal'Agnar. I and my brothers have already stationed inside the walls despite our bad relations\nwith the Dwarves. All aid is accepted in this siege, for all have the same purpose- to bring death to Iblees and his minions.\n\nThe Seers claim there to be a devastating end to the Dwarves to come, yet under their direction we arrived to aid in the\ncombat eitherway. Kaegaz'arai claims it to be worth it for the defeat of as many Undead as could be slain. He and I have been hunting the Daemon's creations since their beginning. We've become quite too used to the smell of miasma by now, but it was our\nduty.\n\nFew of the dreaded Aengul's chosen ones have shown themselves, and if they have they would not stay long. Clerics, Paladins, Ascended. None do well in battles. Not that it matters- More Holy taint will only add to the fire.\nSnow's Maiden, 1476.\n\nThe Seers were correct. Despit all attempts at defense, the Dwarves have lost their Kingdom to the Undead General. My brothers and I escaped along with several others through the mountains after the brunt of our forces were\ndefeated. This merely meant the Undead were closer, and easier to reach.\nThe First Seed, 1479.\n\nThe Dwarves have reclaimed their home in a non-satisfactory battle. The kindred of Iblees have begun to disappear as they lose more and more skirmishes. This means our attention may turn to the Ascended and their elks. A few clerics\nhave begun to sprout up around the region. Kaegaz'aria and I have located one amongst the Dark Elves. \n\nThe cleric was alone when we arrived, but as blades were drawn his fellow Dark Elves began to appear one by one. Soon we were outnumbered\nseveral to just two of us. We were separated and fighting in a mere defensive operation, until finally a scream arose from those not near me.\n\nKaegaz's master had arrived. He had no true form to show, merely sound and power. He aided us in bringing\nthe Dark Elves to their knees until the cleric was butchered. Never had I seen such power, and I believe I never will again.\n1513, Date Unknown.\n\nThe remaining years of the Iblees War led to his inevitable downfall. Our Order has adapted to change, and now makes for the land known as Vailor.\n*Several pages, previously ripped from earlier chapters, are stuffed here. They're water logged and crumpled, unable to be read.\nThe Deep Cold, 1516.\n\nOur Guild, formerly known as Acerbus, has formed to become the New Order. Kaegaz'arai still holds reign, and I have risen, along with several new members, to a higher position amongst the ranks. We have grown in numbers with the\narrival in Vailor.\nThe First Seed, 1528.\n\nOur ranks have grown to never before seen numbers. Kaeg, although going by a different name, has become a Necromancer of great skill and has included the aid of Darker beings for our battles against the 'Holy' beings that still\nplague the realm. Our members hold high positions in numerous nations, and control more men than that of the Elves combined. Kaeg speaks of Xionism, though few know what it is.\n1540. Date Unknown.\n\nOur peace with the Clerics is beginning to recede as new beliefs arise. Xionism becomes well known by all for its Anti-God belief. Several Clerics attempt to reason with the leaders, but they have begun to hide in the cities of others\nI've begun delving into the Dark Arts, though the idea of being crippled stuns me. Kaegaz'aria seeks to search for ways to enable the Dark Arts without crippling our states. The belief comes from Dragon blood.\n1547, Deep Cold.\n\nThe Sunless Sanctum has been opened to all believers of Xionism, as a safe haven for dark beings. They say a beast of Old walks there, and we now serve him. Though I've seen not, I simply believe.\nThe Deep Cold, 1575.\n\nThere's been little to no action in Axios. Xionism is rarely spoken of, and Kaegaz'aria is rarely seen. I've adapted to the life as a Wood Elf, yet my hope for the Dragon Blood work does not end.\nSnow's Maiden, 1576.\n\nI've rarely slept for days, yet I find myself in a state of mind greater than any I have experienced before. My swordsmanship has rosen far pass that of the average Man or Elf. Several times I fell upon groups of bandits only to come\nout with their heads piked before the Wood Elf gates, whom, after my entry into their city, have grown more familiar with me. I've joined them in the defense of their lands on numerous occasions. With them I have also grown more acustom with Sutican\nlife, though as a guard it can get rather boring. Sutica was merely a stepping stone for me as I rose to my full strength, but provided a way to speak with the clerics that reside there and learn of their ways.\nAfter communing with the Clerics and Druids I once fought against. The ideas they hold beneath the layers of disgust are not to the point of disbelief. It is to my knowledge that the only real use of both are to protect the Descendants, which is fine by\nitself, yet both still wage war against the scum of Iblees. I find very strange of them to continue their reign in the mortal realm despite Iblees' passing.\nDate unknown.\n\nThough I've never been able to meet the fabled man, I've spent numerous hours reading what he's written in his own library, and mark his words. It is a truely interesting to read of a Harbinger, brought to his lowest,\nwho would later rise to the position as a peaceful Librarian. If this is indeed the end, I write this to mark the end of a leaf, and the beginning of a new.\nI, Koraz, Hand of Kaegaz'arai, past General of Acerbus, and later known Xionist under the false Old Lord, now end my story.", ""),
        StaticBook("Adam", "Beelzebub", "There once was a boy, he was named Adam.\n\nAdam loved to sit on his chair.\n\nAdam's mother warned him.\n\n\"Stop! The more you sloth upon that chair, the more you shall become but a potato!\"\nAdam said \"No!\" and continued to sit upon his chair.\n\nHe cried and cried, and whined and whined. He would not move.\n\nAdam loved to sit on his chair.\n\n\"Why should I move?,\" Adam asked.\nAdam grew fatter and fatter.\n\nHe began to bald.\n\nAdam grew larger.\n\nHe sat upon his chair and all night.\nAdam's mother warned him once again.\n\n\"You shall become a chair-potato, Adam!\"\n\n\"That is what happens to boys who idle upon chairs!\"\n\nHe did not move.\nAdam grew spotty.\n\nHe became very pudgy.\n\nHe slumped to the side and released a large sigh.\n\nHe was a big chair-potato.\nAdam's mother found him upon the chair.\n\n\"Now, he is more useful!\"\n\nAdam's mother brought him into the kitchen, and he was cooked.\n\nAnd his family feasted for many days!", ""),
        StaticBook("Aden's Ailment", "Idolom", "Aden's Ailment\n-------------------------------\n\nWritten by:\nIdolom Somari'gi Loh\nThe sun rose slowly over the sprawling harvested fields of Tonawanda, all was silent save the chirping birds of the adjacent forested lands.  The first men arose within the conglomerate of huts that made up their village, center and largest of them all\nhoused the chief, secular overseer of his people, and judge of those who violate their sacred tenets.\n\nTwas naught but half past six when the village had finally awoken in full, children dashing past the men preparing for the day's work as they playe\ntheir trivial games, woman ensuring each haven was in an acceptable state before they left to tend the fields and prepare the village feasts, as Harvest season had finally come to a close preparation for the comming snowfall was working in full force.\nAdditional bindings were tied down to each tent to keep them safe and stable for the additional weight and icy blowing winds, storage holes secured with bindings and nailed wood, children saw an end to their lessons from the elders as they transitioned\ninto schooling under their respective fathers for the wintertime.\n\nA lad named Aden, lived on the very outer edge of their conglomerate, an outcast in a greatly lessened sense, for Aden violated a tenet, as punishment his Chief deemed him unworthy of\ncivil life among his fellow tribesfolk, and demanded he recieve arcus.\n\nArcus, is an archaic and traditional lash upon those whom are disliked to such a degree that those in the village would rather see them perish slowly then sit before them in casu\nconversation. And such was poor Aden's fate.\n\nPoor of course, is a subjective label, his worthyness is not for I to hand out.\n\nIt was distributed by the eldest and most intelligent in their community, a vial\ndeposited into his cider during the daily communal feast, a blight that would implant itself into the darkest and most secure corners of his body as it slowly devoured his soul.\n\nFour weeks and a day had past before the very first outward signs of th\nailment were known to the public, his skin darkened in blots, boils appeared and he began to sweat like a pig wrapped in wool. Evenings past as coughing fits lasted well into the night and early morning, pure agony rocketed through his abdomen and chest,\neven his very heart struggled to beat.\n\nThe afternoon in which he finally met the Creator, his wife and daughter lay beside him in mourning, but his kinsmen knew what they had done was right and just in the eyes of law. Yet not all saw\nas clearly as they.\n\nTwas first the seas that shook, a leviathan like that of a man with tendrils who's height breached the clouds. Hell was layed upon the fields, salt from the oceans purged the future crop.\n\nNext the forests shook, from withi\nglowing yellow eyes emerged to ravage the passing children, the future of the village was wiped away in an evening. \n\nFinally, the swooping rage of the seas calmed, the howling rage of the woodlands dissappeared almost in an instant. The only sound\nremaining were the weeping mothers, and the depression-ridden fathers. Till it struck them, for Aden's ailement had evoked the rage of God himself, a punishment so cruel handed out by mere mortals was a crime alike none other.\n\nFor to play God, was t\nbathe one's self in acid.\n\nNearly a year had gone by, and the howling wind filled the landscape as it passed through the hollowing corpses and abandoned homes.", ""),
        StaticBook("Adunai : Rangers", "Arnorian", "~*~ Origins ~*~\n\nDuring the reign of Braen Elendil, the Adunian Lord came to see that with the ever rising population of their race, he would soon need a military to keep order instead of relying on the good will of his citizens. And so, he asked his\nbrother Cirimas to gather the most elite and loyal soldiers he could find, and bring them before him. The names of these four men were never recorded, but it is known that one was of house Lorsmor, and another of house Loyola. The five men stood before\nBraen, all were well trained in the arts of stealth, the longsword, shortsword, dagger and longbow, learning from their fathers and their father's fathers who had to outrun and hide from the Rhodir and other men of Owyn Godwinson during their exodus from\nIdunia. Braen decided that based on their traits these five men wouldbe known as the Adunian Rangers, and they would serve as their race's first personal military force. Over time, Cirimas and the four rangers slowly built traditions and organization\nthroughout the order, and gained more men along the way.\n\n~*~ Uniform ~*~\n\nTraditionally the rangers had no official uniform, but they were told to stick to a certain code to do with what items of clothing they wore;\nthe rangers would wear leather boots, fingerless leather gloves, a leather belt, a large coat and a cloak over the top. The coat of Cirimas was passed from Captain to Captain in the rangers, as was occasionally his cloak. All of this would be over the top\nof whatever other clothes they wished to wear.\n\n~*~ Fort Arendil ~*~\n\nThe rangers lived in a large cave system under the hill which Al'Ildic stood upon, though the caves had been renovated to be more like an underground\nfortress. Said fortress was named after Arendil, the last King of Idunia, and was meant to be used as a fallback point if Ildon ever came under a siege that it was unable to hold off. Sadly, in the final battle of Ildon in which it fell to the undead, few\nmanaged to get inside the fortress before it's gates had to be shut to save those inside.\n\n~*~ Watchtowers ~*~\n\nAs the Adunian Kingdom spread over the land, the ranger's decided that they could only keep order in Ildon itself from\nfort Arendil, and would have to find a way to do so in the towns, farms and estates that surrounded it. And so the watchtowers were built all over the kingdom. In each watchtower, a single ranger would live along with up to 15 other initiates whom he\nwould use to keep order in the surrounding area. The Garrison of each watchtower was rotated every fortnight.\n\n~*~ Traditions ~*~\n\n-The Final initiation for a ranger was for him to be taken out into\nthe woods where he would swear an oath of service to the Adunian Race.\n\n-No women or men of non-Adunian blood were permitted to join the rangers.\n\n-An heir to the Adunian throne would have to spend five years in service\nto the rangers before he could become lord.\n\nIt is often thought that it was tradition that all young men joined the rangers for several years after they turned seventeen, though in reality seventeen was simply the age where one was permitted entrance\ninto the order, and over time it became a popular trend to join the rangers as soon as one was allowed.\n\n~*~ Ranks ~*~\n\nRanger Captain: Supreme commander over the Rangers.\n\nFirst Ranger: The Captain's most\ntrusted ranger, and therefor second in command.\n\nRanger: The primary rank of the Adunian rangers, and only the most elite warriors in Adunian society were offered this position.\n\nInitiate: The recruit rank of Rangers, most never made it\npast this rank. \n\nAuthor's note:\n\nI myself attained the rank of First Ranger, in the time of Elysium.\n\nWritten and annotated by Arnorian Elendil.", ""),
        StaticBook("Adunai: Rangers", "Arnorian", "Origins\n\nDuring the Reign of Braen Elendil, the Adunian Lord came to see that with the ever rising population of their race, he would soon need a military to keep order instead of just relying in the good will of his citizens. And so, he asked his\nbrother Cirimas to gather the most Elite and loyal soldiers he could find and bring them before him. The names of these four men were never recorded, but it is known that one was of house 'Lorsmor' and another of house Loyola. The four men stood before\nBraen, all were well trained in the arts of stealth, the longsword, the shortsword, dagger and longbow, learning from their fathers, and father's fathers, who had to outrun and hide from the Rhodir and other men of Owyn Godwinson during their Exodus.\nBraen decided that based on their traits, these four men would be known as the Adunian Rangers, and that they would serve as their race's first personal military force. Over time, Cirimas and the four rangers slowly built traditions, and organization\nthroughout the order, and gained more men along the way.\n\nUniform\n\nTraditionally the rangers had no official uniform, but they were told to stick to a certain code to do with what items they wore; the rangers would wear\nleather boots, fingerless leather gloves, a leather belt, a large coat and a cloak over the top. The Coat of Cirimas was passed from captain to Captain, as was occasionally his clock. All of this would be over any other clothes they wished to wear.\nFort Arendil.\n\nThe rangers lived in a large cave system under the hill which Al'Ildic stood upon, though the caves had been renovated to be more like an underground fortress. Said fortress was named after Arendil, the last\nKing of Idunia, and was meant to be used as a fallback point if Ildon ever came under a siege it was unable to hold off. Sadly, in the final battle of Ildon in which it fell to the undead, few managed to get to the fortress before its gates had to be shut\nto save those inside.\n\nWatchtowers.\n\nAs the Adunian Kingdom spread over the land, the rangers decided that they could only keep order in Ildon itself from fort Arendil, and would have to find a way to do so in the towns, farms and\nestates that surrounded it. And so the watchtowers were built all over the kingdom. In each watchtower, a single ranger would live along with up to 15 initiates whom he would use to keep order in the surrounding area. The garrison of each watchtower was\nrotated every fortnight.\n\nTraditions.\n\nThe final initiation for a ranger was for him to be taken out into the woods where he would swear an oath of service to the Adunian race.\nNo men of non-Adunian blood were permitted to join the rangers.\nAn heir to the Adunian throne would have to spend five years in the service of the rangers before he could become the lord.\n\nIt is often thought that it was tradition that all young men\njoined the rangers for several years after they turned seventeen, though in reality seventeen was simply the age where one was permitted entrance into the order, and over time, it became a popular trend to join the rangers as soon as one was allowed.\nWritten and annotated by Arnorian Elendil.", ""),
        StaticBook("Adunaii I", "Arnorian Elendil", "In the first years of Aegis there were no Kingdoms or cities, but scattered and pitiful tribes of Men, Elves, Orcs, and Dwarves - The four great races. Buildings of stone and wood were unknown to the primitive people, who roamed the\nwild lands, fighting and travelling, never settling down or pausing to build. The chief tribe of mankind was the Harenite branch, a group of fair and tall warriors, strong hearted and led by Haren, the firstborn of Horen. Soon, as the tribes of mankind\nbranched out further and delved deeper into the more wooded areas of Aegis, the Harenites soon encountered a tribe of Elves - the Mali'Dun, let by Sarai of Malinor. Violence broke out very quickly, and a tribal conflict\nsoon developed. The war lasted for twenty years, until both tribes expended all that they could in a high-stakes battle over a hill called Caragdur. The results were catastrophic to both sides, with many of the warriors of either\ntribe being slaughtered in battle, or dying soon afterwards. Peace was declared out of necessity, and Haren and Sarai wed to cement the peace, and alliance.\n\nThe Descendants of Haren and Sarai\n(And the Harenites that followed their chieftan's example) found themselves physically different from their respective parent races, warranting a new name for themselves. The origins of the term Adunian, or 'Adunaii' are\nunknown, but that is what they called themselves. These new people were energetic, strong, healthy and fair, and found that in them was mixed the ambition of man, and the caution of Elves. This hybrid vigour led them to become the dominant race of\nancient Aegis, paving the way forward with many breakthroughs and records. It was the Adunians who first mastered the art of boat-building, as well as the Adunians who built the first fortified settlement - Idunia.\nThe Early Adunian society was hierarchal, Adunians on top, then Elves and remaining Harenites, and finally at the bottom, were the common Highlander folk that made up most of the rest of the human population.\nAs with most systems like that, this ended up breeding strife - A Highlander named Owyn fashioned himself as a rebel after two or three centuries of Adunian rule, and with surprising speed, force and tact overthrew the Adunian Royal\nfamily in a daring palace raid. The rebellion was swift, ending almost as quickly as it had started. The Monarchy was executed, and the Adunians were barred from ever stepping foot in the Kingdom of man under 'Godwinson's rule.\nThe King that had taken the blow was Arendil - Godwinson had slain him in the attack on the palace, but Arendil's son Elendil was more fortunate. He had been out of the palace on a hunting expedition, and informed by fleeing Adunians to not\nreturn to the Palace, but instead to turn North, to the White Coast. He quickly took his role as leader of the refugees, ordering a fleet of ships built, the flagships being called the Alcondaras and the Nen-Girith. They\nset sail as soon as the ships were sea-worthy, as it was a great fear that Godwinson would recind his choice of exodus, and simply chase the Adunians into the sea with horse and spear. The Exiles sailed for three months, braving\nSea-Blizzards, Iceburgs, and towering waves. Many ships did not survive the trip, as they were hastily put together. In the decades after this, every town or public building would have a shrine to the Atalante, those who were consumed\nby the icy depths. Tragedies behind them, at the begining of the fourth month, land was sighted in the form of a great Island, rich in all goodly things. The Island was called Al'Ildic, and the shore upon which the Adunii first landed was called\nCape Pelori.\n\nThe Adunians settled the shoreline and interior quickly, building simple thatch houses and wooden walls, to give them some solace while they surveyed the rest of the Island. Paths were soon\ncleared around the Island, and particularly around the signature landmark of the Island - Three Marble white peaks, dubbed 'Lalaith's Three' in memory of Arendil's slain wife. Elendil ordered a capital be build at the foot of these mountains,\nand this was soon done. The Capital was called Meneltarma. Many years passed under Elendils rule, and he had carried the Adunians through one of their most pressing times. By the time he passed on, the capital was no longer an shanty\ntown, but a solid city, of marble buildings and gold inlays. He passed the crown down to his youngest son Braen.\n\nBraen Elendil started many of the traditions that Adunians for centuries after held, the most\nnotable ones being the wearing of circlets, the carrying of Durendal and the system of inheritance. He set up the inheritance so that the youngest son of the family would always inherit, thus ensuring the longest possible\nrule for the Adunian Lord. The Circlet wearing came from Braen's possession of the Circlet of Horen, which the Adunians believed to have belonged to Horen himself, before it was given to Haren, and eventually passed down the line. Braen also\nhad himself a blade forged named Aranruth, a sword that held within it some strange power to fight evil. (Later, this blade would prove it's historical merit by being used agaisnt Harbingers effectively.) Braen also started an official military\nof the fledgeling Kingdom - the Adunian rangers, men skilled in the ways of the wild, with knife and bow, stealth and secrecy. Of course, there were very few actual threats to the Kingdom, so they were rarely used.\nBraen's grandson Amandil did a striking thing to highlight his reign as Lord; he ordered an expedition be made back across the sea to the original homeland, and mainland Aegis. More than 600 years had passed since the original\nexodus, and the old nobles of the court had desired to see what had become of Godwinson's people. They loaded the Alcondaras and the Nen-Girith with sailors and soldiers, along with the Royal retinue and crossed. They made it to the shorelines of\nAegis, made a camp, but for reasons unknown to us did not stay very long at all. They quickly returned, and Amandil had a quiet reign after that. Amandil's son Anarion led another expedition, this time laying eyes upon what the old world had\nbecome. Anarion encountered scouts from the Kingdom of Oren, and was brought to the city of Al'Khazar. Once the language barrier was breached, friendly relations and trade agreements quickly followed. This was the first contact\nthe Adunians had had with the outside world in over 700 years.\n\nPart I of Adunaii written by Arnorian Elendil, son of Artorus, son of Halboron.", ""),
        StaticBook("Aegian History", "The Pine Druid", "Aegian History\n\nThe Age of Founding and the Before Times\n\nDuring this time, Druids were brought together and the Druidic Order was founded by Wayward Druid Respiren Knox. The records of these times may never be fully\ndeciphered, however. Although we know of some of the first Druids in Malinor, such as Respiren, Swifthide, and Native, very few details are known. Perhaps someday the past regarding these times will be revealed.\nThe First Age of Reconstruction\n\nThe First Age of Reconstruction was ushered in by the initiation of Dragon Druid Apollan into the Order. Apollan was the first to express interest in joining the Order after reading\nthe \"Myth of the Great War\" and spent long hours in the Druid grotto after he was famously murdered outside the gates of Laurelin by a bandit. Apollan quickly moved up the ranks. Meanwhile, the Order gew. After initiating Apollan, Respiren expressed\ninterest in 'rebuilding the Order'. Everyday, new citizens of Aegis would show up in the grove wanting to become Druids. Most were immediately innitiated. As the Order grew, so did a number of things including property and knowledge. During this time the\nOrder gained a lodge in Ravenhold, as well as one in the Laurelin docks. Additionally, two stalls were bought at the Laurelin Marketplace and hundreds of books were added to the Order's library.\nThe Age of Abandonment\n\nUnfortunately, due to extremely casual and non-uniform rites of the Wayward Druid, people were joining the Circle, only to wander into the wilderness never to be seen again. With no established Hierarchy\nthe Order lacked organisation and purpose. This was made apparent when an Elf named Rowan arrived. Archdruid Rowan, arrived from a far off forest, seeking to re-establish his lost Circle, the Druids of Mother Aegis. Rowan is likely the only citizen to\nhave argued with the Order's ways. His only backing for title of Druid was that he 'had a love of nature'. When Rowan became annoyed by the fact that he had to be initiated in order to be allowed into the inner library he stormed off claiming he would\nstart his own order somewhere else. Rowan was last seen entering the wilds outside his home in the Ravenlodge, and was never heard from or seen again. Fortunately the dark Age of Abandonment was brought to an end by the light of the Dawn Druid Maiavel.\nThe Age of Reformation\n\nThe Age of Reformation was brought on by the ideas and drive of one Maiavel. With her determination, she was able to rally the remaining Druids to create an official lore, hierarchy, rules and sense of order.\nThe Second Age of Reconstruction\n\nDuring the second age of Reconstruction many more books were written and a Grand Tree in the Laurelin Grove was planted. New Dedicants were brought in but less frequently than in the first age, and the\ninitiation process was more careful and uniform. During this age, the current Hierarchy was established with Apollan as Archdruid.\nThe Age of Knowledge\n\nThe Age of Knowledge was ushered in by Petyr, the Salmon Druid. His creation of the \"Dedicant's Vow\" and the \"Attunement Ceremony\" greatly helped the Order as did his other ideas, including spearheading the wiki.\nThe Age of Mushroom Transgressions\n\nThis age was brought about by the conflict caused by the deffering ideals in the Order. Some Druids were becoming increasingly annoyed at the restriction that came with having a grove in the\ncity (where trees/mushrooms could or could not be planted and the structure of the Druid tree). Other Druids argued that it was their duty to promote balance, and that the runway growth of mushrooms flowing from the grove was nowhere near\nnatural. Some Druids wanted a new grove to be created outside the city and far away. Others wanted the grove to be close. Still others did not want a new grove at all. However, it is important to note that most of these arguments were caused by\nmisunderstandings, which is why this age is sometimes called the Age of Misunderstandings or the Age of Confusion.\nThe Age of Renovation\n\nIn the end, most mushrooms were removed from the grove when Maiavel stamped her foot and the mushrooms instantly broke (but were unthoughtfully left on the ground, only to be swept up by Deltaro). A new,\nsecret grove was set up in the wilderness outside Ravenhold, (notable work from Mahten and Aeaira whom spent days working on the road leading to civilization). The Laurelin Grove continued to be renovated and improved on, with notable work\ncontributed by Lunar Druid Bircalin and Dedicant Deltaro.", ""),
        StaticBook("Aeromancy", "Great Ser Arhadir Owl", "Aeromancy\n\nBy Master Of Air Ser Arhadir Owl\nDate 23rd of The Amber Cold, 1474\n\nChapter 1: How to use Air Evocation\nChapter 2: Extra Information\nChapter 3: Spells\nChapter 1:\n\n1. The first step you should take to learning Aeromancy is by observing the wind itself in action, but to do this in a windy area where you can see what the wind does to nature.\n2. The next step is to learn how to clear your mind and focus just on the void.\n3. After these first two steps you must learn to pull from the void, you do this by clearing your mind and focusing on the void and by thing about the wind itself which you have been studying.\n4. Slowly you will be able to bring small amounts of Air from the void into the world. During this stage you will find that your body is weaken and you may faint a few times trying to hold a connect to the void or by trying hard spells.\n5. Once you learnt how to pull Air from the void you can start using it practically. one of the first spells you can learn is Force push which is a basic burst of Air, this can be used to blow out torches and maybe even push people back a bit.\n6. From here it is up to you to develop Aeromancy better to learn new and intresting spells.\nChapter 2:\n\nQuestion that people have about Aeromancery's and what they can do with Aeromancy.\n-Aeromancer’s cannot control Air in the natural world only control and summon Air which they have summoned from the void.\n-Aeromancer’s cannot fly because the cannot hold a stable connection to the void while moving that fast.\n\n-Aeromancer’s do not have the ability to change the weather.\nChapter 3:\n\nBasic:\n-Force Push: The most basic of all the spells this essentially allows of an Aeromancer to push a target away or knock them over.\n-Air Ball: Another basic spell which allows for the Aeromancer to summon a ball of Air which can be fired at targets at different speeds.\nAdept:\n\n-Tornado: This is a much more defence spell which allows the Aeromancer to summon a tornado around a person or the Aeromancer him or her self,-\n- this tornado can protect from distance attacks (Best against other elemental magic attacks) or weak melee attacks.\n-Air Jump: An Aeromancer can fire a blast of air underneath him or her self which can then propel the Aeromancer up a few meters.\n-Air Burst: This spell fires a blast of air out from all around the Aeromancer which can be powerful enough to knock over many targets all out the Aeromancer.\n-Air Stream: This spell is when an Aeromancer fires a constant stream of Air which can push objects or people back, even knocking them over or stopping them from moving.\nAdvanced:\n\n-Air Leap: The same as an Air Jump but allows you to jump much higher with much more precision.\n-Speed Run: While the idea of this speed seems easy it requires a lot of concentration for a mage to be moving at a speed and be usings his or her magic, this spell allows for the Aeromancer to run slightly faster than normal.\n-Air Bolt: This is a sudden and extremely power blast of air which which can be fire long distances. When this bolt hits it can knock a person out cold or even break bones.\n\nThis concludes the Tome.", ""),
        StaticBook("Age of Rebirth", "Valmir", "The Age of Rebirth\nAuthor Unknown\ntranscribed by Elorna Avern (birdwhisperer)\nLet us start from the moment all the Arch Druids declared that The Circles of Cerridwen and Currnunos are no more. The Druids quickly found a proper spot to start the construction of a new Grove. The Aspects told Jameson, an Arch Druid, that the land is\nplentiful, and a right place us. A place we could call home. Our numbers low, hope not high, the Druid found themselves with a wonderful island of the own. One by one we grew, one by one the hope escalated. So, with the ever growing numbers of Dedicants\nand Druids, the new Grove was suddenly finished. Their home was complete. For the second time, the Druids have four acting Arch Druids. Arik, Valas, Ouity, and Jameson. What they look to do next is for the next Age...\nThis book was originally found in the Druid's Grove in Elandriel, in Asulon. It was brought over and transcribed by Elorna Avern.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ah Znagah'z Gyde", "Turkûrz'Raguk", "Znaga'z Geyd Tuh dah Uzg\n===================Ug = 'Hello, Hail'\nThrom'ka = 'Hello with\n               Respect'\nYub = 'Yes'\nNub = 'No'\nGug'ye = 'Goodbye'\nLat = 'You'\nMih = 'Me, I.'\nRulg = 'Thank you'\nBlah = 'Speak/Tell'\nGruk = 'Understand\n          Know.'\nDabu = 'Yes, My                    'Pleasure'\nBub'Hozh = 'Great\n               Very                        Good'\nZnaga = 'Zlave'\nNub'Hozh = 'Bad'\nPushdug = 'Stinky'\nGlob = 'Fool'\nFlaht = 'Dead/Kill'\nKlomp = 'Fight'\nBlahrg = 'House/Home\nUruk = 'Orc'\nBruddah = 'Male Orc'\nZistah = 'Fe Orc'\nOnly Use Bruddah and Sistah if you're an Orc\nNames for Animals/People\n=========+=========Stowtz = 'Dwarf'\nGazat = 'Dwarf'\nTwiggehz = 'Elf'\nTrihuggahz = \"Elf'\nAlbai = 'Elf'\nSqueals = 'Halflings'\nQuickspawn = 'Human'\nShara = 'Human'\nHoomie = 'Human'\nNuutshara = 'Ascended'\nNubded = 'Undead'\nBuub = 'Pig'\nBuubshara = 'Zombie\n                Pigman'\nHowlur = 'Wolf'\nZkah = 'Curses'\nAgh = 'And'\nBuurz = 'Dark/Black'\nMojo = 'Magic'\nShomo = 'Shaman'\nKrimp = 'Control'\nUzg = 'Land/World'\nGoi = 'City'\nLusk = 'Axe'\nO'lig = 'Bow.'\nLigz = 'Arrow'\nZult = 'Sword.'\nZtik = 'Staff'\nZteemies = 'Redstone'\nSan = 'Home'\nHozh = 'Good'\nRex = 'King'\nGoth = 'Lord'\nWaagh = 'War'\nKub = 'Child'\nThe Blah Numbers\n===================\n\nAsh = '1'\nDuhb = '2'\nGakh = '3'\nFufh = '4'\nH' = '5'\nAnything from 5 and up becomes H'(Number)\nFor example:\nH'Duhb = '7'\nAshty = '10'\nDubty = '20'\nAnything above 9 gets the suffix 'Ty'\nThe Clans\n===================Raguk:\nThe Reigning clan of 1571. They exist of mostly Miners, Blacksmiths and more.\n\nLur:\nA hunting Clan. Consisting of Skinners, Hunters and Leatherworkers.\nOx:\nGoblin Clan\n\nFud:\nOlog Clan\n\nAzog and Gorkil:\nTwo whitewashed Clans\n\nYar:\nA Reputable Clan, mostly having Shamans in their ranks\nHonor\n===================The most Important thing to the Orcs. As a great leader once put it: \"Our honor is the foundation of the fire of anger that is inside of us.\"\nIf you are a slave, you will get beaten for being dishonorable.\nIf someone challenges\nYou to a duel, it is not dishonorable to refuse, but as The spirits Control everything, it is disrespectful.\nFighting without Honor can get you whitewashed as a Orc.\nStrength\n===================One of the most important parts of the Orcish Culture. If a Orc Disagrees with another, they can Fight to decide the winner, as The spirits decide the outcome of everything. The strong and Honorable Get Respect\nHighly Reputable Spirits\n===================\nKrug: The first and Greatest Uruk to ever live, and ever to live.\n\nGentharuz: Spirit of Smithy\n\nTrokorl: Spirit of Redstone and Tinkering\nLak: Spirit of Swamps\n\nVotar: Spirit of the Hunt\n\nOrgon: Most likely the Most hated Spirit of all. The Rex, Kharak'Raguk Built The biggest Shrine for this Spirit, and in the process Orgon Created a Plague unmatched by any -\n- Other. This plage destroyed Vailor, a place where the Descendants settled for Decades.\n\nEhnrok: The Spirit of War\n\nKrothol: Spirit of Pain\nSacrafices to the Spirits\n===================\nSacrifices are commonly Misunderstood as just killing a Human, or a Elf. But Not all spirits like this. Gentharuz, the spirit of Smithing, would likely Enjoy the sacrafice of a well smithen Sword.\nWhile the spirit of Pain, Krothol, would like for a Living sacrafice to die in a slow death.\nSacraficing to the Spirits may bring you fortune, such as a Quick and Painless death, or a Wealthy Child. Sacraficing to the Spirits is Highly Reccomended\nBecoming an Honorary\n===================To have your Freedom, you will have to Prove yourself to the Rex, once Seen in the eyes of the ZnagaGoth, He will approach you and ask you a load of Questions, if answered correctly, You will go to a small -\n- Meeting, which Includes the Rex. He will give you a lot of Trials, and you will will have to complete them. To which you will be released and have \na Choice: Stay with your New friends, The orcs, or Go back to your Old Friends and Family...\nHow to be Useful to the Uzg\n===================So, You want to be useful? But you know being a Warmachine isn't the only way?\nWell, you can become a Blacksmith, Leatherworker, Lumberjack and even a Fisherman! Go ask the Rex or Dominus -\n-  What the Uzg need's the most, and work your way up to be the Most legendary of that certain Profession!", ""),
        StaticBook("Aheral Hst. V1", "The Scribe.", "Mali'aheral means blessed Elves. The Mali'aheral ancestors bathed in a golden pool. This is how they gained their appearance and conciousness that is kept today. The Mali'aheral have a tendency to dislike the other races. Many\nbelieve this is a burning hatred, and that the Mali'aheral seek to rid the world of the other races. However, this is not true. The Aheral merely are annoyed at the chaos and lack of intelligence of the others.\nIn Aegis, elmaliaheral resided in the leafy cities of Laurelin. Upon the High Council, only one Mali'aheral sat, Prince Toren. He alone stood as a bastion of order against the chaos that was seeping in from the rest of Malinor. However,\nit can be assumed he may have actually been impure, as the strict and just laws of purity had not been established as of time he lived in. \n\nPrince Toren was tasked to feed all of the Elves of Malinor. As such,\nhe grew one of the largest farms to ever be seen in the lands og Aegis. He named it the Laurelin farm. That is, one is afraid, all that is known of the people in Aegis.", ""),
        StaticBook("Air Evocation", "Ayche Raven'kor", "Air Evocation\nThis book was created by Master Air Evocatonist Alatar of the Mage Academy\n\nRecopied By\nAyche\nTable of Conents\n\nChapter 1: The Void\n\nChapter 2: All About Wind\n\nChapter 3: Summoning\n\nChapter 4: Basic Spells\nChapter 1: \nAccording to legend and study, the void is an infinite plane of potential thought and energy surrounded by absolute darkness. Since complete darkness is rather hard to imagine, think about a blank canvas.\nHypothetically, anything can be drawn out of the void if it does not contradict nature. The first steps of becoming a mage is learning to understand the void and it's 'blank' \nconcept. Meditation is the most efficient and common way, as I have found.\nLearn how to blanket yourself in a blank state of mind and prepare to paint your canvas.\nChapter 2:\nWind and air are essences to all life around the realm of existance. You breathe it into your very self.\nWith the art of air evoction, you manipulate it. Wind is unpredictable and one\nof the best studied branches of arcane magics, You must experience the element in it's true form before you can even think about summoning it.\nIn order to achieve this, I suggest moving to a  region of high altitude and feel the wind rush over your very body. Experience how pressure seeps through the cracks of all substance. Taste it, feel it, smell it.\nAnother useful experiment is placing something in the air that can float. Watch how the wind carries the item and gently cradles it back down to the earth or carries it away.\nChapter 3:\nAfter you have learned all there is to know about your element, and your meditation is firm; you may begin your attempts in summoning your element from the void. Firstly, you must find a place of peace.\nNext, use your blank canvas to imagine air, allow the thought to fill you and the energy of yourself to course through your very blood. Through combining all of this. A small amount of wind may be conjured.\nWarning: You may pass out a few times upon first connection.\nChapter 4: After you have mastered basic connection, you can move onto some of the key spells in an air evocationist's arsenal.\nThe first and most obvious, is the force push. Essentially, a novice air evocationist musters all of his strength for one large and forceful push at the enemy.\nSecondly, and slightly more defensive, is the protective tornado.\n\nA skilled air evocationist can create a spiraling current of wind that could potentially keep most objects out of your sphere. That concludes this tome.", ""),
        StaticBook("Air Evocation", "princetonlax21", "This selection will offer insight into the subtype of evocation named Aeromancy. I do not particularly enjoy the name, so please do not use it.\n\nThis book was created by Master Air Evocationist Alatar of the Mage Academy.\nTable of Contents\n\nChapter 1: The Void\n\nChapter 2: All About Wind\n\nChapter 3: Summoning\n\nChapter 4: Basic Spells\nChapter 1:\n\nAccording to legend and study, the void is an infinite plane of potential thought and energy surrounded by complete darkness. Since complete darkness is rather hard to imagine, think about a blank canvas. Hypothetically,\nanything can be drawn out of the void if it does not contradict nature. \n\nThe first steps of becoming a mage is learning to understand the void and it's 'blank' concept. Meditation is the most efficient and common way, as I have found. Learn how to\nblanket yourself in a blank state of mind and prepare to create your canvas.\n\nChapter 2:\n\nWind and Air are essences to all life around the realm of existance. You breathe it into your very self. With the art of air\nevocation, you manipulate it. Wind is very unpredictable and one of the least studied brances of arcane magics. You must experience the element in it's true form before you can even think about summoning it. In order to achieve this, I suggest moving to a\nregion of high altitude and feel the wind rush over your very body. Experience how the pressure seeps through the cracks of all substance. Taste it, feel it, smell it. \n\nAnother useful experiment is placing something in the air that can float. Watch\nhow the wind carries the item and gently cradles it back down to the earth or carries it away.\n\nChapter 3:\n\nAfter you have learned all there is to know about your element, and your meditation is firm; You\nmay begin your attempts in summoning your element from the void.\n\nFirstly, you must find a place of peace. Next, use your blank canvas to imagine a air, allow the thought to fill you and the energy of yourself to course through your very\nblood. Though combining all of this, a small amount of wind may be conjured. WARNING: You may pass out a few times upon first connection.\n\nChapter 4:\n\nAfter you have mastered basic connection, you can\nmove onto some of the key spells in a air evocationists arsenal.\n\nThe first, and most obvious, is the force push. Essentially, a novice air evocationist musters all of his strength for one large and forceful push at an enemy.\nSecondly, and slightly more defensive, is the protective tornado. A more skilled air evocationist can can create a spiraling current of wind that could potentially keep most object out of your sphere. \n\nThat concludes this tome. Feel free to\ndiscover your own path in the special art of air evocation.", ""),
        StaticBook("Al'Khazar Watch", "Ebonsquire", "Watching Al'Khazar\n\n(Originally written by Improviser))\nMy name is Darfin. As many people know, I always stand near the AlKhazar main gate. In this book I write all the things I see there. Since I stand here at the Al'Khazar main gate where there are some attacks of the undead. It all started with a guy named\nJohn. John was an Oren guard. When he suddenly got cursed by a fire. The fire at the orphanage. He was possessed by dark magic. He vomited blood and was very aggressive. Many have tried to cure him. And so many have failed. Till it became uncontrollable.\nHe got cured by a guy in a black robe. When John was possessed. He was talking about a lord he had to serve. He walked to guys in purple robes asking them if they could help him out of his misery. Since guys in purple robes are seen as undead.\nHe may be possessed by one of them. After John was cured, he hasn't been spotted in Al'Khazar. After this happened, their wasn't anything interesting. So I went with the guards on patrol. Till we met this strange guy in Whisper Isle. I was him with\nanother guy. He became a recent visitor of Al'khazar. When he came he was always asking people to challenge him. No one really accepted it. Words that no one ever understanded. He spoke of. He said he was praying to Iblees. After many visits I asked his\nname. He said Cerrawin Menethil. When Cerrawin came to the gates, he was denied entry. Somehow I always get into the city. When he is not in Al'khazar, he meets with an undead in Whisper Isle. I see him a lot on the King's Road when I travel.\nAbout his undead friend. Sometimes he comes by. We have killed the undead many times. Also his supporter. But they keep coming. I noticed that the undead keep getting more and more supporters. But I never see any new undead. Maybe\ncause of rules. Or cause the undead uses the supporters as slaves. Also, since the undead attacks Al'Khazar in small numbers. After these small attacks there came a big one. The undead were attacking Alstion. When the guards arrived they were too late.\nThe Undead already demolished the gates. So the guards had to find in the center of the city. But they weren't alone. The pathfinders, Guardians from Kal'Alras, Princess Arianna, the Orcs and even the Wizard himself came to help. It was a tough\nbattle. But the Human Kingdom came to a victory. Now Oren is preparing for another battle. And they will fight against the undead again. But who knows if they will succeed again?", ""),
        StaticBook("Al-Nur Menu!", "Amin Al-Sham", "Al-Nur Spring Bakery & Inn\n-------------------           Menu\n-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-Owner: Amin Al-Sham\nLocation: Al-Khaleed, Haria, Isle Of Asul.\n-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-Baked Goods 2-5 Pg\nMeals 6-9 Pg\nDrinks 10-11 Pg\n-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-\nBaked Goods\n-------------------Bread 0.5 Minas\nbaguette 1 Minas each\nCroissant 1 Minas each\nPancake .5 Minas each\nToast .2 Minas each\nCaramel Bread .5 Minas each\nHoney Bread .5 Minas each\nChocolate Pancake 1 Minas each\nCream Pancake 1 Minas each\nButter pancake 1 Minas each\nMaple Pancake 1 Minas each\nHoney Pancake 1 Minas each\nCake 15 Minas each\nCheese Cake is 15 Minas\nChocolate Cake 20 Minas\nSweet Cake 20 Minas\nPumpkin pie 15 Minas\nChocolate Pie 15 Minas\nCream Pie 15 Minas\nMushroom Pie 15 Minas\nCannoli 15 Minas\nPlain Cookie .5 Minas\nButter Biscuits .5 Minas\nChocolate Cookie .5 Minas\nTriple Chocolate Cookie 1 Minas\nBrownie .5 Minas\nCaramel Cookie .5 Minas\nMa'amouls 1 Minas\nFortune Cookie 1 Minas\n-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-\n\n\n\n------------------>\n-------------------          Meals\n-------------------Sandwich 1 Minas\nPotato Chowder 1 Minas\nMashed Potatoes and cheese is 3 Minas\nMushroom Stew 1 Minas\nPumpkin Soup 1 Minas\nFondue 1 Minas\nChicken Soup 1 Minas\nMutton Soup 1 Minas\nRabbit soup 1 Minas\nVegetable Soup 1 Minas\nBeef Soup 1 Minas\nChicken Stew 2 Minas\nBeef Stew 2 Minas\nMutton Stew 2 Minas\nRabbit Stew 2 Minas\nChicken Noodle Soup 2 Minas\nMutton Noodle Soup 2 Minas\nRabbit Noodle Soup 2 Minas\nVegetable Noodle Soup 2 Minas\nBeef Noodle Soup 2 Minas\nCooked Chicken 1 Minas\nFried Chicken 2 Minas\nStuffed Chicken 3 Minas\nSteak 1 Minas\nPrime ribs (beef) 2 Minas\nMeatloaf 3 Minas\nCooked Rabbit 1 Minas\nRabbit Fricassee 2 minas\nMaple Rabbit 3 Minas\nCooked Mutton 1 Minas\nLamb Chops 3 Minas\nBurger 2 Minas\nBaked Potato 2 Minas\nChips 1 Minas\nStuffed potatos 2 Minas\nSteam Carrots 1 Minas\nCheese Omelet 1 Minas\nMushroom Omelet 1 Minas\n-------------------         Drinks\n-------------------\nChocolate Milk 5 Minas\nMilk 4 minas\nDandelion tea 5 Minas\nApple Juice 5 Minas\nRose Tea 5 Minas\nEggnog 5 Minas\nLemonade 5 Minas\nPumpkin Juice 5 Minas\nOrange Juice 5 Minas\nWater bottle is .5 Minas\n#Many Drinks may or maynot be available.", ""),
        StaticBook("Alavara", "Alavara", "Filth\n\nFilth is part and parcel with society and nature. Filth not only leaves a person with an oder and stains their apperence, it can create bad air that could make one ill. There are two ways to avoid the bad air created by filth:\nRemove the filth or remove the bad air. To be effective, one must do both.\n\nFirstly, filtering bad air is the easiest to perform. The most direct method is to wear a clean cloth over your mouth and nose. however, it must be washed daily as the air will\nsoil the cloth. Another option is to burn incense or anything that is non-toxic and good smelling.\n\nSecondly, and most importantly, is removing filth. To remove filth means to remove it in all it's forms. The nearest filth is one's clothing. To clean\nclothing, aggitate the clothing in a solution of saltwater and vinegar and dry in a clean place. Then, one must remove all dust from all surfaces as it can be kicked into the air. Do not neglect the cealing. Keep your door's closed . Remove objects from\nsurfaces and clean under and behind them with a vineger rag. Sweep the floors, but before cleaning anything else. Dust will be swept onto surfaces. move furnature and clean the area's covered. Never keep food in your living place. use the\nrestroom far from your home and always bathe afterwards.", ""),
        StaticBook("Alc. Potions II", "Sarrion", "Alchemy: Potions\nPart II\n-------------------Please read Part I.\n\n-Sarrion di' Pascali\n=[Potion of Smoke Whispers]=\nike in the magical stories where the hero throws something into the floor and a cloud of smoke is formed, this potion creates the same as the vial shatters. Creating black smoke which releases fast, it allows the\nthrower a distraction or the ability to temporarily incapacitate the adversaries. Brewed over the course of four weeks, this potion is literally smoke in a bottle, and like it, it creates irritation when breathing and to the eyes.\nRecipe:\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n4 Air Symbols (Flight, Transport)\n1 Fire Symbol (Combustion)\n=[Potion of Sleeping]=\nUsed by people of all kinds, this potion’s objective is placing someone to sleep. It might act fast, it might act slow, it might be to help with sleeping at night, or it might be to knock someone out. The use will dictate the power\nthe potion has to be made. Minor potions will have more of a relaxing result, making resting far more easier to achieve. Lesser ones have to be drank, and soon after the potion will take effect and the one to drink it will not even\nnoticed he passed out. Greater potions on the other hand, might not even need to be drank, only smelled. This is particular good for doctors when needing to operate, as it does not force the patient to drink something.\nTo force it on someone might not have some good results, as the potion does take it’s time to give it’s effect, enough to fight back. It is worth noticing, that if the recipe is only read by the symbols needed, the lesser potion\nof sleeping is the same as the lesser potion of mending. Always be careful with the representations of the symbols you use.\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n2 Symbols of Water (Calmness and Relaxation)\n1 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n- Lesser\nBase: Distilled Water\n2 Symbols of Water (Calmness and Relaxation)\n1 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Resilience)\n- Greater\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n2 Symbols of Water (Calmness and Relaxation)\n2 Symbols of Air (Flight and Speed)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Resilience)\n=[Potion of Freezing]\nA peculiar potion, which it’s only intent is freezing or cooling something, or perhaps to just keep cold. I invented one of the potions, the potent one which quickly freezes water on contact, making it something impossible to drink\nand dangerous to the touch. This potion must actually be kept in a thick glass vial, and it then must be surrounded by fur, or something of the like as to keep it your hands from freezing and stopping water from getting\nstuck to the vial. Another recipe was made by my student, it is far weaker, and more for a use in someone’s body. To put it simply, it is a protection of a person’s cold, which seems quite horrible for most times.\nYet if you’re travelling into very hot climates, it could save one’s life.\n\n- Minor\nBase: Water\n1 Symbols of Water (Cold)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Resilience)\n- Lesser\nBase: Distilled Water\n2 Symbols of Water (Ice, Cold)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Protection)\n- Greater\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n2 Symbols of Water (Calmness and Relaxation)\n1 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Hardiness)\n=[Waterbreathing Potion]=\nA remarkable potion which allows someone to breath underwater for long periods of time. The biggest side effect of this potion thought, is that you stop breathing air. Made of rare ingredients,\nmainly the liver and gills of a mermaid, it turns someone temporarily into a sort of human fish. Your skin between the toes and hands starts webbing, only falling out a few hours after the effects of the potion are gone. Being too long inside\nof the water also makes it so that when you finally leave, your ears seem to pop, and you can not hear for a few minutes. The most important part of the potion, is the creation of gills on the side of your neck, it is these that allow you to breathe\nunderwater, yet not above. For the first few moments of the transformation, someone can not breathe in or out of water, it may create panic in some people, yet they must remember to go into the water, because they will not be able to breath out of it.\nRecipe:\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae mixed with a bit of seawater\nMermaid’s Gills and Liver\n1 Symbols of Water (Fluidity)\n1 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Resilience)\n=[Frost Oil]=\nAs an oil, this concoction has an external use and is not to be drank. As I move on to the oils, the type of base fixes itself to only lard. Lard has a more viscous consistency which allows it not to drip when coated on something.\nThe Frost oil, or Oil of frost, is the first I will speak of. An oil which has similarities with the potion of freezing, but whose effects last longer, enabling it to be coated in weapons, armor or clothes. It’s effects are quick cooling what it touches,\nbut it reacts weakly to air, meaning it’s effect happen mostly through contact. Using such an oil on clothing or armor might make the wearer cold, but such could be the objective if one is travelling into hot deserts or inside volcanos.\nOn the other hand, while it might make the cloth or armor it was placed cold, it also offers a protection to the same cold, as something already cold, can’t get colder. As such, if one would use the oil of frost in cloth,\nhe would find himself getting colder, yet if the oil was used on the outer layer of plate armor, it would assist with protecting against cold winds. If placed on weapons, it has the most use on swords. A slash of the weapon on\nsomeone’s skin would slightly freeze the area. You might think a frozen cut would mean that the blood loss would be lower, and it’s true, yet freezing the area will make the skin turn slightly blue, and the opponent will have difficulties moving\nthe area, this way slowing him down. The cut makes the cooling enter more into the flesh, perhaps even forcing someone to have to remove the frozen flesh affected, perhaps even amputating a limb. The biggest worry of using it on tools\nand armor is that it also cools it down substantially, and when badly brewed will make metals very easily shattered, when well brewed they will still suffer some problems in lesser effect.\nRecipe:\n\nBase: Lard\n3 Symbols of Water(Cold, Ice, Frost)\n2 Symbols of Air (Speed)\n1 Symbol of Earth (Fortitude)\n=[Flame Oil]=\nThe Oil of Flame does almost the exact same as the Oil of Frost, yet instead of cold, it works with burning. In contact with the skin it will leave huge burns, but of course you do not apply it to the skin. On weapons,\nwhen striking the enemy, it will cause great pain a kind to burning. The flesh will become red and start to create puss. While the Oil of Frost might slow the opponent, the oil of Flames inflicts greater damage on him. The weapon does not escape\ndamage, while with the Frost Oil the weapon may shatter, with the Flame Oil it corrodes it. While the effects do seem like those of a flame, it does not actually heat an object up. It actually make it more flame repellent and heat resistant.\nOf course the resistance isn’t perfect, and a wooden sword coated with the oil will still catch ablaze, it does tarden the effects. This means if applied to armor or cloth that is not touching the skin, it creates a sort of heat and flame\nprotection. In the recipe it is found the use of a water symbol, and one might ask why such a representation is required in such a fire centric concoction. The explanation is rather simple, it calms the fire symbols of destruction and power, balancing\nit to not create an extreme reaction, which normally is represented as an explosion while brewing.\nBase: Lard\n3 Symbols of Fire (Power, Destruction, Burning)\n2 Symbols of Earth (Endurance)\n1 Symbol of Water (Calmness, Balance)\n>=[Alchemist’s Fire]=<\nOne of the greatest means of attack from an alchemist, their creation of fire. Many times referred to as ‘fire in a bottle,’ a more correct term would be ‘liquid fire.’ The alchemist is able to access the properties of fire and\nadd them into a liquid, making it highly volatile and dangerous. Getting hit by a bottle of it would result in being completely covered in flames. Of course it’s not always used for combat, and many times it is used to cleaning metal utensils,\nsuch as cauldrons and knives from contaminants and impurities. Some may say it’s a waste to use such a powerful potion on cleaning, but it’s use that way may also be associated with it’s danger. Alchemist’s fire is highly dangerous,\nboth while brewing and after. To brew it, not only must the temperature of the concoction remain constant, that temperature must be almost freezing. The room must be made almost specifically to create it,\nas it must be freezing cold. Some alchemists have started using Thanhium to have a cold room in their labs instead of heading to the tall mountains, or the cold Tundras. In those conditions the alchemist’s fire is made, and even brewed in freezing\ntemperatures, it still can burn your hand. The bottles must be made of glass, and to fill them, thongs of sorts must be used, as if your hand touches the liquid it will be burnt, even will gloves. Never should an alchemist fall\ninto the mistake of using a wooden cork, as the liquid will burn right through it and most likely blow in the hands of the handler, they must always be made of glass. Now onto the dangers of the bottles. If they are kept stationary,\nthey won’t be dangerous, it’s problems start when they are moved quickly or hit. Some alchemists keep them in Athin made sacks to prevent some of it’s force if the bottle is broken, yet even so the alchemist would be burnt. Falling\nwhile carrying these bottles, or being pushed, punched, thrown to the ground may result in a bottle breaking. For them to be useful they must break, but that is the bane of the carrier as well.\nThe safest way to use the bottle is to drop it, it results in it blowing up when hitting the ground in almost all cases. While it does explode, it’s explosion is not one like tnt, but more of many tiny shards of glass and fire sent flying everywhere.\nRecipe:\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n4 Symbols of Fire (Destruction, Power, Consumption)\n2 Symbols of Water (Fluidity)\n1 Symbol of Air (Speed)", ""),
        StaticBook("Alc. Theories #1", "Rafiel Halkon", "It was a bone-chilling day of the Deep Cold. I'd been toiling with my usual speculations and experimentations when a friend took stride through the door. He made proposition of an idea that could ultimately prove a\ngreat accomplishment of alchemy. Without delay I set to work, braving the various climates of this realm in search of the necessary reagents to turn this small proposal into a reality.\n\"At the least,\" I mused,\n\"It could make for quite the party trick!\"\n\n----------\n Its inspiration aside, creating a potion of shrinking would prove a truly unique matter. It's effect would reduce the volume of one's body, rather\nthan directly amplify an attribute. And of course, one must note how vast a work the body is. Assuming that the body shrank proportionately to begin with, muscles all about would be influenced. The most prominent example\nwould be the vocal cords. Were they reshaped in any way, the result would be one of many fluctuations in tone. Quite humorous, to think about.\n Then of course, the matter of clothing. Such would likely be unable to shrink\nwith the user, lest they were a race of fur.\n\n Then from an alchemical standpoint, there is the duration for which the potion would hold effect on the user. In lesser potencies, mere minutes would prove remarkable.\nWhereas greater variations could span many hours or even days, given the alchemist in question was critical enough toward the reagents.\n Shrinking past half the user's original volume too, would likely have repercussions.\nSuch a change would require a much larger dosage of the elixir to be taken, and likely risk damaging the user. \n This is not to mention the elixir's time frame of effectiveness, after which the user could potentially find\nthemself between a literal rock and hard place. Which would certainly be a humiliating way for the monks to locate one's self.\nNow, this is where I conclude my speculations. Much farther would be delving into realms of pure uncertainty, which the powers that be do not take lightly. -=- -=- -=- -=-\n~Rafiel Halkon", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemical pt.3", "Marc Volaire Zuberzuri Proudfoot", "Frost Vine Tests\n\nAthin\nSerpent's Stalk\nFrost Vine?", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemist Intro.", "Unknown", "An Introduction to the Alchemist.\nForward\nGreetings, my dear reader. Within the pages of this tome I shall be explaining to you what it takes to be a true Alchemist. I shall run you through our mindset, qualities, and traditions.\nChapter I: The Recipe for a Good Alchemist.\n\nIt takes a special breed of person to be an Alchemist: a rare combination of patience, creativity, intellect, enthusiasm, and of course, an unusual resistance to explosions. For those who wish to follow the\nnoble path of an Alchemist, you must first consider if you possess these key traits, for if you do not, much like a potion made with a flawed recipe, you will be ineffective.\n\nDo not mistake what I say for supremacism, but rather realize that it is simply\nan effort to save you. If you do not possess these qualities, dear reader it is quite possible that you, in the attempt to follow our path, may indeed end up harming yourself and others. Now, allow me to go more in-depth about what these traits mean, in\norder of their importance to an Alchemist: Patience. Alchemy, I'm afraid, is not a fast nor particularly interesting process. An Alchemist will spend most of their time for their elembic to boil so that the extract of your ingredients may be collected for\nuse, or simply waiting for a potion to brew. Very little of the time it takes to create a potion is actually spent creating the mixture, lthough this time is indeed the most interesting part of Alchemy in my opinion. Because of all this, dear reader,\nPatience is vital to an Alchemist, as it would be, in my opinion, apt to give Alchemy the formula consisting of 25 \"parts\":\n\n7 parts waiting (brewing, waiting for the elembic's water to boil.)\n5 parts setting up, cleaning up, and inspecting your equipment.\n\n5 parts testing (making sure your potion works.)\n\n3 parts failure (realizing that your recipe is bollocks and starting over.)\n3 parts extracting (boiling down the ingredients and collecting the condensation using an alembic.)\n\n1 parts explosion (surprisingly common.)\n\n1 parts mixing the bloody potion.\nIntellect. You may be wondering why this is not the first on my list... Well, someone whom is completely dull, yet is willing to give up hours upon hours to make up for it, s much more likely to succeed than someone brilliant who will become frustrated\nand rushed the second they see that their brew will be taking a very long time to finish. So, while this trait is near-vital, it can be made up for by a massive amount of patience and dedication.\nCreativity. Please note that I am writing here to explain what it takes to be an Alchemist, not a good Alchemist. Creativity is something completely vital to anyone who wishes to be a master alchemist... but it is not to those whom only wish to dabble in\nthe art. Creativity is essential for those who wish to truly excel at the art, because one cannot just follow recipes forever. There comes a time, when an apprentice Alchemist must take these matters into their own hands. No more books, no more\nexplanations as to why these particular ingredients must be used... there comes a time, when every Alchemist must learn to rely on their own extensive knowledge of herbs and ingredients, of what they represent... To craft a potion of their very own. This\nis much more difficult that it may seem... but I shan't go into that, as this is no true Alchemical textbook, but rather, an introduction to the science.\nLastly, Enthusiasm. This trait may seem unusual to list, as most would automatically think of someone who is impulsive and stupid... 'overly' enthusiastic. No, Alchemy requires a special type of enthusiasm. One tempered by logic and patience. Enthusiasm\nis needed because without the drive to learn more, to improve your skill and to create potions of wonder... you are nothing, and shall go nowhere. The reason this is listed at the bottom of the list instead of the top, is simply because by reading this\nfar, you have shown an ounce of this quality, and as such, you may now take pride in knowing that you have the potential to learn this art.\nChapter 2: The Life of an Alchemist\n\nThe Lifestyle.\n\nThe life of an Alchemist, despite its labours, is a rewarding and interesting one. Many nations, many companies, and many guilds need Alchemists, which means that an Alchemist is very rarely without\noptions. Indeed, one of the most fulfilling parts of being an Alchemist is the satisfaction of knowing you are needed and wanted. An Alchemist rarely stays in one location whilst unemployed, often traveling from kingdom to kingdom, seeking a guild or\ncompany that has use for them. In my experience, this does not take a long time... Although it is important to measure one's options before taking a job. This is not to say that an Alchemist /must/ be employed, gods know many Alchemists choose a life of\nreclusion and introversion--preferring the company of their ingredients and their equipment to the company of people or currency. As an Alchemist, there are many different paths one may choose from; be it creating poisons or creating potions to heal the\nill and wounded. These paths will play  large role in deciding how easily one can get jobs, and from whom. For example, one who makes poisons may find it harder to get a job than one who creates healing serums as poisons are hard to mass-produce, which\nmeans are of no real use to an army, and they are certainly of no use for a peaceful company of a guild. This is much the opposite for healing potions, as oddly enough they are some of the simplest to make, and can be made in quite large quantities\nwithout losing much of their effectiveness, so all the aforementioned employers could have a use for an Alchemist of healing. Do not think that these are the only two paths that an Alchemist may follow, however--and certainly no Alchemist sticks only to\none branch of the science--think of it more as a specialization. I, for example, specialize in incendiary and explosive concoctions. Alchemist Fire, if you will. This means that I am often asked to work for militaries of small offensive guilds... even\nresearch companies.\n\nIn conclusion, the lifestyle in terms of business, for an Alchemist, is much dependent upon what they specialize in, and regardless of their business opportunities the life of an Alchemist is rarely full, and in my opinion, very\nfulfilling.\n\nThe Morality of an Alchemist.\n\nMany jobs seem to have a set \"morality.\" Soldiers are expected to be lawful and trustworthy, Mages to be intellectual and neutral... the Morality of an Alchemist is much the same as that of a Mage, and is, much\nlike one's business opportunities dictated by the Alchemist's specialization. To use the same examples as before, it would be more likely for an Alchemist specializing in poisons tend to fall into darker, more \"evil\" morality, as they would likely find\ntheir work with small groups of assassins of the like. Whereas an Alchemist specializing in healing, would be much more likely to fall into a \"good\" morality, as by nature, their job of choice is to help people. There are not set rules, of course, a\nhealing Alchemist may very well choose to assist an unlawful organization, and an Alchemist of poison may choose to supply a small vigilante group with his poisons. In essence, the Morality of an Alchemist is neutral, and is dictated completely by the\nindividual.\n\nThe Living Conditions of an Alchemist\n\nThis section will be somewhat small, as there is very little that can be written about this. An Alchemist's abode will generally have only one constant: a lab, usually located in a basement. This lab\nwould contain an Alchemist's supplies, equipment, and is generally kept locked, as much inside would be fragile or dangerous. As for why it is usually found in a basement... Well... The earth seems to be a bit more resistant to explosions than wooden or\ncobblestone walls.\nFarewell:\n\nI thank you, reader, for taking the time to read my small introduction to the Alchemist. I bid you good ever, good day, and farewell.", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy", "Venator", "Liriel's Book of\n    Alchemy Recipes\n==================\nBasic Teas using one ingredient and water:\nRose\nPoppy\nDandelion\nCactus\nBlue Orchid\nJuices, brewed with one ingredient and water:\nPumpkin\nCarrot\nMelon\nAlcohols:\nPale ale - made with wheat\nMulled Wine - Dark Resin\nEggnog - Egg\nPoisons: To be added", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy Recipes", "Rosso", "Sleeping Draught\n\n==================\nWild Lettuce\nWater\n\n>Break leaves from plant\n>Steep leaves 1 hr. in hot water.\nBurn/Rash Oiuntment*\n\n==================\nWild Lettuce\n\n>Break leaves from plant\n>Grind leaves into poultice\n>Add water and a whole Wild Lettuce\n*Helps with dry skin\nRespiration Potion\n\n==================\nWater\nCaterpillar Fungus\n\n>Grind up fungus\n>Add fungus to heated water, and mix well\nHealing Water I\n\n==================\nWater\nLily pad\n\n>Heat up water\n>Cut up Lily Pad and mix with water\n>Pour some in wound\nWARNING:\n    Stings slightly\nHealing Water II\n\n==================\nRose\nSpider Eye\nBone\nWater\n\n>Gring up Rose, Spider Eye, and Bone\n>Mix with water that was once snow\nHealing water II\n                (Cont’d)\n==================\nLily Pad\nHealing Water II\n\n>Place a few drops in wound\n>Place Lily Pad on wound and bandage\n-If Lily Pad turns brown, it’s healing\nPoison\n\n==================\n3 Spider Eyes\n2 Bones\nWater\n\n>Grind up all 3 Spider Eyes\n>Grind up both bones into bonemeal\n>Add both to heated water\nPoison II\n\n==================\nPoison I\n2 Spider Eyes\nBone\nRose\nDead Lily Pad\n\n>Grind up Bone, Rose, Spider Eyes, and Lily Pad\n>Mix with Poison I\nBone Pain Reliever\n\n==================\nShroom of Immortality\nWater\nLily Pad\n\n>Grind up Shroom of Immortality\n>Gring up Lily Pad\n>Mix together, then add heated water\nStomach Pain Reliever\n\n==================\nJelly Ear\nWater\nSugarcane\n\n>Grin up Jelly Ear and mix with heated water\n>Extract sugar from sugarcane, and add to mixture\nDraught of Thought\n\n==================\n2 Magic Shrooms\nWater\nWild Lettuce\n\n>Grind Wild Lettuce into a poultice\n>Grind up Magic Shrooms\n>Mix together, then add water, and heat\nLimb Pain Reliever\n\n==================\n……. Shroom\nWater\nLily Pad\n\n>Grind up Lily Pad\n>Grind up ……. Shroom\n>Mix together, then add to heated water\nEnergy Potion\n\n==================\n2 Cocoa Beans\nSugarcane\nWater\n\n>Grind up Cocoa beans\n>Extract Sugar from Sugarcane\n>Mix together, then add to heated water\nEnergy Potion II\n\n==================\n3 Cocoa Beans\n2 Sugarcane\nWater\n\n>Steep Sugarcane in water half an hour\n>Grind up Cocoa beans\n>Get rid of sugarcane, and add Cocoa Beans, ground, to water\nPain Reliever\n\n===================\nTippens Root\n\nCopiers note: The book ends here, it is unfinished.\nAlchemy Recipes\n\nUnknown Author\nCopied by Rosso\nVaerHaven", ""),
