        StaticBook("Comp. Alch.:V2B1", "Aislin Maar", "Comprehensive\n      Alchemy\n\n      Volume II\n         Book I\n------------------\n\n       Written By\n       \n       Aislin Marr\n\n          1577\nTable of Contents\n\nVolume II\nHerbology\n\n-Water\n-Fire\n-Air\n-Earth\nChapter One\n        Water\n\nMandragora:\n\nWhile not an herb by definition, this plant is used very often in alchemy as a strong healing reagent. The tuber can be found in pools of water, spotted by their leaves on the surface.\nThe leaves are commonly used as garnish and even for making tea, while the meat of the plant is below the surface, suspended by the leaves. The meat is the part of the plant you seek, but be very cautious, as the plant can at certain seasonal times,\nrelease spores that cause paralysis. Caution is advised when harvesting this herb. \n\nRepresentations: \nRejuvenation, Life, Rebirth, Regrowth, Fluidity\nSerpent's Stalk:\n\nA fairly common plant, Serpent's Stalk is often found growing next to water in dry biomes. It is easily mistaken for sugar canes or reeds, as it has a very similar color to it and it grows in the same manner, as a stalk going\nstraight up into the air. It has wonderful properties for healing, being mostly used for the treatment of burns. The inside of the stalk is almost gel like, and when collected from the stalk and mixed with water, it has a cooling effect.\nRepresentations: \nLife, Rebirth, Calmness, Fluidity\nFrost Vine:\n\nA blue hued vine growing among snowy cliffs in mountainous regions, Frost Vine has an aptitude for healing as many other Water symbols do. It can be rubbed on the skin to cool a burn or loosen stiff muscles.\nProlonged use is not advised, but will not cause permanent numbness. \nRepresentations: \nLife, Calmness, Ice\nBlissfoil:\n\nA plant characterized by thin stems topped with small blue flowers, blissfoil is a plant that can help relieve pain and numb an area. Be cautious in it’s use, as too much can cause permanent numbness.\nA brew of pure blissfoil should be used sparingly and never consumed directly. It’s natural home is in dry regions among grasses. \n\nRepresentations: \nCalmness, Peace, Apathy\nAthin:\n\nA blue colored algae that grows in icy waters. Athin can only be found in cold regions, and those seeking it should come prepared to ward off the elements. Fishing nets can sometimes get this algae caught in themselves.\nThe algae is extremely heat resistant, and is one of the best reagents to use for that sort of potion or oil. \n\nRepresentations: \nFreezing, Life, Ice, Balance.", ""),
        StaticBook("Comp. Alch.:V2B2", "Aislin Maar", "Comprehensive\n      Alchemy\n\n        Volume II\n         Book II\n-------------------\n\n       Written By\n \n       Aislin Marr\n\n          1577\nChapter Two\n        Fire\n\nCrimson Vase\n\nA flower with deep red petals that curve gently upwards in the likeness of a vase. It’s petals are very delicate and harvesting it for use as a reagent should be done with case or\nit will become ineffective. Touching the petals gives the skin a warm sensation, though not unpleasant. It is found in dry forests among grasses and can be easily spotted by it’s remarkable red color. \n\nRepresentations: Heat, Warmth, Love,\nPassion, Compassion.\nDrake's Tail\n\nA thorny plant growing in hot, humid places. It’s fairly rare to find, often growing in shade near a source of water, such as an oasis. It has red hued, thorny stems, and short roots beneath the sand. When harvesting, you must bring\nthick leather gloves, as being struck by one of the thorns will cause a high fever. \n\nRepresentations: Heat, Destruction, Chaos.\nDrawf's Pumpkin\n\nAn orange-leafed shrub which is found in swamplands, Dwarf’s Pumpkin is short and stumpy, with thick leaf stems and broad leaf blades. The leaves themselves are what you want to harvest, but be prepared for a strong odour on your\ntrip back to the lab. This plant is often used to brew a strong ale that dwarves prefer.\n\nRepresentations: Rage, Envy, Bitterness, Power, Strength.\nBlood Lotus \n\nA vining plant that crawls up the walls of sandstone caverns, it’s well adapted to it’s hot residence. It has incredibly nutritious properties, and there are those who saw you can live entirely off of a diet of these alone.\nRepresentations: Hope, Courage.\nFlameroot Tongue\n\nThis plant appears almost like a rose in a forest field, opting to live in sunny, water abundant regions. It’s thick stalks are covered in brambles and has bright red roots. The brambles, if allowed to prick the skin, will cause\nintense, blistering pain, the likes of which will cause most to pass out. The use of thick leather gloves is highly recommended when harvesting. \n\nRepresentations: Heat, Anger, Destruction.", ""),
        StaticBook("Comp. Alch.:V2B3", "Aislin Maar", "Comprehensive\n       Alchemy\n\n       Volume II\n        Book III\n__________________\n\n       Written By\n\n       Aislin Maar\n\n           1577\nTable of Contents\n\nVolume II\nHerbology\n\n-Water\n-Fire\n-Air\n-Earth\nChapter Two\n         Air\n\nLarihei's Fingers:\n\nA plant with smooth, burrowing roots and small, short leaves in the warmer summer months. It grows exlusively in snowy regions, and can sometimes be hard\nto find among the snowpack. It has no immediate healing properties and is not particularly edible, but when brewed into the right mix, it can make you much more acute and aware.\nRepresentations:\nGrace, Beauty, Steadiness, Focus, Swiftness\nElf's Hair Vine:\n\nA sturdy white vine that is covered in dozens of small, white flowers. It grows on high mountain cliffs, out of the direct wind, as the flowers are very fragile and will blow away very easily. Harvest with great care, as the\ngentlest touch can send the flowers floating away on the lightest of breezes.\n\nRepresentations:\nGrace, Speed, Flight, Travel\nCrouching Feather:\n\nA flowering plant that is a very rare sight. It has three, white curved petals that flow downward from the top of the stem, looking like a feather. It only blooms at night, and usually grows beneath the shade of trees in\nforests. The petals are very delicate and liable to break and float away with a strong gust. \n\nRepresentations:\nSpeed, Travel, Grace, Beauty\nHalfling's Grass:\n\nA tall plant that grows in damp, well lit forests. The roots are soft and round, and are the bit that an alchemist needs. The leaves can be smoked and have an effect similar to cactus green.It can also be processed into an oil,\nwhich has an even stronger effect. It’s named for being able to grow to the same height as Halflings, and also for its preference by that race as pipeweed.\n\nRepresentations:\nCalm, Joy, Lifting", ""),
        StaticBook("Comp. Alch.:V2B4", "Aislin Maar", "Comprehensive\n     Alchemy\n\n       Volume II\n        Book IV\n-------------------\n\n      Written By\n\n      Aislin Marr\n\n         1577\nChapter 4\n       Earth\n\nTippen's Root\n\nA very foul smelling weed which is the bane of many gardeners and farms. It grows rampant and it near impossible to get rid of, as any remnant of the root when it is evicted from it's place in\nsoil will simply continue to grow and sprout up once more. It has an odd appearance of purple flowers above the surface, and an reddish-brown root with black spots below the surface. It is used in nearly all healing potions, and has blood clotting\nproperties within the root itself, and can be used directly on a wound. That is, if you can stand the horrid stench of it.\n\nRepresentations: Life, Regrowth, Resilience, Strength, Endurance\nAlabaster Leaf\n\nA small leafy plant found growing on fallen trees and up the trunks of those still standing. It can blend in with the leaves of its host tree, and it’s leaves hold a residue that can cause intense nausea, though it isn't particularly\nharmful until brewed into the right mix.\n\nRepresentations: Immobility, Perseverance.\nSaffvil\n\nA green grass that spreads over the bodies of dead animals and rotting plants. It is extremely hard to get rid of, and smells like death itself. It’s commonly known to be used in potent poisons.\n\nRepresentations: Resilience,\nEndurance, Death.\nClutcher's Straw\n\nA plant that grows about as tall as a common grass, but has a brown color, appearing almost like it’s dead. The blades are not as sharp as normal grass, but the roots beneath the soil are pitch black and razor sharp, so caution is\nadvised. It grows in hot, dry regions.\n\nRepresentations: Impairment, Loss, Brutality.\nMetzli's Tangle\n\nA thick, green vine found in jungles. It has few leaves protruding from it’s main stalk, and relies mainly on it’s host tree for water and nutrition. It cannot survive on it’s own, and therefore can only be found against a large\ntree trunk in the humid, dark jungles.\n\nRepresentations: Dependency, Fear\nGoblin's Ivy\n\nA murky brown colored ivy that spreads like a weed in deserts. It is extremely hard to get rid of and loves growing up the sides of any building, ruin, or mountain it can get onto. However, it doesn't get very tall, but remains the\nbane of those who must deal with it. It’s a weak reagent usually used in poisons. \n\nRepresentations: Resilience, Poison, Life.", ""),
        StaticBook("Comp. Alchemy:V1", "Aislin Maar", "Comprehensive\n      Alchemy\n\n        Volume I\n\n-------------------\n\n       Written By\n\n       Aislin Marr\n\n          1577\nTable of Contents\nVolume I\n\n-Alchemy Introduction\n-Basic Materials\n-Symbols\n-Gathering\nPreface: These volumes were originally written when I realized that the most widely spread and commonly used texts at the time were outdated, inaccurate, or simply misleading. I had many requests from people asking for apprenticeship,\nand would often point them to these books. It stuck me one day that these books had become more of a hinderance to my students than a help, and so I set out to write my own. That being said, these are written to be used by alchemy students as a textbook\nand a learning tool so that they can further themselves in the skill, with or without the aid of a teacher.\nChapter One: The Basics of Alchemy\n \nThe first thing you should know about alchemy is that is requires patience. It is tedious, long winded, and tiring. You will find yourself isolated in your lab for many hours,\nalone in fields gathering herbs for days on end, and struggling with a recipe or brew for months. You cannot act in the way a blacksmith acts, taking one chunk of metal and seeing it transform in your hands,by your own hands, before your eyes.\nIt is, in fact, a very complex and time consuming skill to learn and to practice. However, it is one of the most rewarding. Alchemy at it’s core is about discovery, exploration, experimentation, and in the end the finished product\nwill bring you such a sense of joy and achievement that you will never question the hours, days, and months you spent working towards it. Patience is the companion of wisdom.\nTo define alchemy in its most basic sense is to say that\nherbs are combined to create a potion. While true on the surface, alchemy requires much more thought than simply placing herbs into a flask and brewing them for some grand drink. Every herb we use on a frequent basis has an\nassociation of meanings to it, often pertaining to its physical features, herbal uses, and overall symbol. This is where we delve into the subject of Equivalent Exchange. I speak not of turning dirt to gold but of exchanging something of equal value to\nreceive a new product. In this sense is where we find the correlation between alchemy and equivalent exchange. When I first began to learn alchemy, the concept made little sense to me. I was teaching myself alchemy, stubborn as I was, refused to seek a\nteacher. Determination and independence can be a drawback, though, and it took me far longer than it should have to understand the concept and how it pertained to alchemy. As many do, I assumed you could simply mash some herbs, throw them in a pot,\nand get something out of it. On a very basic level, this is possible, but you will never find anything of true substance this way. To truly begin to unlock the potential of alchemy, we have to use the concept of equivalent exchange.\nIn the sense that I will teach you, equivalent exchange does not deal with the physical sense of the herb per say. Instead, we look to the symbolic meanings of the herb, and then to the aspects of the potion we want to create. A very basic example, the\nmatters of which will be detailed in later volumes of this series, will be presented as a learning tool. A potion of mending is a very common, yet often life saving brew. It is in nearly every healers supply, and even found in households as a sort of\nfirst aid. It is one of the most simple and most widely sold brews, as it is extremely beneficial in our world of violent natured men. We seek a potion that can heal wounds both external and internal, often applied directly to a wound, soaked into\nbandages, or in some occurrences, consumed directly. A minor potion of healing cannot repair a shattered bone, cure an illness, or mend a severed appendage, but it can still save a life in the right time. With this, we find our symbolic meaning: life.\nFrom here, we can take this idea, a word, feeling, or sense of being, and look to our arsenal and find herbs which correlate to it. Here, I will touch briefly on the symbology of herbs. Water, Fire, Air, and Earth. All herbs are categorized as such,\nand all wield a different sense, feeling, and state. For simplicities sake, I will not go further on that here. For now, I will tell you that we can, within our index of herbs, find two that represent life. Tippen’s Root, a common tuber used\nin nearly every healing potion, and Serpent’s Stalk, a thin plant with healing properties. Both these herbs are symbolic of Life, and therefore can be used in exchange for our life giving potion. Life for life, as it were, but in a sense of meaning\nthrough symbols. In this way, you must think of the recipes for a potion as a mirror to what the potion is used for. This is how we address the concept of equivalent exchange.\nChapter Two: Basic Materials\n\nSome of the most basic tools an alchemist should have within their lab are, of course, an alchemy set, also known as an alembic, a pestle and mortar, several cutting boards, spoons, knives of\nvarious sizes, leather gloves, safety glasses, lab coats, cleaning supplies and a sink with fresh water (preferably spring water), vials, bottles, and jars of all shapes and sizes. Filters are also necessary, both paper and metal, for keeping stray\nbits of your reagents out of the finished product. You’ll need plenty of storage space and a large, clean worktop on which to practice. You should aim for a lab with a relatively neutral room temperature, if not cold for those who can stand it.\nA hot lab is a dangerous lab. I also suggest a writing desk for note taking, research, and study, as well as bookshelves for keeping notes and textbooks organized. When gathering herbs, you’ll need to equip yourself with several pairs of alchemist's\nshears. These are specialized shears that are made with aurum, which is a soft and easily broken metal. Due to this, you’ll find yourself wanting to have many of these in your bag when you go out gathering, as it may be a waste of your day if you come\nback with too few herbs. It is wise for all alchemists to befriend a skilled blacksmith, as they can not only craft you the shears (hopefully, for a discounted price) but they can also repair your nearly ruined shears with ferrum. You can also have your\nshears enchanted, so using Unbreaking and Efficiency is recommended. Ensure that you wear your leather gloves when gathering more volatile herbs, and in some cases, safety glasses or even a respiratory mask is recommended.\nChapter Three: Symbols \n\nOnce a student has a basic grasp on the idea of equivalent exchange, we can begin to progress into the topic of symbols. As stated in chapter one, alchemy is ruled by the four basic elements of our creation:\nwater, fire, air, and earth. These symbols make up the four greater groups of herbs, like four great pillars holding up an all encompassing roof. Each symbol bears it’s own personality, so to speak. Water is calm, fluid, and life giving.\nIt refreshes, but it can also be dangerous. Freezing ice and powerful currents can cause death just as easily as any other element. Fire is hot, rageful, destructive. It is also powerful, passionate, and emotional. Air is light, soothing, and graceful.\nIt’s also lawless, and quick, whisking away a leaf in the blink of an eye.  Earth is steady, strong, and resilient. It can also hold dangers, entombing us in it’s unyielding grasp. With these four symbols we can generalize nearly everything we see at it's\nvery basic core. Through both the idea, concept, and physical attributes of anything in our world, we can associate it to one or more of these symbols in some way. We also must recognize that these symbols must be placed in a specific order,\nor you will find yourself failing every time. Water, Fire, Air, Earth. This is our order for brewing, and we must use it every time, even when not all symbols may be used in a specific potion. Now that we have laid in your mind the basic foundations\nof symbols, let’s take a look at the most how to gather these herbs, and what precautions we should take when venturing out into the world.\nChapter Four: Gathering \n\nAfter having a general understanding of the basics, we’ll talk about how to physically gather your own herbs. As previously stated, you’ll need to equip yourself with alchemist’s shears and leather gloves when gathering.\nI also recommend bringing several jars or containers to properly store the gathered plants. “Herbs” is a very general term for the kind of reagents you’ll use, as some are not technically herbs at all. This being said, you’ll be searching in all manner\nof places for herbs, from scorching deserts, to muddy swamps, to snow peaked mountains. Make sure you’re properly dressed for the occasion and that you have adequate amounts of food and water for your journey. As well, bringing a minor healing potion for\nfirst aid would be smart, in case something does go wrong. Alchemist’s shears are made with two aurum ingots and two basic sticks, and can be crafted by adequate blacksmiths. They can be repaired with a single ferrum ingot by more highly skilled\nblacksmiths, though, so make sure you don’t ruin them entirely. You may also want to find an enchanter to imbue your shears with runes for Unbreaking and Efficiency so that they well last longer. You’ll have to search for many hours for some herbs,\nand you will more often than not find yourself unsuccessful when gathering. Worry not, though, as all alchemist’s must progress and learn to better spot the herbs they need.", ""),
        StaticBook("Comp. Alchemy:V3", "Aislin Maar", "Comprehensive\n      Alchemy\n\n       Volume III\n\n__________________\n\n       Written By\n       \n       Aislin Maar\n   \n          1577\nTable of Contents\n\nVolume III\n\n-Brewing Basics\n-Recipes\n--Practical\n--Healing\n--Offensive & Defensive\nPart One: Brewing Basics\n\nNow we have our herbs, we have our lab, all our tools are laid out and ready to be used.. But, now what? Now, we have to put those herbs to use. Herbs can all be prepared in some way,\nand you will use your tools and your lab for this. Every container, every vial, every cutting board and spoon and knife and bowl must all be perfectly clean, and free of any kind of foreign contamination. If they are not, your potion is likely to fail,\neven if you put in all the right ingredients. Alchemy is a science, and it must be done correctly. First, water symbols, then fire, then air, then finally earth. Always in this order, and never any other. Before this, though, we need our bases. Some\nsimpler potions only require distilled water. We can get distilled water by filtering regular water through charcoal in our alembic. A simple, easy process to do. Aqua Vitae is our more powerful base. This is created by distilling a peculiar red fungus\ncalled Nether Wart into water in your alembic. Making Aqua Vitae is very good practice for budding alchemists. Nether Wart can usually be procured from skilled farmers, and if that fails, try buying it in the auctions.\nNote that when I recipe calls for two air symbols, one of grace and one of calm, the two symbols will not be the same herb. You will need to experiment and rationalize to find out which herbs you will be using. Herbs typically do not need to be brewed\nin a specific order within their own symbol. You should also think about which herbs you’re choosing. A student of mine once came to me quite perplexed. He asked me why he wasn’t able to make the simplest of potions, a minor potion of mending.\nI asked him which herbs he had chosen for his two representations of life. He informed me he had chosen Serpent’s Stalk for the water symbol and Goblin’s Ivy for the earth symbol, but that is still wouldn’t work. He said they both had representations\nof life, and that surely it must work. I instructed him to look back at his notes again, and to read off the various representations of Goblin’s Ivy, and he did. Resilience, poison, and life. Now, an herb that also represents poison wouldn’t do very well\nin a healing potion, now would it? When looking for the right herb for a recipe, you should always take into account the other representations of the herb, and consider if they are consistent with the potion you are trying to brew.\nPart Two: Recipes\n\nChapter One: \n\nPractical Potions\n\nChapter Two: \n\nHealing Potions\n\nChapter Three: \n\nOffensive and Defensive Potions\nChapter One: Practical Potions\n\nSoft Relaxant:\nA calming potion for those with high levels of anxiety or those who simply need to calm down after a hectic day. This potion will also relieve muscle pain, though it is not a healing potion.\nBase: Aqua Vitae. \n2 Air Symbols (Steadiness, Calm)\n\nBeauty Sleep:\nA potion with a sweet taste that causes the drinking to fall asleep quickly. It is a natural, rejuvenating sleep, and the drinker can be woken by normal means.\nIt will not render the drinker unconscious, but simply aides those who have trouble falling asleep.\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n3 Air Symbols (Steadiness, Speed, Calm)\nPotion of Beauty:\nThis potion will give the drinker softer, more even toned skin, as well as thicker, fuller, and stronger hair. It will brighten the eyes, getting rid of bags from tiredness, and even helps with acne.\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n3 Air Symbols (Focus, Beauty, Joy)\n\n\nCleansing Incense: \nThis liquid, when exposed to air, will begin to release a gas with a very pleasant, calming scent.It works well as a non burning incense,\nand will make any room smell better, or any date more romantic. \n\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n2 Fire Symbols (Heat, Courage)\n1 Air Symbol (Focus)\nChapter Two: Healing Potions\n\nPotion of Mending:\nA very basic, and very useful potion. Capable of healing minor cuts and wounds, as well as stopping bleeding. Every healer should have this,and it can be found in many household cabinets as it\nis a common potion used for first aid. \nBase: Distilled Water\n1 Water Symbol (Life)\n1 Earth Symbol (Life)\nSoothing Ointment:\nA watery ointment that can be applied to the skin to relieve itching and burning. It leaves a pleasant sensation behind. Apply liberally and allow to sit on the affected area for up to five minutes. Remove excess with a damp cloth.\nAvoid contact with eyes and do not ingest. \nBase: Aqua Vitae\n2 Water Symbols (Rejuvenation, Life)\n1 Fire Symbol (Compassion)\nQuick Healing:\nA simple potion meant to heal up a cut or a would in when you find yourself in a bind. This brew is popular among soldiers on the battlefield, as it’s quick and efficient effects can save lives when on the move.\nBase: Distilled water\n1 Air Symbol (Speed)\n1 Earth Symbol (Life)\n\nFrozen Blood:\nThis thick potion will act as a liquid bandage when applied to a wound. While not doing much for healing or blood clotting, sometimes it can be the best you can do\nin a certain situation. It is a slimy substance that steams profusely after being applied to the skin.\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n2 Water Symbols (Ice, Freezing)\n1 Air Symbol (Speed)\n1 Earth Symbol (Life)\nGreater Potion of Healing:\n\nThe strongest healing potion that is commonly available, this brew will seal wounds, stop bleeding, repair fractures, and can be consumed to stop internal bleeding. It’s said to reduce tumors and even to\nhelp cleanse internal organs or poisons and other toxins.\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n2 Water Symbols (Ice, Freezing)\n1 Air Symbol (Speed)\n2 Earth Symbol (Life, Perserverence)\nChapter Three: Offensive and Defensive Potions\n\n\nPotion of Celerity:\nThis particular brew is one that can be quite useful on the battlefield. It provides the drinker with a burst of energy that allows them to move faster\neven with a set of iron armor on. \n\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n1 Fire Symbol (Power)\n3 Air Symbols (Swiftness, Speed, Travel)\nStoneskin:\nA legendary brew, Stoneskin. Tales of old have told that entire wars have been won with this concoction. A handful of men have taken down hundreds with this flowing through their blood, charging through lines and slaughtering\ntheir enemies with ease. A miracle to some, perhaps, and the greatest bane of others. However, it comes with a price. Stoneskin has a terrible side effect known as Akmen’s Syndrome. Continued usage of Stoneskin will cause nerves to break down,\na loss of feeling in the skin, degradation of the body, and eventually death. Still, this potion can be life saving, if you’re up for the risk.\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n2 Water Symbols (Life, Rebirth) \n3 Fire Symbols (Power, Chaos, Anger) \n1 Air Symbols (Speed) \n4 Earth Symbols (Fear, Perseverance, Brutality, Resiliance)\nSmoke Whispers:\nThis potion is thrown down, breaking the glass and causing the liquid inside to make contact with open air. When this happens, a violent reaction occurs, and a large cloud of black vapor will expand from the point of impact in the\nblink of an eye. This can be extremely helpful to those who wish to escape someone without being seen, or even to prank your friends!\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n2 Fire Symbols (Chaos, Courage)\n2 Air Symbols (Swiftness, Speed)\nAlchemist’s Fire:\nA famed and fabled potion. Alchemist’s Fire is a favorite in any army’s arsenal. It can be an effective combatant, or it can be your worst nightmare. This potion is thrown down onto the ground, where the glass explodes and releases\nthe liquid inside. The potion itself is EXTREMELY flammable, and highly reactive to any air that touches it. It must be brewed in a cold lab, under very careful circumstances. This potion is just as liable to kill the user as it is to kill their intended\nvictim. The liquid, when released, will immediately ignite and spread over an area of about 4 meters by 4 meters. The reaction itself releases air, adding more fuel to the fire that has already started. It’s also known well to arsonists who wish to light\na building aflame in a matter of seconds. This potion is not for the faint of heart.\n\nBase: Aqua Vitae\n2 Water Symbols (Fluidity)\n4 Fire Symbols (Heat, Destruction, Rage, Chaos)\n1 Air Symbol (Speed)\nFrost Oil:\nAn oil used on arrows to cause the victim so be slowed greatly. A useful item to have in combat.. If you’re a sure shot, that is.\nBase: Lard ((Aqua Vitae))\n3 Water Symbols (Fluidity, Freezing, Ice)\n2 Air Symbols (Speed, Flight)\n1 Earth Symbol (Endurance)", ""),
        StaticBook("Concerning Time", "Ornias", "The Importance \n               Of Time\n\nChapter 1: Time for men and time for war.\n\nChapter2: Time for elves and time for preservation.\nTime, we either have too much or none at all, when we are small we lack it and when we're adults we need more.\nEating, walking and resting they all take time: hours, minutes even days that some might have and some might not.\nBut what is the definition of time?\nSome say it's just a made-up thing by our ancestors that stuck around.\nI asked the definition of time to humans and elves alike and some of the elvish answers were: The continued progress of all things\ncreated/existed or the past, present and future represent time.\n\nMen. They don't have much time, they produce, consume and fight for the higher that command and backstab to get higher and higher on the piramid that is the social class in the human\nsociety.\n\nI will be viewing most of the points in the following statemants trough the eyes of the common working class.\n\nMen ,when they are children and even further in life, work at farms and pastures as shop owners, craftsmen and street\nurchins.\n\nThe size of the H.O.E. (The Holy Oren Empire) is playing a big role on the lives and the shaping of the future of soliders and workers.\nChildren in the H.O.E.\nbecome ready for battle when they are as young as 12 years old, shocking like it\nmay be for many of the other races men die often and children are trainded from a young age. I have seen cases of great cruelty being pressed upon the soliders of the Empire, the higher-ups saying that it makes them tougher and it will make them not flee\nin the midst of a battle. The last years of a human's life are almost non existant as most of them die in battle or gutted by bandits in the hidden streets of the capital or other cities, that if only they don't become beggars first. I did not mention\nnobility and knightdom in this book because it is a whole diffrent social classso I will keep the rest of the writings brief.\nThe elves, I shall talk mostly of high elves and wood elves for I find mali'ker culture very obscure and not defined by many aspects...\nAn elven child pretty rare because of the infertility in the female elves the children are cared for until\nadulthood and beyond that would be about one hundred years in the case of the high elves, they are taught how to behave and to have manners from a very young age.\nLines of elven families can be traced back to Aegis and Malin himself, and these documents\nmight be found in the silver city of Haleun'or.\n\nThe second part to elvish life the longest is after reatching one hundred or two hundred years, mostly peaceful or rather \"boring\" as seen by other races the elf is social and outgoing in this period.\nElves, like men produce and consume in a tight small comunity that is strong and doesn't draw weapons in attack but just in defence of their lands, the type of political freedom the elves have is fair and rules with an iron fist while alliances are and\ncan be formed, like example the Dominion of Malin.\n\nElves are born with advantages over humans due to extended lifes and the will and the power to wait for things to fall in place and things to be set in motion. A man might want to kill a high elf\nthat sits atop his silver city but the man might die of old age before the elf decides to go down to fight him.\n\nHigh elves don't like war, not like the other races of elf might like war, but they are more savage than the pure high elves that try to\nstay out of political disputed with other states or kingdoms like the present example of the Dominion of Malin and The Silver State of Haleun'or they don't mix and stay out of eatch other's business\nSo the conclusion on time and races of elves and humans is that a human life is a hard and short one and the life of an elf is longer and not as hard. Things vary all the time everything can change at any moment, politics, time itself etc.\n \n     -Ornias", ""),
        StaticBook("congrats loser", "Laurence Baldwin de Vimont", "If you've found this\nyou've killed me\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n{\"text\":\"\"}\nhehe", ""),
        StaticBook("Conjuration", "Mythras Sylvari", "Conjuration:\n          \nConjuration, is the bringing of life forms formed in the void by the conjurationist, into the world itself.\n\nThe three sub-magicks of Conjuration, are.\n- Primodrialism\n- Perenial\n- Morphonic\nMorphon, is the conjuring of animals, and the creating of them as a entity. \n\nIt's to bring a animal into existence, after lots of study put into the animals insides, and outsides, essentially, interior or exterior.\nPerenial, is the conjuring of plant life, after the study of the interior and exterior workings, to do such, is the arcane art of the conjuring of perenials.\n\nEssentially, the conjuring of a studied plant.\nPrimordial, is the bringing of elementals into the physical plain.\n\nYou must be learned in the art of evocation, and the added art of conjuration.\n\nIt's essentially summoning creatures made of your arcane element.\nConjuration, is as said, a hard art, the many theories enacted suggest slowness in the power of the art.\n\nTo evoke animals and plants, and even elementals, is a valuable skill.\nSources:\n\n- Goliath\n- Random Magician \n- Learnings in delver bas.\n\n- Inquisition\n- Theories", ""),
        StaticBook("Contemplations 1", "Catarrh", "The Contemplations of Malog, Goj in San’Thraka agh Wargoth of the Yars:\n\nHear what mi blah, young one, for mi blah lat h’dub honors by which the orc lives, agh h’dub pillars on which he stands.\n\nSTRENGTH\nVALOR\nBOLDNESS\nCONTENTMENT\nWISDOM\nFORTHRIGHTNESS\nDISCIPLINE\n\nStrength is the simplest honor. It is by nub means easy to attain, but very easy to lose. It comes with the weight of earth, yet drifts away on the currents of the river from the unwary. Yet, for he who keeps watch, it\nstands firm as iron. By it, the body stands strong. Without it, the bones fall as dry reeds. By the strong outer self, the strong inner self is protected.\n\nValor is that which compels us to stand. By it, we face our enemies agh conquer our fears. Turn nub\nfrom the challenges of life. Cower nub before latz foes. Stand agh face those who oppose lat.\n\nBoldness is that energy by which we charge into the flames. Be unafraid of risk. It is the gate to many rewards. As valor makes us stand our ground,\nboldness commands us to press forward.\n\nContentment is the key to satisfaction. Trust nub in wealth, for gold flees as the sands on the night winds. Trust nub in land, for many lands agh whole continents have been lost before this ash. Be content with\nlatz lusk agh latz bread. If you have a blarg, a mate, agh cubs, lat be truly blessed beyond measure.\n\nWisdom is born of a strong mind. It is more practical than philosophy, agh goes beyond mere knowledge. It is the ability for right living, common sense,\nwit, resolution of life’s problems, agh success beyond material gain. Gruk for latself, but heed the blahings of those more experienced with the respect agh consideration due them. Learn from life, agh apply latz learning in a way that means something.\nForthrightness is honesty agh directness. It is intentional clarity of blahing, agh the absence of deception. Blah truth. Blah to blah, nub to flatter. Do as lat blah, agh let latz blah be latz bond. If lat blah it, mean it. Live it\n\nDiscipline is that\nwhich keeps latz life in order. Without it, all other honors forsake lat, for honor demands that the honorable governs his life with the will of iron. Hard work agh control of the self hold body agh mind together.\n\nStand firm in all these things, agh lat\nwill stand indeed.", ""),
        StaticBook("Contemplations 2", "Catarrh", "The second book of the Contemplations of Malog, Goj in San’Thraka, agh Wargoth of the Yars:\n\nHeed mi warning, all who are wise, for mi blah lat h’dub dishonors by which an orc is ruined, agh h’dub shames which may befall him.\n\nWEAKNESS\nIRRESOLUTION\nCOWARDICE\nAVARICE\nTHOUGHTLESSNESS\nDUPLICITY\nCOMPLACENCY\n\nWeakness, blah’d plainly, is the absence of strength. Be nub frail, agh neglect nub the body. He who can-nub defend himself, how can he defend that for which he stands? To give place to weakness is\nto keep latz bub’hoshest treasure in a fortress of straw.\n\nIrresolution is the way of he who falters in the presence of adversity. Do nub quail when the storm thunders. Do nub turn away when latz enemies roar. Be nub like the rabbit, who, when he is\nfrightened but a little, flees to his hole for safety. The rabbit is prey, agh the orc must never be prey.\n\nCowardice halts the feet, agh binds the spirit. Nub hosh thing comes to the coward, for the coward takes nub risk. The coward never takes the\ndifficult path. There is no success for such an ash. All his ways are worthless, agh his life profitless.\n\nAvarice is the heart of the empty spirit. Lust for gold blinds the eyes to truth, agh numbs the mind to wisdom. It makes the senses dull, such that\nhe whose life is governed by the mina can-nub perceive honor, agh hears nub the call of Krug.\n\nThoughtlessness is to the mind as weakness is to the body. The ash who gruks nub is nub’hosher than the blind or the deaf, for he chooses himself nub to gruk\nthe way to live rightly. He is blind to the way in which he should walk, agh the advice of the wise enters nub his ear. All his workings are senseless, agh his feet lead him to shame.\n\nDuplicity is the forked tongue of the dishonorable. The duplicitous\nash blahs “yub” with his mouth, but “nub” in his spirit. His blah can-nub be trusted, for he is a serpent agh a companion to serpents. With his face he grins agh flatters, but behind his back he bears a double-edged dagger to betray lat. Do nub be like\nsuch an ash, for the number of those who will hear him dwindles by the hour.\n\nComplacency is the quicksand upon which the glob builds a blarg. It is a hosh-smelling poison, agh a smooth road which leads to ruin. Complacency lulls lat to sleep with a soft\ntouch agh sweet songs, but, when lat have awoken, it has stolen latz strength, latz mate, agh all latz bread.\n\nThe ash who falls into any of these falls surely agh quickly to his destruction.", ""),
