ind these symbols, the next chapter will explain\nherbs that will best aid you in the process of finding symbols for all the elements.\nThe Tales of the Traveling Alchemist, Vol I-2\n\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven\n*Strange Numbers*\n((13/6/13))", ""),
        StaticBook("Elements of Mind", "Aris", "Elements of Mind\n           /\\\n         /\\ /\\\n         ><  ><\n         \\/ \\/\n           \\/\n     /\\         /\\\n     \\/         \\/\n\nThoughts on the mind and its most basic concepts.\n             Aris, 1461.\nEvery living being has a soul and a body, but what makes them unique, is their Mind. Mind itself is what defines you, and your actions. However, mind is different, it can be viewed in many ways, but it isn't as simply explained the body or a soul. But\nA simplistic way to explain would be this;\n If a being was a ship\n\n Your body would be the hull,\n\n Your soul would be the sails,\n\nBut the ship would never sail without its Captain, the Mind.\nAn example simple as that makes everything easier to understand, yet the Mind is more than that, a Mind can be described and divided in different ways. You can say a mind is inferior or superior, but you cannot say a mind is right or wrong. Continuing\nwith the same example from before, Mind, the Captain, can fit in three categories;\n  The Captain can be..\nCurious, jumping into adventure in search of answers.\nIntelligent, a person of logic and reason.\nKnowing, from experience and books\nCuriosity, Intelligence and Knowledge are the key points of the Mind, but ultimately, there is  Wisdom. Now, let me break down the four.\n\nCuriosity, the most innocent of the elements of mind for it is the base of all knowledge. A child is curious,\nbecause it is important and natural for them to gain experience from the world they have stepped their foot in. Curiosity is the desire, the craving to gain information or learn something. Curiosity is important to me, because it is what activates me,\nand more importantly, motivates me. It's very\nimportant for me to be\nopen to try new things\nand find out more, as there is an endless\namount of information.\n\nKnowledge is, in my opinion, the outcome of curiosity. It the term used for skills and\ninfromation gained via experience and / or education. Knowledge is something many seek, without truly knowing what they are looking for; knowledge isn't only facts laid out by letters in a book, but the prize of feeding one's curiosity for they can\nbecome truly aware of how things are. Knowledge covers familiarity, awareness and understanding of\nsomeone and something\nbut simply put, it doesn't mean you grasp the concept.\nOne can say they know\nor understand evocation, yet never be able to actually\nevoke a single droplet of water, a lone spark, or a tiny huff of wind.\nKnowledge is something\nthe mali'aheral, the High Elven, hold very dear. It could even be\ncalled the national\ntreasure of the race.\n \"Kae maehr evar\"\nKnowledge protects me\nCommon-\nphrase from the High Elven, is something that also fits me. Knowledge is what I seek, and something I already hold in some topics. It is also something that has saved me many times in seemingly helpless situations. For me, it is important to know the\nright people, and know\nthe right things. Knowledge is also the very reason I originally picked up\nthe graceful art of\nMental Magic, because for me, knowledge is a seemingly endless goal\nas new information keeps surfacing, and\nmental magic is inevitably\nthe door and also the key to new knowledge.\nBefore I started learning, it was a door\nthat remained locked for me, but now the door is opening to me\nagain, slowly, bit by bit.\n\nIntelligence, the ability to acquire and apply new information and knowledge.\nAn intellectual mind adds and overwrites\nold knowledge. You can not learn to become intelligent, for you are someone who can grasp concepts, or you will never be such.\nWhat makes intelligence different from knowledge, is that you\ncan be born\nintelligent, but you cannot be born knowledgeable. An intelligent person has great mental capacity for judgement, reasoning and logical thinking. A person that knows alot doesn't necessarily have all this, sadly, superior minds are often thought to be\nsly, only becasue they are vastly more intelligent than the\npeople around them.\nTo me, intelligence isn't something you can train, but something you do or do not have,\nshould it be the latter,\nI have trouble taking\nsuch people seriously.\n\nUltimately,\nWisdom is the prize of attaining the most of these three key elements of the mind.\nBecoming wise isn't something you can decide yourself, it is more like a title you\ngain from your surroundings. The Wise\nis intellectual, knowledgeable, yet still curious\nfor more. In my opinion wisdom is what an individual should truly\ntry to reach for, but\nmany are simply unfit.\nWisdom is the quality of having experience, vast amounts of knowledge, good judgement and logical thinking, and that isn't something you can\nattain within a short time frame; it is the\nultimate achievement.\nA wise person to me, is someone to look up to,\nand someone who should receive endless respect, but unfortunately the truly wise are scarce.\nThough, you can be wise without knowing\na lot, as the only true wisdom is in knowing you know nothing.. and one must beware of false knowledge for it is more dangerous than ignorance itself.\nThe path to wisdom is\nendless and eternal.\n------------------- \n-------------------", ""),
        StaticBook("Elements of Mind", "Farley III Stafyr", "Elements of Mind\n\nWritten by Godwein Stafyr\nUpon the writing of the Theorem of Wisdom, my very own mind came to the conclusion that there must be more to it. Every person knows of elements such as fire, earth, water, wind or air and lightning.  But aside from external elements which we\ncan feel and use, there are also other elements, in a completely different range, of which we unknowingly make use and into which we tap, without realizing it.\n\nThe Elements of Mind.\nThe mind is a strange fabric, giving us the ability to think, to consider, to act, react, to learn, to remember and many things more. But the mind itself, such as the outer world we experience, has its own elements. To underline this, an example will be\ngiven.\n\nFeelings. Feelings, such as love, guilt, hate, happiness and many more. This is one of the elements of the mind, we are able to use it, express ourselves with it, to tap into the power of this element.\nFurther explanation is not necessary, I presume, as everyone has at least one feeling within him.\n\nOther elements do exist. Wisdom is one of them, as I aforementioned. Also knowledge and experience are\nelements of the mind. But there are more!\n\nConcentration, Purity, Peace and Balance, and Common Sense.\n\nHow each of these work, will be explained in further, upcoming books. e\nFor the element of wisdom, I refer myself to my work “Theorem of Wisdom” for further information. The book “Knowledge and Experience – Elaborated” may give additional hints about two further elements.\n-: Primary Edition :-", ""),
        StaticBook("Elle's diary", "Gilondir Oronar Frostbeard", "The 13th of Malin's Welcome, yet 1612-\n\nLife, depicted at birth which we all did not choose, but endure. \n\nPerhaps the observer of this life bears no heart to allow certain actions be allowed. \n\nYet somehow, GOD is who I run to for help.\nMy name, for the time being is hidden. For a story such as this caters to the eyes of unknowing perception.\n\nAnd this, this, is my story.\n-----------------------It was a cold, crisp night. Every new year begins with one. Just, this year had been\n-\nmuch different. Why? You may ask and I shall tell. \n\nThe North, angered by an action they took various times now rebels. Contradiction. A word, not in House Ruthern's vocabulary. \nA group of them gathered that night.\nOutside the gates of Courland. A traitor, a swine, rejected from birth. He entered this world as a bastard to a Northern house.\n\nAs the Staunton house accepted him, nurished his body of welcome. Yet, his eyes fed for the approval of the North.\n-\nFor I saw much this day, and it started with him. When he opened up the frontal doors.\n\nHearing the men, bewildered like beast coming for prey, my mother grabbed me. Crying out her worst fears so that we may escape.\nUp the tower, climbing the mucky ladder and into the moat's way we swam to our safety. \n\nFrogs croaked, fireflies danced in their floral homes and for us, we ran. And ran. \n\nIt almost seemed like water in a dessert.\n-\nFor there it was. Our safe house. A woman who joined us. \n\nShut, locked and hidden away they placed me. In this life, I never been fond of being a lanky woman until that night. \n\nSnug, I fit into a hole\nThis hideout could fit no more than I and breath. \n\nTime passed, slowly, churning my stomach more as we waited for word of safety. \n\nWe soon got ahold of someone, but it was not written or expected.\n-\nIt was them. The rebels and they smelled of malice. \n\nDoor by door they broke it down. Grabbing both my mother and her accomplice as if they were men. Degrading their every exhale.\nI couldn't move. How cowardic. For an outspoken woman, always upsetting the peasantry with honesty.\n\nI froze. \n\n{!} Upon the page was a damaged flower crown as if it had been stepped upon.\n- \n\nWhen they left, I came out. Silence, took over my soul. The crown just gifted to me, stepped, crushed and dead in silence as I.\n\nKnowing that my mother, my last parent was gone. Will it be forever? It can't be.\nForever is what if felt like. I ate, nor slept, and couldn't even be at home in the palace.\n\nMy Uncle's keep is large and spacious enough for some alone time. Time I hated, but needed.\n\nBirds, casted with letters of threats and negotiations\n-\nFor my hand, for money, for ransom and my own life.. in place for my mother.\n\nIt was the day, the day I was about to give up my life in trade for my mothers when an angel showed it's light upon evil. \n\nHis name, Peter.\nI've met him once in my time before. Fortunate enough to do so, because other than my father, Tobias. He's one of the greatest men I know. \n\nHe saved not only my family that day, but, unknowingly me as well.\n-\nI was made stronger, better and wiser. A lesson in this life learned from someone of my position who too was dealt the wrong cards. \n\nAnd for that, I am eternally grateful to this man. \n\nReunited atlas.\nShe came home, beaten, defeated and distressed. But, she indeed came home. \n\nIf you haven't figured me out yet, leave my name at rest. For I was just the girl, scared like any when life is about to take away the most precious of nature.\n-\nWinning or losing, I am ready to face the fate of this war. \n\nFor even if good is at a loss, good shall prevail in a world filled of evil. \n\n\n- Elle\n{!} An image of the Queen consort, Lisette Henretti and her daughter, her Highness, Elizabeth Maria Staunton. The pair would be embracing at the palace gates. Beneath the image was written. \n\"Fruuntur in Gloria.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Eloluer'ne", "Dio Astóre", "Excavation and research within Haelun’or continued alongside restoration efforts up until migration became necessary. Prior to this many discoveries and translations enabled us to fulfill our cultural re-envisioning.\nPoetic and prosaic texts offered glimpses into our way of life, whilst a collection of cryptic texts located within the dark depths of the land riddled something of complexity. For the sake of preservation, the dig-site was made restricted.\nFollowing the remains of the subterranean water conduits allowed us to happen upon a stairwell leading to a cavern, containing rocky islands partially submerged in water, all linked by bridges.\nThe wall furthest from the stairwell had hewn out of it a hollow stone shell, within which stood a freestanding archway.\nWater flowed through the shell and the archway caught the stream in such a way as to create an aquatic curtain, which itself drained through a crack in the floor barely a finger-width across.\nThe entire surface of the archway was covered in symbols and meticulously wrought, each deeply etched into the stone and encrusted with an assortment of gemstones and metals, of mostly purple and gold varieties.\nA haze shrouded thoughts and made wakefulness a seemingly difficult task when in proximity to this unnatural edifice.\nReferences to this Gate stored around rooms of the subterranean network provided the confidence required to proceed with more elaborate interactions.\nLucidity changed in a linear manner that matched distance to the archway. Moving under the curtain of water induced a brief mental oblivion, upon the conclusion of which an awakening would occur and one would find oneself placed in a dreamlike scene.\nProlonged exposure loosened notions of selfhood and induced a disconnect with tangibility. Remaining longer within would increase the likelihood of feeling pain upon returning, the means for that being submersion in water.\nOnce safety procedures were established for exploration, I invited a select few to experience immersion and their proceedings helped shape the conclusions drawn.\nWitnessing each of them enter produced the same result: the water would appear to carry their corporeal form away as rain does to clay. Returning saw the process similarly reversed.\nFeatures within the dreamscape appeared to be influenced by thoughts, memories and emotions - stronger by those of the subjects entering.\nObjects both found and brought lacked substance, constantly shifting toward and away from definition, possessing amorphous qualities that lent themselves to manipulation. An exception was the soulstone, which proved resilient to the effects.\nOur early observations showed things primal in nature, with mortality and fertility as dominating themes.\nIntroducing various animals produced scenes that reflected their habits, however they often would not survive returning and the simpler life-forms would become translucent before fading away entirely.\nRemnants of exposed personalities would very briefly linger and continue to give shape to the surroundings. A conclusion drawn from my observations is that strength of will corresponds to longevity, and shared intent accomplishes this exponentially.\nSigns of Mali’aheral influence pre-dating our own remained for observation; shadowy images of immense regal structures, monuments with celestial and aquatic motifs, figures of proud stature attending to their noble works - all like murky apparitions.\nThose that had entered before us strived greatly to preserve our legacy; the magnitude of willpower required to endure that plane befits only our ancestors.\nTheir genius birthed constructs that reflected their memories, but time had diminished their potency. The immensity of energy required to enact such creation suggested a great sacrifice and a constant link to a formidable source of power.\nNevertheless, none of the things produced within could be withdrawn for application elsewhere, narrowing the possible intentions for its construction.\nMere historical preservation remains an unsatisfactory explanation, for it is too close to vanity for our kind and the medium has a nature of brevity. The possibility of a contraption having been built inside the Gate is a promising theory.\nAmong my conclusions regarding the Gate is that the Golden Pools had the additional function of acting as a primary mechanism in the preparation of the body for the Gate, whilst offering a way to passively channel mana into it.\nSeveral agents capable of manipulating said mana would nevertheless be necessary - the maehr’sae hiylun’ehya surely cultivated the appropriate qualities of mind.\nSuccessive generations of purity would certainly compound this, however the process is long and all too easy to interrupt.\nImpurity is a great hinderance and death must be avoided. Corruption too must be kept at bay. Without life, the edict of the maehr’sae hiylun’ehya cannot be fulfilled.\nReplication of the Gate is possible, however a location with the requisite qualities must be established, as the Nodes or Pools. Each realm varies in its properties and migrations act as a setback.\nThe time necessary for a sole person to proceed with the undertaking is not negligible, however I am certain that the ascension of our race is tied to its principles.\n\n\n<<The following page appears to have belonged to another book.>>\nIt transcends flesh and doubt; not faith is it, nor reasoned conviction. \nCast yourself within the exalted device and behold!\nWhole and meticulous are these constructs rendered.\nEndlessly does the dream unfold ‘fore your stellar awakening.\n- Dio Astóre", ""),
        StaticBook("Eloluern'ne II", "Dio Astóre", "Further study confirms that the mana required to sustain Eloluer’ne is prodigious. Mana fed into it will diminish passively, disappearing entirely within moments unless anchored or linked to suitable shielding.\nEven so, such protective measures must be actively maintained. Modification to the Gate component could reduce the required frequency of re-entering.\nDue to its intermediary nature, certain artefacts evoked within Eloluer’ne can be sustained even by unshaped mana, so long as the artefact was initially engineered to work at various stages of damage.\nOnly highly skilled practitioners could feasibly arrange such a thing, but the range of applicable disciplines is not limited.\nI theorise that apart from utilisation of the soulstone itself, the most effective alternative would be any magic that has a nullifying effect on mana or which actively feeds on it.\nGreat care must be taken and precision exercised to avoid accidental damage to any of the constructs. Diligence too should be afforded to ensuring their continued operation.\nThe effect of the passive diminishment does not seem to take place from a fixed point or direction, but rather occurs at all positions simultaneously. Anyone introducing or maintaining constructs within must therefore take this phenomenon into account.\nFollowing the design logic of the Golden Pools and the water conduits of old Haelun’or, the redirection of mage-gold and seamless integration of discrete water features could allow for a slow yet progressive siphoning of mana to power Eloluer’ne.\nShowers, fountains and bathing areas are all viable for connecting to Eloluer’ne. Heated water is recommended to mask any fatigue that may be noticeable after a prolonged session of immersion.\nIt is advised that children are prevented from entering such areas and instead be directed to any that are unconnected.\nIntroduction of a sanitation policy would compliment progress, however whether foreigners too should be used is a matter of discretion. In the positive case, such facilities are to be kept to the exterior of the city proper.\nCorrupted beings may yield unpredictable results, though the purification of Eloluer’ne shall indubitably cleanse those parts it deems unfit.\nWith regular attention it should be possible the construct a device within Eloluer’ne that operates in a similar manner to a portal, delivering those that are worthy to the promised lands.\nLye’sae ito celian.\nOOC Notes:\nNo positive or negative effects need to be roleplayed.\n\nAlmost everything disappears inside the pocket dimension of Eloluer’ne. Exceptions include soulstones, people and anything with mana.\nHow fast something disappears depends mostly on mana levels, intellect and willpower. Any devices built inside need to be protected and fixed regularly.\nSiphoning is mild and could make a character tired or hungry, depending on how energetic / healthy / rested they are. If a character is sickly, optional prolonged weakness is possible if they choose.\nA character staying too long inside Eloluer’ne will feel pain when returning, but this is never fatal. Absent-mindedness, drowsiness, forgetfulness and emotional changes would scale with time, but are all optional and effects vary from person to person.\nThe portal - if completed - would be powered by a soulstone. If used, an imperfect copy of the persona would be made in another universe.\nThis either has no effect on the current character or perma-kills them and destroys the soulstone used. Visual effects depend on personality and aura colours.\nIf you want a part of your character’s story to continue in another game / world, feel free to use this to link them together.\n\nThe dream goes on...", ""),
        StaticBook("Elven Lessons I", "Kalenz Uradir", "New Elven\n      Lessons:\n     Volume 1\n\n   by Kalenz Uradir\nIn Elven grammatical structure is often different to that of Common. For one instead of placing an adjective before a noun it is placed after said noun. This becomes particularly important for the most basic of greetings.\nKarin'ayla - Goodday\n\nKer'ayla - Goodnight\n\nVan'ayla - Goodbye\n\nEach places the time of day before the adjective describing it. Noun and adjective are separated by an apostrophe.\nOther important words of the beginner in Elven include race names:\n\nMali - Elf\n\nUruk - Orc\n\nValah - Human\n\nBortu - Dwarf\nIn order to form sub-races of Elves, adjectives are taken to add more detail to the word Mali.\n\nMali'ame - Wood Elf\n\nMali'aheral - High Elf\n\nMali'ker - Dark Elf\nIn order to form sub-races of other cumtures the suffix tali- may be used. This word most directly means 'part of'.\n\nTali'uruk - Goblin \n(Lit: Part Orc)\n\nTali'valah - Halfling\n(Lit: Part Human)\nOther basic phrases include exclamations including:\n\nAyla - Good\n\nSul'leyun - Brilliant\n§i(Lit: Beautiful Light)", ""),
        StaticBook("Elven Raiders", "Amelia", "Case Name: The Elven Sinister\n\nSuspect(s): A fully armored figure under the Mali'fenn. Nothing known about the group, assumed to be elven knights. Armor consists of gold trimmed silver plate, highly ornamented with gold.\nCrimes: Murder of 3 Felsenic officers, assault on various others.\n\nWitnesses: Giancarlo Gradic(Captain of Felsen Guards)\n\nCase details/updates:\nGiancarlo notified detectives of a lorannian guard who came into the\ninspector's office dropping a sack of 3 heads, identified as felsen guards. Subject last seen fleeing the scene.\n\nCASE CLOSED:\n\nRaiders.", ""),
        StaticBook("Enchanting Runes", "Laerean", "Enchanting Runes\nX is Lapis\nO is an Empty Space\n\nMagic Resistance\nXOXOX\nOXXXO\nXOXOX\n\nLuck of the Sea\nOXXOX\nXOOXO\nOXXOX\nSmite\nOXXO\nXXXX\nXXXX\nOXXO\n\nUnbreaking\nXOX\nOXO\nXOX\nProtection\nXXXOXXX\nXXXXXXX\nOXXOXXO\nOOXXXOO\n\nLure(Hook)\nXO\nXO\nXX\n\nSilk Touch\nXXXXXXXXX\nEfficiency\nXOO\nOXX\nXOO\n\nAqua Affinity\nXOO\nXXO\nXXX\nOXX\nEnhanced Unbreaking\nXOXOXOX\nOXOOOXO\nXOXOXOX\n\nFortune\nXXOOOOOO\nOOXOOOXO\nOXOXOXXX\nXOOXOOXO\nLooting\nOXOOOOOXO\nXXXXXOXXX\nOXOOOOOXO\n\nRespiration\nXXXX\nXXXX\nOXXO\nXOOX\nPunch\nOXXX\nXXXX\nXXXX\nOXXX\n\nKnockback\nXXOOO\nXXXXX\nXXXXX\nXXOOO\nBlast Protection\nOOOOOXX\nOXXXXOO\nXXXXXXO\nOXXXXOO\n\nInfinity\nOXXXOXXXO\nXOOOXOOOX\nXOOOXOOOX\nOXXXOXXXO\nProjectile Protection\nXOOXOOXXX\nOXXXXOXXX\nXOOXOOXXX\nOOOOOOOXO\n\nSharpness\nOOXOOOOOO\nXXXXXXXXO\nXXXXXXXXX\nOOXOOOOOO\nPower\nXXOOOOXOO\nOOXXXXXXO\nOOXXXXXXX\nXXOOOOXOO\n\nThorns\nXOXOXOXOX\nXOXOXOXOX\nXOXOXOXOX\nXOXOXOXOX", ""),
        StaticBook("Encounter Wisdom", "Elindor", "The Encountering of\n         Wisdom\n\n\nA reflection on the universe and the perception of self.\n\n\n\nBy Elindor\nSection I\nTo Embark.\n\nThe trouble with remembrance is that it is so often untrue. To succumb to nostalgia is the bliss of the weary eyed, but the act is mere self inflation. Ones memory is tainted by ones own perspective and all reminiscence can\nachieve is an inflation of ego. Even if the memory is of sour taste and of humbling nature, the forced remembrance of it is to turn to ones own perspective and confirm that it is true and without faulter, and in being humbled, to congratulate ones' self\non the acceptance of humility. For memory is in its nature broken in that reality is perceived through one set of eyes and no more. It is only politicians and fools who expect their own perception to be the unwavering truth. Yet in memory we are all\nweakened to beleiving. Sensibly so. Prejudices are formed by the observation of something repeatedly. Thereby if our thoughts are solidified by repetition, then to reminisce is to encourage ones faulted perception, and confirm that it is\nthe unwavering truth. Once observed. Twice noted. Thrice confirmed. And moreso under the influence of awareness of repidity (whereby one is more likely to see something once attention is pulled to it. Example: there are a lot more Southerons. Observation\nof Southerons is heightened into beleiveing that whether the hypothesis is accurate or not, the statement is perceived to be confirmed).\nSection II\nTo Change.\n\nA finite perspective from which to view the universe is the taint upon all our thoughts. Higher cognitive awareness starts with the self realisation of this fact and the embarking upon a new journey to perceive\nself, the universe, and everything from a perspective wider, broader, and deeper than that which finite personal perspective can offer. The only way this writer has discovered to escape the emprisonment of finite perspective is to observe the thought\nlandscapes of others. There is no enlightenment through which all things can be perceived anew, much talk of such is true to the preacher and a falsity to all others. To see the world from the eyes of another one must actually see the world through the\neyes of another. The practicalities of such I have observed in other texts, and will heretofore assume that those reading further are already familiar with the sharing of thought space with another. While it is an important step to enter the thoughts of\nanother being, the specific experience upon which I write is to move beyond the observation of thought and the impression of ones' own, and into a deeper plane of thought consciousness through which one can experience shared perception.\nAssuredly one can never expect to gain insights into the biological experiences of another, but in an environment in which consciousness and self are a fluid concept, the psychoexperiential stimuli of another's perspective opens new ideas on thought\nitself. From such a standing can one come to a comprehension of more than the thoughts of another, but the patterns of their mindscape, and their comprehension of reality, through which all the world is perceived and understood. One does not need to know\ntheir doings and personality to embark upon understanding their mental cognition. So it is often good practice to experience the perspectives of those who you do not know, and more suggested to avoid experiencing the perspectives of acquantances or\nanyone you would share company, as conversion becomes incredibly dull when, from your experienceing their cognitive mindspace, their speech and reactions can be foreseen.\nSection III\nEmbrace Difference.\n\nThere is seeing which is juvenile and easy, and there is knowing which is the lies of the aged. No one truly knows anything. But one can comprehend. The great strenght drawn from observing the ways in which others perceive\nthe universe is the subsequent self reflection. Escape the finite imprisonment of your perspective and you will be capable of comprehending any little thing for what it truly is, instead of what you expect it to be.\nSection IV\nTo Influence.\n\n\"He awoke as from a dream and turned\n\"His gaze looked through me as a looking glass\n\"And his head rolled upon the pillow\n\"Eyes resting upon a candle by the bedside\n\"Into which his gaze became transfixed\n\"As though the universe trembled in its tongue.\n\"His voice did raise though his attention stolen\n\" 'I know you yet somehow you are new to me ' \"\n\nThose whose perception is larger than themselves will see through falsity\nand know that it is only true to he who utters it. But others will beleive what their own perception tells them. And all the more what remembrance confirms of their own perspective. To imprint ideas is only difficult in translating the subtleties to the\nperspective of the receiver. Knowing their mindspace, or at least having a comprehension of other cognitive processes and placing stimuli that is open to interpretation, transforms the closed door to canvas. Weave their perception into what is fabricated\nand the self indulgent reminiscence will reinforce anything therein as fact.\nOnce observed.\nTwice noted.\nThrice confirmed.\nSection V\nOn Universe.\n\nAll that exists is as infinite or miniscule as the thinker allows. One can suppose to the thinker that it is contraty, but they will forever see the stars and heavens as paint upon the sky until they themselves experience\nsomething that forces a change in comprehension of perspective. So how big the universe or how small the self is a question of infinite layering. And all will answer a differing value. The truth is that none are wrong and all are true.", ""),
        StaticBook("ENLIST NOW!", "Ravodan Dragovij", "Are you looking for a stable job?\nAre you looking to fight the good fight?\nWell then join the Eshevurd Company, the De Savoie Levymen!\nYou'll be supplied with armour and arms, also a good roof!\nIf you're interested, send a bird to Ravodan (Brazilianski)", ""),
        StaticBook("Ennulia T.", "Ol' Man Sano", "Dear Ennulia,\n\nI apologize for all the times that we had bickered between eachother, but I do wish to offer you something that you have been lusting after for some time, since you-\nwere a child at Rosethorn, in fact. You are a delightful child, one which I was honored to meet during my lifetime, and I thank you for such. Armin should deliver this note to you, as well as a certain skull.\nThank you.", ""),
