        StaticBook("Fox's Choice", "Aislin Maar", "Fox's Choice\n\nWritten by\nAislin Maar\n\nIn the year 1612\n\n\"For my love, Karyssmov.\"\nOnce upon a time, in a forest far away, there lived a fox. This fox had many friends, and was brave and strong. He was cunning and clever and knew all the tricks to fend off the predators of the forest. Despite being a fox, he had many woodland friends.\nRabbits, squirrels, chipmunks, hedgehogs, and all manner of birds just to name a few. Fox was very popular, and he helped protect all his friends from the predators of the forest.  Fox had one friend in particular who he had known since he was just a\nsmall pup. Raven, a fierce black bird with a scowling expression. Raven was quieter than the other birds, and not nearly so colorful with her jet black feathers, but she was loyal and cared greatly for Fox, making sure he was safe and always giving him\nadvice. Fox knew lots of birds in the forest, and they all adored him for his bright red fur and clever wit. Fox liked the attention, too. He would spend time with all the birds, letting them fly about and show off their pretty feathers and sing their\npretty songs. Sometimes he spent more time with one or two birds than the others, and they started to think Fox wanted to spend all his time with them. Those birds would fly down, singing their greatest song to Fox, telling him how much they loved him.\nFox always turned them down though, because all Fox wanted was to be their friend. He didn’t understand why all the birds wanted to spend so much time with him. Raven, meanwhile, simply watched as Fox pranced and played and spent time with all the other\nanimals, and all the other pretty birds. She wasn’t terribly jealous, because Fox spent a lot of time with her too. She knew that the conversations that they had together were different from the ones Fox had with the other birds. They spoke about the\nforest around them, and the problems that plagued the creatures there. They spoke about their feelings, and Raven even confessed to Fox that she felt different than all the other birds and creatures with their bright colors and pretty songs.\nFox assured Raven that the other birds weren’t so great. They sang songs, sure, but that was about it. Raven remembered this, keeping their time together close to her heart while Fox was off playing with the other animals.\nOne day, a great Stag wandered into the forest. He was quite massive, with antlers towering so high into the air that they raked through the leaves of the trees. He was truly a formidable sight. Stag gazed about the forest, looking quite cross.\nHe had heard that Fox was living here peacefully among the other animals, the ones he should be hunting, and he was not happy. Stag thought this was not the way things should be, and he wanted to change it. So Stag came into a clearing where all the\nanimals were frolicking and having fun. Fox was there, with all the other animals and birds, and looking fearfully to the Stag. Stag was much, much bigger than Fox, and would easily be able to hurt Fox if he wanted. Stag stomped into the clearing\nangrily, and shouted that Fox needed to leave the forest forever. All of the animals around Fox began to cower in fear of the great Stag, afraid to get in the way for risk of being hurt themselves. One or two animals began to speak up though, telling Stag\nhe couldn’t make Fox leave. The Stag reared his legs in anger, stamping his hooves down into the dirt with a thunderous noise. All of the animals and birds scattered, terrified of the raging Stag! Fox was left alone, his eyes wide as he stared up at the\nangry Stag who drew nearer and nearer. The Stag told Fox that he would make him leave, and he would never come back. Stag reared up onto his hind legs, waving his front legs menacingly, ready to stomp down onto Fox! Suddenly, though, from the tree line\ncame Raven, swooping in and letting out a great loud caw. Raven flew at the Stag’s head, beating her wings as hard as she could in his face. She drew up her talons, striking at his nose, and Stag pulled himself away.\nStag turned, letting out a pained bleat as he shook his head, his nose stinging from Raven’s strike. Raven swooped at Stag again, flapping her black wings in his face, sending Stag turning and running off into the trees! Stag was not expecting that, and\nstarted sprinting off into the forest. Raven called after him, a loud caw telling him not to come back. As Stag ran away, Raven swooped back into the clearing, landing next to Fox. She hopped over to Fox, making sure he was okay.\nFox stared at Raven, awestruck. Soon, though, he nuzzled up against Raven, wrapping his neck against her in a hug. Fox told Raven he was thankful she had not run off like the rest. Raven wrapped a wing around Fox’s back, hugging him.\nShe told him she would never run away when Fox was in trouble, as Fox had never run away when she was in trouble. Fox looked at Raven, and gave a nod. As all the forest animals and all the pretty birds began to creep back into the clearing,\nFox told Raven that he loved her. Stag never came back to that forest, and Fox and Raven lived happily together, protecting everyone from any threats that came to the forest after that.\n\nThe End.", ""),
        StaticBook("Friend NOT Foe", "Klaudia Crast", "-------------------\n\n\n\nYou Are Simply Perfect\n\nby Lady Klaudia Crast\n\n\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=\n\n\n------------------\nyou are simply perfect, \nIn elegance and grace, \nFor you're the only lady, \nWho's brings a smile to my face.\nWere not even together, \nFar from that I should say, \nBut every time we talk, \nyou brighten up my day.\nI've always had a thing for you, \nI don't know if That's okay, \nBut things had never been clearer, \nUntil the other day.\nyou lent over to look at me, \nWith those beautiful blue eyes, \nI can't believe it took me all this time, \nBut now I finally realise,\nThat your not some passing fancy, \nOr some passing trend, \nI really want to get to know you, \nBut Im just a 'kinda' friend.\nI'm afraid of what you'll think of me, \nSo I'll never make you feel, \nEither awkward, embarrassed or ashamed. \nAnd if I ever do, Im sorry,  \nI've only myself to blame.\nI need some time to think about us, \nThis is something I know, \nI'll only think about what's best for you, \nSo chances of me there are low,\nUntil then tough I fear, \nIt's up to me to decide, \nIf we'll ever be together, \nStanding side by side.\nMaybe we'll be together, \nAll in due time, \nBut until then tough, \nWould you be my valentine?\n\n-------------------", ""),
        StaticBook("Friend or Enemy?", "Sofetios", "Friend or Enemy?\nBy Sofetios\nEverywhere I am I hide from your sight,\nIf it’s in the day,\nAnd especially at night.\nI am one of sleep’s worst enemies,\nAnd one of fear’s best friends,\nI grow ever more larger,\nAs your will begins to bend.\nIn the day I am more friendly,\nEspecially when it’s hot and I’m in sight,\nBut when it turns to night time,\nI’m sure to give you a fright.\nI am a mimic of sorts,\nI try to copy you while you walk,\nI even mimic other people,\nAlthough I cannot talk.\nEven when you go about the day,\nI skulk and kind of stalk,\nFear or fear not, I am always here,\nI am the shadow on which you walk.", ""),
        StaticBook("Friend or foe c", "Faolan", "Name: Uther Grimlee \n\nAge: 26\n\nUnmarried\n\nLives in the fort or around the fort (does not specify)\n\nCaries weapons\n\nMaybe a sexual predator", ""),
        StaticBook("Friend or Foe?", "Regale Ba'ikana", "Gather some intel on a dreadlander from the Dunamis Fort outside our walls.", ""),
        StaticBook("Friendship", "Klaudia Crast", "-------------------\n\n\n\n       Friendship\n\n\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=\n\n\n\n-------------------\nA friend is like a flower, \na rose to be exact, \nOr maybe like a brand new gate \nthat never comes unlatched.\nA friend is like an owl, \nboth beautiful and wise. \nOr perhaps a friend is like a ghost, \nwhose spirit never dies.\nA friend is like a heart that goes \nstrong until the end. \nWhere would we be in this world \nif we didn't have a friend.", ""),
        StaticBook("Frum'Staaz", "Thurak'Yar", "[!]               [!] \n \nIt seems to be a small, wrinkled scrap of paper, torn from what must have been a great tome. The writing is faded, though easily made out against the yellow parchment.\n\n\n\n [!]               [!]\n“… all forms one could imagine. They gathered about the great Traf accompanied by their Shamans as Frum’staaz; they obeyed, but only in companionship, for these beasts were never to be controlled.\nBy one Uruk’s side only a flat, grey shadow could be seen, trailing from his feet and blinking back at those that glared…”", ""),
        StaticBook("Fund. Alchemy", "Rosso", "The Tales of a Travelling Alchemist\n------------\nThe Fundamentals of Alchemy\n\nVolume I of V\nForeword:\nI have heard many a man ask why one, such as myself, would take such interest and dedication in the art of alchemy when there are ready-made spells that could do just the same, if not better, a job than an apothecary’s potion. My response to\nthis statement is rather simple, whereas one must have a certain gift for spell-slinging, rune-craft, and spirit-speaking, one mustn’t need these frivolous commodities to use a balm to mend a wound, shroud themselves in a nigh-invisible cloak\nwith the swig from a bitter bottle, or set someone ablaze with bottled flame. Alchemy is not merely reproducing a wizard’s spell, but it is shaping the spell in a way for the common man’s use. While magic still has its place in our world, Alchemy allows\nthose without the gift to reap the same benefits as any mage might.\nChapter 1: The Fundamentals of Alchemy\n------------\nThe Principle and Law of Equivalent Exchange:\nAs I was providing my services to a rather quaint hamlet, which also provided me information of the local flora and more of the same legends I always here of, I was approached by a man of common repute, and he produced a sarcastic, yet thought provoking,\nquestion. This question was, and I will attempt to reproduce the dialect as best as my phonetic ability will allow, “ ‘ow come, Mister, ‘hat ye’ can make all ‘dis ‘onderful stuff outta nothin’?” Now as shameful as his speech may be, the question remains\nthe same. How is it, that alchemists are capable or reshaping the spells of sorcerers for the common folk out of, seemingly, nothing? The answer to this question is simple to state, but fairly difficult to grasp, and this is the Principle of Equivalent\nExchange. Much like the money one gives to the merchant for the apple or slab of meat, one cannot have something for nothing. As the saying goes, “there is no such thing as a free brunch,” there is no such thing as a free potion. This fundamental rule\napplies to old alchemy, through the use of symbols and rituals, far more than what I call “Modern” alchemy. Although I will not get into ancient alchemy, I will state why this rule applies to Modern Alchemy. To craft a potion, for example a bottle of\nalchemist’s fire, one requires the materials. Much like a spell casted by a wizard, one cannot make something out of nothing. Know that to make a potion, and the possibilities are truly endless, one must take plants, objects, and other representations of\nwhat they wish to make. For example, alchemist’s fire could be best produced with an - -, a mixture of -  -, -  -, and a dash of -  -. Why would one need these items?\nThe - - provides the basic mixture of what we will make, and of course the better quality of the - - the better quality of the Alchemist’s Fire, but of course we will get further into this in a later chapter. The -  -, a -  -\nplant that grows most often in deserts, is often -  - and often associated with -  -. This provides an superb symbol for the element of -  -. The -  -, another powerful symbol of -  -, mixes well with the crushed -  -\nto produce something truly strong. The -  -, an optional portion, is another symbol of -  -, albeit both weaker than blaze -  - for both a -  - fire burns far greater than -  -.\nNow then, although you have these symbols of flame, rage, and warfare, one mustn’t simply toss objects into the metaphorical cauldron. Precision is necessary, as always. One must measure their mixture carefully, or they will give too much. And giving too\nmuch will tip the scales of Equivalent Exchange, causing unpredictable results. Why is this you might ask? When balance is achieved through Equivalent Exchanged, a mixture of the elements for the bottled spell of your desire, than all is well. But when\nyou disrupt this balance and push on the world, taking and forcing in too much of one or more element then the world will push back. Of course a word of caution is required, for one may think taking less is the best option when balance is not a certainty.\nThis is a common misconception for not providing enough of one element will result in the same conclusion, imbalance and chaos. This can be easily associated with cooking, too much of one ingredient will spoil the recipe, not enough of an ingredient will\nproduce similar results. Of course it could also produce blander results, and in that case a potion may not be spoiled but it will simply be weak. This is preferable to exploding, of course.\nPreparation and You:\nNovice Alchemists often question me, what advice would you provide the budding alchemist, what sip of wisdom could you provide one who desires to tap your fountain of knowledge? The answer to this\nis rather simple, preparation is key to any potion. What is meant by this is that to mix a potion that you have not mixed before, or do not mix often, you must have all the facts and supplies before you begin. This is best provided in a short list\nof required procedures if one wishes to keep their eyebrows, fingers, and life.\n1. Have all the Facts\n2. Know the Recipe\n3. Clean and check all your instruments are in working order\n4. Ensure you have plenty of whatever ingredients you are using\n5. Keep and Calm Dispostion\nI believe the only topic that requires explanation is “Have all the Facts” and by this I mean that one should not mix a potion they know nothing about. Even having the recipe is not enough to craft a potion safely, but knowledge of its origins,\ningredients, and uses are crucial. Although budding alchemists often overlook this step, it may save your life one day.\nThe Tales of the Traveling Alchemist, Vol I\n\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven", ""),
        StaticBook("Gathering Herbs", "Katherine Fisher", "===================\n\n       The Art of\n    Gathering Herbs\n\n\n\n       Written by\n      Liriel Fisher\n   \n\n\n\n==================\nForeword:\nThis book was written by myself, and the research done by my close friend and alchemic colleague Nemir. As we consider ourselves budding and self-taught alchemists, we have spent many, many hours in libraries and pouring over every tome\nrelating to the profession that we could get our hands on. Through our experimentation and unwavering dedication to the art of alchemy, we have found ourselves in need of a comprehensive collection of knowledge on just where to find the herbs we depend on\nThus, The Art of Gathering Herbs was born. I hope you find it’s contents helpful.\nChapter 1-1: Introduction to Gathering\n-------------------Herbs are the most vital and important part of an alchemist’s professional life. They provide the basic necessary ingredients involved in all proper potions, and thus, must either be purchased\nor procured on your own. Purchasing dozens of herbs can be not only expensive, but extremely time consuming as you seek out sellers or wait for that one vital herb to show up in auction. Herbs can be found through various professions other than alchemy\nitself, such as lumberjacking, farming, and fishing. Herbs can also be obtained through treasure caskets retrieved from gathering. However, the best way for an alchemist to retrieve their own herbs while continuing to advance in the skill is to gather\nthem on their own with a pair of handy alchemists shears.  These special shears can be obtained from an adequate blacksmith at minimum.  They are made from two gold ingots, and two sticks. In my experience, I would recommend obtaining a large amount\nof these tools if you are unskilled as they will break quite easily in inexperienced hands. Personally, I carried ten pairs of these on my person when out searching for herbs as a fairly skilled alchemist. My more experienced companion, Nemir,\nwas able to obtain many more herbs than I was, but finding an herb can be very enlightening when you discover it with a pair of alchemists shears.  I would not recommend using this method of obtaining herbs until you are at least fair in skill, as even at\nthat level of knowledge you will not be very successful.\nChapter 1-2: Regions\n-------------------Now, finding the various herbs has been a rigorous and lengthy process. Nemir and I have narrowed the various types of regions into four types:\nHot, cold, wet, and dry.\nYou can group various types of biomes into one category, such as taiga, ice Plains, and extreme Hills, which are all cold, etcetera.\nDry would be plains and forests, hot is desert and mesa, and wet is jungle and swamps.\nChapter 2: Herbs and Where to Find Them\n-------------------Water Symbols\nMandragora: One of the most basic herbs in alchemy is mandragora. These rooty plants can be found in wet biomes, typically swamps, within ponds. They are usually found clinging to other aquatic plant life, such as lily pads and algaes.\nSerpernt's Stalk: A staple in any alchemist or healer's stash, Serpent's Stalk is often found alongside ponds and rivers in dry climates. In my search for the herb, I commonly found this snakeish plant growing up the stalks of sugar cane near water.\nFrost Vine: This interesting blue vine can be found snaking up the trunks of trees and rock walls of cold regions. The vines are often clearly visible and easy to find.\nBlissfoil: This healer's friend can be easily spotted in fields and forests in dry regions by their brilliant blue hued flowers. Though the stalk of the plant is the useful part, the flower petals are what will help you find them easily.\nAthin: An intriguing, blue colored algae, Athin is commonly found in cold regions, growing remarkably among icy waters. Though it can be a bit hard to spot in the bleak landscape, finding an iced over pond will surely yield this herb.\nFire Symbols:\nCrimson Vase: Found most commonly in dry regions, this interesting flower can be found in grassy fields and spotted by its - you guessed it - crimson colored petals.\nDrake's Tail: This herb is truly remarkable in my opinion. It grows where few other plants can, in the hot desert heat, perched among the pale yellow sandstone rocks. Wearing leather gloves when harvesting this is highly advised.\nDwarf's Pumpkin: One might think it's bright orange petals which give it it's name would be an excellent indicator of this herb's presence, but surprisingly, I had the best luck finding this peculiar plant by rummaging through the short grasses of swamps.\nBlood Lotus: Another remarkably persistent plant, this deep red herb can be found in hot regions snaking it's way up sandstone cliffs, and winding it's way through the sands.\nFlametongue Root: Like Drake's Tail, caution is advised when harvesting this volatile herb. As the name suggests, this root grows in the ground amongst the grasses of dry regions.\nAir Symbols:\nLarihei's Fingers: This particular plant grows in cold regions among the long grasses and ferns of the forest. Not much is known about this herb, but it is always advisable to have a few of each herb on hand.\nElf's Hair Vine: Finding this herb will lead you on a trek up a mountain to find the cold, snow topped peaks where this beautiful flowered vine grows. A hardy plant, this flower can be seen peaking out through snowy cliffs.\nCrouching Feather: This beautiful plant is named for its feather like white petals, and can most commonly be found among the tall grasses of dry regions. Though not as rare as some may believe, this herb requires patience to gather.\nHalfling's Grass: This plant is named for its common use by the Halfling race as a smoking herb. Typically it is found in dry climates throughout plains and forests alike. Search for this plant in tall grasses.\nEarth Symbols:\nSaffvil: This grassy plant is found in wet biomes predominantly, and can be found by searching through the short grasses that grow close to the soil.\nTippen's Root: An extremely common place, but also very useful plant, this root is widely used by both alchemist and healer. This plant is found in the grassy plains and forests of dry biomes.\nAlabaster Leaf: This small green leafy herb can be found snaking it's way through the branches and hiding amongst the leaves of a host tree. They attach themselves to all types of trees in dry regions.\nClutcher's Straw: A dry, brittle plant that can be found growing in hot regions. Search for this herb in low-lying shrubberies.\nMetzli's Tangle: A controversially named herb, this plant can be found snaking it's way up jungle trees in wet biomes.\nGoblin's Ivy: As it's name suggests, this herb can be found growing in hot, desert climates that Uruk's prefer. It is most commonly found on stone surfaces, but it can also be found growing up cactus plants.", ""),
        StaticBook("Gemstones", "Anwyn Izalith", "~*Gemstones*~\n\n     A copy written by:\n        Anwyn Izalith\n\n                ~*~\n\nThis book has been created in order to spread the cultural significance of gemstones.\n\n-Written in 1542\n-Ruby\nIt gives people increased wisdom. It is particulary useful for rash.\n\n-Emerald\nIncreases the fertility of a person, both during conception and bearing.\n-Diamond\nThe most powerful of all gems. It amplifies all other traits.\n\n-Amethyst\nCalms the nerves. Useful for study or meditation.\n\n-Sugilite\nThe healer's stone. It is used in healing arts, both magic and item.\n-Carnelian\nImproves analytical skills.\n\n-Garnet\nThe bloodstone. It is used to draw toxics from the body and aid in blood clot.\n\n-Onyx\nUsed to obtain enlightenment.\n-Hematite\nReduces stress.\n\n-Sapphire\nEnvokes peace and happiness.\n\n-Topaz\nIncreases both metal and physically stamina.\n-Tiger's eye\nDestroys anxiety. It promotes non-reckless courage.\n\n-Peridot\nUsed for growth.\n\n-Pearl\nUsed in chakra. Improves the connection with both mind and body.\n-Soladite\nAdsorbs negative thoughts from both the wearer and those around them. This negates the thoughts.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ghashn Baltbrodh", "Travista", "The Lak Mindset\n\n\n      Praise Laklul\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n*The book would be\nwritten in Blah*\n1. Laklul First\n\nYou must act to further Laklul's influence. When you fight, do so in Laklul's name, when you triumph over you foe, do so in Laklul's name; lead your life in Laklul's name. Currently, Laklul is in battle with his\ntyrannical master, Freygoth, spirit of nature, in the immortal plane. This means that you must hinder the influence of Freygoth and those who follow him be they spiritual or mortal. Druids, although they worship nature and not Freygoth directly, are a\nquintessential example of our mortal foes. Drive them from their homes, burn their lives yet spare the ones who dedicate themselves to spirits loyal to Laklul such as swamp and jungle druids. Additionally, destroy all shrines dedicated to Freygoth and\nreplace them with ones in Laklul's esteemed honour for such is the will of Laklul. Aside from Freygoth, his followers and other dishonourable spirits such as Tukki and Ixli, you should praise them for they too are great and worthy, Krug especially.\n2. Orkish Supremacy \n\nThe Ork is the only race which has worshiped the astute \nspirits; the ork is the only race which can defy all others mentally and physically. Although humility is acceptable while treading on ill, foreign lands do no\nsuch thing when they dare to take on the privilege of treading our lands. Especially the swamplands which Laklul deems his home; they must be free of foreign filth unless they pay grand spiritual reparations.\nIn addition, do not lay amongst foreigners or\nallow them to lay among you for their weakness shall diffuse into you. A strong people who can serve the spirits must have the slaves squabble far from equal. All clans which are weak in terms of the individual have issues with this just command.\n3. Strive Selfwards\n\nThe most useful servant is the most skilled one. As a servant to Laklul and most spirits we must view the world with such a lense. The two main aspects of a mortal, the mind and body, must be toiled and trialed consistently into\nimprovement. One cannot forsake one in the name of another; a Lak must be as formidable with wit as he is in the fields of war. Every Lak must be bulked to their limit, every Lak must be trained in craft and  weapon to their limit; every Lak must be\nat their peaked for this is the best way to serve Laklul. This is the will of Laklul.\n4. Lakkish Loyalty\n\nAll Laks are to be utterly devoted to the clan; to the Swampgoth. As the Swampgoth and the clan in extension act on behalf of the mighty and rightous Laklul any severing of dedication severs Laklul's positive view. It likewise\ndictates the spirits to view you with an unfavourable lense due to forsaking the oath you took before the spirits which was sealed in the blood of your initial trial. Furthermore, do not fear death if it's in the name of Laklul. Welcome it. The greatest\nof Krug's kin transcend to the ancestral plane to live gloriously as Krug does himself. If the enemy takes you captive hold sensitive details to death; if the enemy drive forth, unless the spirit of hope himself would proclaim it suicidal, fight.\nConclusive Notes:\n\n-Avoid degeneracy\n\n-Respect swampland\n\n-Respect ALL spirits bar the ones detailed previously\nPunishments:\n\nFirst Offense:\n\nThe offender is beaten\n\nSecond Offense:\n\nThe offender is beaten, a finger taken\n\nAnymore results in death", ""),
        StaticBook("Glyphs for Bgn's", "Joseph Alexander", "Table of Contents\n\n1: Unbreaking\n\n2: Efficiency\n\n3: Smite\n\n4: Material Information\nUnbreaking\n\n(x is where the material used is placed.)\n\nx-x\n-x-\nx-x\nEfficiency\n\nx--\n-xx\nx--\nSmite\n\n-xx-\nxxxx\nxxxx\n-xx-\nMaterials\n\n*Lapis: cheapest, fastest material. Weak\n\n*Emerald: Moderate speed, chance for higher power\n\n*Gold: Slow, unstable, offers high power.\n\n Continues on -->\nMaterials (Continued)\n\n*Diamond: Expensive, fast with moderate power", ""),
        StaticBook("God Dream Theory", "Daniel 'Vince' Ward", "\"In the beginning there was nothing, only the Void, and from this Void came a light, although in truth it wasn't a light, but that is the closest one may come to describing the existence of such a thing, and this light was the Creator, whom spoke the -\n- world into existence.\"\n\n   Look upon the world around you and how do you see it? Is it a thing of many colors, yet totally separate from you? \"There is a window, a chair, a cup\", I hear you say, but in truth they are all the same, including yourself.\nExistence is that of an illusion, a perception brought about by a paradoxical knowing between all beings. A dream of sorts, that is ever-changing and fluid, yet we remain unaware as a dreamer does his unconscious ventures. Except we are not the only -\n- dreamer, we are all a fragment of the single dreamer, that of a single being that encompasses the hemisphere of realms. It is an unknowing, unconscious god in an eternal sleep, resistant to the unending nothingness around it. In this way, one may call -\n- it the 'god dream'. Who this eternal slumbering god may be is up to you to decide. Is it the Creator? Willingly and consciously directing the flow of change? Or is it everything, and the Creator is but another fragment, an infinity within a being -\nbeyond infinity?\n\nBy the power of the conduits, some have come to realize the truth that we are all souls connected, creating a network of unending ethereal glow unseen by our physical eyes, but a truth nonetheless. What this truly means may have -\n- several answers, as does many things in this existence. It is because we are all just a single part of this dreaming entity, unknowing to the eternal truth. The truth being that there is none. What you make of this is up to your own perception, but -\nknow that because we are all one, each answer is correct, even if they are entirely contradicting, such is the nature of existing: contradicting and paradoxical.\n\n   The implications of such a revelation is vast, and staggering. Because of this, -\nperhaps one may come to understand what 'magic' truly means, and the nature of existence. When a person realigns their mind into the state of nothingness such as the Void, they pull their minds closer to the ultimate truth, much as a dreaming mind may -\n- start to realize it is dreaming, and may thus become lucid. This revelation does not always come to us in full, as many act instinctively, bending the realm without fully knowing what it means, acting by a spreadsheet of pre-conceived notions.\nRegardless, when a mind realigns itself to the ultimate truth, and connects with the Void, the true existence beyond the pre-mentioned 'light', one can realize that the world around them is apt to be changed from their will, much as a dreamer may -\n- alter their dreamscape by the force of their will. The difference here is that we are but fragments, and not the whole known as the sleeping god, and thus do not hold ultimate sway over the world. We are limited, and cannot change the fundamental -\n- illusions of law placed upon the world. Although in truth these fundamental existential laws of the universe too may be changed with enough force, force that cannot be contained within we mortals, nor even the gods. Yet, together, perhaps...\nAnd so we are often forced to follow such pre-established law, but they are loose and malleable, capable of stepping around in many instances. With this, a fragment with limited power, gaining lucidity towards the external existence such as the Void, one-\n- may come to realize their ability in altering the world around them much as a dreamer does when becoming lucid in their unconscious ventures. \nWe are all one sleeping entity, but perhaps one day we may realize that existence is but an illusion, a -\npeaceful dream we are all having together. What we do with such a dream is up to us. Will it be nightmarish and harsh, or calming and free-flowing like a river? With the revelation that is 'magic', we come to lucidity and enlightenment, and realize the -\n- power that holds. To contemplate such things is the way of a wizard, understanding that things are not so regimented as one may hope to believe. But regardless, one day we may all discover lucidity. What this means is yet to be explored, but perhaps in-\n- such a way we may reconvene as an awakened slumbering god.", ""),
        StaticBook("Goodbye", "Kalen Forseth", "Dear Charis\n\nI don't really know where to begin, or how to say this...\n\nI have to go. Somewhere else.\n\nI've missed you dearly... I don't know where you've gone. I tried searching\nbut I can search no more. I fear the worst... but I do not know. I believe you are safe, but why have you not come back to me?\n\nI still hold the earings I wanted to give to you... I was hoping to much to see the look of excitement\non your face when I gave them to you. Now, they sit in my hand as I write this... a solemn reminder of what once was.\n\nYou can stay in our home. Consider it yours now, I've taken a few things... not much from it.\nIf you want to keep my name you may. However... I have to find comfort elsewhere. It's a struggle to not have my tears stain these pages...\n\nI wish you happiness in all that you do. Live a good life.\n- Kalen", ""),
        StaticBook("Grandaxe", "Kami Frostbeard", "Grandaxes\nThe Grandaxes are an elder clan of fire-haired mountain dwarves who value honour and tradition above all else. Founded by Bogrin Grandaxe in the ancient dwarven mountains of northern Aegis,\nthe clan holds a fierce reputation for their skill with an axe. Renowned for their strong influence on dwarven politics for over a millennium,  many of the most revered Grand Kings of Urguan are descended from its lineage.\nDuring the fourteenth century, a dispute between its elders led the clan to become fractured into two distinct bloodlines; the Braveaxes,the purest of the first lineage and loyalists to the dwarven crown and the Blackaxes,\ntheir unruly kinsmen who struck out on their own as honour bound mercenaries. It was not until faced with the threat of an invasion from the orcish War Uzg that the clan reunited under a new leader, Bazian Grandaxe,\nsuccessfully forming a vanguard against the green scourge from its mountain holding of Kal’Anart. From that day forth,the clan became united around its defence of the dwarven realm,\nleading at the forefront of its armies and serving dutifully on its councils as marshals, statesmen and ambassadors.\n\n(OCC) Was taken from wiki just wanted it so I can read In game", ""),
        StaticBook("Gravity&MoreVol1", "Steven", "Gravity is the force that everything towards the ground. But with Gravity present, how is it that birds may fly? That stars remain in the sky? These are some of the questions that I don't have a confirmed answer to yet, but simply theories that I will go\nover in this book, along with facts I have found about the force we know as Gravity. \nOne thing that I have noticed is that most objects, when dropped from the same height at the same time, will land at the same time. If this is so, how come other\nobjects, such as feathers, fall at different speeds?\nThis is the work of Air Resistance, the counterpart of Gravity. I, myself, jumped off of a roof to showcase this opposing force. The 'wind' you feel when falling from a high place, or even running\ndown a street at high speeds isn't wind at all, but Air Resistance. I believe this to be what causes certain objects, such as feathers and paper, to fall slower. This is because these objects are less dense and aren't as capable of breaking through\nAir Resistance, while denser objects, such as stones and bricks, can fall quite easily. \n\nIt is also worth mentioning that Gravity doesn't necessarily only work as a downward force. Telekinetics, for example, use Alteration to change\nthe Gravity acting on an object, along with its velocity. \nI believe that the reason that certain gasses such as steam can float is also related to density, in that the gasses are less dense than the air itself. \nI tested this theory out by dropping\ndifferent-density objects into water, and seeing if they floated or sank. As was expected, less dense objects such as feathers and flowers floated, while denser ones such as bricks and stones sunk.\nI'm still unsure of ways to measure not only density, but\nAir Resistance, or even Gravity, though I can make rough estimates based on their speeds of descent and ability to float or sink. \nAnother perplexing property of Gravity is the Stars. I've spent a time studying these celestial objects, and with a mediocre\nmagnifying lens I was able to hypothesize that they lie outside of our realm. If that's so, why doesn't Gravity pull them closer? Perhaps the stars are kept in place by the Gravity of their own realm. This is a question that will most likely not have a\nclear answer for a long time, however it's interesting to ponder on. \n\nFurther studies on the forces governing our world will be conducted at a later date.\nStudy done by Steven Vega.\nCredits to:\nGlacio for catching me when I jumped from the roof.", ""),
        StaticBook("Great Sandworm", "Eath'Lur", "The Great Sandworm\n-----------------\nThere are many, many sand worms, thousands upon thousands. Used on shamanistical brews or for baits for kowakelps, they are generally harmless.\n\nBut we're talking of something completely different.\nThe great sandworm is one of a kind, created by a shamanistical accident of the current High Shaman, Malog'Yar some time after our arrival at the fresh lands of Vailor, still, this is just a theory that can't be confirmed.\nIt's overly huge, I couldn't measure it's size, but it must be around the size of ten ologs, same for it's strenght and weight.\n\nInside of the beast, large tooths swirl. Once it devours something by errupting beneath the sand with a great leap,\nthe great sandworm will excrete a paralysing liquid upon it and wound, deteriorate and heavily damage it.\n\nThe last time it was seen was sort of a long time ago, still, I believe it remains beneath the sands of the Vailor orcish desert.\nI found this beast when me and a few other orcs went on a hunt to offer it as food to Thurak'Yar as a sign of gratitude.\n\nWe managed to find a jabbernack, we killed it, but before we could once again reach it, something erupted from the sands.\nThe sandworm devoured our hunt, so we decided to fight it.\n\nWe barely managed to damage it, being able to tear off a chunk of it's skin. The strike was dealt by Glumpuz'Gorkil.\nIt's skin appears to be as resilient as a more reinforced iron, alike to steel. We never really tested it, and the beast is unconfirmed to still be alive up to these days since it was last seen.\nWith this, I conclude the last sight of the great sandworm. Mortal, dangerous, strong, it's a foe to be met with a large group, and It's even better if you don't ever, ever search for it. I fear it might be resting beneath the sands.", ""),
        StaticBook("Greater Dragons", "Orithur", "There are two species of dragonkin known to have descended from Dragur, though corrupted and defiled forms of both have formerly existed. Dragur, the Daemon of Knowledge, oftentimes appears as a dragon in his true form.\ndescendants, the Dragaar, are sentient “greater dragons” that seek to collect and share knowledge with mortals. They can conceal themselves under the guise of mortals and other creatures, though it is a rare occasion to meet a Dragaar in person.\nDragons, on the other hand, are more common -- though proportionately rare compared to other living beings -- than greater dragons. They are generally referred to as dragons, though the proper classification is “lesser dragon.”\nThey are non sentient, and are commonly viewed as natural predators that roam our world. However, like all living beings, they are susceptible to taint and corruption. Though a rare occasion, dragons have been known to alter their very nature into a\ntwisted, foul portrayal consumed by hatred and rage for the mortal races. The outcome of this calamity is referred to as a drake. Little is known about the purpose of the dragonkin as a whole, except that each species’ intentions vary greatly.\nDRAGAAR:\n\nBestowed with the gift of immortality and sentience, the Dragaar exist chiefly to seek and gather knowledge. Each Dragaar is assigned the role of gathering a distinct aspect of knowledge, and this role defines their characteristics and demeanor.\nHowever, they do not solely desire knowledge and wisdom. The greater dragons, or Dragaar, act as ever watching wardens over the mortal races. However, most prefer to live in seclusion in deep thought and meditation, only to emerge when the races call upon\nthem in their time of need. The Dragaar can and often disguise themselves under the guise of a mortal or creature. This is their preferred method of watching over mortals, as it is both subtle and inconspicuous.  There are only two Dragaar that have\nrevealed themselves to the mortal races, and the decision to do so was not lightly concluded. Though the Dragaar seek to protect the mortal races, they often decide to do so by living in secrecy. Many discern that if they chose to associate\nthemselves with mortal affairs too frequently, dire consequences would arise. The primary concern held by Dragaar, in regards to involvement in the matters of mortals, is that the races would eventually resort to relying on them to protect\nmortals from any form of evil. However, the greater dragons aspire to teach the mortal races self-preservation, without them depending on the intervention of immortals.\nDRAKAAR:\n\nTo attempt to bend the will of a Dragaar would be all for naught, and thus what can cause a Dragaar to devolve itself into a twisted abomination of hatred is unknown. The ancient ancestors of the four mortal races once believed that the choice\nfor this horrendous transformation to occur was their own, though the aftermath of such a decision would contradict their entrusted duty. The greater dragon itself is a terrifying opponent, but a Dragaar that feels no remorse and fears no consequence?\nThat is the true fear of corruption; the perversion of the mind and distortion of one’s morality is a dreadful circumstance. In past times, the outcome of such an event would often be referred to as a “greater drake.” It is the combination of a Dragaar's\nsentience and a drake’s all-consuming hatred. The likelihood of a Dragaar devolving into a being tormented by rage is next to none, as most would, in their right mind, turn themselves into stone to prevent the conversion. The only verifiable event\nrecorded of a Dragaar turning to stone is that of Velketzar. The Guardian Velketzar, consumed by self-condemnation and despair due to his failure, dramatically transformed into a stone statue to prevent the inevitable calamity.\nIf a Dragaar were to fall under the hand of corruption, it would hold the power to politically corrupt nations with ease. Additionally, detecting and pursuing such a foe would be toilsome, all due to its shape shifting ability.\nFortunately, deep meditation and concentration can inhibit this rare occurrence, along with immersing themselves in their aspect of knowledge.", ""),
