        StaticBook("Little bunnies", "Vhesina Thaorihle", "A hungry bunny once found the vegetable garden of a rich man who never, never, never ate vegetables. So the hungry bunny ate a cabbage, two heads of lettuce and three carrots. Then he went hop-hippity-hop right home.\nThe next evening the hungry bunny brought another bunny and then there were two hungry bunnies. They each ate a cabbage, two heads of lettuce and three carrots. Then they went hop-hippity-hop right home.\nThe next evening the two hungry bunnies brought another hungry bunny and then there were three hungry bunnies. Each ate a cabbage, two heads of lettuce and three carrots. Then they went hop-hippity-hop right home.\nThe next evening the three bunnies brought another bunny and then there were four hungry bunnies. They all ate a cabbage, two heads of lettuce and three carrots. Then they went hop-hippity-hop right home.\nThe next evening the four bunnies brought another bunny and then there were five hungry bunnies. They all ate a cabbage, two heads of lettuce and three carrots. Then they went hop-hippity-hop right home.\nThe next evening the five bunnies brought another bunny and then there were six hungry bunnies. They all ate a cabbage, two heads of lettuce and three carrots. Then they went hop-hippity-hop right home.\nThe next evening the rich man who never, never, never ate vegetables wondered what happened to his vegetables garden. He never guessed the six hungry bunnies hiding behind the fence ate his cabbages, his lettuce and carrots. But we know better do we not!", ""),
        StaticBook("Locations", "Archimedes D. Doomforged", "Snow Elven Fortress:\nX: 3538.189\nY: 67.85000\nZ: 590.234\n\nFallen Dome:\nX: 2585.183\nY: 75\nZ: 160.876\n\nRuined Bridge/Stone Troll:\nX: -1521.700\nY: 62\nZ: -1199.300\n(This was taken down by the river, it's only a rough location!)", ""),
        StaticBook("Log", "Kenneth Rackstraw", "Piero Gradic\nLoan due of 245\nNext Saint's Week\nTaken out 18th of Snow's Maiden, 1568\nDue 18th of Snow's Maiden, 1569\nPay via the Felsen Bank\n\n((Pay to NordLord1))\nLady Grey\n\nWayrest 2\nIlvermorny\n1568/1569 deposit: [X]\nEvictions To Be Unlocked:\nNewport Blvd 11\nSteel Lane 4\nWayrest 10\nDON'T EVICT NEWPORT 11\n[!] All have been crossed out but Wayrest 10.\n12th of Malin's Welcome, '69\nEvictions:\nSilisbury Street\nByzabro\nde Beaufort\n[!] The first eviction has been crossed out.\nREMEMBER: Mayor's property\nRoss Road 6\nLoaned:\n100 minas\nTo The Captain\n((Captain_Burnzie))\n200 minas owed back\n18th of The Grand Harvest, 1570\n\n\n\n\n\nLOAN PAID\n[X]\nDeep Cold 17, '69\nEvictions:\nBath Street 4\nBath Street 8\nCopper Court 5\nWayrest 8\nBath Street 2\n\n\nEVICTIONS NECESSARY ARE COMPLETE\nNeed to make plans for the ship with the Mayor\n\n-The Poverty Sucks Vessel\n-Large and perfect for merchant work, but mostly for living\n-Has more ammenities than an average home\nTO DO:\n-Get Milly Turner a bodyguard\nI've changed everything. I've gotten new skin fully, and all. I'm going underground, calling myself Kenneth Rackstraw. I hope Milly likes me this way... Hopefully all will be well now and I can live again as new. Soon I shall have my ship, the Poverty\nSucks.\nI may be etching my confession into this log, but it may never be enough. Somehow, despite my desire to live a life of peace, I want my story to be known. That girl, Everett, I haven't seen her in over two years, but she wanted to be a writer or a\nbookshop owner or something. She could tell my story! The world will remember the tale of Dan Cooper, even if my name is not paired with it.\nThe end is here. I sign this book now for me to view later, or for whomever reads. Farewell, reader.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lord of Undru", "Zelak", "The Book of Undru\n\n\nLord of Undru\nThe world of Athera and it's secret villain.\n\nA journey of a fabulous Dark Elf.\nFirst there were the ruins on the mountains. A huge complex of once was supposed to be a hold to worship a specific being. A small, thin and broken stone bridge lead to the entrance of the ruins. Large stone pillars raising proudly up to the sky.\nThe architecture was different and unknown, the pillars and multiple different constructed walls wrapped around each other like snakes. Paths lead to small prisons and cages, to an open area with a large white and fully functional Organ...\n...to a deep black hole in the center of the ruins and the mountain it proudly sat upon. The ruins were oddly quiet. No birds, no animals not even the wind blew around my face. But I always felt like that something was there, something watching my steps.\nI had no ideas left of how the people were able to build this large complex on top of a mountain, it doesn't even have a path leading up to it. I had to climb on my own. Only one extremely damaged road below the mountains where already nature took it back\nbut even the road lead to no destiny, maybe there was a path but it's already gone. While I was inside the ruins I remembered the damaged road and that vines and plants already grew around it. But up here, no plants. Nothing. Like nature fears to grow.\nI continued my exploration deeper into the ruins until I reached the many cages attached to long stone pillars growing out of the walls. No skeletons, nothing. Did they even had prisoners? I wondered to myself. Not even signs of people inside those cages.\nThe path lead me down into the open area where the white organ was standing in front of the deep dark hole inside the mountain. I slowly approached it and tried to press a key. To my surprise it was completely functional as the tune boomed throughout...\n...the whole complex. I fear'd that the sound might cause the old ruins to shake and break but no huge damage that day. What I also noticed was that the tune of the organ was perfect, it was like the ruins were constructed just to the organ the tune of..\n...heavens, beautiful. I moved on towards a bridge leading across the deep black hole in the mountain and what I can call the heart of the ruins. It was uncomfortable standing in the middle of this dark area. It was like a void. Nothing.\nCompletely nothing, hearing nothing was too uncomfortable for me so I shouted into the abyss, but not even echo returned to me. It's like a black hole put into a mountain and a complex built around it. I sat myself down and waited.\nAfter what felt like hours of waiting that something might happen around this place. I started to hear quiet whispers, it was like somebody seductively whispered into my ears. But the language of the words was unknown to me so I started to greet the black\nabyss in front of me. To my surprise an echo came back but it wasn't my voice. It was metallic, deep and rusty. I asked what it was and I waited another hours for a response until it finally came. \"Undru.\"\nAfter the ruins there was the shadow. \n\nDays passed after that one metallic response I got from the ruins on the mountains. \"Undru\"\n\nWas it a name? A word? I knew I will find out soon enough.\nMultiple people I met started to talk about their own experiences in the ruins. The organ was playing on it's own and if the people approached it, it stopped.\nSome heard \"Undru\" multiple times.\nOthers heard the unknown whispers and even questions of their families and race.\n\nAt this point I don't know anymore if we are the explorers or something else is exploring us.\nI kept away from the ruins and listened to the stories of the people who explored it on their own. The wood elves had started to build their new town underneath the mountain where the ruins of \"Undru\" sat upon.\nI asked the wood elven citizens if they entcountered something unsual while living here in the new town. They had seen a shadow sometimes sitting in a tavern on a chair, in their own home or standing in their own bedroom while they were asleep.\nThe shadow was humanoid, as they told me. It just seem'd to watch the people from secure places from time to time doing their daily work and living their lifes. I called it the Watcher as it fit the role perfectly in my opinion.\nBut I did not know yet that the shadow was connected to the old ruins yet because it was spotted in multiple places around Athera, just watching the people and what they do. Watching them fight, conflicts, talking. Maybe it learned?\nI myself have spotted the Watcher once, I was in a situation where a woman tried to seduce me and I noticed that the Watcher was staring at us from afar on top of a house. Never removing itself, not moving at all. Learning.\nAfter the Watcher, there was Undru.\n\nCouple of weeks and months passed and I returned back to the ruins of \"Undru\" after the Watcher disappeard and never was seen again. It was peculiar for me to see people in the ruins.\nThey were just exploring as everybody else would. Wood elves from the new town below the mountain. They told me that the organ kept playing at night for hours and that they can't sleep during it because the tune was horrible and out of this world.\nAs we approached the organ to look for evidence of somebody who might play it at night, it suddenly started to play. We all were in shock since nobody was sitting on the chair, only the keys were moving.\nThe ruins shaked from the huge booming instrument and an invisible force grabbed the coat of one of the elves to rip it off from his body before it flew off into the dark black hole in the mountain.\nThe wood elves started to run out of the ruins and I followed them as I noticed that a flickering figure slide out of the black hole in the mountain, the lost robe from one of the elves wrapped around it's figure to cover the body.\nThe organ stopped and we did too as we just watched the figure below. \n\nIt's green coat covered the whole body, like rubber the cloth stretched out to act like a robe for the figure with it's metall mask and gauntlet hands.\nIt took a deep metallic breath as it looked around. It's frame appeard to shift and flicker like it's not in this world yet, not physically atleast.\n\nThe wind returned to the ruins at last yet we had to move as the ruin...\n...was still shaking from the vibration of the now quiet organ.\n\nFrom that day on, the figure which people would soon call: \"Lord of Undru\" appeard in our world.\nAfter Undru came there was the Lord itself.\n\nThe Lord of Undru, a figure I can only describe as curious and not from this world. It started to appear around Athera. Some people thought it was another being just like a wraith\nand they attacked it. But even if it got stabbed, it just vanished for a bit before reappearing once more at the same spot. Yet it did no harm to anybody. My theory that it is not fully in this world yet was true.\nUndru was between his own world and ours, not able to enter our world physically at the moment. The mask was his source and reason why he was able to shift slightly between the worlds on his own.\nThe Mask of Undru had two holes for eyes yet the animal or being it represented was nothing I or anybody else has seen before. It was slightly uncomfortable to look at it for too long.\nUndru was a being that had to rely on objects in our world, he was not able to cast over complicated magical abilities. Just like the coat he stole from an elf to create his robes. But the mask was obviously from his own world.\nNot rely on magical abilities I thought. I was wrong. Somehow he was able to copy the magic of others who used it against him even the healing powers of the Clerics. From time to time he would appear to injured people or those who are about to die.\nOffering them to heal their wounds if they sacrifice something for him. Undru was collecting different objects with it, from a simple finger of somebody who had a lover in danger or even the eye of an injured person.\nWas Undru constructing a body by collecting parts of a body from different people? Imagining that causes me to shiver, a being made out of everybody. Elf, Orc, Dwarf and Human in one person.\nBut I was true with my theory, he truly was constructing a body for himself to stay in our world for a longer period of time since he was always able to stay in our world for only a short period of time until he had to return to his own once more.\nAtleast he still was wearing his robe, only a few people ever saw the abomination he created. He even stole the voice of somebody for himself to have a less metallic one.\nAfter the Lord there were the Doors.\n\nPeople reported of getting transported into a dark world after trying to grab Undru's coat while he was about to vanish into his realm.\nIt was like a void but with many, many doors of all kind floating around the dark realm. A path made out of stone lead towards no destiny at all. White glowing eyes were watching the intruders from deep within the darkness.\nUnknown, extremely large figures moved over the heads and the longer the intruders stayed in this worlds, the closer and more the eyes get.\n\nOpening a door would open the door to a random world, unknown and strange for our likeness.\nUndru was nowhere to be found and people had to be smart to find the correct door into Athera. Even if the exit lead to the top of a mountain or a random cave, or even the house of normal citizens.\nAfter the Doors, there was the Terror.\n\nUndru kept coming back and started to recruit people to help him collect different kinds of materials. Even the necromancers and co. had joined him after they tried to corrupt him...\n...like they would do to a Ghost, but it didn't work. Undru's followers were never harmful, they only collected materials. Yet some took their task to far and did it by force, even if the lord told them not to.\nThe people of Athera got angry about the constant attacks by the followers and started to assault them and the Lord itself. They knew he can't be killed, yet they kept trying it over and over again until Undru murdered his first victim.\nAfter the Terror there was the Black Hole.\n\nUndru collected stone and materials and built a large stone circle above the ruins which floated in mid air...[A large pool of ink caused the next words to be unseen.]\nUndru has opened the black hole, it is sucking up the entire ruins...\n[!] [More black Ink covers some words]\n\nUtopia, he is calling it Utopia, is it real? I don't know but he keeps saying it will change our lifes.\n[!] [Ink, Ink everywhere!]\nThe Dark Elves and wood elves joined together to fight the being which calls itself Lord of Undru. [!] [Something scratched around this paper while black ink is all over the next words.]\n\nI understand why it calls itself Undru now.\n[!] [A large pool of Ink covers some words once again]\n\nUndru has physically entered our world and he is gigantic. It's like watching Iblees..--return.-..It exploded...the mask--...shatters...Undru...defeated?,..--...No...--..", ""),
        StaticBook("Loren's Book I", "Loren", "Loren's Travel Book\n\nDay 1\n\nI will be traveling to the silver city, far in the west. The boats are missing, so I shall go on foot.\nDay 2\n\nMy plans have gotten somewhat sidetracked. I am going back to the legion. I've explored much of the world already, and perhaps the Legion is my way to becoming a diplomat. I will pursue this course until it is no longer wise to do so.\nDay 3\n\nI have a dog now! I found him in the plains. He is coming with me to Johannesburg.\n\nI am starting to wonder about the Legion. Why do they do what they do? What connection to they have to the church?\nI've never really thought about this. What do they do when there are no wars to fight? I must find out.\nDay 4\n\nStill no word from the Legion. I will resume my journey, and speak to them when I return.\n\nSlight change of plans. I'm going to the Northern Mountains. There are soldiers in the woods, I'm unsure why. They wouldn't speak to me.\nNote to self: Head back east to find signposts.\n\nThere are a great many dead ends out here.... someone should mark them.\n\nI'm rather lost. I've come across a decrepid looking castle, or city. Will take a closer look.\nIt was an abandoned temple. No need to linger here. \n\nJust heard thunder. Must hurry.\nDay 5 \n\nI returns home using my soul stone, only to see a company of men with sheilds at the enterance to the city. What is going on?\n\nThey may be from teh Archduchy of Lorraine.\nI made the journey to the Westerlands. Will go inside the city in the morning.\n\nI am still waiting. It is raining heavily. I will survive this. \n\nI know very little of the Westerlands. Perhaps I will know more by th end of this.\nTurns out: I wasn't able to get into the city. No one was manning the gate, so I returned home.\nDay 6\n\nI found SIr Robert Smyth and resumed my challenge, but midway through he left without a word. Did I do something wrong? He appeared to be carrying a bird when he left...\nDay 7 \n\nStill in town. Will resume my trip to the Silver City now.\nDay 8 \n\nI have arrived at the city. I am worried they will turn me away the moment they see me, though. It is elven territory. \n\nNo one appears to be at the front gate. I do not enjoy the thought of having to yell in order to gain entry.\nI think there is no one here... perhaps I should leave. \n\n...Someone is within the gate....\n\nI'm unsure if he sees me. Are elves and humans on good speaking terms? But I see elves in Oren all the time, perhaps they are fine with us.\nHe certainly saw me... and he isn't alone. I'm trying to look busy, but I'm afraid of their reaction to my presence. Should I say something? If I spark a war between the elves and Oren, the Legion would kick me out at once. \nLet me describe the city, in\norder to continue the illusion that I'm meant to be here. The city is made of marble and deep blue stones. It is high on a mountain, and one must follow a steep and winding path to reach the city gates. \nI need to stop putting this off. I'll go speak to\nthem now. But what should I say? There is no reason for me to be here...\n\nI hear the bubbling of lava. Surely there isn't a trap at the gate?!\n\nEnough of this. I will tell the truth, or as close as is safe.\nNone of them can hear me... I will have to shout. God be with me.\nDay 9\n\nBAck in Oren now. The elves were very helpful.\nDay 10\n\nNot much to report. Unsure what to do now... may head south...\n\nThere's a \"pork man\" in town. He has the face of a pig! Truly. the world is full of magic.\nDay 11\n\nI will now head to Sutica. I have no idea what awaits me there.\n\nIt was mostly abandoned, except for a dark elf that lived there with his wife. I now head to Orc territory. I must remain vigilante.\nDay 12\n\nI return (once again) to Oren. The Orc \"city\" was abandoned, like most of the places I've seen. Where is everyone?\nDay 13 \n\nI was asked to join an adventuring party today. Of course, I accepted. Whether they pay me or not is no trouble, they are kind and willing to provide food and bord during the trip. I know not where we will travel to, but rest assured I can handle\nit. \n\nThey even gave me a uniform! I look far more formidible in this armor as well. I must find out more about this house, and the plan for our travels. \nThis appears to the abode of House Vanir.\nDay 14\n\nI was introduced to Lord Brynden Vanir. I still have no clue how to adress someone of his stature. M'lord sounds flippant... Although I can't simply call him 'Sir' anymore. I suppose M'lord will have to do. on other subjects, I am allowed to\nwander until they call upon me. What a kind gathering... the Legion would never allow it's people to stray. \n\nI must admit, I lied about why the Legion wouldn't have me to one of the men. I said it was because I wanted to travel that they didn't like me.\nThis is a more flattering version to the truth: They were upset because I only wanted to join in order to become A diplomat. I now realize that I haven't spoken much about my dream. Perhaps I wasn't born to be an envoy, but an explorer. What a grand idea.\nDay 15\n\nI participated in a great deal of undead killing last night. I still don't know why Lord Vanir doesn't simply close the gates at night. I suppose it isn't my place to question it. I also met the child of one of the Vanir women today. He is only 8,\nwhich seems young to be traveling alone.\nDay 16\n\nI returned to Johannesburg to see if James was still there. I did not find him, but I ran into the child I met yesterday. What a small world! \nI've realized that this journal is getting quite long. I will purchase a second one to house more of\nmy writing in. A whole 31 pages! I'm rather proud. \n\n~Loren Allson", ""),
        StaticBook("Loren's Book II", "Loren", "Day 17\n\nAh! I'd forgotten how nice new journals are. This will be the second installment of my travels. Hopefully it will amuse someone. In the meantime, it gives me something to do. I noticed that I failed to properly introduce myself in my first one.\nMy name is Loren Allson. I am a human who hails from Oren. I was 19 when I first started out, but now I am 20. A full year of traveling the islands! It's been rather intense. I hope to be able to continue traveling and writing for some time now.\nDay 18\n\nNot much to report! Hopefully my journal won't be full of pointless pages like these.\nDay 19\n\nI decided to venture to dwarven lands. Will write more when I get there. \n---\nI'm standing beside the statue of the Grand King Torvin Grandaxe. I've never seen a statue so large! Truly, the mind that buily it must have been\ngrand.\n---\nI am now inside a great carvern with some sort of glowing fungi near the ceiling. A rock path curves along the outer edge of the cavern, and a deep pool fills the rest.\nI am now at a wide bridge that crosses a yawning chasm filled with lava. The entire dwarven city in contained within a enourmous cave. \n\nCracked blue stones mark the path. Looking at the city, the main word that comes to mind is \"mighty.\" This is a\ncity no man could build, not in a century.\n\nAlthough the city is mighty, it is also silent. I have seen only a handful of its people. It seems many cities are empty, these days.\nThe second thing that surprises me is their apathy to my presence. No\none seems to care that I'm here. In Oren, if a Dwarf so much as looked at the city, they would be flogged! Perhaps our ways are the primitive ones...\nDay 20\n\nI have decided to scope out the lands of the Wood Elves. I know little of them, perhaps this journey will be enlightening. I may visit Ker'nor as well, if I don't get lost. \n--\nThe wood elves live in wooden houses under looming trees.\nTruly, I've nver seen trees that are so large. Perhaps it is the magic of this place that causes them to grow like this.\nInterestingly, this city, or town, seems to be inhabited. A rare but welcome sight, these days. I'm not sure if they will be\nbothered by my being here... Rather, there are more in this city than I anticipated.\n--\nKer'nor, on the other hand, is completely abandoned. Such a shame, too.\nDay 25\n\nI must apologise for my lack of writing. A good deal has happened. Oren layed siege to the snow elves in a great battle. I fought alongside House Vanir. I have never been so frightened and enthralled at the same time. There was death\nthat day. Truly, it will go down in history. \n\nWe gathered in Johannesburg before going to the fortress. I've never seen so many people in one place before. I wish I could say that I helped, more. I just followed the swarm of fighters and did what I\ncould. It was impossible to keep track of my friends or my foes. I believe I was struck with an arrow at some point. I now have a battle scar. \nAfter the fight, I returned to the keep. Sir Eric promoted me to footman! What a thought! Many of the men\nreturned wounded. I cannot imagine what might have happened if we hadn't been able to breach the snow elves' fortress.\nDay 26\n\nI defeated someone in combat today! I have never been able to before, perhaps I have finally learned something. \n\nThe water around the Keep has frozen. Eric told me that one of Vanir's friends\nwas showing off water magic and froze the water. What a feat! Truly, to be able to do such acts of magic is something I envy. \n\nI have been thinking about the future, lately. It seems I could go far staying with Vanir, and I don't plan on leaving. It\nseems that effort is something I can do with moderate efficiancy, but what of my own life? Someone asked me if I had a woman to love. It has made me think, that is all.\nDay 27\n\nI had my first drink today. Imagine that, a 20 year old who has never had ale before. \nI must admit, I have no stomach for it. Eric was the one who convinced me to try, he was also the one who witnessed my vomit in the alley behind\nthe tavern afterwards. I wanted the earth to swallow me up whole.\nDay 28\n\nPerhaps some good came of all that drinking nonsense. I met a wonderful girl. She lives in a keep not far from Haense, just past dwarven lands. I know I sound ridiculous, but what a day! I escorted her home from\nJohannesburg. There is a keep, or a fortress, out in the desert. The people there are kind, although we didn't speak much.\n\nAfterwards, she wanted to see where I reside. I wasn't sure Lord Vanir would be\npleased, me bringing a person I barely knew to the keep, but he was very kind. He even invited her inside, which just proves how good natured the House of Vanir is. \n\nWhat a day indeed! Her name is Elise, and she wants to travel. Very admirable.\nDespite being blind, she fullfills her dreams! I wish I was as steadfast as her.\nDay 29\n\nI attended the wedding of the sister of Lord Vanir. It was quite lovely. I've never been to a wedding before, but me and the other men simply stood by the door and watched. Once it was done, they all carried the bride and groom\naway to a room upstairs. It all seemed very improper, but it seems I was wrong about many things. I don't think I will marry in such a way; the bride seemed rather uncomforable when they carried her away. The people here are good natured, though.\nDay 30\n\nSomeone has stolen from the Vanir Barracks. I heard one of the men telling Lord Vanir. Apperantly, the thief left behind notes gloating about stealing from us. \nI only write this because Lord Vanir himself summoned me\nto ask about the incident. He suspects one of us, I think. I cannot blame him for worrying, especially with the new recruits. I would be wary of us as well. \nI suppose you may be wondering if I did it; no, I did not. I don't even know how a lock works,\nmuch less how to pick it! If anything, this is a lesson of trust. Lord Vanir trusts his men. I feel very responcible all of a sudden. \n\nToday a man asked me how much Vanir pays. He was a Legion man, and I couldn't help but feel angry and ashamed when I\nhad to say, \"Nothing. Just room and food.\"\nHe had such a look of pity for me, but what does he know? The oppertunity to serve someone like the Vanirs is payment in itself. I have wondered about payment before, though. Perhaps I should ask Lord Vanir,\nbut I just can't bring myself to. They have been too kind, and I should be grateful. I suppose Sir Robert warned me of this, back when I wanted to join the Legion...\nDay 32\n\nI don't believe I've ever told you about the ships docked outside the keep. There are three of them, each with different banners that I can only assume signify various houses and kingdoms. I asked Lord Vanir about\nthem recently, and his answer was rather surprising. Evidently, the House of Vanir is head of the Imperial Navy. What a thought!\n\nIn other news, I am getting used to being refered to as a \"Vanir Bannerman\" \"Levyman\" \"Vanir lad\" etc. I suppose my own name\ndoesn't come with a uniform, but being a \"Vanir _____\" is rather bittersweet. On one hand, it is amazing to be a part of something so grand and important. On the other, I wonder how much of myself has been lost in the shuffle. It's enough to make me\nquestion whether I should pursue my dream of being a diplomat again. I just can't quite bring myself to picture it again, like I used to as a child. To negotiate alliances between warring factions, to be summoned to deal with clashing empires and be able\nto save lives. It is a dream, and only ever will be. I must let it go.\nDay 33\n\nI wandered north from Johannesburg today. I came across a small camp with white tents at the base of the mountains. There was no way to identify who was staying there, but there were roughly half a dozen beds in the three tents.\nI know not what it is for, but I may inform someone of it.\nAfter stopping at the camp, I decided to climb the mountains. There is a large fortress being built on one of the peaks, which was grand to see. I came across a valley, tucked away in the range. I\nnoticed many lights below, and so desended to see what it was. It was a terrible sight. Heads on large spears shoved into the ground, and a campfire burning at the center. It was plain that a group had been sitting there at some point, and I was\ngreatly worried about running into them. Banners that I didn't recognize stood along a path that led to a cave. I went inside, through a passage filled with blood and decapitated heads, and emerged on the other side to see some sort of large cavern in the\nprocess of being dug out in the side of the mountain. The sign above it read 'meeting place' or that's what I assumed. It had the illiterate sounds or Orc speak, which did not calm my fears. It looks as though a clan may be settling in the mountains,\nwhich is rather concerning. Perhaps I should inform Lord Vanir, but I'd hate to bother him over nothing. \n\nAs it turns out, Oren is aware of the Orcs. I was talking to a man in the tavern, and he says a raid is being planned.\nI've confirmed this with several other people, so it seems reliable enough. The man left in a hurry after the other patrons confirmed. I know not why, but he acted suspicious for some reason. Perhaps it is nothing, although I do wonder about the white\ntents at the foot of the mountain. Do they have something to do with this?\nDay 34\n\nI believe this shall mark the end of this second journal. I still have much to write, and I shall do so. \n\n~Loren Allson", ""),
        StaticBook("Loren's Book III", "Loren", "Day 35\n\nAh, a new journal and a new day. What a feeling! This year has passed rather fast, I must say. I am 21 years old now. What a thought!\nDay 36\n\nThis journal must start with terrible news, I'm afraid. Elliot, a dear friend of mine, was captured by Orcs. He could barely tell me of the horrors he endured there. He said it was an Orc camp in the desert, west of the Vanir keep.\nI think I will go see for myself where these monsters live.\nDay 37\n\nBad news and good news. The good news is that I am still alive. The bad news is that I am terribly confused. \nI found the Orc settlement that Elliot was talking about, or I think I did. I snuck around inside the city for a bit, before going\nback to the front gate so they wouldn't suspect my. Orc language is difficult to understand and I had an interesting conversation with three Orcs at the gate. They had blue skin; I thought Orcs were typically green. How odd. They told me no halfling\n(Elliot, of course) had been there. I fear I may have been too passive in my approach, and thus they may have lied. \nThey also demanded tribute before I left. I was worried they would skin me if I didn't give them something, so I tossed the leader of\ntheir group a gold nugget that I found in a cave and went on my way. I plan on returning for round two soon, this time armed with many gold pieces to bargain for information. Perhaps they know of another Orc group that would have done this. I must speak\nwith Elliot further. \n\nIn other news, Elise and I are friends once more. I know I haven't spoken much about the events surrounding us, and that is not without purpose. The most sI'll say is that we are friends, and nothing more. I need nothing\nmore than that. She is a good person, but my duties lie in other places.\n\nOn the subject of duties and places, I met a rather rude man named Lincoln at the Crown and Cup. He asked me if I liked other men in a horribly loud voice.\nI don't, of course, but try telling that to a crowd of insane townsfolk who are ready to hang you on sight. \n\nThe Decency Act was passed in Johannesburg. It says that anyone who isn't human is basically a monster, which makes\nit very hard for any other races in the city. In fact, protesters have been lining the gate for the better part of today. Amazing. I'm not sure how I feel about the Act, but I suppose it's law now. \n\nThere isn't much else to write, for today at\nleast. There has been no great quest of late. My only objective currently is to track down the Orcs that tormented Elliot, though I'm not sure what to do when I find them. Perhaps I need a plan, first. \n\nI write this while standing outside the\ngate to the keep. Night is falling fast, and I don't want to yell for someone to come open it. I suppose I'm rather passive like that.\nDay 38\n\nMy name, as you may know by now, is Loren Allson. My mother and father raised me in Johannesburg. This is where I spent most of my youth. I have no memory of a world before then, and from what my mother has told me, it is best that\nI don't. When I was young, maybe fourteen years of age, my father died of a fever. It struck fast and without warning. My mother barely had time to grieve before taxes and payments came crashing down on us. My father had earned a living working for the\narmy. Now that he was gone, the money was too. I don't remember much from this time. It was tough; we moved from house to house, hardly settling in before tax collectors took the very beds we slept on. On a trip to Sutica (mainly to escape the previously\nmentioned tax collectors) my mother fell in love with a merchant. He was her age; they both had lost someone. Perhaps it was the perfect match for the time. He offered to let mother live with him, and they married shortly afterwards. I still visit,\nsometimes. I, however, took an instant dislike to the merchant (I should mention that his name is Georgen. What a name, hmm?) that my mother loved, and elected to stay behind in Johannesburg. My mother, angry to see me give up my studies and terrified of\ngoing back to the city, let me go without so much as an argument. She gave me 10 minas and sent me on my way. At this time, I was eighteen. I made the long journey back to the city, but found my home was like a stranger to me. So much had changed in four\nyears, I hardly knew what to do with myself. At this time, I rather accidently joined the Legion. They were harsh. I was forced to beat a man with a stick to show my resolve, which struck me as an odd way to go about it. They also beat me, but I didn't\ncare so long as I could join and finally have a purpose. I still wanted to be a diplomat, and the Legion seemed like the fastest way to gain traction in the Empire. Finally, the man who recruited me asked, \"Why do you want to join the Legion, boy?\"\nIt was a choice I was faced with, in that moment. I thought that being honest in my intentions would earn his respect, but I should have lied to save my chances. He kicked me out, told me he never wanted to see me again. I tried again later, of course.\nI was lost and confused, with no path to lead me through life. I took a few months to travel the surrounding lands, to see what I could see, and returned to Johannesburg only to realize that I hadn't changed at all. I was so close to giving up, then.\nBut as I stood there in the street, pondering who I wanted to be, a man by the name of Lukas Vanir approached me and asked if I would like to join an \"adventuring party.\" This was by no means my first offer to join a house or other group, but something\ndeep within me cried out \"yes,\" and so I agreed. This is how I found myself employed (although 'employed' is a loose term for it) to the noble House Vanir. \nI have pondered my journey a great many times now, and I wished to fill in any gaps in my story.\nI hope this short detour has acomplished that.\nDay 39\n\nI did more searching in the desert today. I finally was able to talk to the Orcs in their city. They are not half as bad as I have been led to believe, if you speak in the right way. They denied seeing Elliot again, but I was more inclined\nto believe them, this time. One of their people even showed me a settlement they thought could be behind the \"event\" but it turned out to be the place Elise is from. \n\nI did ask someone inside about Elliot and he too claimed not to have seen him.\nSomething about him struck me as odd, but I cannot quite place it. Maybe more questions there would be good. I must find Elliot and ask him about it; something isn't adding up. The Orcs in the city said that they saw the halfling quite frequently, which\nmakes little sense. Did he... lie to me? I don't understand why, but it would make sense then that he didn't want anyone else to know. I feel like a fool now. I must speak with him, again. And this time, I shall get the whole truth.\nDay 40\n\nI have never felt so much shame in my life. I finally found Elliot and was able to talk with him, but it seems I am guilty of ruining his quiet life. \nApperently, the men burned him rather horribly. This was to stop the infection, of\ncourse, but it still makes me feel sick.\nDay 41\n\nHow to heavens have smiled on me, today! I may be a horrible friend, but I am good enough to be loved by someone. Elise confessed today. I was so shocked, there was no way I could have seen it coming. She even kissed me!\nOr, rather, I kissed her. I shouldn't speak of such personal things here, but I think of this as a relic of my life. Surely no one would care if I wrote of my... \"sweet heart\" in here. Eric will be pleased, I think. He nearly got me killed asking if I\nliked other men, since I didn't have a girl yet. That man has no problem with embarassing people. At the same time, I have never felt like I had more of a family than now. I should distance myself more. I can't get too close to these people. I am\nwhole-heartedly expendable to them, and I should never forget that.\nDay 42\n\nI think I was slain. I awoke in a strange place, and upon making my way back to Johannesburg, was hounded by a terrible feeling of dread. Part of me is glad I can't remember how it happened, perhaps it is too terrible\nfor a simple man like me. \n\nI think this will mark the end of this journal. I have much more to write, but this book has served its purpose for now. \n\n~Loren Allson", ""),
        StaticBook("Loren's Book IV", "Loren Allson", "Day 43\n\nSutica is rather quite today. An elven woman seemed to think me a bandit. To be completely fair, I have been simply wandering the city. The hollow feeling of death follows me wherever I go. Perhaps there is no escape,\nbut that does not mean I shall not try. \n\nI visited the library in Sutica again. I cannot remember the exact name, but I was impressed by the selection. It has two floors, the second of which I hadn't seen before. I always enjoy reading\nthe literature, no matter the author. \n\nJohannesburg is snowed over. Every now and then tiny flakes drift down from above, joining their brothers on the street. It's peaceful, the way it muffles the clamor of the city.\nI was never one for snow in my youth. I know now why people seem to enjoy it so much. How wonderful the world is, sometimes. And yet, my expirience follows me like a shadow to my mind. How many times might one die and come back before they aren't\nalive anymore? I need some form of distraction. Perhaps traking down my killer (if there is a killer) will bring some sort of closure to this feeling.\nDay 44\n\nI must waste no more time in this life of mine. There is much work to be done now. I know not what I work toward, but every moment that goes by without action is a moment one can never earn back.\nDay 51\n\nYou may notice the large gap of entries in this journal. I had a rather nasty fall from a mountain, and part of the book was destroyed. I have comprised a summary of the events that took place since the\nlast entry.\n\nA pair of Cyclops attacked Johannesburg. The fight was long and terrble, but the fighters won the day. \n\nThe winter festival began, and a giant snowman of all things\nattacked the city as well. Seems to be the season. \n\nWithin all of this, Elise somehow regained her ability to speak, which she lost after someone chopped off her tounge. \n\nI am entertaining the\nidea of proposing, but I feel I should own some sort of home, first. I cannot have a wife without a house, right? Besides, I need one anyway. \n\nI also encountered a friendly Golum, who was searching for its elven creator.\nThis should get you mostly up to speed.\nDay 52\n\nUndead continue to plauge Johannesburg. They come in waves, with everything from liches to pale knights. I encountered one battle between three necromancers and the imperial guard, who had disbatched one of the foul creatures\nalready. I stood ready with my sword, but nothing was required of me as an elven mage appeared. He was able to destroy the remaining abominations with ease. I have never seen a feat like that. \nAfterwards, a knight of the Westerlands\ndemanded I prove my mortality. Apparently my uselessness made him believe that I was with the undead. \nThe test for this is shockingly simple; a cut must be made on the hand.\nI, of course, am not an agent of evil. I just hover near the edge\nof any fighting that may be happening around me. \nI spoke with the elven mage for a moment as well. He was very striking, and tall for an elf. He even healed the cut from the doubtful knight. It was amazing to watch, but I won't ramble about it.\nThe true digrace was the fact that he was made to pay a fine! A fine! For saving the lives of no less than half a dozen men from a pale knight. \nI offered to pay it, but he was able to bargain his way out of most of it.\nDay 53\n\nToday I made yet another mistake. A man confronted me for no real reason other than the fact that he was a foolish troublemaker. I told him that I saw no need to fight, and he said something along the\nlines of, \"Why, does your mother clean your wounds for you?\"\n\nNow, I do not consider myself the sort of man to speak without thinking. Today, however, was a different story. In reply, I said,\n\"Nie, your mother does.\"\nAs I found out later, the man's mother is dead and he intends to hunt me down for slandering her. \n\nI am filled with shame. I am not one for petty words like that. My character has fallen, I fear.\nDay 54\n\nToday was the battle against the dwarves. I know little about why this fight started, but here is the footnotes version:\n\nOren attacked Orcs in the west. Apperently, the Dwarves were\nprotecting them. Oren told the Dwarves to stand down. They refused. Oren got mad, and started a war. \n\nWe battled on a large mountain. Oren won, of course. We always win. I suppose I should be glad that I got through without a scratch, but it feels\nlike a bitter win for us.\nDay 55\n\nI proposed to Elise today. She accepted! I have never been so happy in all my life!\nDay 56,\n\nPart of why I chose to marry Elise was the battle against the dwarves. I fought in the seige against the snow elves as well, but there was something vastly different about this fight. I had a reason I wanted to live, and that reason\nwas her. I have no regrets. You only get one shot at finding true happiness, and I'm taking mine now.\n\nIn other news, Elliot agreed to be my best man. I was happy to be able to tell him, he encouraged me when no one else would. The only other person\nwho knows is Lincoln (who works for the Johannesburg Tavern), but I highly doubt he cares. I haven't brought it up to any of the Vanir men. It just doesn't feel right. I am not particularly close to any of them, exept Sir Eric perhaps. I will say\nsomething when the time is right.\nDay 57,\n\nI watched the death of the Dwarven king today. I don't believe I've writted much about the dwarf-human-orc war here, but it is something that has dominated the islands in these past few years.\nBut enough of that. The Grand King is dead, which is a severe blow to dwarven moral. We needed a win like this.\nDay 58,\n\nI don't think I'll be able to walk properly again. \n\nI was outside of Johannesburg when a pair of bandits approached me. They forced me to go with them, marched me not\nfar from the city. I was then held there and tormented for what I can only assume was hours. They stabbed me in the kneecap, shattering the bone and cut my chest. They didn't take any money from me, they must have been madmen.\nI passed out from the pain not long after, and by the time I woke up they had dragged me back to Serpentstone. I don't remember much from that point onward, but somehow I lived. \n\nMy left leg, on the other hand, is ruined. I pray it will heal in the\ncoming weeks, but I doubt it will make a difference. What use is a crippled man to House Vanir? None. I suspect my time here may end, soon. \n\nBut there must be some way to fix it. I've heard tales of mages who can heal great wounds, and\nperhaps someone can fashion me a metal limb. There is hope yet, but I am unsure where to begin the search. \n\nI must contact Elise once more, as well. She's been travelling lately, and has no idea of the events that have transpired in her absence.\nI wonder if she could still love me, after so much time.\nDay 35,\n\nMy leg is healed! \n\nI am getting ahead of myself, I'm afraid. I'll tell the story in order. \nI took our newest recruit, Zenneth, up to wood elf lands today. The plan was to ask them to teach him\nhow to heal (a cause that I am rather invested in, of late), but we got rather sidetracked and ended up at the Trinity Keep. It's a large fortress located atop a steep hill. When we reached the gate, I was loath to break the silence. Nothing seemed to\nmove or breathe. But the people here are nice enough. They are a mix of clerics, elves, and Ascended that make me feel small and weak. But I was hardly going to let Zenneth walk away from his dream of becoming a healer. We spoke with a pleasant man who\ntold us that there were several clerics who might be willing to teach the lad. Good news, for I had began to lose hope of ever finding a teacher for him. \n\nAs we sat in the hall, Zenneth brought up the god-forsaken injury\nthat I had sustained the week before. I was hesitant to ask for favors, but it seems they were eager to help. \n\nOne of the Ascended, Opal (if my memory serves me right), told me that my kneecap was completely shattered\nand my only hope lie with a metal replacment. Luckily, it did not come to that. One of her fellows (Selina was her name) was able to heal it. The magic was unlike anything I've ever seen. The process broke two of her ribs in exchange for my being able to\nwalk once more. I am forever in their debt. Truly. \n\nI think I will stick around here, for a time. At least until Zenneth finds a teacher. It is rather strange though. The people here seem afraid of us, and to be completely\nhonest I'm more than a little fearfull of them. They possess almost endless power, and if they wished it they could kill us in a moment. \n\nI don't intend on causing trouble or imposing on them, but I refuse to abandon one of our men here.\nI will stay, for now.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lori Report", "Lori Fhociin", "Black furrum axe sharp I \n350 minas\nDiamond tools Pick/Axe 100 minas\nshovel 40 minas \nhoe 70\nFront entry a bit small strong stone walls\nDiamond helm 1700 minas\nDiamond chest 3200\nLeggings 2700\nDiamond boots 1500\n4 towers ish\n2 on the front side\n2 on the back side\nAn orc was seen\nIf siege ever happens food outside of the walls so would die quickly of starvation", ""),
        StaticBook("Lost Journal, v1", "Unknown", "It seems I no longer have the skills I once had, or at least the ones I seem to recall having, once upon a dream. As a matter of fact, my skills are so far dwindled, I have difficulty with even the weakest o f monsters. As well, my ability to draw a bow\nis weakened. I can barely draw the string taut. But that is not the least concerning of my weaknesses. Aside from the consistent night horrors which plague me when I sleep, it also seems as if I can no longer practice my \"craft\" as it were. The\nloss of my powers and my skills are a surprise to me. I can remember vividly the last few hours I spent before I....wound up, here. But I am beginning to become confused, disoriented. Instead of attempting to discern why this has occurred, I have\ndecided to focus on the task at hand. I will blend in with these peoples, and hopefully, in my work, I will find the answers to my questions.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lost Journal, v2", "Unknown", "I feel my skills are increasing, ever so slowly. They are nowhere near the level they once were. But I do feel stronger now. I, along with the Lord of Whisper Isles, have begun something of a quest. We and our two comrades, a dwarf and an Elven\nambassador, are searching for a tome. Having found the first tome here in the Whisper Isles, we now seek to find the second. It is said to lie in the High City of Laurelin, home of the elves, and concerns \"The Lost Ones.\" However, after searching the\nlibrary at Laurelin, we found nothing. The Elf's High Prince himself even assisted us, but to no avail. Yet I have seen many things, which soothes my soul somewhat, the great structures of the capital of Oren, and the beauty of Laurelin. But while these\nsites bring me pleasure, as does the knowledge I found in Laurelin, I am still yet uneasy. I feel as if I have...forgotten something. A feeling of foreboding, and confusing. If what I have read is true, then there is not much else in this land beyond\nwhat I have seen already. The home of the orcs, and the dwarves, I have yet to see, yes... But if what I have read is true...then it is not possible for me to have come from where I recall as my home. Now I lie awake and ponder, is it the history and\nknowledge of this world that is wrong? Is there perhaps something beyond it that these people are unaware of? Or...is it I, and my mind, which have failed? I will seek further answers to my questions. I cannot accept that the powers I once\nyielded, and the people I knew... And these...injuries I sustained, are mere figments of my imagination.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lost Journal, v3", "Unknown", "To know anything at all is to know nothing at all. After all, the very act of being a sentient being is to know that there is an obscene wealth of things you do not know. I've walked headfirst into this wall, this problem, many times now, and it blocks\nmy advance in this world at nearly every turn. I've kept quiet about the memories that I seem to have in my mind, about the things I have done, and the wars I have fought, and the things I have lost. And most importantly, about the vivid details I have\nof the time right before my arrival here in the Land of Aegis, and the man I met directly before it all went black.. Of all my memories, of fighting, killing, surviving, living, loving, those are the most  vivid. But whether what I remember was\neven real...*the text here is blotted out by some sort of dried up, red stain, which seeps through onto the next page* :: I've still no idea where the book of the Lost Ones lies. It doesn't matter. More important things have come up. I've run into a\nstrange man in the Isles. He is, by all accounts, and even my own, a Wizard. I learned this first hand when dear Rinscher, my dwarf friend, irritated the man, and he shot us both with lightning. While I can understand wanting to smoke his pipe in\npeace (Though I would have enjoyed a smoke as well), it did seem a bit of an overreaction on his part. No matter. I saved an elf later that same day, a woman named Aerovine. I had been passing through Kramoroe when I heard a few\nfellows yelling things along the lines of \"Kill! Go out, loot!\" in their home. I hid in the alley and watched as one of them, armed, left the small hut. I followed him quietly along the road to Oren, never getting too close. We passed armed men he paid\nno mind to, then came about the Elf woman. Hardly armed, she immediately ran as he attacked. I ordered for him to stop, to surrender. Alas, he did not. I was forced to slay the man. I escorted the woman, with her thanks, back to the Isles and the road\nto Laurelin. It seemed all roads pointed to Laurelin lately. An expansive library, an educated people... Although, there were a few of her residents whom I have had trouble with...", ""),
        StaticBook("Lost Journal, v4", "Unknown", "My physical health has deteriorated as of late. I do not wish to go into details, but regardless whether or not my memories are fact or fiction,\nit seems the injuries I sustained are all too real. But what is worse than the physical pain is that which\nkeep me moving when it grows almost too much to bear. I wish to find out the truth, or at least a hint to it.\nI ran into the Wizard again. But what caused our meeting is what I sit in Diplomat Lafthi's home, waiting to explain. You see, I decided I\nought to move to Laurelin. It has been...calling me, it seems, for far too long.\nWhen I arrived, I ran into the elf woman I had rescued before, Aerovine. She was gracious enough to show me around the town to any properties for\nsale. I found a home I liked quite a lot, which suited my purposes well.\nPlenty of room for the piles of books I would like to accumulate, but sadly, outside of my meager price range. Aerovine however, has a kindness which knows no bounds. Or\nperhaps she is simply Virtuous enough to not know the sin of Greed. She offered to give me the money I needed to buy the home, and after some insistence on her part, I accepted, and promised to pay it back (And pay it back I shall) Afterwards, I went\nback to the Isles to turn in my Guard Uniform.\nI did not tell the others of my ailments though, and only mentioned them to Lord Tyrion. I will continue to defend people on the roads, make no mistake. A few events occured, but afterwards I\nheaded to Laurelin and purchased my new home from High Prince Native. It seems I was not the only one eyeing it. An elf named Elvador and his brothers Had also hoped to buy it. I'm afraid I got there first, though. They were not upset though, which\nmade me glad. Aerovine assisted them in looking for an apartment, when Elvador mentioned something to me as I was about to leave. On his way back from the Isles, he had heard of a plot by a group of Orcs and Elves. This plot was the kidnap the Queen\nof Oren and overthrow the Orc Leader.\nI could scarcely believe my ears when I heard it, but I knew I had to act. I sent Elvador and Aerovine to tell their High Prince, while I set out for Oren. It was as I passed through the Isles,\nlooking for a guard to tell my tale to, that I happened upon the Wizard again. And oh, how it vexxed me!!! He weaved and bobbed, moving quicker than light, perhaps even teleporting. He commented on the rain and mumbled to himself....and oh, how I\nwished I could speak to him, to ask him if anything I remembered could possibly be true. But I felt my wounds clawing at me, and I knew I would not last long before I had to rest. I had to reach Oren before then.\nThat duty came before my own\nwishes. So I apologized to him, stated I wished I could speak...and left. I know not if I'll run into the man again. But I had to deliver my message...", ""),
        StaticBook("Lost Journal, v5", "Unknown", "As I reached Kramoroe, I ran into Sergeant Urg of the Isles' Guards. I quickly told him what I knew and bid he be on his way quickly, as would I. We parted, he for the Isles to warn Lord Tyrion, as the Isles sit dangerously close to Orc lands, and I to\nOren. But I ran into one more acquaintance on the way. A man named Dalejin. We had scarcely talked, only exchanging a few words on a pleasant basis before, but still I liked and trusted the man. I felt he could be trusted. So I told\nhim what I had to say This proved to be a good idea indeed. It seems Dalejin is a close friend of the Queen. So off we went to Oren, to find her and her guard and warn them ourselves... But...we could not find any of them. Not a soul of use in the town\nwas there. I felt my wounds again, and knew I had to leave for Laurelin before something happened. I left Dalejin to deliver my message, and headed back. The Wizard was gone from the Isles, as I expected, so I didn't even stop. I arrived in Laurelin\nagain, after a time, and collapsed in my house. Well, I assume I collapsed. I blacked out, you see. I woke up there many hours later. When I was lucid again I went out to the library to begin some work. But I ran into Lafthi on my way, which was good\nluck as he has not heard of the plot against the Queen and the Orcs yet. So I went into his office as he took care of some business, and here I wait. I can only hope he returns soon, as I feel that journey has taken more of my strength than I originally\nthought. I will continue this journal once I have informed her of the situation. wished I could speak to him, to ask him if anything I remembered could possibly be true. But I felt my wounds clawing at me, and I knew I would not last long before I had\nto rest. I had to reach Oren before then.\nThat duty came before my own wishes. So I apologized to him, stated I wished I could speak...and left. I know not if I'll run into the man again. But I had to deliver my message...", ""),
        StaticBook("Lost Journal, v6", "Unknown", "I've come to the Pathfinder's new base in search of information. It seems that he has lost perhaps 3 men to banditry, and I wish to know their names.\nZevandir is one who left to become a bandit. Tailon left, but is an Elven Lord, so Kalen does not\nbelieve him a bandit. I've come here after informing High Prince Native of\nthe dangers at hand. He is quite kind, and more willing to assist me in finding the culprits. The Black Guard was started by Zevandir in hopes of converting the\nPathfinders to\nbandits. That is why I saw Val the Pathfinder claiming himself a Black Guard. Val was supposedly tricked, and Atles has agreed to vouch for him that he has\nchanged his ways. The Pathfinders are still, and always shall be, loyal to the Prince\nand the nation. Lord Atles serves in Lord Blackthorn's stead. Until his return. Lord Atles claims he found something far north of the boundaries of Oren. Some sort of dark village, with lava at the gates. But I do not know if I believe him. Pathfinder\nKalen states that town is only the town of North Alister. Kalen reports seeing strange, dark beings walking about, with cloaks pulled about their faces. Could these have been members of the Dark Brotherhood Lafthi mentioned? Kalen is a bit hard to\nunderstand, but he claims a group of the cloaked individuals Attacked Laurelin at some point. Present here are Swift Elf Alenom, Pathfinder Kalen, and Lord Atles. I've run into many of the Princes of Laurelin at Ravenhold, and the HighGuardian,\nMayctor He claims the Dark Brotherhood are no threat. He refuses to tell me how he knows of this fact, which I find very suspicious. Mayctor has informed me the Brotherhood seeks alliances with the \"do-gooder\" guilds. Could they be behind the Cripsons\nassault on a Holmes fellow? I'll have to talk to Lafthi. Macytor is a worshipper of Iblees, this much must be true. He cannot be allowed to get away with this. He guarantees the safety of the elves, on Iblees' name.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lost Journal, v7", "Unknown", "I grow increasingly frustrated As Prince Toren commented, \"Spider webs within Spider webs.\" I have stumbled head first into these webs, and now I am stuck. I've determined no more about my past, but that matters not now. This plot I mentioned reaches\nfurther than I imagined, clawing its way into nearly every race in Aegis. I have confirmed that this plot is being perpetrated by the Dark Brotherhood. They wish to see the empire of the humans fall, and likely the Orcs as well. It seems they plot to\nattack within the next month. Elvador also made mention of a portal they wished to create. Inside lie some sort of monsters that the Brotherhood wishes to kill. It seems the elves are the ones most manipulated by this evil. The leader of one of their\narmy branches, the Sentinels, is a worshipper of Iblees. High Guardian Mayctor... He is evil, yet his intentions appear good...he wishes to protect the Elves. So he assures me that Laurelin will remain safe, and that the Elves will not be attacked. What\nhe has done to gain this assurance, I do not know. But I believe he may be a threat to the High Prince, if the Prince decides to act against him. I have found a new ally in the Pathfinder's current leader, Atles. He also believes a great evil is\ncoming...but I will believe that when I see it. For the evil he predicts is far greater than that of what the Brotherhood could manage.  Had I the strength for it, I would go to the North, where he claims to have found a small, dark\nvillage. Yet this same village his associates say is part of Oren. But as I have mentioned, my condition worsens by the day. As I spoke to Mayctor in Ravenhold, I was prepared to fight him should he strike me. Yet in truth, I do not believe I could\nhave held my sword up. Prince Toren, who I had met at Ravenhold along with two other princes and the Princess, agreed with me that Mayctor seemed quite mad. I sent him with a book of my notes to deliver to Native, in the event I was attacked. All\nturned out fine however, and he has become a quiet ally of mine, as the other Princes are far too blinded to see Mayctor's true face. Mayctor claimed he had information I sought, but refused to tell me. He also told me that only one other knew of\nwhat he did, and that he would never talk... In addition, Atles told me that some of the Pathfinders that had deserted had become bandits. I will have to see if they have anything to do with this matter. But for now, I've but one lead... a name Lafthi\nand Prince Loren both told me. An elf by the name of Rolok, who supposedly lives in Laurelin. I must find and speak with him.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lost Journal, v8", "Unknown", "As I ran down the King's Road towards Kal'Urguan, Mayctor always at my heels, that devilish cackle of his ringing through the air, I began to wonder to myself... Where, or when, exactly, had I gone wrong?\nI realized it was a moot question at\nthat point...I had bigger problems to worry about. Namely, the High Guardian, who I feared was drawing ever closer. But now that I think upon it, I realize it is quite obvious. I relied too much on others, and the assistance they gave. I should have\nworked for myself, done all of my research myself. I should not have involved innocents, like Prince Toren, or Atles...whom I can only hope is safe, after disobeying orders in order to assist me... But I am ahead of myself. I suppose I should start at\nthe beginning.\nI believe I had mentioned Mayctor in my last journal entry, though I have lost count of the volumes and being away from Laurelin, am unable to check the numbers. But I will assume I have mentioned him, my suspicions about\nhim, and the whirlwind of evil which seems to have engulfed the Elves of Laurelin. I will assume that I had also mentioned the Dark Brotherhood and some of their possible members. Now, I will mention the new threat that has emerged...the Undead. I\nfirmly believe, now more than ever, that Mayctor had something to do with them... That the Brotherhood at least works alongside them, if not for them. I had just finished a meeting with the  Pathfinders, along with other allies of mine, including\nDalejin.\nDalejin had left back to Oren with the news I had given him, of Mayctor's involvement in the scheme to kidnap the Queen. I believe he reached the capital in time, right as there were shrieks of horror from the North which we in\nLaurelin did not hear word of until a messenger came a bit later. As soon as we had heard it though, we left. I and a number of the Pathfinders, including Atles, headed North. Unfortunately, in my condition...I did not reach Winterfell,\nor Alstion. I am ashamed of this fact. I nearly collapsed on the road, bidding Atles to go on ahead. Yet, he returned a short time later. He had made it to Alstion, but had been ordered back to Laurelin by High Prince Native. I left with him. He told\nme what he saw, of the Undead and the chaos, as we left. When we reached the gates of Laurelin, Mayctor was standing there on top of them... Looking down on me.... I lost control, screaming at him.\nHe had to have had something to do\nwith it! He hated the humans...He sought our end. But he only laughed. I refused to enter the gates of Laurelin at that point. I would not be inside the city with that...devil. I headed to Kal'urguan after that, to make sure the dwarves were prepared.\nA merchant by the name of Algor told me Hiebe was preparing the army for war, and that he was making axes for Hiebe, but had run out of wood. I went to get him some, knowing it was the least I could do. As I headed back, I ran across a group of strange\nmen, one of whom bumped me off the road. All of them wore white robes...I had no time to question their intentions though. I can only assume they were some foolhardy, arrogant Templars. Or some other guild which thought it could defeat this evil\neasily.\nI returned to Laurelin after delivering the wood to Algor. Mayctor was no where in sight...I attempted to locate him, but failed. Then Princess Araina announced she was going to the Capital to help the wounded. I could not let her\ngo alone, and I knew I had many spare supplies I could deliver. So we set off with them. The whole of Oren was chaotic. I found Dalejin right upon entering and we gave him what supplies we had, for the soldiers. As many arrows as I had and iron for\narmor. An uproar started suddenly, the guards yelling about an escaped prisoner. One of the Undead, apparently. I ran off to search the city, but coming up empty handed, returned just as the guards were escorting the Queen up to the\nKeep....when I saw him. Mayctor, hiding in the crowd. I yelled at him and he immediately ran for the gates. I gave chase, but he leapt off the road and I lost sight of him. I desperately wished to follow, to capture and question him....but I dare not\nleave the Princess alone. I found her and we went to the Keep together. She mentioned some kind of Traveler who was going to escort her back to Laurelin. We found that Traveler in the Throne Room, causing a ruckus. He was apparently trying to convince\nthe Queen to come to Kal'Urguan, to hide. She refused, and the guards grew angry, attempting to arrest him.\nHe fled quickly, and then the escaped Undead was discovered. Dalejin, I, and the Princess moved the Queen to the Barracks, as\nshe requested. I left them there to speak, while I went to look around the city, mostly for Mayctor. I knew he was still hiding there somewhere. But then the Princess sent word that she sensed something happening in Laurelin. I immediately found\nher again and escorted her back to Laurelin... But not without running into the Traveler again. He refused to go to Laurelin with us, and the Princess refused to go anywhere without speaking to him first. By this time we were accompanied by 2\nPathfinders. So I took us to the Whisper Isles. I still had access to the Guard Barracks there. The Princess and the Traveler spoke privately inside for some time, then without a word, we left for Laurelin. When we arrived...well, I\nhad no time to rest. Mayctor was skulking about, odd as usual...then a man pulled me aside.\nI recognized him. Relok. One of the possible members of the Brotherhood... He wished to know what I knew, how much of it, what I knew about Mayctor. He\nclaimed to be a spy, who was sent to spy on Mayctor. I did not believe any of it. I refused to speak to him, and he threatened me. I do not remember the exact wording, but he ordered me to tell him \"Or feel the wrath of the Dark Brotherhood.\"\nThe Princess pulled me aside then, right as Relok claimed there was no such thing as the Dark Brotherhood. I could not comprehend what he was up to. Was it a plot to discredit me? To fool me? To drive me mad? I found out quickly what they wanted from\nme though. The Princess wished to speak to me in private about my....condition. She is quite observant, unfortunately. But I did not know what was the subject of our discussion until we reached a quiet place.\nBut before\nthat....Mayctor followed us. He crept along, hissing and making odd noises, half bent over all the while. We finally got somewhere quiet and had our conversation in whispers... And the Princess told me I should leave Laurelin. That it\nwas dangerous. That she had overheard word that the Brotherhood wanted me captured. That was when Mayctor came across us. And the Princess bade me, no....commanded me, to flee. And flee I did. I ran as fast as I could from Mayctor as he yelled for\nme to halt. Claiming I was under arrest, and cackling. As I neared the gate, one of the Princes joined him in his attempts... I could not believe he had tricked them, too. So I screamed at the Pathfinders, who guarded the gate, to open it. While the\nPrince yelled in retaliation to keep it closed. Someone, I do not know who, but someone whom I owe my life to finally opened it. And I ran out of the city. But I am injured, and slow. The young Prince caught up to me easily, as did Mayctor. I\nthought all lost. The Princess had commanded me not to let Mayctor catch me, no matter what. But I could not attack the innocent Prince. That was when Atles, my savior, arrived, demanding an explanation. Mayctor claimed I was under arrest for\nconspiracy, and Atles asked for proof... I could not stay any longer.\nI yelled an apology to Atles, then ran. Now we return to the beginning of the journal... I ran down the road from Laurelin, Mayctor cackling behind me. As I entered the\nKing's Road, I thought I saw someone familiar, up in the trees... But no. Even if it was him, I could not expect him to assist. I reached the Isles, then hung a left, running as fast as I could. I believe it was at that point Mayctor's cackles seemed\nto fade... But I could not be sure. My blood was pounding in my ears. I ran past the marketplace I had met Agnor at, down the trail... Until I finally reached Kal'Urguan and began banging on the gates, demanding to be let in. And directly behind them\nstood the Traveler, whom the Princess had bade me to meet when I arrived, saying he would help me, and I should tell him all I know. I was allowed in and we quickly retired to a private room. I told him of Mayctor, of the Brotherhood, of my wounds, of\nthe Princess... When finally the story was done, King Charles came upon us. He graciously offered me his protection…\n...ah, I drifted off. I'm afraid there are more people here, including...the Princess? And one of those men in the white robes....", ""),
        StaticBook("Lost Journal, v9", "Unknown", "I have not felt this way as far back as I can remember. My head is cleared of my maladies, as is my body, and I feel...alive. There are many things which lead to this, and many unfortunate occurrences. But I will not bore the world with my past. It\ndoes not matter any longer. It is only the future, to which I look forward to, and the skies of Aegis which I look to for guidance.\nI will not, no, cannot explain how it came to be. But Hwaldar has played a large part in my recovery from the pits of\ndarkness. I remember not of my previous memories, which were revealed to me to be fakes. Only my recent time in Aegis lives on in my mind. I have been\nvindicated, in a way, by what Hwaldar revealed to me. It was never intentional to make me the way I\nwas, a half-full shell of a woman. It was merely necessary, to defeat evil and to spare my life. But that is not a story which needs to be revealed. The past is gone, done, and far away. Now, I search for my next calling. I was correct in my remembrance\nof my former powers, that of magic. I wish to regain those powers now, to help fight the undead and the other evils which plague Aegis. I am setting out now, on a new journey. I wish to gain more knowledge, as much as I am able. I also hope my leaving\nMalinor will draw some of the evil that haunts it to come after me. But it matters not. I feel strong enough to take on anyone who attacks me. And I will continue to protect those innocents around me, as well. I will cease to chase after my past and\ninstead chase after my future. So I have written it, and so I swear to do. I will not allow evil to corrupt me as it once almost did.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lost Journal,v10", "Unknown", "I've come across an interesting concept as of late. The premise of it is quite interesting. And it is quite in-depth for such a simple matter. To explain it initially is simple. The concept is that of\nequivalent exchange. The idea that you cannot have\nsomething, from nothing. You must first give up something of equal or greater value. But it is much more complex than that....and has a greater impact on the current situation than it appears. I will write more on this subject as I continue my studies.\nBut I believe it may be very useful against the undead…\nI have travelled quite a bit since my re-awakening as it were. I cannot help but take advantage of the fact that I feel well, and alive for once. I had the pleasure of speaking with Arcane Mage\nSlayer at Laurelin the other day. He's quite the gentleman, which was especially useful when that idiot Rinscher was buggering me. He was able to cast a spell on him to ward him off, which hopefully scared him away from me. Which is good, considering\nwere it not for the Monks of the Temple, Rinscher would have succeeded in killing both King Perea and High Prince Native, though I am more upset about the latter of the two. I've also travelled to Alkhazar again, take a...less travelled route, and met a\ngroup of travelling merchants who go by the names of various sins of man. I would have no trouble with them at all had one of them not sought fit to threaten me on the rooftops of Alkhazar. (I have become a bit enamored with running atop the\nroofs, I am embarrassed to admit.)\nParanoia is still at a dangerous high in Alkhazar. I can understand it, to an extent. I myself do not trust many people at all. Though I have been trying to judge people on their actions, not how I\nsee them. Yet my instinctive judgments have usually been correct, so far. Now I have set out once more, to the Valve. I have passed through before, but only now have been given a proper tour by the Grand Champion, Whurili. They seem to be well prepared\nin the event of an undead invasion. But when I first arrived, they and a few orcs were discussing alliances namely the idea that orcs and dwarves would ally, and humans and elves...but against whom? Each other? I will have to find out as much about this\nas possible. But for now, I've decided to begin officially leaving my old life behind. Or, atleast, the agony and bitterness I had because of it. In a symbolic gesture, I have decided to leave one of my journals hidden outside the Valve. I have all\nthe valuable information from them written down elsewhere. But there is no longer any need to try and recall the false memories which once plagued my mind. I leave part of my insanity here, behind me. And still, I look forward to the future and the\nseven skies.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lost Letters, ??", "Unknown", "An unsigned letter, written in careful hand. It seems to be a copy of the original.\nWe know little of the one called Dworkin, and what we do know is steeped in mystery of its own. I can say for certain the man was a mage; a sorcerer of great\nhis time. His specialty is lost, but what I have managed to infer from the tome found in his tomb in the tower far to the North implies that he was not a master of merely one magic, but of several. Indeed, perhaps even all of them, for there seems to\nmuch implied of his omniscience. (Though you and I both know such a thing is impossible in practice.)\nAside from being a master mage, it is known he was something of a wild card. Rather than sit in a dreary room pouring over\nbooks as you or I might, he opted instead for practice in the field. He lead a band of adventurers, each and every one a master mage in their own right. Though there is no known name for his fellowship, it may very well have been\nfoundings of our very own Guild, seeing as so much of our own history was lost. They were known for their work against the Fallen One, and I can connect their work to several vague as-of-yet-unexplained incidents from that era. They are referenced in\nthe Tale of Nine Cities, in the locating of the lost relic of the cult of Sondar, in the rescue of Queen Yi'naethia from the hordes of the Dreaded One, and in some ancient writings concerning their failure to save something called \"The Jewel of the\nSouth\" before it was lost, though they were valiant in their attempt to do so, surely.\nThere is so much we do not know of Dworkin, but so much potential to gain should we endeavour to rediscover that which was lost. The Guild's archives\nMalinor could be of significant use in this matter and it would serve the Guild's purpose if you permitted me to send one of my graduated apprentices to investigate this matter on my behalf, as I of course cannot depart from my post.\nKind regards,", "")
    )

    private fun _AXIOS_TAHN_3(): List<StaticBook> = listOf(
        StaticBook("Lost Love", "Monk Scribe", "By chance it seemed\nAnd nothing more,\nThat we would meet\nDown by the shore.\n\nWe sit on the hillside,\nAs I push back you hair.\nYou smile at me,\nAll I do is stare.\n\nI came to you,\nFlowers in hand,\nBut you said no,\nI would not understand.\n\nI cried that night,\nWhen you left me alone.\nI sit still,\nAs still as stone.\n\nNever again,\nHave I seen your eyes,\nbut they will stay with me,\nuntil our final goodbyes.\n\n- Written by Myro in Asulon.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lost Memories", "Nami Dalivous", "Hello, I am Nami, this is my story. I was born to a snow elf mother and a dark elf father. I have long white hair and am around six feet in height. I have light grey skin because of being a halfbreed and light blue eyes. My father was a gaurd.\nOne day while on patrol, he came across my mother. It was love at first sight. Even though my father knew the rules of having to marry his own kind, he married my mother. When others found out about this, they were inraged.\nThey attempted to exacute my parents, but they escaped before that. They found a home in the woods and had me and my brother. One day I awoke to my father taking from my bed, the smell of smoke stinging my nose and eyes.\nMy father ran me down the steps, just as we reached the bottem an arrow flew past my face, I turn to my father as he dropped me to the ground, blood pooled from his mouth, my mother ran over, picking me up, she looked to my father one last time.\nTears ran down her cheeks as she bolted to the window, smashing it open she tried to get out, she got stuck. She looked to me and kissed my head, her last words to me being 'I love you my dear Nami.' before she tossed me out the window.\nI screamed her name as I ran, her screams still haunting my memories to this day. Then it all goes black.", ""),
        StaticBook("Love", "Levana Olaris", "Love is a thing no one knows\nA hidden thing not able to show.\nWe fear the pain, we fear fate\nA burden we have, but we do wait.\nI cry, I frown, it turns my life upside down.\nWe can try to hide, but it will find us.\nIt craves our care, it craves our trust.\nIt burns our skin like ink on paper.\nBut it is easy to lose, like water to vapor.\nLove is something we can't hold.\nIt doesn't do anything, but turn us cold.", ""),
        StaticBook("LoveAmongCulture", "Steven", "Love Among Cultures\n----------------\nA study conducted by Steven Vega Novokain\n8th of The First Seed, 1576\nLove can be considered to be quite the controversial topic. From what it is, to how it works, to how it should be celebrated. I've found that different cultures each have different views and ideologies on love. For example, orcish culture have no value\nto love whatsoever, simply caring about reproduction. High Elven culture, while reproduction is their priority, do put more value on love, even having their own carefully laid out method of courting. High Elven love is more focused around the intelligence\nof the 'aheral of interest. The Wood Elves, on the other hand, take a relatively high value on love. I even interviewed one who said they found the concept of monoamourosity to be foolish, remaining faithful to multiple partners. The Warhawkes, seeming to\nbe the prominent culture of the Dark Elves, value love quite highly, as well as faith to their partners. The Dwarves have said that they do not value love as highly as other cultures, with the exception of the Orcs. The most that they'll go to is getting\nmarried- and of course, reproducing- as they don't see the point of going to the trouble of anything further. That being so, they are quite loyal, to their partners and in general. \nHalflings, following their already passive and colloquial nature,\nhave their own method of courting, being a picnic before they get into anything serious. As for the Human nations, Orenian and Canonists have their own high values on love. Orenian love comes in the form of a man and woman spending an ample time courting\neachother, before forsaking all others to marry. Anullments are extremely rare for Orenians and Canonists as a whole. Haria, while they do hold a high value for love, they are known to have arranged marriages, especially for nobles. Finally, the denizens\nof the Dreadlands claim to hold love as their highest value. While they are extremely faithful to their partners, they do tend to go through quite a few. \nNow, of course, cultures aren't static. Cultural views and ideologies change with the times, making\ncertain things obsolete.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lucienism", "Baudric von Aesterwald", "===================The Essential Handbook for the Followers of Saint Lucien de Savoie\n-\nDictating the Reformed and Reaffirmed Doctrine of Saint Lucien\n-\nRecorded by Pious Ser Baudric von Aesterwald\n==================\nLucienism -\n\nLucienism is the traditionalist-oriented branch of the Orenic faith that identifies with the theological teachings and theory of Saint Lucien of Savoie, or Velvyn Ashford. His teachings form the basis of the Lucienist sect of the Church of\nthe True Faith.\nIn its time, Lucienism was a radical and reform-oriented movement brought about by the remainder of the Order of the White Rose and various Kaedreni settlers. In its beginnings, the sect was only in the limited membership of those in the\nformer Kingdom of Kaedrin. There is no one man responsible for continuing on his teachings, and most were spread by word of mouth for nearly half-a-century after his death. Saint Hadrian of Leuvaarden, the Count of Sarkozy, was responsible for the\ncollection of Saint Lucien's works post mortem.\n-------------------The five basic principles to Lucienism are dictated around ones own belief and understanding of the Creator, and the instructions and guidelines He set forth for humanity to adhere to.\nI. Belief\nA follow must put his faith wholly in the Creator as the sole true God. Contrary beliefs to the principles of Saint Lucien must be removed like a malignant tumour - for surely if such a tumour was left to grow the results would be most\nunfortunate. Lucienists are fervent in their beliefs and are often hard to convince and convert. Their primary tool for conversion is intellectualism and zealous missionary effort, but they have been known to use the sword if absolutely necessary.\nII. Worship\nThe one true God, Creator of the universe and of man, must be worshipped according to the clergy's set guidelines and methos. This includes attending holy sermons whenever possible, usually once every Saint's Week, and through self-prayer.\nIII. The Study and Preaching of God's Word\n\nLucienists, being the most conservative sect of the Church of the True Faith, had in the past often encouraged fellow Lucienists to become respected clergymen. This, however, is not the only way in which\nthe faith may be observed. Lucienists, since their incarnation, were allowed to freely study the works of God and the Church at the beckoning of Saint Lucien. A cornerstone of Lucienist belief centers upon the individual's ability to freely read and\nexpress opinion on such matters.\nIV. Sanctity of Marriage and Appropriate Gender Laws\n\nThe sanctity of marriage must never be disturbed. Violations of the sanctity of marriage can include, but are not limited to:\n- Sexual or other carnal relations outside of marriage. (Offspring of such unions may be considered violations)\n\n- Sexual or other carnal relations with a nonhuman of the lowest degree, such as a Dark Elf or Orc.\n- Sexual or other carnal relations with a member of the same gender.\n\n- Adultery, impropriety and disloyalty to one's spouse\n\n- Carnal thoughts such as the coveting of another man's wife\nAdditionally, women cannot hold clergy or chivalric titles or inherit land titles unless no male alternative is available. Males, unless proved infirm and unable to inherit accordingly, always come first in the line of succession, by tradition.\nV. Veneration of the Saints\n\nIt is believed that those consecrated as saints are holy and exalted and as thus should be revered and spoken of in a light of praise and adoration. While they are of course second and inferior to God,\neach saint and their aspect can teach a valuable lesson about the ways of the world and theology in general.\nWhen there was a Pontificate, the High Pontiff acted as God's representative and was hence able to determine God's chosen. In the absense of the\nHoly See, the process of canonization ahs been halted until a new prophet is found to re-establish the Church.\nIn conclusion, the violation of these five pillars or \"rules\" is believed by Lucienists to remove one's self from the grace of God. While this stands, though, Lucienists believe in the forgiveness of sin, so long as they are not repeated.\nThe Rule of Lucien is further expanded upon by various lower guidelines. Although not as prominent, these guidelines are equally important for the Lucienist man and woman.\n1. The Sinfulness of Idolatry\n\nIdolatry is a sin and is absolutely heretical, promoting pagan ideologies instead of the grace of God. God is the only deity worthy of worship, as He is the only deity. The worship of saints or other idols is strictly\nforbidden. It should be noted that this takes primacy over pillar five - while saints can be venerated, anything bordering on idolatry  is a grave sin.\n2. Sacrilege\n\nSacrilege is a sin beyond no other sin and almost literally deadens the offender's soul. They are damned to burn in the fires of the stages of the Nether. To harm a holy individual, clergyman, or to harm something of holy value, as in\na house of worship or holy symbol is sacrilege.\n3. Lay Preaching\n\nWhile Lucienist clergy no longer exists, it is taught that the word of the faith can be spread and preached by any ordained or not. It is expected of all follows of the faith to spread the grace of God to any non-believers, in the\nattempt to enlighten them of their flaws and guide them to the True Faith.\n4. The Family of God\n\nAll who believe in the True Faith and adhere to the teachings of Lucien are part of the family of God. Those who know and spread the faith should be revered by their peers, ad they are who guide the future to the knowledge and wisdom\nof Lucien.\n5. Excommunications\n\nAfter the corruption of the Holy Mother Church during the Imperium Tertius, the act of excommunication is rare. Without the guidance of the Pontificate to separate apostates from the faithful flock, it is up to the individual to\ndetermine the intentions and beliefs of those whom they may communicate with and aid. Hence, it is impossible to conclusively determine the credibility of one's faith. But when definite doubt is cast, or when speaks openly against the Faith, it is the job\nof the faithful to give no service, no aid, and no gift but that of the word of God.\n-------------------\n\nIn the name of God, use this knowledge to preserve yourself and live within His grace.\n\nDeus Magnus.\nSpecial thanks to the Rovin-Winter Banking Company for investment into the research and preservation of this document and former documents. House Winter had nothing to do with the production of this document.\n------\nPublished 1551\n3rd Edition\n\nWritten with the use of the original doctrine of Saint Lucien of Savoie, as recorded by Saint Hadrian of Leuvaarden.", ""),
        StaticBook("M. Report #2112", "Vicelin", "19th Deep Cold 1550\n\nJosse of Ervemark\n\nToday I have lost two of my brothers. We met before the trials and faced all those dangers together. We feared, we cried and we bled together. We finally became fully fledged Hexers and then we were\nhired for this faithful contract.\n\nSeemed standad enough. Stolen children, reports of scary beasts. Nothing stood out about it.\n\nWe soon learned that the villagers knew of the source, a decrepit little hovel deep in the forest.\nOur training kicked in and we tracked down the cabin thanks to old tracks left by the beasts, and, once closer, the smoke rising from its chimney. We meditated until dark before ingesting our potions and springing into action. But even our stealthy\nadvance was detected by those... things. There were at least a dozen of them, growling and pacing around the perimeter. Some were more deformed than others, no two looked the same apart from the dorsal tubes protruding from their rears.\nThere was no entry in the beastiary for such creatures, so we treated them like we would a ghoul. Big mistake. Hamelin's sword was warped the moment he swung at one of them. His head was ripped off by its jaws in an instant.\nIn my battle trance it did not dawn on me that I had just lost such an old friend. I focused on the foes ahead of me and adjusted my tactics by swapping from aurum to steel. Jorge did the same.\n\nWe cut through most of them, the head and spine were th\nweaknesses, though the spine was often heavily shielded by carapace.\n\nJorge was wounded at this point, one of the beasts had broken his ribs with its tail. He told me to go and take care of the summoner and thus I did.\nI never saw him again.\n\nIn a stroke of luck the summoner was just about to exit the door when I blew it in with a kinetic hex, sending him reeling back and crashing into his cauldron, which injured him quite badly.\nI wrapped him in steel wire and rendered him unconscious before throwing him over my horse and galloping away. I heard Jorge's scream, but I could not turn back, there were five more of the beasts on my tail.\nI managed to evade them by daybreak and handed the Dwarf in to authorities. He's being sent to the peak aboard the Helderenberg.\n\nToo light of a damned sentence.", ""),
        StaticBook("Magic Dic. P1", "Lefkos Amethil", "-=- What is Magic? -=- \nIn essence, magic is a way to use ones mana to manipulate energy of that of a greater power. The more one uses their 'mana', the more it grows. This is added along with the fact that you may draw more energy from said higher\npower the longer you control it.\nWhat is the difference between Voidal and Deific Magics?\n\nAnother simple one. The Void has a different type of energy, used to create or control what you wish either before or after being summoned into our own realm. Deific magics use mana in order\nto control an Aengul or Daemons own energy, limited much more than Voidal, yet also free from several downfalls. It is hypothesized that AenguDaemons use voidal magic as well, put 'purify' it in a sense for their followers so they do not grow weaker.\n-=-=- Evocation -=-=- \nWhat is evocation?\n\nEvocation is generally the act of creating substances within the void using its massive energy, and then pulling it forth with your mana. You must fully understand the element you wish to summon as well\nas how the void functions. From then you will the energies to form into what you wish, and utilize your mana to pull it forth.\n-=Electric Evocation=- \nElectricity is a dangerous and unpredictable element, able to be controlled only by those with sharp and clear minds; any wavering will result in self harm. It is most often used in a 'bolt' form, being summoned electricity before\nones hand thrown at an opponent. The only ways to avoid getting shocked once attacked with it would be to have a ward, abjurgation, or three large pieces of metal in the close vacinity to redirect it. There are also streams of electricity, and lightning\nstrikes. The latter is usually thought of as one of the most powerful evocation spells.\n-=- Fire Evocation -=- \nFire is yet another mostly dangerous element, sucking our very oxygen to fuel its flames. The common use of this is for combat, along with electrical evocation. It is formed in spheres aimed for launching at people, yet may also be\nused for lesser tasks; such as lighting a fire or keeping warm. It is one of the most commonly used evocations, and the most rapidly spread.\n-=-Water Evocation-=- \nA lesser used evocation, sometimes thought of as a 'fluff' magic. Water is simple, yet along with its fluidity comes a great deal of flexibility when using it. About halfway through ones normal learning, they begin to cast ice,\nbeing when it is generally though of as just becomming useful. The most common use is a spike of ice used in order to launch at opponents, but making a shield or wall is also possible. A mastered may go so far as to make a small whirlpool for bashing foes\n-=- Air Evocation -=-\n\nThe least common evocation to see. Air evocation is difficult to master not because of the skill of the mage, but because of the very essence of the element. The hardest bit of such is studying the element at all, seeing as how\nair is invisible to the naked eye. This is the cause of Air Evocationists being unable to ever make a condensed and perfectly focused space of air. Air evocation is used in a variety of ways, be that using a sphere to bash someone back, a gust to keep a\nfoe at bay, or simply a light breeze. Mastered air evocationists are known for their ability to increase running speed for a short period of time, as well as jumping- using their summoned air to guide their movements.\n-=-Earth Evocation-=- \nA lesser used evocation. Earth Evocation users often are found to be one of the most potent in combat and in defense, capable of pelting boulders at their opponent as well as easily forming a wall for them to shield themselves\nbehind. It takes a great deal of mana, and a good amount of time to summon anything with Earth Evocation, but it often proves deadly when used in combat.\n-=Arcane Evocation=- \n\nA magic generally held with High Elves rather jealously, Arcane Evocation allows the user to summon pure voidal energy and form it into that of a crystal like method. Such can be shaped however they wish.\n-=-= Conjuration =-=- \nSimilar to evocations, Conjuration is casted just the same; create what you want in the void, and then summon it. The difference here is how such is learned. One must disect what they wish to summon, be it plant or animal. They must\nlearn how the creature functions and how it acts before ever dreaming of knowing enough to create one him/herself.\n-=-= Alteration =-=-\nWhat is alteration?\n\nIn general, I've always viewed alteration as using mana to change the world around us. Be that Telekinesis or Transfiguration.\n-=-= Telekinesis =-=- \n\nTelekinesis generally involves using ones own mana in order to manipulate the gravitational pull of an object. They create the mana anchor inside what they wish to control, and work from their.\n-=-Transfiguration-=-\n\nTransfiguration is split into three uses. Enchanting, Transmutation, and Warding.\n\nEnchanting consists of flowing mana through an object, keeping a small portion aimed to cast a certain and specific spell\nwhenever the enchantment would be activated. A 'mana gem' would be used to fuel it. Mana gems being often perfectly carved gems of any sort aimed to hold mana and keep an enchantment going.\n\nTransmutation is generally when one would coat an object\nin their own mana, and control properties of it much akin to how one may control their voidally summoned evocations- yet not generally for combat in this case. The main rule for this is that one can not create something of nothing, nor change one object\ninto another of a different state.\n\nWarding is generally enchanted onto objects, but can also be casted normally. It is a 'web' of mana placed before oneself aimed to catch hold of an oncomming spell, and send puffs of aura onto the mana anchor in order\nto get rid of it and return such to the void. Generally, a mage must fully understand the magic he wishes to ward, and must make one ward per magic guarded against. No ward may keep all magics at bay.\n-=- Voidal Shifting -=- \nNot many know too much of voidal shifting, but from an outsiders view it is theorized as a way to create a mana anchor elsewhere from yourself, and pull yourself momentarily into the void and out where the mana anchor was placed.\nSuch results in teleportation.", ""),
        StaticBook("Magic Square", "Gilondir Oronar", "Magic Square.\n-------------------\n\n 06 | 04 | 13 | 11\n -------------\n 15 | 09 | 08 | 02\n -------------\n 03 | 05 | 12 | 14\n -------------\n 10 | 16 | 01 | 07\n\n------------------- \n-->\nWhat is special about the square on the previous page is that in whatever way you count the numbers up in a row of 4, you will always end up with the number 34.\nThis symbolizes the balance that can be brought to the world if we would do...\n-->\n...everything properly.\nIf we wouldn't put one single number in a wrong spot this square is in total balance.\nThe same goes for our realm.\nBy: Gilondir Oronar\nPublished on:\nGrand Harvest 1601\n\n\n\nFood for the mind.", ""),
        StaticBook("Magic Trick", "Regale Ba'ikana", "Search for a mage who may be willing to share their knowledge with the warhawkes.", ""),
        StaticBook("Making AFAK", "Azuline Elverhilin", "--------------------Making a First Aid Kit-\n \n          By\n       Azuline\n\n\n\n\n\n\n6th of The Amber Cold,\n1572\nItems you would need:\n\n*Strips of cloth for bandage and cleaning wounds. \n\n*A vial of vodka to clean wounds, needles, and hands.\n\n*Gauze to use for putting herbs on the bandage.\n* Suture needles and a spindle of thread for stitching up deep cuts.\n\n* Water bottle. For mixing herbs into a solution or cleaning eyes...or even hydration. \n\n* Honey. Allows the medicine to go down easier and helps with\ncolds and with new borns to start feeding.\n\n* Medical mask. Well you don't want to inhale anything that can harm you...or allow any body fluids to come into contact with your mouth or nose.\nThis is only for performing first aid until real help can arrive. Please have someone contact a local healer when performing first aid  on a person. For any other questions, please ask your local healer.", ""),
        StaticBook("Malog's Proverbs", "Malog'Yar", "*As you open the book, you see in its pages a collection of proverbs written by the shaman, Malog'Yar. Each one is individually numbered for ease of memorization, it seems. They don't appear to have a particular order to them, but must\nhave been written as they came to the shaman's mind.\n((As in my other books, only those words which are actually different in the Blah are written in said language. The rest are in Common for ease of reading.))\n1. The lust for power is the beginning of shame. It concieves, agh brings forth arrogance. Arrogance, when it reaches maturity, brings forth humiliation.\n2. The path to honor is paved with sacrifice.\n3. Gold is a heavy thing, agh bub'hosh is the burden of he who hoards it. It will crush the heart, agh bring low the spirit.\n4.He who forgets his ancestors of the past will have nub future for his descendents.\n5.Food, water,\nshelter, praise; these things are wasted, when given to ash with nub honor.\n6.All is vanity for the whitewash, for he has abandoned his ancestors. He has departed from his purpose in life.\n7.Find that skill which lat are hosh\nat. Do it with honor, for even the lowly chicken farmer can glorify Krug.\n8.Do nub scorn simplicity. It is a chest of many treasures.\n9.Hosher is a small blarg with a mate agh cubs, than an empty palace.\n10.Integrity,\nuprightness, honor, discipline agh wisdom are the bones of the spirit. Without these, lat can nub stand.\n11.Hosher to be a blind man with wisdom, than a fool with many eyes.\n12.Luxury is an open gate to complacency.\n13.Complacency is a downhill path to the flatting of the spirit. It is an easy path to descend, but returning uphill is strenuous, requiring diligence.\n14.Bring up lats cubs in the way of honor, for they will take lats place in the future.\n15.This year's swineherd may be next year's Rex.\n16.Remember your brother, agh be quick to share his burden.\n17.The hardship of life is a refining furnace. It hurts for the time, but melts away that which would make\nlat weak.\n18.Lats body is a gift. Use it hoshly. Do nub abuse it, agh do nub let it wither.\n19.Life is work. Work hard or starve.\n20.The cactus lives in the dry sands, needing little nourishment. It is stronger for it.\n21.Hosher is a bag of rotten flesh, than a feast in the blarg of the dishonorable.\n22.He who lives with nub honor is like a fortress made of dry twigs or a zult made of still air: useless.\n23.Taking a title simply for the\nsake of taking a title is like eating sharp blades simply for the sake of eating.\n24.A wise blah is more precious than all the gems beneath the earth.\n25.Hosher to be flat for what is honorable, than to abandon lats\nintegrity simply to extend lats life.\n26.Honor is nub a currency to be traded. Do nub treat it as such.\n27.A life with nub honor is as meaningless as letters with nub order.\n28.Do nub tolerate weakness in body,\nmind or spirit.\n29.He who works hard will have his bread.\n30.Feeding the lazy is like planting the wind. It accomplishes nubthing.\n31.Be satisfied with food agh a cave. It may be all lat have ash day.\n32.Do nub envy the rich. Their decadence will make them soft.\n33.Do nub peep down on the poor. They are nub blinded by the glimmer of gold.\n34.Minas often do little more than disappear.\n35.Deceit agh\ntwisted words only clutter agh clog the mouth. Eventually, lat will choke on them.\n36.Blah what lat mean, nub what others want to hear.\n37.A slimy heart will slip, agh fall to its destruction.\n38.Rulers, do nub\nbe haughty. Ash day, lats reign will end, agh another will take lats place.\n39.He who is wise heeds the truthful rebuke.\n40.The fool surrounds himself with flatterers, agh is nub aware when ruin comes.\n41. Hosh is the\nfemale who bears strong cubs, agh works tirelessly to nourish their bodies agh spirits.\n42.Happy is the male who finds her, agh keeps her.\n43.Hosher is ash honorable lifemate, than ash hundred weak mates.\n44.As a blarg will\nnub stand on mist, cloud or vapor, neither will a family stand on weakness, foolishness of dishonor.\n45.Walk as Krug walked, agh honor the spirits. This will keep lats path straight.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mana and Gods", "Kirrekith", "Of Prayer and Mana\n\n   Kirrekith Reffahc\n~                        ~\n       A Summary\n\nBooks to complete the set.\n\nSaints and Paragons\nThe Four\nAenguls\nCreator, Yemekar & Gods\nA Beginning\n~                        ~\nThis book is a theory I have stress tested as best a man within an era of doubt can perform. I believe, the gods, aenguls and daemons all  aspects of mortal religion, are in essence, real. However\n--->\nthis theory isn’t based on who is true and false, it is on their birth and creation. I’ve lived across all our worlds, I’ve lived long enough to see the first of our kin, the original four.  I’ve witnessed the power of all the deities, and felt them \n--->\nrise and fall across the ages. So, let me begin this ideology.\n\nGods, and Aenguls have been around for a great deal of time, and we’ve felt their influence over the ages. However, after the numerous battles and strain they’ve put --->\non themselves in mortal confrontations I have to ask the question; how do they sustain themselves?\n\nClearly the aenguls don’t need water or food, so physical sustenance is impractical for them. So what else could --->\nfuel them? Mana? If so, how do they attain mana? They surely don’t surround themselves in it like mortals, or have mana pools like the Kharajyr. Perhaps it is given unto them from a creator or godlike figure, and so the question is    --->\nsnuffed before we can even explore it. Or, perhaps, prayer is the way the obtain mana to fuel their being.\n\nPrayer fuels them? It would explain why prayer is such a key focus of all the deities, and why they --->\nhold it close and will fight to have a larger stake in prayer. By this very concept though it begs the question, can anything given prayer then become, an Aengul? Well no, and yes. But I’ll explain the process later.\n\n--->\nThis would explain the rise of alternative deities after Asulon. With the fall of the only Aenguls we knew of in Aegis, new ideas and social norms came into being. The belief of the creator, Yemekar, the Brath, Aspectism and more commonly the       --->\nalternate Aenguls. With new faith in place, the channeling of prayer diverted from two thirds of key faith, and began to fuel the birth of the new Aenguls, the power of Yemekar and also the Creator. We can divert questions from true faith in this \n--->\ntheory, as all have presented their powers at different times, and all have valid claim to this.\nFrom Mortal to God\n~                        ~\nSo, let us discuss the power system of these entities, the top being the most powerful and the last the weakest in the chain.\n\n[Next Page]\nGod/Yemekar/Creator~                        ~\n   Aenguls/Daemons\n~                        ~\n        The Four\n~                        ~\n Dragons (not to be    mistaken for drakes)\n~                        ~\n   Saints/Paragons\n~                        ~\nA saint or paragon emerged after Asulon, and was the first indicator that mortals could forge their own deities by the channeling of faith and mana. Oren created saints, and their will and influence has been seen long after their deaths,\n--->\nthe same can be said of Paragons of the Dwarves, returning in the Dweds darkest hours to rally and guide them.  However these were never part of Aegis history, back on the homeland we had many honoured dead pass and be held highly by, though\n--->\nnone prayed to, not until Asulon. Likewise in Oren their religious society besides the Aengul Arial was non existent until Asulon, where Godfrey Horen reformed them and their religious belief, leading to the first of the saints, and then the first\n--->\nrecorded effects of those saintly individuals after death.\n\nDragons are a big uncertainty, I am still unsure as to whether to identify them as magic or natural, though if they’re of magic they can be placed in this      --->\nstructure, as I without doubt believe a saint could be crushed (likewise a paragon) by a dragon. These creatures could do with further study, however finding one content to talk to mortals besides to mess with them is difficult. These    --->\ncreatures aren’t directly prayed to like Saints, Aenguls or the Gods, however the fear and myths of them are pushed into a similar channelled system, pushing power onto them. Now I could be wrong, and they may be natural animals merely more attuned --->\nthan others to magic, but I do not believe in coincidence.\n\nThis area is unclear, are the original four now Aenguls, or are they beneath them? The first of each of the races these entities in early Aegis took on a great deal --->\nof our faith, like the first Aengul and Daemon to our homeland. These creatures lost a lot of their prayer once we came to Asulon, often more a kind word spoken at times, be that Krug, Horen, Urguan or Malin.  I am old enough to      --->\nremember Malin when he returned to us, and his appearance could be considered near saintly in approach, likely his mana far more than a saint and affecting how my vision was able to visualise him.  I believe like in the long since forgotten past they\n--->\nwill return to us once more, and guide us in our hour of need, though faith to them needs to be strengthened to allow for them to once again come back to us.\n\nAenguls need no explanation, they’re prayed to more than --->\nmost, and so have a bigger share of the magic channelled from mortals, these creatures seek the most prayer to fuel them, and so most of our cataclysmic events have been over a deities desire to be more powerful than its rivals.\n--->\nHowever the counter to this that may cause confusion, will be with Daemons. These creatures present  the opposite to Aenguls, and the minority of prayer, so how can they be powerful? These creatures must feed of the fears of    --->\nmortals and stories told of them, though I’d present another idea, what if prayer intent for good only helps to fuel evil? Due to them being specifically aimed to praise and state the holiness of an Aengul, it may be similar to an elastic effect, \n--->\nonly enficisizing the mal qualities of the counter partner, be that Daemon or Aengul, this in turn explaining how something so maleficent can be equal to its adversary.\n\nGods, Yemekar, Metztli and the Creator… All --->\nare unknown entities, their powers yet to be measured. The Dwed claim Yemekar created the world, Metztli made all animals and made the Kharajyr superior, the Creator claims to have made all, Oren viewing it to have made humans the --->\nrace to ascend past the others. We can imagine the powers of any of these, yet we’ll never likely grasp the scope of their abilities. It also adds another question, are others as powerful? Are their other worlds forged by creatures similar to\n--->\nYemekar or the Creator, do these entities fight between themselves, are their deities bigger than them?\nTo Conclude\n~                        ~\nThough this has been very brief, my further documents will do an indepth  study into each entity and their place within the power scape of faith and mana. Though I can outline for now, that these creatures\n--->\ndo have a scale to them, each with its own power created by mortals, as to what we do with this knowledge is for the reader to interpret and act upon. In time perhaps our Aenguls will ascend to Gods, and Paragons to Aenguls and new Saints rise -->\nup as more heroic and the best sides of the races is shown.", ""),
        StaticBook("Many Thanks!", "The Pale Brotherhood", "Hail friend. You have been visited by the forces of misfortune.\n\nThe Pale Brotherhood sends its regards.\n\nYour things have gone towards a good cause. That is our cause of course.\n\nWe thank you.\nBy blood and by smoke.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n                       D.G.", ""),
        StaticBook("Map for Traps", "Regale Ba'ikana", "Scout out a new city, listing its none residential buildings.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mardon Scouting", "Ikurza", "Heavily fortified gate, arrow roosts on both of the front towers. Each tower has a set of arrow roosts, only a small area of walls have scaleable areas, most are quite large, the towers are quite linear and unable to be climbed without a hook or ladder. A\nsmall fence rounds the farm area, but lacks any hefty defenses like the main city. The farm wall has a ridiculous amount of arrow roosts, but this wall in particular can be easily scaled. The gates also lead to a manor on a hill, it appears to be where\nthe nobility lives. Further investigation needed. The Manor is extremely large, its size compensates for its lack of foreseeable defenses, but archers could take roost upon the four balconies and release volleys of arrows. The right side has a small\nwindow, can be reached. The back of the manor has a large fence around it, very easily scaled. The left side is blocked by another wall, can be scaled. Thats all the exterior of the manor has, moving to the city now.\nThe back of the city has a large window, can be scaled. The back Gate is closed, but has less defenses and arrow roosts. The left wall has an open window, large enough to sneak into. But it has more arrow roosts. The front gate is a double door.\nThe barracks are just inside to the left, shops just to the right. An arena on the left side. A tavern on the right. Town hall in the back left. A church in the far back. The marshall is in the far back left, his house is highly defensible. There's a\nforge. The tax office is in the far back of the city. The slums are the back left. The Palace is at the back of the city, extremely large, defensible. At the moment, I see no guards, we're going to look at the barracks to see if we can get an estimated\nhead count. We were unable to get a headcount, however.", ""),
        StaticBook("Marked Combat I", "Sighard of Bastion", "~~~~~~~~~~~~~~        Marked Combat                    I: Basics             \n\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\n[!]\nhttps://goo.gl/8HBzAv", ""),
        StaticBook("Marked Combat II", "Sighard of Bastion", "~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\n     Marked Combat II\n  -Advanced Guards-\n\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\n[!]\nhttps://goo.gl/QAKd9J", ""),
        StaticBook("Masatur", "Casaga Ranhold", "23rd of Grand Harvest 1626\n\nToday I set off for the ruins of Masatur, deep within the golden sandy plains of the Easterwald.\nDespite it being only my second mission from Lyons, the difference between it and my first are worlds apart. From the biting\ncold of Huangdi, to the scorching desert ruins of Masatur. Rumour has it there's also a hidden fortress out there not a few days from the town. Perhaps I'll be lucky, and find it? \n                      -\n26th of Grand Harvest 1626\nAfter an\nuneventful three day trek, I've finally begin to catch a glimpse of the desert. Maker above, why did I have to make this journey in the summer months? The heat has left me sweltering. At least I've water supplies to be going on with. I just hope that when\nnight falls, it'll grow cooler, at least.\n                      -\n28th of Grand Harvest 1626\nHaving spent the past few days charting the ruins of the town, I can finally rest easy knowing that I've finished.  My wrist tired, and my eyes grew\nweary after that much work. Although now, I can properly explore the ruins of the town, without distraction.  \nWhat seemed to have been a great palace of sorts lays beneath an enormous mountain of sand. Though I could have sworn I'd caught a glimpse of\nlight coming from the buried building, my gut tells me anyone down there now wouldn't be in any sort of state to return to civilisation with me. \nTomorrow, at first light, I set off for the unnamed fortress.                        -\n1st of Sun's Smile 1626\n\nAfter careful and scrutinous surveillance of the location where the fortress is located, I've established that finding a way in is indeed impossible, at least by myself. Flags dangle limply from posts rising up to the sky, and it\nseems that no one- bad or good - is here to greet me. Though it certainly appears imposing, part of me remains glad that I cannot find a way inside.\nMaker only knows what I'd find.\n                       -\n\n\n- Casaga Ranhold", ""),
        StaticBook("Mask of Undru", "Zelak", "The Mask of Undru\n\nWritten down from the old ruins in Athera.\n\nBy a Fabulous Dark Elf.\nThe Mask of Undru is a artifact not from our worlds as we know it.\n\nIt's origin are from the world of Undru. An unknown realm where everything we know is upside down, nothing is reality.\nThe being that possessed the Mask was the Lord of Undru. An unknown person from another dimension who used this artifact to enter our world for a short period of time.\nIt's metall is thick and extremely hard, unable to be broken by normal force or even our most powerful magical abilities.\n\nIt has weird runes all over it's metall face, two eye holes and it represents an unknown animal.\nAfter the elves have defeated the Lord of Undru, the mask exploded and shattered. The tiny shards flew off into the world, far far away. Some collected the shards in Athera, even in Axios there can still be found these shards.\nThe shards would cause people to have different kinds of visions from a world with darkness and beings with white glowing eyes staring at the person having the vision.\nThe theory is that if somebody collected enough shards and the green gem from the inner workings of the original mask, the person would be able to recreate the Mask of Undru.\nCausing so would probably bring back the Lord of Undru into our world as it is unknown if he is still alive or not. It's unlikely that a powerful creature like he was died so easily.\nIf anybody ever tries to bring him back or recreate the Mask, be cautios as it is unknown how the results will be.", ""),
        StaticBook("May I Compare?", "Klaudia Crast", "-------------------\n\n\n    May I Compare?\n\nby Anonymous\n\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=\n\n\n\n-------------------\nTo compare you to a flower would be a tragedy,\nEven the most beautiful rose wilts and dies.\n\nTo compare you to anything beautiful would be a tragedy,\nYou are worth more than beauty.\nTo compare you to a book would be a tragedy,\nKnowledge is sometimes lost without rediscovery.\nTo compare you to anything would be a tragedy,\nYou are indescribable.", ""),
        StaticBook("Medical Guide,", "Eradan Dune`dien", "A Sutican Ranger`s\n        \n         Guide To\n \n\n\n     M E D I C I N E\n\n\n\n\n\n   By DT\nChapter one.\nAssessment\n\nThe key to any successful medical treatment is first identifying the problem.\nFrom arrow wounds to suturing the most important thing is getting a good idea of what your dealing with.\nDetermine if the wounded person is in pain, Adrenline can still be coursing through their body from the battle they just fought or they could be slipping into shock from bloodloss, infection or coma. Knowing how much pain a person is in key.\nThe next step is to check for pupil dialation pupil dialation is controlled by a series of muscles and nerves around the eye. Pupil dialation is found when a person has suffered a head injury or orbit trauma. Knowing will help you effectivly treat them.\nCheck the pulse of the wounded person, doing so will give you a general idea on how much blood theyve lost if any. Or what state the person is in. To check pulse put to fingers on the arteries below the chin on the neck, or the arteries on the wrist.\nSituation assesment. In the case of an active combat environment you should make sure the area where you are is safe enough to procede with treatment or if you will have to retreat.\nEven when not in combat, whatever harmed the person may still be around.\nChapter one checklist\n\n1. Pain\n\n2. Pupil dialation\n\n3. Pulse check\n\n4. Situation check\nChapter two.\n\nSterilization.\n\nThe most important step in treating someone is to help them rather than hinder, however infection of the wound will hinder alot more than it will help so sterilization is a key part in any medical procedure.\nWhen packing a medical kit you should include pre-sterilized tools, sterilized bandages as well as alcohol to clean wound and the area of the medical procedure, when about to begin a procedure such as suturing you should take a clean cloth,\ndouse it in alcohol and scrub the wound and the area surrounding the wound to prevent infection. The next step would be to use your sterilized tools. Then bandage with clean bandages. Key to after procedure treatment is cleanliness,\nyou should change any dressings and bandages at least twice a day. An operating table should always be clean and free of blood. As well as the recovery bed. Little contact with body fluids is paramount during patient\nrecovery.\nChapter three.\nAfter care\n\nAfter completing a medical procedure you should quickly usher the patient into recovery. Once in recovery the primary things a patient should be getting is food, water and care.\nWhen a patient is in recovery thet should NOT\nbe given any alcoholic drinks. During recovery the practice of sterilization should be assumed at all times. Bandages and dressings changed and constant attention will lead to a healthy and speedy recovery.\nWhen recovering its important the patient isnt in any risk. They should be looked after in a clean safe area with ample room between patients. This isolation prevents the spread of germs and disease while speeding the healing process.\nChapter four.\nGeneral lacerations\n\nLacerations or deep cuts are those which are caused by an accident in the kitchen or a slip of a knife. Regardless of the way the laceration was caused you must be quick to act. Blood\nis the bodies most important fluid,\nstopping the bleeding is your first priority when dealing with lacerations, you should take the time to Sterilize the wound and area before applying pressure on the wound with a clean bandage depending on the type of laceration it can require anything\nfrom a bandage, cauterization or suturing.\nThe most common is suturing. If you chose to bandage simply clean the wound again, apply fresh bandage and tell the patient to apply pressure for the time. If you chose Cauterization your first step is to clean!\nClean the wound again and switch out the bandages as you heat your cauterizing device. Once the cauterizing device is hot and ready for use its the delicate process of pressing the hot metal into the wound fusing any torn blood vessels and stopping the\nbleeding. This is reccomended when  used on small veins and should be avoided when the laceration is in a delicate area. Your third and final option with lacerations is to suture.\nWhen begining to suture you should first make sure your tools are clean.\nOnce you are ready clean the wound once again and begin, push the needle through one side of the skin of the laceration and across to the other, pulling it through and then tight. Repeat the process in a criss cross pattern until the wound is closed.\nthe next step is to tie the suture and cut the needle away. Once this is done you clean the wound yet again and apply bandages.\nWith this you have sucessfully stopped the bleeding and can safely move the patient to aftercare. If bleeding resumes reassess.\nChapter five.\nArrow wounds\n\nContary to popular belief arrow wounds are some of the most deadly wounds you can recieve when in battle.\nThe main problem with this is the fact that arrowheads that become disconnected to the shaft in turn become lodged.\nOnce lodged the head of the arrow will not be pushed out by the body leading the area to become inflammed and infected. Step one in any arrow wound treatment is to establish what type of situation you are dealing with.\ntwist the shaft extremly lightly to determine if the arrow head is disconnected or connected to the shaft. If disconnected you can simply remove the shaft and set to work dealing with the arrowhead. If still connected it becomes a more delicate process.\nOnce your ready to procede clean the area of the wound with alcohol and make sure you have clean tools. Pain relief herbs and potions are reccomended as a tense patient can cause a muscle contraction that can worsen the wound.\nNow that the patient is ready you should carefully take a surgical knife and at the bottom and top of the wound make a small cut allowing you to spread the wound. Once spreading the wound carefully taking mind not to hit any blood vessels.\nThen carefully remove the arrow head if disconnected or head and shaft if the head is still connected. You should remove all foreign debris before cleaning the wound once again. You should then suture and bandage before moving patient to after care.\nChapter Six.\nFever and infection\n\nFever and Infection are some of the most dangerous illnesses contracted. A fever is the bodies heigtened temperature ussually caused by the bodies response to infection.\nWhen dealing with a fever it is important to keep the body cool. Wetting a cloth and pressing it to the forehead is good treatment, you should also have them drink lots of water along with hot fluids such as tea or coffee.\nThe best foods to feed someone with a fever and infection are hot soups and stews. Its important to keep a healthy balanced diet when in aftercare. The body should be able to fight of most infections by itself.\nHowever if it looks as if the patient will die you should immediatly intervene and give them the more valube medical herbs and or potions of healing. If all else fails its important to contact a mage or ascended. Its important to seek help when needed!", ""),
        StaticBook("Medical Std. V1", "The Scribe.", "A Study of Medicine. \nPossibly Volume 1,\nAnatomy.\n\nAssisted by Salazar the Mali'Aheral.\n'One learns to figure out the limits of one's body as one travels.' ~Salazar.\n\nThe cadavers of less fortunate travelers make for good study; \nTheir bodies can be found suffering from illnesses or ailments,\nand studying them was an urge that could not be repressed.\nChapter 1: Circulation of Blood.\n\nThe circulatory system runs throughout the body. It provides blood to various muscles and organs, replenishing them with every beat of the heart. Blood provides Anima to the body, which is the life\nforce that drives us all, and gives us energy. Anima may possibly contain a variety of elements that we require to function in life. As one works more strenuously, the circulatory system does as well. This may be because as the body\nuses energy, it requires more anima to function at the previous levels.\nAilments of the Circulatory System;\n\nA disease of the circulatory system causes the blood to not coagulate where it may normally do so. This causes the patient afflicted with such a disorder to bleed profusely, until magic is introduced.\nThis disease is highly rare.\n\nAnother disease is caused by the blood vessels themselves being left open to the air or to water for a long time. This causes blood-poisoning, an infection of the blood itself.\nThis can be treated a variety of ways, though none of which are easy. Amputation is the most rudimentary form of this. The next is quick cleansing of the wound with pure water and alchemically based healing potions. The third is to use clerical magic, but\nsuch can be considered a panacea.\n\nThis condition is uncommon, as most open wounds are wrapped.\nChapter Two; Digestive.\n\nThe digestive system consists of the stomach, smaller intestine, large intestive, and the excrementory system. \n\nThe stomach contains within it a large amount of naturally produced\nacid which dissolves food that is consumed through the mouth. After the food is broken down, it seems to be passed through both intestines, where it is absorbed into the body. Of course, these broken down materials most likely filter into the blood system\nwhere the blood and the body converts the food to anima. This is why death by starvation can take place. However, the body itself can overstock these necessary nutrients, and cause fatness and obesity.\nDigestive diseases. \n\nThe stomach acid can become too concentrated and can break down the lining of the stomach. This can cause severe pain. The most common cure for this is the direct consumption of tippens root, as it brings the pH of\nthe stomach acid down to natural levels. \n\nAnother digestive issue is an illness that causes constant vomiting, or constant bowel movements. While vomitting is the bodies natural defense against bad food, this vomit is\nvery common even with high quality food. The best way to deal with this sickness is to consume mass quantites of salted water, as such replenishes the bodies well-being after their body exterts it. Sugar should also be added to the water.\nThis disease can be common or rare depending on its severity. \n\nOne may also vomit if they exert themselves to a point of exhaustion.\nChapter 3; Respiratory.\n\nThe Respiratory system contains the lungs and the windpipe. The windpipe is located behind the thick protective cover at the front of the throat. The lungs suction air from the environment\ninto them through use of a muscle underneath the lungs called the diaphragm. The lungs expand to hold the inhaled air, and then the air is pushed out. However, the body somehow utilizes the gas, most likely to produce more efficient anima.\nIf blood is deprived of the air, it turns purple and sickly looking. When re-exposed to the air, it turns bright red. My belief is that blood uses one of the gases that comprises air to convert nutrients into anima.\nBetween both of our knowledge, no diseases or ailments of the respiratory system were known, but the respiratory system can be damaged through the inhalation of coal fumes, causing 'Black-Breath'. This cannot be cured except by\nthe magic of the monks.\nChapter 4; Muscular.\n\nThe muscles are connected to the bones by tendons, stretchy bits of yellow 'flesh' that allows for the full range of motion. The muscles of the body tend to work in groups of two, one to push, and the other to pull.\nThe larger the muscle, the greater the force exerted by the movement of the muscle. As muscles are damaged through use, they heal and grow larger, thusly more powerful. This is why one is sore after strenuous exercize.\nNo ailment of the muscle is cureable. The degrading of muscle is either through use of magic, or by some ailment. Both of these result in the eventual loss of the majority of muscle.\nThere appear to be three types of muscle, involuntary, mixed, and voluntary. Involuntary muscles can not be controlled at all, such as the heart or the digestive muscles. \n\nMixed can be controlled some of the time, but for the\nmost part, operate on their own. This includes breathing, blinking, and swallowing. \n\nVoluntary muscles are only able to be used with input, such as the arms or the legs.\nChapter Five; Bones.\n\nThe bones are the structure of the body. They also offer protection. Bones are incredibly dense, most are stronger than steel. The amount of bones in a body is 206, with six tiny, tiny bones in the ears that some\nhow are linked directly to balance. The bones can heal themselves if set correctly, though clerical magic allows this process to finish faster.\nThere are no known ailments of the bone.\nAddendums; \nBruises are the pooling of blood from broken blood vessels.\n\nThe heart is an involuntary muscle.\n\nThe circulatory system involves vessels that take blood to the heart to replenish in anima,\nand arteries that take anima-enriched blood away from the heart to the muscles and other parts of the body.", ""),
        StaticBook("Medicine Vol. 1", "Kale", "A Dose of Solace\nVolume 1\n\nWritten By: Dr. Kale\n\n\nIn this book you will find effortless remedies to common day sicknesses.\n1. Stomach Pain\n\n1 cup of hot chamomile tea\n3 peppermint leafs\n\nPlace the leafs on top of the hot steamy cup of tea and drink it twice a day to ease the churning pain.\n2. Flashes (Fever)\n\n4 rags\n1 big bucket of cold water\n\nSoak the rages with water and place each rag on the forehead, under each arm pit and behind the neck. Do so as needed until it subsides.\n3. Burning Throat\n\n1 hot cup of water\n5 drips of honey\n2 tsp. of sugar\n\nMix it all together thoroughly and sip until your throat begins to feel soothing.\n4. Coughing\n\n1 rag\n1 pot of boiling water\n\nPlace it rag over your mouth and bend your head close enough to the steam and breathe it in. Do this until your coughing has calmed itself.\n5. Ear Pain\n\n   Warm up oil until it is at a lukewarm feel and pour it into the ear. Lay on the opposite ear that isn't causing you pain. The pain should stop momentarily.\n6. Bruises \n\nThere's multiple remedies to heal a bruise quickly \n\n1st- Cold Herbal Tea Compress\n2nd- Ice Block compress\n3rd- Cold Poppy Tea Compress\n7. Rashes\n\nCacutus Paste\nHerbal Tea\n\nMash the two together until it become a thick like paste. Rub the paste on the rash until it drys or can be scraped off.\n*Here would be a sketch showing how to make the rash paste*\n\n\n*Here would be a sketch showing how to apply a compress to a bruise*\n\n*Here would be a sketch showing how to make different teas*\n*Here would be a sketch showing how to mix honey with tea* \n\n*Here would be a sketch of a person breathing in the steam with a rag covering their mouth*\n\n[!] The sketch would be drawn very well", ""),
        StaticBook("Melunis the Mad", "Sofetios Jayamen", "Melunis the Mad\n\n             ~By Sofetios\n                  Jayamen\nI was travelling to a place called Geedin in search of a philosopher called Melunis. This man was said to be the wisest in the land, according to my friend who went there a while back. So I figured I would see what wisdom he could offer me.\nI arrived in the town and walked up to a guard. I said,\n\n“Excuse me sir, could you direct me to the man named Melunis?” The guard just laughed and said,\n“The only Melunis I know is a madman, you can find him over by the alley.” I thought to myself, this madman couldn’t be the one that my friend was talking about. My friend was a very smart person and wouldn’t confuse a wise man with a madman.\nSo to satisfy my knowing of whether or not this was the correct man, I went to the alleyway and saw an old man in rags. I said to the man.\n\n“Are you the man known as Melunis?” to which he looked at me, smiled, and said,\n“Indeed so! How can I help you this fine day!” The day was overcast, and cold. I couldn’t see how it was fine.\n\n“Would you happen to know my friend, James Linson?”  The man looked overjoyed at the name and said,\n“Why yes! I remember the lad. I do hope he is doing well.”\n\n“Well he told me you were the wisest in the land…” I said, to which Melunis replied with a frown,\n“Wise? No I’m not wise. I am just a ragged old man in an alleyway. Do I look like I’m Wise?”\n\n“No you don’t.” I said. “You don’t look wise at all.” The man then got a sparkle in his eyes.\n”Not wise at all?” said the man. “Well I must agree. You being very well off must know what it means to be wise, as you are in a far better place then I.” The man thought for a second, then said, “Tell me, what does it mean to be wise.”\n“Well it means that you know a lot about things.” I said, “Like a smith knows how to smith well, he is wise in smithing.”\n\n“Ah..” said the man. “ So in a sense, being wise is having knowledge.”\n\n“Yes.” I said.\n“ Alright, well what is knowledge.” Said Melunis. “As in how would I be able to obtain it to become more wise.” I thought for a second, then said,\n\n“Knowledge is knowing something, and obtained by learning.”\n“I see!” Said the man. “But… how do I know I know something.” At this I was a bit perplexed. I said,\n\n“Could you explain what you mean?” The man nodded and said,\n“In other words, How do you know what you know is true.”\n\n“Because you know it works when applied to things.” I said.\n“Alright.” Said the man. “So knowledge is what you know works when applied to things, but how does that make it true?”\n\n“What do you mean?” I said.\n“I mean for example, we could see an abstract painting.” Said Melunis. “You could think it’s one thing, I could think its another. Both works when applied, at least to the individual, but who is right?”\n“I guess we would both be right.” I said. “It would be up to the individual.”\n\n“Ah I see now!” Said the man. “So knowledge is what works with the individual. But still, how do we know it is true? Because isn’t the truth universal?”\nI began to get frustrated. But… Then I came to a realization.\n\n“I guess the truth is up to the person.” The man looked at me and said,\n“So there is no real truth? But what about common knowledge?” I thought for a second and said,\n\n“The common knowledge is what people have a common belief in.” Melunis nodded, seeming to understand.\n“But then what is the real truth?” The man said.\n“The real truth…” I began, “The real truth is… unknowable. Even if we got to it, people still would have different opinions. And those opinions might have more people believing in it than the real truth.” Melunis smiled at me and said,\n“Thank you, I think I understand now what it means to have knowledge.”\n\nSoon after this conversation, I left. Slightly confused, but more understanding.\nThough now I realized what my friend meant by him being the wisest in the land.\n\nThe End\nThank you for taking the time to read this book! I will be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing, please tell me by bird or in person!\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen", ""),
        StaticBook("Memorabilia", "Illanoria Calithil", "Book Request\n       Illanoria Calithil\nDaughter to the late Ariadni Lanyathir, I request ownership of my mother's book, \"Druidic Studies\". This can be located in the Druidism section on the second floor. I am happy to provide books for a copy to be made to replace this book, however I wish\nfor the original book located in your collection to be returned to my family, as it is one of her few possessions that must be treasured in her memory.\n   Kind Regards,\n       Lady Illanoria\n(( LadyAmaya_ ))", ""),
