        StaticBook("Sorcerer Types", "Gerald Elgan", "===================                                                                                             Sorcerers: Recognizing the Various Varieties\n\nTranscribed by\nGerald Elgan\n\n==================\nAmongst those who possess distinctly closer bonds to the void, a few different kinds have been recognised. This short excerpt will attempt to bring the reader through these varied distinctions, and hopefully provide aid in attaining some small measure of\nenlightenment in the subject matter.\n===================Types outlined:\nThe Common Robed Mage: Page 4-7\nHousehold Mage:\nPage 8-13\nBattle Mage:\nPage 14-22\n===================\nThe Common Robed Mage\n==================\nThe typical conjurer, often accompanied by a staff- draped in long cloaks, adorned in all manner of strange runes and odd colours, is often depicted as an old man with long, grand beards and a vast\n==================\n-reservoir of knowledge esoteric. There is no doubt that this variety is the most renowned and favored. The Common Mage is often the most well-versed in the forbidden art of magical combat- with stronger spells and\n===================\n-hexes, and (depending on the proficiency of the caster) all the more perilous.\n\nHowever, with these strengths, come weaknesses, for the Common Mage is often by tradition, frail and\n===================-lacking in physical prowess- endurance, hardiness and such- due to the almost fanatical obsession that they so often possess with the search and acquisition of more knowledge, and in turn, more power of origins profane.\nHousehold Mage\n===================The common Mage's humbler counterpart of lesser renown, the Household Mage could very well be the lowly servant a small measure too efficient at the task of cleaning, or the unassuming cook who heats the furnace a moment-\n===================\n-too quickly.\n\nThe subtlety of the magic these beings possess and and put into practice makes them quite difficult to discern. One could, perhaps rightly, assume that such beings might hardly\n===================-pose any considerable amount of threat, due to their lack of experience and utility in the field of combat.\n\nHousehold Mages can be either shunned members of the secluded Mage's Guild\n===================-or apprentices who, having acknowledged their inferior ability or lack of talent, had decided to follow an entirely different path, and seek a livelihood in a different environment. \n\nTheir spells are most\n===================\n-often simple and pose little to no danger- these sorcerers are seen as more benevolent than the common Mage, and physically more robust; though such things are quite difficult to confirm, given the elusive and subtle-\n===================nature of the mages in question.\n(Witch Maid/Mage of Cleaning.)\nThe Battle Mage\n===================The rarest and least liked of the known types- The Battle Mage is seen as the bane of chivalry and honorable duels, with little care for learning more of their art. The Battle Mage is more often spotted in places where\n===================-war and conflict are as commonplace as frost on a cold winter's morn. He is often the result of a fighter who, through rigorous and excruciating training, has learnt both the way of the sword and the art of magic-\n===================-applying both theories and practices in harmonious synergy. The existence of this being suggests the existence of some clandestine groups or orders that have handpicked and set aside some select candidates for the\n===================-rigors required to achieve this balance.\n\nThe training of a Battle Mage is the stuff of tales and legends- often known to be one of, if not the most dangerous path to the art of the arcane. Apprentices that were\n===================-found to have been fit candidates were often heard to have died of various accidents-including but not limited to: Death by fire and cinders, of spell failures and eternal sleep, of ice and water, and of broken shards of\n===================-marble or glass. The fortunate few- if one could call them such- that did manage to live through the horrors of their training and ire of the common folk, quite often found themselves unable to resume the lives they one led, now bent\n===================-purely on suffering and death as heralds of doom and ruin.\n\nBattle Mages are often not as proficient in the art of magic as their more focused counterpart: the common Sorcerer. However, their skill-\n===================with and mastery of the blade may, at times, more than compensate for this lack of further magical prowess and potency. Hence, it would be most unwise for the regular mage to step within range of the Battle Mage, for the Battle-\n===================Mage, neither frail nor unfit, could quite as easily close the distance, and deal a devastating blow.\n(Warrior-Witch/ Spellblade/ Witchman /Mystic Knight. )", ""),
        StaticBook("Sorcerer's Book", "cyber_king3", "===================     The Book of the\n        Sorcerer\n===================\n===================The First Edition of the Book of the Sorcerer, penned by Laethis Izalith upon the 24th of the Amber Cold, 1568.\n===================\nThe Arcane Dictate serve as the pillars of a Sorcerer’s life. Honor, militance, scholarship, loyalty, eradication and the void, six principles which define the path that each Sorcerer follows. Through these, each Sorcerer stands apart from other mages,\nmore righteous and devoted to the void and its proper usage.\n~ The Dictate of ~\n           ~ Honor ~\n   ===============\nThe Sorcerer wields the arcane with honor and righteousness.\n\nTo repudiate the amorality of the void and its boons.\n\nThey shall protect the innocent and aid the needy.\n\nHolding true to all their oaths and vows.\nTo uphold their morality in service of creating a better world.\n~ The Dictate of ~\n       ~ Militance ~\n  ===============\nThe Sorcerer must begrudge the use of voidal violence.\n\nYet wield it confidently when their ferocity is just.\n\nTo deny the powermonger and the upstart.\nRisking their lives in devotion to their honored arcane probity.\n~ The Dictate of ~\n     ~ Scholarship ~\n   ===============\nThe Sorcerer will go beyond militance and devote themselves to scholarship.\n\nExpanding knowledge upon the void and other pursuits.\n\nTo serve as learned authorities upon various expertise.\nFor unlearned mages shall be admonished and scorned.\n~ The Dictate of ~\n         ~ Loyalty ~\n   ===============\nThe Sorcerer gives their life to their Order and its cause.\n\nServing their ruler dutifully and rightfully.\n\nNever shall they harm their brothers and sisters of the Silver Sun.\n~ The Dictate of ~\n     ~ Eradication ~\n   ===============\nThe Sorcerer is bestowed with a noble duty.\n\nFor the noble understand the plague of the rogue.\n\nThe unfit use the arcane with selfish and reckless intent..\nEmploying persuasive tongue or rending spell.\n\nThe Sorcerer shall eradicate these most perverse of mages.\n~ The Dictate of ~\n        ~ the Void ~\n   ===============\nThe Sorcerer shall become a master of voidal practice.\n\nTo preach its every value and encourage its use.\n\nIndoctrinate others into the path of connection with the arcane.\nTo hold oneself free of vile godly magics and renounce their loathsome usage.\n\nAnd decry the use of darker arts and their repugnant power.\n\nFor we are of the void, enlightened by its endless offerings.", ""),
        StaticBook("Soul Puppetry", "Fury_Fire", "The following pages in this book cover a sparsely known Dark Magic known as Soul Puppetry. If this book is found by anyone, it is likely stolen and should be placed somewhere safe and where none will find it.\nSoul Puppetry, unlike common belief, is not used to control people in any way, sense, or form. There are no set list of spells, and there are drawbacks on the user. The closest control goes is forcing emotions upon people to the point that they may act\nupon such. Mages who practice this will notice a percentage of what they cause coming back upon them in return.\nA Soul Puppeteer begins by first learning how to make basic puppets, mimicking the race of the target they wish to inflict their magic upon. These puppets would begin by the molding of clay, making the basic shape of the person. Normal humanoid for\nhumans, larger puppets for Orcs. Kharajyr would have ears and a tail. Elves just having ears. Once the clay is shaped, the Puppeteer wraps a layer of moss around it. These signify the 'veins' of the target. After, a layer of hay is wrapped around it all\nto both act as the 'skin', and keep it from falling apart. The next bit is when it gets tricky though. The Puppeteer must find a way to extract the blood of the person who they wish to inflict. Such can be done however the puppeteer wants to do such.\nWhen the blood is got, even so much as a drop, the puppeteer would push eye sockets in and place the blood in each. They would take hold of the puppet and take a breath, holding it into their chest. They would invision the person that the puppet will be\ntied to, imagining him or her as the puppet, before letting the breath escape them and imagine it flowing into the puppet. The connection would be made, and the target would feel a dark presence awakening within them.\nThe next step would be complicated. In the end, it would also be similar to the first bit. The Puppeteer would imagine either an emotion or feeling- likely starting with something along the lines of anger. They would take a breath and hold it, imagining\nthe very concept of the emotion, before releasing it and willing it into the puppet, likely holding and squeezing its head whilst doing such. Holding the head allows for ease when doing mental curses. As a begginer, the Puppeteer would only make the\ntarget mildly annoyed, whilst recieving about half of said anger back upon themselves. Other emotions are easily practiced and learned even without the teachers guidance. Later in learning, the Puppeteer may learn how to inflict pain upon their targets as\nwell as horrible curses. Anything the puppeteer can think of can be done, and the more skilled they get the less drawback they recieve. Lower skilled Puppeteers would only hold the connection for twenty elven minutes. Higher skilled could hold them for\ndays on end, casting different curses upon someone, although only one at a time. Eventually, the Puppeteer is taught how to create new curses, which is when it is harder to tell what is happening with their victims.", ""),
        StaticBook("Southern Beauty", "Nazorean", "Southern Adventure:\n\nAs I wrote my last journal, I left off in the north, or at the end of the world and out of it. (See 'To The Sanctum' if in your area, I cant even find it). I will be starting from the north, in a different area now. I am in Urugan,\nhome of Miners and Forgers. \n\nI. Walk:\n\nI decided I would walk the length of Tahn down to the south... Maybe perhaps in another journal I will go to the different isles, but for me it is a long walk now, and the sun is going out, perhaps some mina\nis to be taken from the monsters about.\n\nI walk through the desert, next to the frigid dwarven city... It is funny how the sandy mountain became stony and cold in just a few miles. Across a short river, almost nostalgically taunting me to cross it,\na frigid sand plain awaits. The path leads me on and a daunting shadow rears above me, it is a floating city... How odd...\n\nA vigiliant figure stands of stone and lapis, protecting its city far above the clouds and in the eye of the sun, it awaits for its\ncall to battle I assume.\n\nThis city, it was ripped from the ground itself it seems... I climb the spire to see what lies ahead, there be a mere sign asking me to send a bird to a council member for entry... I will ignore this for now, perhaps later I will\ncome back! I found a base for the orcs I believe... Tusks greeting me at the entrance, cacti guarding its front! Nobody was home.\n\nI see a glimpse of a cavern in the distance, just straight to south... I hope it goes through this sandy mountain. A dried\nup well sits next to it.\n\nThe cave was dug out... Everything in it gone, there seems to be a pattern around this area, probably the dwarves... They like to carve things out.\n\nThe mountain ranges, they never end... I keep on climbing the mountain top\njust too see another one beyond me, maybe I will be at the other end of the ocean by the time I'm done. I think I'm about to reach the last mountain top, I can feel it!\n\nYes! I finally see some hotter plains from below me, rustling leaves in the wind.\nWow! Who could have thought there could be such sweet air when you came down from a mountain, this area is so green and flat... It goes on for miles but beyond the river it goes into a darker green. The river is flat and calm, I see a tower across from it\nI could sit here for hours and admire the beauty of this area! Wow! What a site to only really experience if you were here\n\n*A sketch would be drawn of a beautiful and sprawling castle behind a calm and serene lake*\n\nThis castle seems to have been\nabandoned from wartime... I see stables in the courtyard, but the keep itself has been blocked up with haybails. A war time building that will sit here until reclaimed, it seems. I wonder by who, if I were older I would do it myself!\n\nThe south; An area\nstill gripped and fresh with time of war... So much to be cleaned up. I see battlefields strewn here now, and trenches from the fights.\n\nJohannesburg lie ahead of me, the now thanium pit of a city that had given oren the final nail in their coffin!\nI doubt this area will ever be trekked by a person again... This area will remain forever dead, it seems.\nII Lake:\n\nThe lake I see now, it sits their calm and thinking with the trees around, it does not know and it does not care. The city behind it, frozen and deadly, though it still lingers with health and prosperity. \n*A sketch was drawn of a peaceful\nlake, trees surrounding it, but off in the background was a wartorn and destroyed city, with a setting sun in the sky.\n\nNow more to the south, lay beautiful farm land, apple orchards, pigs, cows, and horses about... Eating and living and just near them\nwere vineyards and farms.\n\nEven more south I venture, into the dirty and darker swamp grounds, it grows darker now. I now see a small outpost of civilization in  this rough swamp, A place called Aldersport, the frontdoor locked, rightfully so. The\ntrees begin to look like beasts to me, but friends almost now, guiding me and keeping me safe through my path... They have a shape to them, of hands and a body.\n\nIII Manor:\nIt grows dark now... I am finished with the swamp and the mountain grows but now\nbetween the swamp and the mountain, there is a house.. I follow the road and I smell wine... And grapes... As I go through the arch I see grapes now.. Horses, wind.\n\nAmazing. I got in... Or broke in more like, this house is so very nice. But something\nabout it has me off and spooked... So much dreary imagrey here. \n\nOh no... I think I am trapped here for now, there are things about outside, the creatures that come out at night in the swamps, I think it is best I keep exploring this house for the time\nbeing, though I hear the rattle of bones about.\nI am going to leave early now... It is far too creepy here for me, I do not want to stay in this eerie place.\n\nI have reached the end here... The sun has set and the sun has rised, and there might be\nsome more too see later, but I have travelled my map.\n\n~ Nazorean.", ""),
