        StaticBook("Spirit Smithing", "Thurak'Yar", "Spirit Smithing\n(Ilzgûl Udalgum)\n\n     [======]\n     [==|  |==]\n         ==\n         ==\n        |.--.|\n         ==\n         ==\nThe original piece was written by the Spirit Smith Gukdank’Raguk, of Angathgul, and was Rescribed by the Farseer Thurak’Yar.  Modifications and corrections of the text still maintain the truth of  the tale it tells.\n~Spirit Smithing~\n\n\nThe creation of items blessed or cursed by the spirits themselves. \nThe creation of these items is difficult and strenuous, and only the true dedicants who have acquired the knowledge of this art\nmay forge successfully the boons of the Spirits above.\nThe story of how this concept came to be is an interesting one indeed, spanning back years untold to a different land and time. If you have come to read this book in hopes of learning the secret behind the art, then I ask that you either put away this\nthis tome of listen very closely.\nAn unwitting Shaman exploring the Immortal Realm on his daily search for Enlightenment would one day stumble upon a very particular Spirit. Sitting, as the Shaman always did, in a open field of bountiful grass,\nit didn’t take very long indeed for him to notice something very out of place; Gentheruz, the Spirit of Smithing. The being was legless, old, and worn. He didn’t look as the Shaman called his name and instead began to mutter.\nBefore him a great black anvil wrestled its way out of a sea of grass, and upon it sat a simple, glass orb. Enraptured, the Shaman strode forward. \n“By what manner did you make this?” The Shaman asked,\npeering over his shoulder. Without a word Gentheruz continued, waving calloused hands about his creation with eyes screwed shut. Waves of power crested and broke within the amber orb and the Shaman, bewildered, stood back.\n“Shakgriig’Gund,” The Spirit named it, bowing his great head. The stone, he explained, would enable a Shaman to invoke his blessings or curses into tools which others of the mortal realm could use.\nLikewise it would allow other Spirits to work with him, to forge items of great variety and power.\n“This is mine, however,” Gentheruz wheezed, in a voice not attuned to talking. \"Yours will be connected to this- the progenitor stone- as will all others\nWith that decree the Shaman was imparted with a schematic which he guarded for many years and, later, as did his sons and their own. The knowledge of the creation of the Mortal Stone, the Matûrz’Gundh, has stayed\nwith this band of Uruks since then, and has allowed them to wield weapons of death and tools and virtue as boons and as heralds of the power of the Spirits themselves.", ""),
        StaticBook("Spirit Smithing", "Elder Gukdan'Raguk", "Spirit Smithing,\nIlzgûl Udalgum.\n\nThe creation of items\nblessed or cursed by the Spirits themselves. \nThe creation of said items is hard and strenous. With only the true dedicants who have aqcuired the knowledge are able to forge such items.\nThe story of how this concept came to be is an interesting one, and I am willing to tell you it. If you have come here in hopes of learning the secret behind the art, then I ask that you put away this tome, and give up on your search, or listen closely.\n~The Backstory~\n\nAn unwitting Shaman, exploring the Immortal Realm on his daily search for Enlightenment, would stumble upon the Immortal Spirit, sat as he always was, in an open field of bountiful grass.\nIt appeared to the Shaman, an unusual item, the likes of which he had never seen.The Shaman approached, questioning the process. Gentharuz ignored him for a time, and when he was done, he presented the final product to the Shaman.\nIt was a glowing amber orb, with a sea of power crashing within. The Shaman grasped it with bewilderment as Gentharuz spoke to him. He explained that this stone, the Shakgriig'Gund, would enable the Shaman to invoke his blessings or curses into tools.\nThat of which others of the mortal realm could use, through the will of Gentharuz and those Spirits that agreed to work alongside him. But the Shaman would have to make his own stone, for this was the progenitor stone,\nthe one from which all mortal stones would be connected.\n\nAnd so, left with the departing Shaman, was the knowledge Gentharuz offered him. The process of how to make the Matûrz’Gundh, or, Mortal Stone.\nThat is the story of the concept known as Spirit Smithing, I hope that you take this knowledge and keep it locked within your mind, perhaps one day you too may wield the blessing of Gentharuz.\n\n- Gukdan'Raguk,\nSpirit Smith of Angathgul.", ""),
        StaticBook("Spirit Tales #1", "Shagarath", "Tales of Spirits\n\n\"Through the Fields\"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n~By Shagarath\nThe orc inhales the fresh air, his arms widened as his wrinkled body catches the breeze around him. With closed eyes he enjoys the surroundings before opening his eyes, narrowed at first but soon opened fully.\nIn awe the uruk stares at the endless fields surrounding him. Off in the distance a large mountain range blocked the view of the horizon.\nAbout the fields of crops masses of stone, carrying small bodies of water atop of them were.. Floating. Slowly the elder limbed forth towards one of them, a flow of water coming down, starting one of the many rivers that broke up the landscapes fields.\nThe shaman ventures on through the farmlands, until his ears catch the sound of singing on the breeze. In the distance the figure of, what appears to be a scarecrow. He closes in and finally gets a grasp on what he is seeing.\nBefore him stood a female, her skin consisting of compressed hay. The singing on the breeze originating from her very mouth, as she works the fertile fields that surround her. She notes his presence, yet continues her work.\n“Arwa?” The uruk utters. The many a bones on his chains and bracelets as well as the feathers dance in the breeze that surrounds them.\nThe woman halts her work, she turns around revealing her straw face. “Oh hello Shagarath.” She replies on a kind and warming tone. “Welcome”.\nThe shaman smiles, inclining his head in respect for the spirit in front of him. “I am honoured to meet, Spirit of harvest. I do hope I am not interrupting you in your work.” He eyes the bag of seeds in her hand and the soil beneath her.\n“I work, day in day out…  soon is the time for the great harvest.” She concluded, locking her hands on her waist. The hay woman turned from the uruk, facing the fields. She inhaled slowly with closed eyes, in bliss in the moment and place.\n“Your fields are magnificent. As are you, Arwa. What advice do you bring those who praise you, those that work endless hours on the fields of the Uzg. What guidance do you give them?” He inquired, his frame straightened, holding his staff in one hand.\n“Thank you\" The spirit hummed softly at his compliment, taking a hold of her chin with two digits, tapping it. She eventually turns around facing the Shaman, to then look at the ground. She lowers her hay form and picks up a single seed, presenting it.\n. “All power, that for food to feed the bravest of warriors. Comes from merely this small and humble seed.” She giggled “Even an old fart like you, would be nothing without these little fellows.”\n“Hrm, I see. So… we must respect that what feeds us, respect the little things?” He arched his thick grey brow.“That, and give it enough water, sunlight, attention… singing helps!” She smiled.\n“Singing?” The orc recoiled of amazement “I don’t believe you.. Are you a trickster as well?”\n“No silly, behold! A mighty seed!” She keeps the seed in front of her for all to see. Softly Arwa started humming, on the tune she started to sing, her voice carried all across the endless plains surrounding them. The breeze catching up as she did.\nAnd with this, the lone small seed in her hand, started to grow, first merely a small green leaf escaping, but soon followed by roots that wrapped around her hand, digging into the hay that is her skin.\nA flower grew in her hand. As it stood there, tall and proud she giggled, taking it and putting it in her hair. The orc stared in awe, slightly drooling even. He stood astonished at what the spirit showed him.\n“Now” Arwa spoke. “Go back, and sing, old orc.” She clapped her hay hands twice, after which the uruk woke up, back in his blargh.\nThe following day an older uruk could be seen in the midst of the Uzgs wheatfields, humming a low tune and occasionally singing aloud.\n\nThe End\n\nAfar-Ilzgul, Lûp Ilzgul\nFear the Spirits, Praise the Spirits.", ""),
        StaticBook("Spirit Tales #2", "Shagarath", "Tales of Spirits\n\n\"Down the Rabbit Hole\"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n~By Shagarath\nWith a grunt the elder uruk woke up. His form lay but weakly atop the moist soil of the undergrowth around him. He huffed through his nostrils, his eyes opening but staying narrowed for a time.\nRays of sunlight hit the uruk through the dense roof of leaves above him, the light carrying a soft green hue. The elder stood up, wincing and cursing at his old joints who refused coörporation.\nHe searched about him, taking a step forward. Another and another, through the dense undergrowth before the silence that surrounded him was disturbed by a low growl.\nThe exclamation of pain caused by the impact of the Shaman and the staff he had been searching for. Rubbing his toe he took the decorated piece from the ground, tapping off some dirt and leaves.\nUsing it as an anchor to lean on, the elder limbed forth into the darkness of the forest. Mere minutes he spend following the narrow path before something arose in the distance, a large wall of stone blocked his path.\nAfter some more limbing he arrived at the start of this wall, and with a quick scan the Elders eyes fell upon a narrow opening, a round cavern broke the wall and bid him a way through.\nInstinctively he looked around, shrugging he ventured on, only to halt at the entrance. With hesitation he peered into the darkness there, nothing more seen.\nHis foot raised from the ground and was set atop the first stone step of the stairway that lead down into the deep, only to be quickly jerked back as an echo emerged from within the dark.\nA low laughter escaped the cavern opening and left for the woods as it passes the elder uruk that stands in front. The orc swallows briefly, standing with what seemed to be fear in his eyes, staring in awe at the opening of the cave.\nHe shook his head in an attempt to rid himself of this unpleasant feeling. With determination he stepped forth and ventured down into the darkness.\nThe further the orc travels, the lighter his surroundings become. The void that darkened the cavern slowly fades as more and more of the stairways tunnel  walls starts to show themselves.\nEventually the orc entered a small oasis, a body of water at the bottom of the stairway. In the middle stood a rock, on which a figure lay idly, sleeping. On a soft tone, the uruk spoke. “Heil Drelthok.”\nHe halted at the edge of the body of water. Awaiting a response. The figure moved, light bouncing off the rocks and water, lighting up the area. The ball of brown fur started to slide down the rock, and landed into the water.\nIn a terrible slow rate the bear stood on its paws and moved towards the shaman.“W-why did you wake me? I was just dreaming” The bear yawned, its voice sleepy.\nThe elder grumbles. “To meet with you, spirit of sleeping and dreams. I am sorry to so.” he inclines his head in respect. “It is an honour” “Yes yes.. Pfft..” The bear yawns again.\n“Tell me, why did you walk here? Why not just fly here, you doofus?” it inquires, apparently quite seriously on the matter. “Errrr…” The elder freezes in place. “Orcs don’t fly” He states blankly.\n“Nonsense! EVERYBODY can fly!” The bear approaches. Upon standing a few feet from the uruk it raises on two feet, and drops on its bum. A paw is brought to the head and a nail taps onto the head of the elder. “Its all in here.” The bear smiles.\n“Once your body is at rest, this is the place where imagination becomes reality.” The tapping intensifies. “All in here, good sleep means good dreams.”\nWith a final tap the uruk drops on the ground. He sleeps for hours, during which a wide smile was latched to his face. The dream flew him to the most exotic places, the largest slave markets and the most beautiful fe-uruks.\nWith two claps of the bearhands the uruk awakens. “W-why did you wake me? I was just dreaming” The orc yawned, its voice sleepy.\nAs the orc looks around the cavern was gone, and so was the bear. He was back in his house, back in his hammock. The orc smirked before closing his eyes again, to sleep and dream some more.\n\nTHE END\nAfar-Ilzgûl, Lûp-Ilzgûl.\nFear the Spirits, Praise the Spirits.", ""),
