        StaticBook("Refugee's Legacy", "§bAn Author Unknown", "*While the inscription is largely faded, some words are able to be gained from it.*\n\nWith each day it becomes increasingly clear that the packed ice and snow between us and the world will not be passed.\n\nTo think, we escaped the\n\nclaws and teeth of a million monsters only to die in the halls of our refuge, with nothing to blame but the freezing winds.\n\nTal'ei is preparing the last of our supplies...", ""),
        StaticBook("Ballads vol 1", "§bIllidar", "Anthology of the Soul§0\n§0       A collection§0\n§0         of crap§0\n§0       created by§0\n§0          Illidar\n\n\n§0You used to call me on my§0\n§0You used to, you used to§0\n§0Yeah§0\n§0\n§0You used to call me on my cell phone\n\nLate night when you need my love§0\n§0Call me on my cell phone§0\n§0Late night when you need my love§0\n§0And I know when that hotline blingThat can only mean one thing§0\n§0I know when that hotline bling That can only mean one thing\n\nEver since I left the city,§0\n§0You got a reputation for yourself now§0\n§0Everybody knows and I feel left out§0\n§0Girl you got me down, you got me stressed out§0\n§0'Cause ever since I left the city, you started wearing less and goin' out more\n\nGlasses of champagne out on the dance floor§0\n§0Hangin' with some girls I've never seen before§0\n§0\n§0You used to call me on my cell phone§0\n§0Late night when you need my love Call me on my cell phone§0\n§0Late night when you need my love§0\n\nI know when that hotline bling§0\n§0That can only mean one thing§0\n§0I know when that hotline bling§0\n§0That can only mean one thing§0\n§0Ever since I left the city, you, you, you§0\n§0You and me we just don't get along§0\n§0You make me feel like I did you wrong\n\nGoing places where you don't belong§0\n§0Ever since I left the city,§0\n§0you, you got exactly what you asked for§0\n§0Running out of pages in your passport§0\n§0Hanging with some girls I've never seen before§0\n\nYou used to call me on my cell phone§0\n§0Late night when you need my love Call me on my cell phone§0\n§0Late night when you need my love§0\n§0And I know when that hotline bling§0\n§0That can only mean one thing§0\n\nI know when that hotline bling§0\n§0That can only mean one thing§0\n§0\n§0These days, all I do is§0\n§0Wonder if you're bendin' over backwards for someone else§0\n§0Wonder if you're rollin' up a backwoods for someone else\n\nDoing things I taught you, gettin' nasty for someone else§0\n§0You don't need no one else§0\n§0You don't need nobody else, no§0\n§0Why you never alone Why you always touching road§0\n§0Used to always stay at home, be a good girl§0\n§0You was in a zone, yeah\n\nYou should just be yourself§0\n§0Right now, you're someone else§0\n§0\n§0You used to call me on my cell phone§0\n§0Late night when you need my love§0\n§0Call me on my cell phone§0\n§0Late night when you need my love\n\nAnd I know when that hotline bling§0\n§0That can only mean one thing§0\n§0I know when that hotline bling§0\n§0That can only mean one thing§0\n§0\n§0Ever since I left the city§0\n§0\n§0End Poem 1\n\n\"Anaconda\"§0\n§0\n§0My Anaconda don't...§0\n§0My Anaconda don't...§0\n§0My Anaconda don't want none unless you got buns hun§0\n§0\n§0Boy toy named Troy used to live in Detroit Big dope dealer money, he was getting some coins§0\n\nWas in shootouts with the law, but he live in a palace§0\n§0Bought me Alexander McQueen, he was keeping me stylish§0\n§0Now that's real, real, real,§0\n§0Gun in my purse, bitch I came dressed to killWho wanna go first? I had 'em pushing daffodils\n\nI'm high as hell, I only took a half of pill§0\n§0I'm on some dumb shit§0\n§0\n§0By the way, what he say?§0\n§0He can tell I ain't missing no meals§0\n§0Come through and fuck 'em in my automobileLet him eat it with his grills,§0\n§0He keep telling me to chill\n\nHe keep telling me it's real, that he love my sex appeal§0\n§0He said he don't like 'em boney, he want something he can grab§0\n§0So I pulled up in the Jag, and I hit 'em with the jab like...§0\n§0Dun-d-d-dun-dun-d-d-dun-dun My Anaconda don't...§0\n§0My Anaconda don't...\n\nMy Anaconda don't want none unless you got buns hun§0\n§0\n§0Oh my gosh, look at her butt§0\n§0Oh my gosh, look at her butt§0\n§0Oh my gosh, look at her butt§0\n§0Look at her butt (look at her butt)\n\nThis dude named Michael used to ride motorcycles§0\n§0Dick bigger than a tower, I ain't talking about Eiffel's§0\n§0Real country ass nigga, let me play with his rifle§0\n§0Pussy put his ass to sleep, now he calling me NyQuil§0\n§0Now that bang bang bang,\n\nI let him hit it 'cause he slang Cocaine§0\n§0He toss my salad like his name Romaine§0\n§0And when we done, I make him buy me Balmain§0\n§0I'm on some dumb shit By the way, what he say?§0\n§0He can tell I ain't missing no meals§0\n§0Come through and fuck 'em in my automobile\n\nLet him eat it with his grills,§0\n§0He keep telling me to chill§0\n§0He keep telling me it's real, that he love my sex appeal§0\n§0Because he don't like 'em boney, he want something he can grab§0\n§0So I pulled up in the Jag, Mayweather with the jab like...\n\nDun-d-d-dun-dun-d-d-dun-dun§0\n§0\n§0My Anaconda don't...§0\n§0My Anaconda don't...§0\n§0My Anaconda don't want none unless you got buns hun Oh my gosh, look at her butt§0\n§0Oh my gosh, look at her butt§0\n§0Oh my gosh, look at her butt\n\nLook at her butt (look at her butt)§0\n§0\n§0Little in the middle but she got much back§0\n§0Little in the middle but she got much back§0\n§0Little in the middle but she got much back§0\n§0Oh my God (look at her butt)\n\nMy Anaconda don't...§0\n§0My Anaconda don't...§0\n§0My Anaconda don't want none unless you got buns hun Oh my gosh, look at her butt§0\n§0Oh my gosh, look at her butt§0\n§0Oh my gosh, look at her butt\n\nOh my God (look at her butt)§0\n§0\n§0Yeah, he love this fat ass§0\n§0Yeah! This one is for my bitches with a fat ass in the fucking club§0\n§0I said, \"Where my fat ass big bitches in the club?\"§0\n§0Fuck them skinny bitches,\n\nFuck them skinny bitches in the club§0\n§0I wanna see all the big fat ass bitches in the motherfucking club§0\n§0Fuck you if you skinny bitches. What? Yeah. Ha-ha, ha...§0\n§0\n§0Yeah. I got a big fat ass. Come on!§0\n§0\n§0End Part 2", ""),
        StaticBook("Notes", "§bSteven Vega", "[!] Detailed star charts adorn the first few pages of the book.\n\n[!] Star charts.\n\n[!] Star charts.\n\n[!] Star charts.\n\n[!] Star charts.\n\nObservations; The stars are varying in size, and when I looked through the prototype seeing glass, some appeared to have different brightnesses, as well as some appearing to barely shine at all. Some appear to change in brightness over time, and some\n\nflicker. I noticed some patterns and shapes in the stars, but I severely doubt they mean anything. You never know, though. I'll have to consult Lanavia to see if she knows anything about it.\n\n[!] More detailed star charts.\n\n[!] More star charts.\n\nObservations: Some of the brightest ones appear in the sky first, unsuprisingly. One of the dimmest objects has smaller objects orbiting it. I wonder if it's something other than a star? Also, another one of the dimmest objects has rings around it. These\n\nseemed quite large, and I could make out a bit of a view that seemed different from stars for both of these objects.\n\n[!] Scribbled onto the page is a drawing of a blurry object, and the words: \"THIS THING FLEW RIGHT BY THE LENS? FAST STAR? CLOSE STAR? BIRD?\" The object also has a slight tail behind it.\n\n[!] Scribbles of star charts and odd types of stars.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Flower Task§0\n§0Gather ten different flowers and bring them back for a lesson.§0\n§0\n§0Lesson given and task complete. *Anthron*\n\nThe World's View on Void Magics:§0\n§0Go out around the land and ask seven people about their opinion on void mages and take note on who they are and what their opinion is. Lesson will come after.§0\n§0\n§0- Tailesin: \"Upsets the balance, so I don't enjoy it.\n\n- Tajo: \"I have no problem with it, except the mages that abuse it, you know.\"§0\n§0\n§0- Za'Kabar: \"It's gross.\"§0\n§0\n§0- Ravondir: \"Powerful thing that the Drui' think is unnatural, without recognizing that it might be the source of all things.\"\n\n- Hecate: \"Not natural, unnaturally bad.\"§0\n§0\n§0- Kiera: \"It's great, I see no problem with it.\"§0\n§0\n§0- Jeremiah: \"It's definitely not good.\"§0\n§0\n§0- Suldaeroth: \"The Void is a hellscape of chaos and madness. The damned wander it and only suffer.\"\n\n- Ryder: \"The Void is, well, complicated. It has caused great joys, and great pains.\"§0", ""),
        StaticBook("Ho", "§bSammy \"Mungo\"", "How to be a Silvertongued Devil- By Mungo the Bandit.\n\nIn life you may come upon times where reaching for your sword may not be the most ideal way to go, you may not be the best fighter, or you simply can't speak for your life, hopefully, this guide of\n\nsorts may assist you in swindling, lying, persuading and overall being a great talker. Now, me being me, I was born with the natural talent of making people absolutely adore me, whether this was being brought up in a tevern or my mother being\n\nquite a social escort, I cannot say, but what I can say is that this skill of mine has gotten me out of many  tight situations and into many fair maidens' dreams. But, enough of me glamourizing myself, I'll try to keep this book short and to the point.\n\n1.\nBe as polite as can be without seeming as if you want to kiss someone's backside, being polite leads to people liking you, and even giving you free things such as minas and food.\n\n2.\nBe eccentric and posh with your words. This of course excludes Orcs, they will most likely cave your face in if you use anything more complicated than six letter words. Use words such as indeed, gent, gentleman, kind, fine and so on, this\n\nmay lead to some thinking you a pompous whoreson, but to others you will seem a much more upper class gent or lady than you actually are, helping you achieve your goals whether it be getting that promosion, lying, succesfully conning,\n\nor simply being all the talk at spiffing Balls.\n\n3.\nSeem lighthearted, relaxed and calm, the calmer and more lighthearted you are, the more it will rub off on whoever you're trying to swindle, leading them to dropping their guard, taking a liking of you, or simply another way to seem that\n\nyou have no idea why there's a dead body infront of you while you hold a bloody knife, you just found it there of course.\n\n4.\nBe kind, even if it might not be in your nature, seeming like a kind person will help you in many ways, tricking people, this will lead to you making unlikely allies, you'll have more sway to your word, and if it comes to a tavern brawl\n\neveryone will believe you when you say you didn't start it.\n\n5.\nLast, but not least, be charismatic, even if you're quite the reserved person, this will inspire those around you, and lead to people believing your word over others.\n\nAnd so, that ends my short guide to being a charming and cunning criminal such as myself, whether you use it for good or evil, is your choice, I was just bored in the corner of a tavern waiting for someone to play some blackjack.\n\n-Signed, Mungo, the Charming Bandit.", ""),
        StaticBook("Git Rekt", "§bNazark'Raguk", "ugLucKz Am duMb mOmosKahurZ", ""),
        StaticBook("Report on Kha", "§bViaiel", "Report on Kha kitten\n------------------\n24th of The Deep Cold 1521\n------------------\nBy Viaiel Elsinael - Evarir\n-------------------Kha was found wandering elcihi. Apprehended by Evarir Viaiel. Cause: Suspicious activity.\n\nKha was to be let out by Evarir Viaiel. Kha stole everythingit could before making a run for it. Kha's life was ended by Evarir Viaiel, using a bow.", ""),
        StaticBook("Journal Vol. I", "§bFjordin Sylver", "- Adventurers Journal of\nFjordin Sylver, Pioneer\n\n- Autobiographic Info\nName : Fjordin Sylver\nRace : Wood Elf\nAge (As of Print) : 117\nInterests (Professions) :\nWoodworking\nBlacksmithing\nLeatherworking\nEnchanting\n\nEntry 1 - It wasn't long after recollecting my conciousness that I realized I was in the grand Cloud Temple. I did not know, and still don't know, how it was I ended up there. Equally, I did not recall what it was my life skills were. Just my identity.\n\n   Eventually, I set about the infamous Stone Road. It wasn't a pleasant journey, prompting me to spend what few Minas I had on an elegantly crafted ferrum sword, but at the time I had few options. Despite its rough reputation, the trip was relaxing.\n\n   I came across many establishments, cities, and sometimes the occasional trail which juts off into the wilderness. Unfortunately, the latter ended up being a cluster of trees shrouding tiny villages or Druid sanctuaries. I favored the Viridium Pub.\n\n   Shortly before sundown along the Atheran mainland there was a man clad in chainmail. He pressed himself onto me claiming to be a mercenary for a man by the name of Renatus. Thankfully, there was not a price hanging over my head, but a proposal.\n\n   Instead of coming to claim my life which had only shortly ago been reintroduced to me, the man offered me a place within the mercenary group. Being a Wood Elf, I was naturally proficient in the usage of a bow, but with some thinking denied his offer.\n\n   From there, the situation almost progressed into a physical altercation, but thankfully I managed to get away without any quarrel. From there I pressed into a pine grove, until eventually emerging before an archipelago off the coast.\n\nEntry 2 - Initially I overlooked the archipelago, and instead favored the idea of circumnavigating the Atheran mainland over the next few days. To my dismay no land seemed fruitful enough to settle in. That is when I made my return.\n\n   I decided to take a swim out to the islands, which were a chunk of purity and paradise. There were chickens, laughably native to the salt-striken shores of that island cluster, but beyond that there was more. Sandy stones jutting out of the water.\n\n   To what I can recall, each one of these islands bore some kind of lore. For example : One of which featured a cobblestone pillar, intricately placed in the center. For all I know, that was the ancient burial site of a long-forgotten hero.\n\n   But those were besides the point. What struck me most was a large, imposing ship nestled against a grand mesa. It seemed mechanical in nature, possibly Orcish in design. However, there were indications of Dwarven engineering. Even now, who knows?\n\n   It took me almost a days worth of careful scaling and climbing to finally cross the mesa and its canyon, but at last I had made it to the fertile woodlands of what will soon be Sylverwood. The land was almost pure, untouched. Ripe for the taking.\n\n   I knew once my eyes cast upon the clearing that this was my homestead. The Dark Oak forest signature to these lands provides a natural barrier between myself (now ourselves) and the outside world. This is the kind of isolation we strive for.\n\n   It took me a fair few seasons, but in some time I managed to construct myself a humble abode towards the edge of the clearing. Thankfully chickens were native species to this forest, and provided me with food throughout the winter.\n\n   It was The Deep Cold of 1484 when I had met Artemis Caryole. He worked, and as of right now still does work, at the Viridian Pub along the Stone Road. I had wandered there after leaving the Standing Inn. one night in search of food, but found more.\n\nEntry 3 - Inside the tavern, Artemis was tending to a young lady by the name of Marie. 'Till this day I remain wary of her seemingly good nature (the next time I seen Artemis, he also claimed she attacked him out of rampancy).\n\n   Apparently Marie was in search of food. The harsh winter winds made farming impossible, and I myself was barely clinging onto life. All I had were a handful of rotten potatoes I had stolen months prior from a farmer.\n\n   Artemis guide us both to a grand Elven city, which was at the time controlled by the High Elves of Athera. We were greeted by a man at the gates, presumably an archmage of sorts, who accused Marie of being a Frost Witch.\n\n   Having done my research on said villainous creatures I can see how she could qualify for a Frost Witch. Her skin color was rather pale, and she was too kind-hearted for her own good. Apparently she also featured a split personality.\n\n   Regardless, I along with Artemis defended Marie, and eventually took our leave with some food from a bin servicing the community with rations. It was on our return that I met Artemis' sister, Alarica, who in the warm season sold me two heads of cattle.\n\n   That being said, the winter did not completely thrash me into oblivion. Once The First Seed of 1485 hit I had begun work on a small plot of farmland. The trade was tricky to learn, but come the Sun's Smile I had become somewhat less inept.\n\n   That season, I had also met Kayla Denn. On my routine trip up to the Cloud Temple I had sensed a lost soul. For a day or so I found myself communicating with it by an unexplainable means until eventually it embodied itself.\n\n   She was lost, confused, and alone. In short she was just as myself when I woke up in the Temple. For a while we walked the Stone Road, and I had forged her a rudimentary set of chainmail along with a sword. The result was a fierce ally.\n\n   It didn't take much convincing for her to join me on my adventure back to the clearing. When we finally arrived she immediately praised the serenity. Eventually I offered her residence within my old shack, and started construction on The Hall.\n\n   The hall was never supposed to be a massive project. I wanted, and still want, to make an example of our simplistic and quaint lifestyle. Instead of being constructed out of solid stone bricks and expensive aurorum blocks, I had settled for less.\n\n   The main goal of my home was and still is the comfort of my guests. While there isn't much space for recreation I had managed to section off a tavern-like space for company. The idea is to provide food, drink, and comfort for a content stay.\n\nEntry 4 - The Amber Cold of 1484 was quite eventful, much like the previous winter. This one, however, was not quite as much a fortune. A Lord stumbled upon Kayla in the Dark Oaks, searching for his brother. He immediately interrogate her.\n\n   She had claimed to know nothing, which was the honest truth, but this was not enough for him. He instead wandered into the clearing and met face to face with me. I gave him a warm welcome, asking him what it was he wanted from us. Not a wise move.\n\n   The man immediately jumped to the bargaining of our land after interrogating us as to why it was we resided so far out. He promised us a plot on the mainland to continue our ventures under the protection of his armies.\n\n   Kayla had looked up to me as a leader, and therefore waited for me to make the tough decision. After biting my lip for several seconds, I had declined the offer. I told him I had hoped this wouldn't interfere with his ambitions, and he said no worries.\n\n   From that moment on, all we did was worry. But it wasn't long before we decided it was time to solidify our foundations. To do so, we settled on the recruitment of more outsiders to shape our soon-to-be-society. That was when we met Dorian.\n\n   Come The Deep Cold, a man by the name of Dorian Hunter came charging into the clearing bow in hand. At first I was suspicious, but permit him to set up a tent outside my Hall. He seemed to me a skilled warrior, which I admired.\n\n   It was at that point we began to warm up. I lit a firepit outside his shelter during a blizzard, just to provide comfort for the both of us while we sat outside and got to know one another. It didn't take much convincing for him to join on my quest.\n\n   I began construction of a house along the river bank later that season. It wasn't until the beginning of Snow's Maiden that I finished it in its entirety. Furnishings, workstations, and intricate craftsmanship of a Wood Elf. What more can someone ask?\n\nEntry 6 - Not long after the completion of his home, Dorian returned to the clearing (now unofficially called Sylverwood) for the first time since The Deep Cold. However, he had brought with him a pair of travellers. More specifically... magical bards.\n\n   Their names were Norgaladir and Lizzy. Norgaladir claimed to be te guild master, who took an interest in music (obviously) and Divine Magic. All he asked for was a 20x20 plot in cubic meters. With some hesitation I accepted, but not for the music.\n\n   Being well versed in the history of Aegis I know very well it was overthrown by the Undead. I do not want Sylverwood to suffer the same fate. Those who are skilled in Divine Magic can help protect Sylverwood from the Undead scourge if it hits Athera.\n\nEntry 7 - It's now the time of The Grand Harvest, 1486, and everything is going... accordingly. Dorian and Kayla find themselves at one anothers necks. Sometimes I swear they are cosntantly waltzing a dance of death. Those hopeless romantics... heh.\n\n   Aside from that, I have finished construction of a few more homes. These will eventually work in sync on the Community Farm. This will be the sole provider of wheat, cacti, sugarcane, pumpkins and melon.\n\n   A typical Sylverian (denonym for Sylverwood citizen) will consist of wheat, beef, and fish products. This is to minimize the amount of farm land required in favor of cattle. In addition, cattle provide us with access to an important industry.\n\n   That industry? Leatherworking. A very simple craft, yet one that most races have the potential of being adept in. Leatherworking is a powerful craft, allowing one to manufacture armor beneficial to archers, and clothing to wear into the streets.\n\n   In order for one to obtain leather one needs cows. In order for one to breed cows one needs wheat. That is where the chain of supply comes in. The farmers provide wheat, wheat provides cows via breeders, and cows provide leather via leatherworkers.\n\n   I myself have picked up this craft in favor of the uses it could provide me as a potential Lord. Having power means having an army and the arsenal to crack down on the opposition. Leatherworking will allow me to equip my soldiers and myself properly.\n\n   Leatherworking aside, the town square has started taking shape. While not exactly constructed from the ground up I have taken the liberty of laying down the roads. My only concern is the fact it was constructed to the far right of the clearing.\n\n   This means an awkward, empty gap will be left in the left side of the clearing. My current idea at fixing this is to create a castle of sorts befit for me. While I am fond of having a small hall like the one I currently own, I feel it won't do forever.\n\n   I am looking forward to the completion of the Guild Master's building. So far it looks a bit iffy, and I fear it will not bode well with my Wood Elf architecture. Regardless I will let him continue his work and see where it takes him.\n\n   Aside from that, I paid my semi-seasonal trip to the Cloud Temple. No bandit encounters, thankfully, but I did come across a bar fight in the Standing Inn. Met a Wood Elf by the name of Sanora who seemed to be interested in the ways of Earth Magic.\n\n   After the bar fight, which resulted in the slaughter of two Uruk, Sanora and I parted ways (she went to a Wood Elf \"city\" to study if the political system has improved). She promised me she'd send word if she was disappointed with her findings.\n\n   Should she dislike what she sees, she will potentially become a Sylverwood citizen. How grand! Further into my travels, I met another Elf. This one, however, was a Dark Elf by the name of Domalvia.\n\n   The circumstances of us getting to know one another were interesting. I found her buying a few loaves of bread from a dealer up at the Temple, so I offered a sack of the stuff free of charge. She was very thankful, and I said I'd lead her here someday.", ""),
        StaticBook("TOTTA: Book I-2", "SapphireShard", "The Tales of a Travelling Alchemist\n-----------\nElements of Alchemy\n    \nVolume I-2 of V\n\nThe Elements:\n--------\nFire\n--\nEarth\n--\nWater\n--\nAir\n--\n\nSkeptics often approach me with a wholly ignorant conundrum, and that is “how can you trace every spell and element back to these four basic elements.”\n\nBut what they do not realize is that an understanding of what is meant by these four elements is required before that question can be answered and in fact,\n\nif that understanding is acquired then this question will never be asked.\nThese four basic, rudimentary elements, Fire, Earth, Water, and Air, are what every potion, oil, elixir, and concept, in Alchemy of course, is based upon.\n\nthey must look deeper than the surface. I shall go into each of these elements in detail, so that you, and budding alchemist or simply a well-read individual,\n\nthey must look deeper than the surface. I shall go into each of these elements in detail, so that you, and budding alchemist or simply a well-read individual,\n\nmay understand how Air may both aid someone’s speed, as well as their perception of their surroundings.\n\nFire \n=== \nThe Destroyer, the Unmaker, the Cleanser.\n\nWe will begin with one of the most rudimentary and used element, Fire. Fire can represent many a feeling, action, and state and because of this, it is often one of the most useful elements of the Alchemist’s arsenal.\n\nFire can represent rage, anger, hatred, as well as bravery, courage, and gusto. Through this a brew can be crafted to make someone unquestionably and savagely furious, but it could also craft a brew to strengthen one’s will,\n\nand allow them to do what other men would shrink away in fear at. These feelings, these emotions can be invoked by the element of Fire within a potion, but feelings are not the only thing that fire can produce, actions are another.\n\n\n Actions are often the most recognized aspect of fire, and by this I mean the action of burning, consuming, and destroying.\n\nAlchemist’s Fire, a handy inferno in a bottle, is an example of this aspect of Fire, for within it is the action of destruction. This action can be further expanded upon with burning oil placed upon a weapon,\n\nbut if one were to use this example they would also have to include the element of pain, another feeling that fire an invoke. State is aspect that Fire can take one within an alchemist’s potion and this is often represented as Chaos.\n\nIt is true that Chaos is often something alchemist’s fear, for it is something athat often ruins a potion, but it is yet another aspect of Fire that must be recognized.\n\nChaos, being the state that it is, may seem like it has little place in a potion but brilliant minds and powerful reason can bring it to use just the same as the first farmer realized a stone and a stick,\n\ntwo completely different objects, could help them till their fields by putting them together. As this is not a book on the philosophy of alchemy, or solely on the element of fire, I will not go into depth on how Chaos can be used.\n\nThe teacher must leave something for the student to ponder.\n\nEarth\n===\n\nThe Enduring, The Lifegiver, the Fortifying\n\nWith the conclusion of Fire, the other elements will not have as long as description for most of the fundamentals of the elements are covered in how they can represent different things,\n\nI will simply go over what they may represent, starting with Earth.\nEarth is a truly powerful element for it may represent metals, strength,\n\nthe notion of being stalwart, solidity, firmness, flora, animals, and countless other, quite obvious, subjects. Earth is often used for potions that increase one’s endurance,\n\nor strength, and it is a must in potions that toughen one’s skin. In fact, a strong series of representations of Earth are required to perfect a barksin, or stoneskin potion.\n\nProtecting oneself is not the only use for the element of Earth, for it is also used in antidote potions, disease-curing potions,\n\nand, of course, fertilizers. Antidotes use this element for it can represent animals, and among those elements are snakes and spiders,\n\noften associated with poison. Of course a symbol of Water is also required for a proper antidote, but this will be addressed later on.\n\nMany of the uses of the element of Earth are quite obvious, but a always, if one wishes to master the field of Alchemy they must learn creativity is their greatest friend,\n\nand using elements in odd and interesting ways can be useful, and quite detrimental, in numerous ways. A final note on the element of Earth is how it is associated with the fable of Transmutation.\n\nNow, as a scholarly and skeptic individual, I have doubts upon transmutation, its existence, and possibility. This will be described more in depth, but know that the element of Earth is the greatly represented by metals,\n\nfor metals are merely ores in their purest forms.\n\nWater\n===\n\nThe Base, the Purifier, the Healer.\n\nWater is the element that seems to get the most amount of use due to how it is a base for many a potion and it is true, for if someone wants a liquid spell they want to use the Element of Water.\n\nOften known as the “base” element, it truly lives up to its name for observations, and personal experience, lead all to believe it is necessary for life. This base element, is often associated with flowing,\n\nliquids, balance, peace, calm, order, life, and rebirth. One requires water for any potion that one plans on drinking, and any oil that one plans on applying for to have a liquid,\n\none must use the Element of Water. Now that and understanding on how Water is a base, we must go into how it is used beyond simply supporting the other elements.\n\nWater’s uses are varied, for it can be used both in antidote as well as poisons, showing that it is quite the versatile element. One may also use it to create a potion of calmness, or perhaps a potion of healing due to its association with life.\n\nBut one can also use this beautiful element in the creation of an oil to coat a weapon, or to coat and strengthen armor. This is not the same as enchanting armor or weaponry, mind you, for doing that is often permanent and requires magic.\n\nThrough oils, one may reap similar benefits of enchantment but instead it is only temporary and anyone with basic sense can apply them. The purest base, and often representation of water, is Aqua Vitae or “Water of Life,” which is best made\n\nwhich is best made by distilling wine or other liquors\n\nAir\n===\n\nThe Nimble, the Traveller, the All-Seeing.\n\nOur final element, the element of Air, is not the least by any stretch of imagination but is often the hardest one to put into representation and use in a potion. Now this element is often associated with agility, quickness, travel, feet, perception,\n\nand even flight, it can be quite difficult to embody the element of Air into a physical item due to our basic fault of lacking understanding of what we cannot see. But with reason and the opening of one’s eyes,\n\nthe ways in which to represent Air are quite obvious in our everyday world.\nItems that can be used to represent air, and subsequently put through your tools to produce a potion,\n\nare rather easy to find during your average day but of course, as with all the elements, purer ingredients are often quite rarer. One representation that you may have already thought of is a mere feather,\n\nwhich is often the most common representation of Air. This, of course, is not the purest representation of the element but it will often due for your more basic potions centered around this element.\n\nAnother, more outlandish representation, are rabbits’ feet. Rabbits are often seen as quick and agile critters, and as Air takes on the aspect of agility and quickness, it becomes quite obvious how the feet of a rabbit and Air intermingle.\n\nWith that said, it becomes rather easy to find out representations of Air through simply looking and observing the world around you. But do not fret if you are still hard-pressed to find these symbols,\n\nthe next chapter will explain herbs that will best aid you in the process of finding symbols for all the elements.", ""),
        StaticBook("Dark Brotherhood", "§bJistuma", "Introduction:\nMany Aegeans now are sheltered. They know nothing of this world and what is happening. For instance, today I saw the Temple of Aegis in trouble. Slimes had started a siege on recently resurrected player, and sending them back to the\n\nCrypt. It was mayhem. And what troubles me even more, as a traveler, is that very few people know about the Dark Brotherhood. This evil guild is intent on the destruction of this world’s inhabitants. And needs to be stopped at all costs. This is why I\n\nwrite this book, so that you may learn to defend yourself against this, this taint… Let me begin on the history of the guild.\n\nThe History of the Brotherhood.\nIt is said they started off as… well… assasins with perfectly harmless intentions. This soon changed with the arrival of one known only as “The Stranger”. This “Stranger” as he is known, is responsible\n\nfor the deaths of many, and is rumored to be the Grand Master of the guild. It has been this way for many years now. If his true identity is found, report it to me immediately! It must be shared!\n\nThe Guild Itself.\nThe Guild is mostly assasins, with a few bandits mixed in. This makes it hard to track down members, commonly referred to as “Danalus’”. These appear to be the average rank, with half the guild seemingly comprised of it…\n\nThere is also the recruit variety of Danalus it seems. Koloth is apparently the next rank. Only two have reached this rank so far to my knowledge. This is the final rank. Only higher is the “unique” ranks. These ranks are only availible to those who\n\nThere is also the recruit variety of Danalus it seems. Koloth is apparently the next rank. Only two have reached this rank so far to my knowledge. This is the final rank. Only higher is the “unique” ranks. These ranks are only availible to those who\n\nhave proven their worth to the Grand Master, lesser councel and High Councillor. This is the Silencer rank. This rank is only available to those select few, and it is limited space. How it works is each high ranking member has one or two silencers, who\n\ncarry out personal contracts for the high ranking members. There is one more rank within the Brotherhood, and it is by far the most terrifying of the assassin ranks. It is “Iblees Champion”, a seemingly obscure term used to describe the best member.\n\nIt terrifies me, but thankfully, there is not a Champion yet.\n\nHigh Ranks\nThe highest ranks available are now closed it seems. These are namely the Lesser councel, High Councillor and Grand Master.\n\nThe Dark Brotherhood\n\nRetold by Leyu’Maehr\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven", ""),
        StaticBook("Records, Vol. 1", "§bEridanis Verina", "Winter, 1575§0\n§0\n§0I will start from the beginning.§0\n§0Almost two weeks have past at this point, and never has my life been more eventful. §0\n§0It started with my comrade Erolas, a fellow dark elf who I happened to cross paths with on my way to Sutica. He\n\nand some fellow friends of his were headed the same direction as I, and asked if I would care to join. Of course, at first I was suspicious--only a few days earlier I had been robbed by some fellows I had met on the road. But we reached Sutica, all\n\nin one piece. We shopped, we drank, explored, and became friends.§0\n§0A week later, I encountered Erolas once more, this time by himself. He led me to Riv'Vallel, one of the homes of the dark elves.§0\n§0What a sight it was! The colorful tents, the totem poles,\n\nthe friendly folk. I felt at home at once. §0\n§0That night is when everything began. A wounded young human came into the city, claiming he had been assaulted in Oren. we did our best to assist him, but once the clerics came they took care of it.§0\n§0And then...\n\nthey appeared.§0\n§0The wisps!§0\n§0Good lord, were they beautiful. Two pairs of shining lights, shimmering and circling outside the cleric tent. And they spoke, these exact words:§0\n§0\"There is a language in the notes§0\n§0Origins all but absolute§0\n§0Strum on the strings of\n\nyesterday§0\n§0The skies were always to blame§0\n§0Pity not the weight of strain§0\n§0Light is the sun, dark is the grain§0\n§0Blessed to the gods below§0\n§0Crops of life are what I sow§0\n§0Ours is the purest sanctity§0\n§0I do this time and time\n\nagain\"§0\n§0And they flew away, like they had never spoken.§0\n§0We alll spoke at once. What were they? Why did they appear to us? After the panic had settled, the cleric set off to find a mentor of hers, to ask questions and hopefully find answers. However, I do\n\nstudy magic--the wisps were a form of Arcanism, an ancient magic, the first form of magic to exist in our realm. With this information, we all set off to explore and wonder.§0\n§0Two days later, Erolas and I met up. We would go exploring, to hopefully find\n\nanswers. We brought along with us a cousin of Erolas and this cousins...\"girlfriend\". They were extremely sappy the whole time, which I did not appreciate. §0\n§0But that is besides the point. We set off on the road, and in the middle of the night at a cross-\n\n-road, we came across another wisp--this time, only one. And it sensed us, interacted with us:§0\n§0\"Ours is the purest sanctity§0\n§0Roaming astray my endless garden§0\n§0Light is the sun, dark is the grain§0\n§0Maker of the lurks so foul\n\nBoth mind and soul are my design§0\n§0There is a language in the notes§0\n§0You can hear it, if you listen\"§0\n§0This one spoke irregularly, circling us and glowing. then it went into the sky, until we could no longer see it. As we pondered the meaning of the words, we\n\ncontinued our way. We came across a bridge, where the cousin and his lover jumped into the deep water to swim. Erolas and I talked for a while, but then--something happened.§0\n§0I fell.§0\n§0The ground was unstable, the rocks slippery. When I woke up, no one\n\nwas near. I was alone.§0\n§0I continued onward, to see if the others were there. But it was also to satisfy my hunger for exploration. I wanted to find these wisps, what they were talking about--I was stubborn and slightly foolish. But that is my nature. The\n\npath led to a body of water, which I swam across to an island. And then I kept swimming.§0\n§0O, I must have been swimming for hours! And when I reached land, it was nothing I had seen before.§0\n§0Souls, souls everywhere! They infested the earth, dragging\n\nme down, guilting me for crimes I did not commit. There was no life, only these souls and their cries--only the bitter cold and the hellish landscape. There was absolutely nothing. I ran around hopelessly--could this be the land the wisps spoke of? But\n\nlo, there was not a wisp to be seen. Weak and sick, I somehow made my way back home, stumbling through the city gates. There, I was cared for and nursed back to health.§0\n§0But this did not stop me!§0\n§0Three days later, Erolas and I went on another adventure.\n\nWe explored the daunting forest of Riv'Vallel, the forest that so many have died in. Erolas was afraid. §0\n§0As we ran through, we found many beautiful things. But then, there was the fire.§0\n§0Trees were burnt down, the ground destroyed. And the souls were there\n\nas well--I saw a connection.§0\n§0But, there was also the lava. The lava, and the water, had created obsidian, lowering the sea level massively. The lava pits remained, and it was truly a disgusting sight. As we left this mysterious land, I saw one patch of\n\ngrass, untouched by the sea. While Erolas speculated it may have been underwater at one point, I thought, perhaps, someone had stood there--the creator of the lava, standing in one spot, unburned and unscorched. But it was only a thought. §0\n§0We continued.\n\nAnd we found the tree.§0\n§0It was the most gorgeous thing I had ever seen-- It was giant, gargantuan. The leaves were purple, the fruit glowing like suns. It was most defintely connected to the wisps! We climbed the tree, to see further.§0\n§0And the view, o!\n\nThe sun was rising, and the clouds were a pastel mix. We could see for miles and miles, the snowy treetops of the forest. Erolas sat next to me. We sat there, sharing something that was unforgettable. And I felt something, but I cannot say.\n\nWe left the tree, and came back home. It was raining, after all. We will report to the others--the cousin, the cleric--as soon as we can.§0\n§0That, my reader, is the story so far. That is the past few weeks, and a brief summary at that!§0\n§0Where to now?\n\nDAY 2§0\n§0\n§0Erolas and I are planning another expedition. I am currently in the process of crafting flint arrow heads to create arrows.§0\n§0I have not used a bow and arrow for quite a few years now, but I believe I will remember the basic skills.\n\nI need to be able to defend myself against enemies close and far, in any situation--therefore, I must be adept in a wide range of weapons. The sword Erolas gave me may not last me forever.§0\n§0\n§0DAY 3§0\n§0We saw a wisp again last night! When I had\n\nalmost given up! §0\n§0The information was priceless. It said these words as it flew back and forth, a melody of a harp playing as it flew:§0\n§0\"What is a melody without its instrument§0\n§0The choir will sing again§0\n§0Fruits of life so beg my pardon\n\nRoaming astray in my endless garden§0\n§0My will sees metal turn to rust§0\n§0And men grow tall from all but dust§0\n§0Find me not in book or tome§0\n§0For I am the breath and life of home§0\n§0Forge noble beasts and kings that reign§0\n§0I do this time and time again\"\n\nThis is incredible! So much to take from. My current theory is the void--after all, it speaks of choirs and gardens and dark gods and fruit--\"chorus fruit\"! and a portal was mentioned by Aveline, the cleric...\n\nDAY 9 §0\n§0Erolas and I head off to the city of wood elves to hopefully find Aveline. While we did not see her, we did see the brother of an old friend--he may be important later.§0\n§0But we heard--most importantly--wood elf war plans. §0\n§0They spoke of killing the\n\nhigh elves, of striking them down--but they also mentioned the dreadlands, so I will warn the chieftain to be on high alert.§0\n§0Erolas and I sat for a while, eavesdropping.§0\n§0Erolas is special to me. I can open up to him, I can aid him, and trust him.§0\n\nAnd today, we kissed.§0\n§0I know, I know, but there is so much to it--the sunset we shared atop the tree, the days and tension have been building up. When we did kiss, in that city of wood elves, it was the most incredible thing I'd felt. And who would have\n\nthought, in the city of wood elves, where dark elves are hated so dearly? Love finds its place in the strangest of times.§0\n§0Our \"talk\" was interuppted by the folk we were eavesdropping on. They mentioned dark elves, and asked if they had ever killed one--\n\nstaging our time to leave. We tried the gate, but it was closed, with a dozen elves in front of it. We did not want to be seen, but it was too late. We ran to the ledge, and without hesitation jumped in the water. We were watched until we reached shore,\n\nwhere they finally left us. §0\n§0I am currently writing this in the safety of my own home, and Erolas will be here shortly. We have heard rumors of something happening here in Riv'Vallel, and our curiousity got the best of us--I hear the Elder shouting right\n\nnow to rally.§0\n§0\n§0Day 10 §0\n§0A warhawke trial! That is what the elder was talking about. I am currently witness the recruits attempting to pass the trial.§0\n§0I myself would like to become a warhawke, but I would like to watch a trial first...\n\nDAY 23§0\n§0SPRING 1576§0\n§0\n§0Not an eventful day. Explored the other dark elf city, Erolas was almost caught being a Warhawke. I smooth-talked our way out of it. We were sent a bird from Asher, who told us he found some interesting statues in the city of\n\nwood elves. Didn't turn out to be anything, but a group of officials walked in while we were looking around--their voices were §0familiar...and§0 then I realized, they were the folk we eavesdropped on! We left as quickly as we could. §0\n§0Currently, we are\n\nsitting in a wood-elven bar, drinking ale and whiskey. It's not the best, but it'll do. Not an eventful past week, all in all.§0\n§0\n§0DAY 30 §0\n§0SPRING 1576§0\n§0\n§0I was on my way to the city of Orcs today when I discovered a land called the \n\nSunset Isles. The cliffs of the islands are jagged and rough, with the sea pounding up against the rocks. I did manage to climb up the cliff with much difficulty. While many of the islands seem barren of civilization, behold--one Island, the southernmost\n\none, holds ruins of what appeared to be a society built of stone. There are remnant structures, and a farm outside one of the houses. But the most curious structure is the temple. It seems to dip underground, and the entrance to the temple is blocked by a\n\nthick, slimey, and almost magical substance, that I cannot seem to penetrate. I am writing this account in the shelter of one of the ruinous houses, wondering if this has anything to do with the wisps--it does seem like an ancient city, perhaps it is\n\nconnected. I will tell the others about this when I return to Riv'Vallel.§0\n§0\n§0DAY 45§0\n§0SUMMER 1575§0\n§0*I have made a slight §0\n§0error in recording the dates. The year has been 1575. §0\n§0It was a dark day for Riv'Vallel. The Oreners have threatened to\n\nattack, and they have five nations to back them up in battle. They are coming, and there was no stopping it. So we had to move. We had no choice.§0\n§0We have made a pilgrimage to a northern field behind the city of High Elves. The view is incredible: there\n\nare mountains surrounding us on one side, while there is the Northern ocean on the other side. There is a forest farther out, and behind us lies the main path and a smaller forest.§0\n§0It is home now. I will miss Riv'Vallel, woe and sadness churning in my\n\nheart. Gods above, I pray that the Oreners take mercy on our town. They attack the dreadlanders, not us, but will surely destroy our city and anything else in our way.§0\n§0The only good is a fresh start and we will, hopefully, no longer be acossiated with the\n\nDreadlanders.§0\n§0Have mercy.§0\n§0\n§0WINTER 1576§0\n§0DAY 104§0\n§0\n§0Much time has passed, but I have been busy. Our new settlement is close to complete.§0\n§0I was scouting our old home today, when I heard yelling from beneath my feet. A\n\nWood Elf about my age was trapped under the soil in a sort of bunker. I dug him out, and--he did not seem to know any of the current events, and asked where everyone was. I told him of Oren and the other Nations. He was devestated. I took him to the ruins\n\nof the old Riv'Vallel, where he decided he wished to build a bunker in the olde cave. It looks quite nice now, I shall visit him.§0\n§0We talked of druids and myths, and I explained the wisps to him. And he told me the most curious thing--he also had a riddle,\n\nbut he had found it enscribed on the side of a gate.§0\n§0A golden gate, he said.§0\n§0This is exactly what i was looking for! He is leading me there now. But he has gotten lost, and I do hope I can find him. A wood elf, lost in a forest. Funny, isnt it? But my\n\nheart beats with anticipation to see this gate. Perhaps it is the gate I was searching for.§0\n§0", ""),
        StaticBook("S.G. Translation", "§bTaeleh Elibar'acal", "A Translated Text\n===================\n\nHail, one who keeps this book. \n\nThis book shall tell all you souls how to control the soul -- dark magic -- the dark fruit.\n\nTo get mortal blood. \n\nA soul bond within a gold vessel.\n\nYou must master this magic.\n\nA mortal's will is in a prison in a small corpse, a good master molds a gold soul.\n\nBut how, do you ask? -- I will tell you.\n\nYou get mage gold (arcaurum).\n\nYou make a vessel out of the gold and magically enchant it.\n\nYou make it look like the mortal. \n\nFor example, we have a Kharajyr.\n\nSoul bond within a vessel.\n\nYou drown it in a fire-like water.\n\nAnd then the vessel will lose shape and become the mortal.\n\n\nAuthor's Note: A very rough translation.", ""),
        StaticBook("Drink Menu", "§bSofetios Jayamen", "Roth Jayamen's\n       Restaurant\n           and\n      Entertainment\n      Establishment\n\n\n      -----------\n           Drink\n           Menu\n     -------------\n\nAlcoholic Beverages\n\nPale Ale\n\nYour run of the mill ale. Made with boiled then cooled water, then put through the brewing process. It has a moderate amount of alcohol.\n\n4 Minas\n\nRed Wine\n\nRed wine made with boiled then cooled water, then put through the wine making process. It has a high content of alcohol. Best to drink slowly.\n\n6 Minas\n\nTeas\n\nRose petal tea\n\nTea made mainly from rose petals and other herbs, made to help the drinker feel more relaxed and rejuvenated. \n\n2 Minas\n\nWake Up Herbal Tea\n\nTea made mainly from Herbs, made to help the drinker more alert. Caution as it is very bitter.\n\n2 Minas\n\nPine leaf tea\n\nTea made mainly from Pine leaves and other herbs, made to help the drinker feel more relaxed and rejuvenated it has a crisp taste. \n\n3 Minas\n\nBirch leaf tea\n\nTea made mainly from Birch leafs and other herbs, made to help the drinker gain more awareness of their surroundings. \n\n3 Minas\n\nDandelion Petal Tea\n\nTea made mainly from Dandelion petals and other herbs, made to help the drinker feel fuller and at ease. It goes great with a small meal.\n\n4 Minas\n\nPoppy Tea\n\nTea made mainly from Poppy petals and other herbs, made to help the drinker feel healthier and helps relieve stress.\n\n4 Minas\n\nChamomile Tea\n\nTea made mainly from Oxeye daisy petals and other herbs, made to help the drinker feel more calm and breath better.\n\n4 Minas\n\nJuices\n\nPumpkin Juice\n\nJuice made from pumpkins, it helps keep you cool when in hot climates.\n\n5 Minas\n\nMelon Juice\n\nJuice made from Sweet Melons, it is VERY sweet. Not for people who can’t handle too much sweetness.\n\n5 Minas\n\nCarrot Juice\n\nJuice made from Carrots, it helps keep you cool when in humid climates.\n\n5 Minas\n\nOther\n\nEggnog\n\nA drink made with creamy and sweet goodness. Makes you filled with energy!\n\n6 Minas\n\nWater\n\nBoiled then cooled water.\n\nFree\n\nPlease remember to return all bottles and menus!\n\nThank you and have a good day!\n\n~Roth Jayamen", ""),
        StaticBook("Scourge Members", "§bUnsigned", "There is a cult in Anthos that has the purpose of following the commands of the Black Scourge. In a way, they are considered part of it.\n\nIn this book there is information on their past members, and some of their current ones.\n\nVithquar\n\nAn experiment created by some of the members of the Scourge, particularly the Judge and the Shadowed alchemist, with aid of one of the Harbinger, Shae'Tan. Physically strong, and slightly sexy, she is not really that smart.\n\nTo identify her is easy, she has horns, a tail, black wings, and black and yellow eyes. She also wears very little slothing.\nShe believes she is a goddess that has lost her powers, and that she should be treated as such. If you actually treat her like\n\nshe wants to be treated, you get on her good side.\nWhile she is part of the cult, she does not worship Setherien. She is only in it because she was created by them, and because she has received power from them.\n\nThe Listener\n\nLeader of the cult, not much is known about him. A warrior, always armored when meeting the cultists. He is in direct contact with the Harbingers, and takes orders from them.\nHe is one of those who recruits.\n\nThe Judge\n\nOne of the higher ups in the cult, a more calm person. He hides his identity through black robes and a skull mask, having a straight posture and speaking in an unnatural voice. Not a fighter himself, he used to stand behind others he\n\ncommanded, or make more use of tricks.\nFrom those captured he would be normally the cause for it to happen. He would interegate them, question them, before deciding what to be done. Some he releaced, some only after torture, some he killed fast, but when\n\nhe didn't like the answers or the person, he usually did something else. It would be a sort of ritual, before he carved symbols into someone's back and skinned them. Why he did so was never known.\nHe was a true believer of the word of Setherien,\n\nmostly the part about adquiring peace by force, yet this soon turned out to be false when he betrayed the Scourge, and destroyed a Harbinger.\nHe is wanted by the Scourge, and as such, he is in hiding.\n\nQa'darath\n\nBefore a higher up of the cult, he too betrayed it, the reasons for the betrayal known. An elf which had quite a few powers. He could tap into the mind of people, and he was a necromancer. If you have not known until\n\nnow, the Scourge and the necromancers are against eachother. The reason for his betrayal is simple, he sided with the necromancers instead of the cult.\nHis description is as simply described as white haired high elf, thin, and with purple robes.\n\nBurzumkil\n\nOne of the stronger warriors in the Scourge. A human with a thick accent. Very powerful in war. Merciless as well, he was one of those who lead raids against towns and villages. He has not been seen for a while.\n\nPrince Ebs\n\nOne of the best spies in the cult, prince Ebs is said to be the one responsible for the information required to access the lands of Old Malinor with the power corruption. He would also keep the Scourge updated with movements of the\n\nelves, and information about politics of Anthos.\nA Prince of Malinor, he has the perfect cover, and would only meet with the Scourge in designated times and places. He would bring great information that would keep the Scourge up to date. While as a spy,\n\nHe never really attacked anyone, and he has great skill in lying. He was commanded to never show himself as a cultist, even to those of the cult, nor to help them. He is a pure spy, one which has not even been given much information on the Scourge, as for\n\nhim to do his job as best he could. If captured, he has nothing to tell, even if tortured. Giving away his position as spy, would only result in his execution, he knows that if he says he worships Setherien, even in torture, he will lose his life, be it\n\nby those who captured him, or by those under Setherien’s will.\n\nThe Oogie Boogie Man\n\nA twisted being, corrupted so much that neither his mind or body is anything good. Black robes and skull mask like the Judge, but the way he behaves is quite different.\nBugs of various kinds seem to be what he is\n\nmade of. Spiders crall from his eyes, ears, and hands.\nOne that enjoys te pain of others, and the bringing of destruction. If he does worship Setherien, it would be because Setherien brings those things. Knowledged in the arts of weird dark magics,\n\nhe was the one that was able to bring a strong ghost into the rule of the Scourge, and he was the one which discovered a way to destroy Old Malinor.\nPowerful in knowledge… weak physically. He is one of those weaker in the Scourge, using tricks and dark\n\nmagic to capture and torment the people of Anthos.\nHe likes to tell stories, and normally just walks unprotected through the lands, with his black robes and skill mask.\n\nThe Shadow Alchemist\n\nAnother one to take intellect over power, this old man is an alchemist that focuses on the dark and twisted sides of it. Experimenting and taking to the Judge were some of the things he did best. Also a great friend of the\n\nOogie Boogie Man, for the same reasons, knowledge.\nExtremelly smart, and twisted to the point of not caring of the life of those he experiments on.\nUnlike the other two, this one can still swing an axe once or twice before he gets tired.\n\nThe Mistress\n\nA twisted elf, which collects heads. Only in the cult to gain more power.\nA fighter and a spy, she also collected relics and information when not killing.\nNothing much is known about her appearance, as she wore masks.\n\nKaos\n\nA human or elf, not known. He is another powerful warrior, leading charges and raids with Burzumkil.\nHe made good use of armor, and hid his identity quite well.\n\nThere are more but I do not know enough details to write about them. This information has been given from a member on the inside, so the informer will remain anonymous as well.\n\n*The book is left unsigned.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Govt. I.D. [001]", "§bGoseph Joebbelstein", "===Heil Vandaloria===\n\nName:\nDecimus Rovin\nMCName: Zhulik\n\nPosition:\nKaiser\n\nClearance Level:\n100", ""),
        StaticBook("The Shun", "§bLeyun Kameki", "The Shun\n\nScribed by Hierophant Callax, the Solstice Druid\n\nAn individual who has proven to be a threat to the Druidic Order might very well be worthy of a shun. One such individual isn’t someone who a particular Druid or a select few Druids dislike or disagree with, but is someone who has offended an entire\n\nCircle or the Order in some way.\n\nOnly an Archdruid may make the notion to shun an individual, which is done while holding the floor at a Moot. He or she will state the reasons for presenting the shun; following, the subject of the shun, if\n\npresent, will answer to the accusations before the Druids of the Moot. Finally, the Inner Circle shall openly vote to pass the shun or not.\n\nOnce a shun is passed on an individual, it shall last until the Inner Circle deems it right, with only the best\n\ninterests for the Order, to end. No Druid of the Order is permitted to speak to a shunned individual or affiliate with them in any way and should, therefore, answer their words with “Much wind pours from your mouth.”\n\nA shunned individual is never allowed to step foot on the holy grounds of a grove, unless requested by an Archdruid in the case of relieving the shun, and shall be forcibly removed from a grove, by any Druid, if he or she refuses to leave. Furthermore, if\n\na shunned individual continuously harasses a Druid outside the grove, and the Druid or Dedicant has spoken the single statement he is permitted to speak to a shun, then hostile actions may be taken upon the individual.\n\nA Druid may also be shunned from the Order, and sometimes unattuned as well, which is the most dishonored action able to be taken upon a Druid. A shun is nothing to ever take lightly and any member of the Druidic Order, or the independent Circle who has\n\ndeemed the shun, should follow suit only the actions allowed to be taken against a shun or face consequences him or her self. Blessed be he who is righteous and loyal and true and never deserving of the shun.", ""),
        StaticBook("H. Schism: Vol 1", "§bThe Eternal Library", "The Haelun'or Schism: Vol 1\n\nBy Lucion Sullas\n\nThe history of Asulon is known to be rich with wars, political usurpations, monstrous drakes, and the rise and fall of nations. However, one history that eludes most modern\n\nnon-Mali'aheral scholars is the history of elhaelun'or, and its many trials and tribulations. One such excerpt (to take from a vast swathe) is the Haelun'or Schism--a short and unusual period of instability and disunity in the motherland. In this three\n\nvolume series, one will discuss the turbulent period for the benefit of future citizens.\n\nThe Haelun'or of Asulon was, in this period, ruled by the echillan'thilln--the Silver Council. Much like its Malinorian contemporary, members were chosen by those\n\nwho were deemed worthy by the current council members - the okariran. Two of these counsellors would begin a chain of events eventually causing the one, and only, demagogic political usurpation in Mali'aheral history. Their names were Ellir'siol, the\n\nOkarir'leyun (Master of beauty), and Nelecar, the Okarir'sil (Master of the Sword).\n\nFrom the very inception of the two working together, friction between their personalities was evident. Nelecar was a soldier with great, and admirable, dedication to the\n\npurity of his kin. His efficient, though admittedly extreme, methods of protection allowed for a distinctly effective military force that has yet to be seen again within the walls of the Mali'aheral. Ellir'siol was an artist, and an architect. Her\n\nmesmerising design of the Haelun'or residential district was one of the finest examples of classical, revivalist mali'aheral architecture available, and is still emulated to this day. Touting overtures of peace, wisdom and forethought, she strove only for\n\nwhat can be perceived as \"the greater good\" in regards to the motherland.\n\nDespite their own good intentions, there were many obvious faults in their actions throughout their years in the service of the cihi'thilln. Nelecar was not a forgiving Mali.\n\nWhen a single, minor wrongdoing was committed, he would often act harshly towards the perpetrator. One such case involved an adolescent Mali'aheral who refused the Okarir's demands, and was summarily locked in a cell for nearly a month as punishment.\n\nHis liberal use of banishment to punish wrongdoers was also a harshly debated affair, and was the main issue of the coming schism. Ellir'siol, however, was by contrast far too forgiving of wrongdoers. She often pressed for impure Mali'aheral to be\n\nre-intigrated to society, and those whose ideals were not congruent with Larihei's vision were protected by her delicate, and ever political machinations. Retrospectively, conflict should have been predicted.\n\nThis is but a tale of two Mali'aheral incapable of compromise, and such shall be shown in the second volume.", ""),
        StaticBook("Haelun'orSchism1", "§bLelien", "The Haelun'or\n       Schism:\n      Volume I\n\n   by Lucion Sullas\n\nThe history of Asulon is known to be rich with wars, political usurpations, monstrous drakes, and the rise and fall of nations. However, one history that eludes most modern, non-mali'aheral scholars is the secret history of elhaelun'or, and its many\n\ntrials and tribulations. One such excerpt (to take from a vast swathe) is the Haelun'or Schism--a short, and unusual, period of instability and disunity in the motherland. In this three volume series, one will discuss the turbulent period for the benefit\n\nof future citizens.\n\nThe Haelun'or of Asulon was, in this period, ruled by the echillan'thilln--the Silver Council. Much like its Malinorian contemporary, members were chosen by those whom were deemed worthy by the current council members--\n\nthe okariran. Two of these counsellors would begin a chain of events eventually causing the one, and only, demagogic political usurpation in mali'aheral history. Their names were Ellir'siol, the Okarir'leyun (Master of Beauty), and Nelecar, the Okarir'sil\n\n(Master of the Sword). \nFrom the very inception of the two working together, friction between their personalities was evident. Nelecar was a soldier with a great, and admirable, dedication to the purity of his kin. His efficient, though admittedly\n\nextreme, methods of protection allowed for a distinctly effective military force that has yet to be seen again within the walls of the mali'aheral. Ellir'siol was an artist, and an architect. Her mesmerising design of the Haelun’or residential district\n\nwas one of the finest examples of classical, revivalist mali'aheral architecture available, and is still emulated to this day.Touting overtures of peace, wisdom, and forethought, she strove only for what can be perceived as \"the greater good\" in regards\n\nto the motherland.\n\nDespite their own good intentions, there were many obvious faults in their actions throughout their years in the service of the cihi'thilln. Nelecar was not a forgiving mali. When a single, minor wrong was committed, he would often\n\nact harshly towards the perpetrator. One such case involved an insolent mali'aheral who refused the Okarir’s demands, and was summarily locked in a cell for nearly a month as punishment. His liberal use of banishment to punish wrongdoers was also a\n\nharshly debated affair, and was the main issue of the coming schism. Ellir'siol, however, was by contrast far too forgiving of wrongdoers. She often pressed for impure mali'aheral to be re-integrated to society, and those whose ideals were not\n\ncongruent with Larihei's vision were protected by her delicate, and clever political machinations. Retrospectively, conflict should have been predicted.\n\nThis is but a tale of two mali’aheral incapable of compromise, and such shall be shown in the second volume.", ""),
        StaticBook("Haelun'orSchism3", "Eenhoornheid", "The Haelun'or\n       Schism:\n     Volume III\n\n    by Lucion Sullas\n\nThe importance of the events that occurred during the Schism can not be trated lightly. The after-effectsm though, were seemingly mundance. With the immediate disappearance of Nelcar, the whole city simply fell to the leadership of what council was\n\nleft, althought Ellir obviously having significant inluence over proceedings.\n\nA detrimental effect of the Schism was the slow dissolution of what remained of the armed forces (then referred to as the 'Legion').\n\nThe size and ability of the soldiery was slowly whittled away, until only a few diligent guardsmen remained. Further affeted was the concept of violence in general-- the methods of the Okarir'sil no longer popular with any citzen.\n\nThe lacking of these two elements may explain why the mali'aheral nation had been a haven of peace for decade upon decade.\n\nOne is sure, however, that the reader is curious of the fate these two political rivals succumbed to.\n\nNelecar's disappearance did not last long, as he hid in the lowest levels of the city-- murdering numerous citizens over the course of multiple years. However, after nearly four decades of being hidden, the ragged Okarir'sil found his way into\n\nthe city, and stood in the square, confronting frightened and confused citizenry. His madness had overtaken himself, and, as a final act, he took his own life-- brokwn, thin, alone, and forgotten by history.\n\nEllir'siol, who strived all her life to keep all those whom she loved safe, lost her entire family. She herself turned bitter, and strayed away from the citizenry in her cold, lonely tower above the Eternal College. Eventually, she disappeard--\n\nalmost as if fading away with the end of her political career.\n\nLet this short history be a message of the dangers that are ingerent within the extremes of political rivalry, and extremist thinking. Should one desire to take message from these\n\nvolumes, then allow this humble writer to give you a final, resolyte statement. Rashness is rewarded only with destruction, and demagoguery is rewarded only with loss.", ""),
        StaticBook("The EXODUS", "§bAelidar Elsil'sirame", "The\nEXODUS\nof the\nAHERAL\n-*-*-*-:=-\nA recount of the\nevolution of High Elf\nculture amidst the\nLast Days of Anthos.\n-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-:=-\nas documented in the\nYEAR 1445, by\nAelidar Elsil'sirame\n\nThe FORERUNNERS\n-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-:=-\nIt began as The Grand\nHarvest crept upon\nthe Realm of Anthos. It\nbegan slowly; it began\nwith only a few, but\nthese few would be the\nfortunate ones.\nThey would leave of\ntheir own accord, and\nwith their own reasons.\n\nThe old Maheral known\nto elcihi as Lucion\nSullas would be the\nfirst to emerge from\nLin'evaral's gates,\nfrustrated and \nfurious. It is known\nthat this elf found\nfault with the present\nmentality among the\nmali'aheral. Those who\nwitnessed his noisy departure\n\n... into the forests of\nthe Old Princedom of\nMalinor, would remember\nhis condemnation of\nthe people of elcihi, \"A\ntain on the maehr'sae\nhiylun'ehya\".\nIn Sullas's eyes, logic\nhad left the mali'aheral.\nDespite the disdain\nwith which such an\nexit...\n\n...might have typically\nprovoked, his\ndissatisfaction would\nprove to be\ncontagious.\n\nOn the heels of Sullas\nscampered Lelien Lazul\nnée Aeléyèlsa, newly\nwedded to Acaele, and\nlibrarian of the\nEternal Library of\nLin'evaral. It seemed,\nhowever, that\nwhatever ties were\npresent, she felt\ncompelled to leave the\nvenerated silver city.\n\nIt was said that she\nintended to return to\nelcihi, but for now,\nMalinor was host to\nanother unwelcome\nguest.\n\nThe mali'aheral\nKalameet would make a\nmore disruptive exit. In\nleaving, he addressed\nletters to selected\nfriends, detailing his\ndisappointment in the\nillogical mentality of\nscorn and disdain\nwhich had permeated\nthroughout the aheral\ncommunity.\n\nThe letters of\nKalameet would\npresent a thoughtful\ndiscussion for each of\nthe mali'aheral. The\nstandards of purity,\nwhich had for so long\ngoverned the Enclave\nof Haelun'or, would be\ncalled into question.\nKalameet argued that\nthose considered\n'impure'...\n\n...should be treated\nwith more\nunderstanding than\nhad been the case;\n\"I've noticed many\nshun others from the\nwalls who offer things\nof value. Things that\ncan aid maehr'sae\nhiylun'ehya have been\npushed away because\nof citizens' deluded\nsense of Purity.\"\n\nThe DESTRUCTION\nof LIN'EVARAL\n-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-:=-\n\n~\n\n*a single scratch marks where the author had begun writing*\n\nI, Aelidar Elsil'sirame, presently find myself in no state to finish this documentation of my people's last days in Anthos. I ask, for the sake of our legacy, that one of my own complete this work.\n\n-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-*-\n*the book is unfinished*\n\n((I have a notepad doc. of this book, if you PM me on the forums: Aelidar))", ""),
        StaticBook("A Draft Speech I", "§bAndria Ith'ael", "Greetings Mali'thill ulliran,\n\nAyelmaehr'sae hiylun'ehya'Haelun'or I hereby withdraw my challenge for the position of Sohaer. I request that those who voted for me vote for who they truly believe will cause maehr'sae hiylun'ehya better\n\nthan I. I shall remain as Okarir'mali, provided Elheial'thilln let me. I wish to attend... \n\n[!]The writing seems to become messy before abruptly stopping", ""),
        StaticBook("The Plaque", "§bUnknown", "elmahnih'vallei biloke el iheiuhii'ame-- oem tayna ito haler tayna'ame ito elefer. ito puer'sae dionne'ne, indor'ne. heya'maehr'sae ito Eliheiuhii'ame. san'thill indor into Eliheiuhii'ame ehier elindor'lin'Malinor.", ""),
        StaticBook("Snow Elf Origin", "§b[Ghost] The Eternal Librarian", "Parasite\n       of the\n        Mind:\n   A Theory on\n    Snow Elven\n    Origination\n\n   by Asthil Haumel\n\nThe emergence of the mentally deranged snow elves, or \"Mali'fenn\" as they have named themselves, has been a subject of my thought for some time now. It is claimed by some of them that their kin were simply always in hiding or had forgotten their\n\nancestry, but there have never been any historical records mentioning Mali'fenn or any similar snow-dwelling elven populations. Immigration from distant and unknown lands is also unlikely, since the snow elves seem to possess intimate knowledge of\n\nthe history of our own lands. In my deliberations, I have been forced to propose that there is only one way for such a divergent and societally abstracted race of Mali could have emerged: a parasite of the mind.\n\nThe nature of the parasite eludess me: perhaps it is physical, as in this dramatic illustration. Or perhaps it is magical, or spiritual, or pathological as a disease or virus. Regardless of its status in reality, the effects of it can be seen clearly.\n\nSometime in the course of Anthosian history, a group of elves encountered the parasite, and became the first generation of hosts. Forced into action by the disease within them, the infected elves sought out other hosts and infected them as well. With an\n\ninitial population now existing, the memories and personalities of the mind-enslaved mali were devoured, replaced by implanted memories and coerced ideals.\n\nIn this, the sudden appearance of the snow elves is well-explained: once\n\nthey were ordinary elves, but have now been repurposed to a sinister cause: to follow the will of the virus and to spread it to as many potential elven hosts as possible. Indeed, on numerous occasions the 'Princedom of Fenn' has sent missives out across\n\nthe Fringe, calling upon the elven race to converge to them in the name of prosperity and unification. It is thought that notably few answered this call, but who is to say that some of our missing beloved did not seek them out an became the slaves of the\n\nsocial parasite?\n\nFrom this theory, the political offiliations of the snow elves are also well-explained. Their vehement hatred of Haelun'or and its associated elven settlements stems from a simple biological imperative: competition. The Silver City\n\nis the last remaining elven power aside from the snow elven movement: if they are to be destroyed, then there shall be none left to oppose the mali'fenn as being 'the' elven home.\n\nMoreover, the infamous words that 'Grand Prince' Thundrak\n\ngave to none other than Oren's own Emperor's face can also be explained as the parasite working with limited information. Likely it would have gleaned knowledge of the unfortunate conflict between elvenkind and humankind in Anthos from the minds of its\n\ninfected. In their deranged minds, the parasite would then conclude that Oren is a force that shall continue to destroy potential host-bodies, and so they must be stopped so that the mental infection may flourish.\n\nFurthermore, the incomprehensible relations that the snow elves have established with Alras and the dwarven nation of Urguan can also be interpreted in this light. It is well-known that there does exist a large population of elves that live within\n\nAlras: those of many, sometimes unsavory, character. Voracious in appetite for more host-bodies, the snow elves settled near Alras in order to have access to their prey, which otherwise remains safely locked away within Haelun'or. Relations with the\n\ndwarves who lived beneath the Alrasians became convenient, and perhaps that explains why an elven population began worshipping a dwarven deity. The culture was adapted to entice the dwarves into alliance, and they unknowingly were blinded by it.\n\nAfter the Alrasians began to resist and repel disturbances in their city lead by snow elves, the parasite decided that it must claim the elves of Alras by force. Thereafter, war was declared, and the world looked in awed fascination as the deranged\n\nsnow elves proceeded to insult the Emperor of Oren and then beg for forgiveness afterwards.\n\nThe ways in which this theory fits so well to the known events instills within me a great dread. For not only are the snow elves simply yet\n\nanother group of elves who descended into insanity, but they bring with them a slavery of the mind that they actively desire to spread to others. Indeed, I have seen with my own eyes an elf who became infected and began to act and speak disturbingly,\n\nbefore he left and was adapted into snow elven society.\n\nThe danger of this cannot be expressed enough: the very sanity and cognitive independence of the elven race is at stake here. The snow elves should not be treated as an asusement, or\n\napathy. Guard yourselves from their touch, mali.\n \nFor they are contagious.", "")
    )

    private fun _AXIOS_TAHN_8(): List<StaticBook> = listOf(
        StaticBook("Study: Sprites", "Valmir_", "=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=Valmir Synalli's study on oem bizzare creature found stowing away on a caliphatian ship. The creature was taken to the Eternal Library of Haelun'or to be studied by the librarian, these were his findings:\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=\n\nThe creature was small, so small in fact it could be lifted by myself with one hand and at the expense of very little effort. Presumably due to this small stature, its main mode of transport was flight... Which somewhat proved its humanoid legs useless\n\nbeyond idle standing and crossing distances short even by its own standards.\nHowever its method of flight was somewhat insectile; It did not glide or soar like a bird, it fluttered like a bee or butterfly and found itself fatigued before long.\n\nI therefore concluded that perhaps the rarity of these creatures (this being the first I had witnessed) was due to their poor adaptation to survive the environments around themselves. This one did with a nod of its cork-sized head that there were others\n\nlike it and that it was a natural creation. Using a similar method of answerism it went on to inform me it came not specifically from the desert of the south, the tundra of the north or the seas of the west but in fact the forrests of the easts from\n\nwhich the mali'ame hail. A location which, as a bright white and immobile creature, it would be made easy prey.\n\nWith that said, the creature seemed intelligent... At least moreso than most of its predators; It wore clothing fashioned\n\nfrom old leaves, somehow understood my common (though not mali) without being able to speak it itself and could use tools. To prove such I offered the sprite an emerald hidden within a bale of hay, beside which I had placed a fork. The creature used the\n\neating utensil to find the gem as opposed to its own hands... One could presume they have similar tools back home. Images of recycled thimbles and knitting needles arming a tiny militia come to mind! That said, even with its clothing I believe the\n\ncreature too small to bear environments with low temperatures, as observed with its desperation to keep quite the distance from my vial of freezing salve I sat beside it. Perhaps if anything it would be more suited to a desert environment... Its small\n\nstature would unlikely require much to stay hydrated and the predators of the sand are far less capable to harm beings in flight.\n\nMy hypothesis for such seemed rapidly rejected by the creature. Whilst it showed much less discomfort for a flame in\n\ncomparison to the freezing salve, it was intent on lingering close to my flower pot. Through some sort of interpretive dance (which to this day I have yet to see topped) the woodland being explained to be somewhat 'dependant' on the forrest, not through\n\nmeans of diet, though. \n\nAt first I assumed the creature whilst able to use tools must also be able to manipulate the magical world (see Blundermore's 'The Spirit of Magic') through voidal means. When informed otherwise I inquired if it was Druidic and\n\nrecieved the same response. All it could tell me in its limited communication was that it and my flower (presumably nature as a whole?) were one and that it had a dependancy of sorts on nature en masse. It was then the being that named itself 'Dax' left.\n\nSeeing the use in keeping the creature further as it had proven rather lacking in its lingual ability, I decided to venture to the grove of the Druidic order and see if they could further inform me.\n\nI found answers in a tome by the followers of the\n\naspects whom informed me that the being with which I had interacted was called a 'sprite'. Supposedly the beings are all of such tiny stature (I was told anywhere below a tweleve inches) and capable of flight, however they come in a multitude of colours,\n\nwhich sheds more light on why they're not extinct as it seems only 'Dax' was white and such an easy target for a predator (perhaps albanism?). The one thing I could not find from my research with the writings of former Druids and environmental enthusiasts\n\nis if and how the 'Sprite' sustains itself from nature as my subject had alluded.\nWhen working with such a scarce creature and inable to contact any of the Aspectist coven beyond their works collecting dust in Dragur, it has become apparent not all of my\n\nquestions can be answered by traditional means.\n\nPerhaps in future I shall complement this tome with my findings, or perhaps the Sprite's survival will simply remain a mystery of nature.\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=This book is property of the Eternal Library\n\nof Haelun'or and written with the intent of education for Maehr'sae Hiylun'ehya. It may be re-distributed on the terms of the current Tilruir'indor.\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=", ""),
        StaticBook("Dire Wolves", "§bArt Camoryn", "Dire Wolves\nD.O.C Euclid\n\nAppearance: \nMuch like in form to their canine cousins, the Timber or Lur wolves, however, Dire wolves are far larger, the size of bears. Their jaws are longer and fur thicker.\n\nBehaviour:\nMost notably, Dire wolves always travel in packs. Seldom will you find them on their lonesome. They are fiercely protective of their young. In combat A direwolf will take advantage of combat in number, using powerful hind legs and jaws to rip\n\nlimbs from your body, the size of it's body provides massive force for ramming, additionally.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ancient Elves", "§bRosso", "Once upon a time, long before the beginning of written history, the lands of the wilderness were inhabited by a single race: The Ancient Elves. This race was the first of Aegis. They built incredible structures in the lands of The Niben bay, and it’s\n\nsurroundings.\n\nThe Niben bay was a large body of water lying in The Niben Basin, largely east of the city of Hawkhaven. It lies across the Niben River, splitting it into two parts, and so is technically a lake with complex tunnels and districts, each\n\ndistrict with different ambience. They say you could feel one with the city, it was filled with statues of nature, exotic birds and wonderful animals… Including… Dragons.\n\nThe Ancient Elves learned how to befriend a Dragon, instead of taming them\n\nlike most races did. They knew that friendship was stronger than any force in the world. This Dragons shared their knowledge with them, they showed them the basics of energy manipulation that are still used now-a-days.\n\nThey soon found how to store energy in crystals and use them for many things. They used them to power their entire civilization, giving power and light to their cities.\n\nOne day, a new Dragonlord took power of the empire. His name was Akron.\n\nHe was foolish and always wished to have more power. He hated other races and considered them inferior. He soon tried to power stronger crystals for war. The Dragons refused and prevented him from doing so.\n\nAkron then called upon the forces of Iblees to drain the dragons’ power. The dragons, foreseeing their end, created the varla stone and powered it with The Dragonfires, sealing the gates to The Nether, and killing themselves…\n\nSome dragons remained as statues, petrified, and waiting to be awakened. They will wake up when the right time comes. After the beginning of the First Era, Akron began to damage nature and attempted to enslave other races. This marked the beginning of the\n\nGreat War wich lasted decades. Akron was finally defeated. To the fall of their Empire, humans took the power of the crystals. Their power turned them blind and foolish. They began to kill all survivors of The Ancient Elves and burned their cities to the\n\nground. Some of them managed to escape and survived.\n\nMany kings tried to restore their old glory but failed. Without the power of the Dragonfires, they weren’t able to do such thing.\n\nThey say that if you look closely to the sunrise, you can still see the ruins of Garlas Malathar, waiting to be discovered, and raised once again…\n\n-Aedan, Dragonlord of the Last Ancient Elves.\nIrba, weth of the Second Seed, 1314\n\nAncient Elves\n\nWritten by Aedan\nRetold by Leyu’Maehr\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven", ""),
        StaticBook("-Fire Evocation-", "§bGarsto 'Spark' Leaf", "{-----------------}         Fire Evocation\n{-----------------}This book is about Fire evocation.\n\nThe Fire Evocationists conjure appears to be real. But it is fake. One does not have power over the elements. For an Evocationist to conjure fire fr\n\nvoid they have to understand the element in the real/physical world first. The easiest way of bringing Fire into the physical world would be to light a torch by using Evocation or by creating a fire which then lights a fire. As a mage becomes more\n\nexperienced they can eventually form balls of Fire which they could shape into objects of Fire.\n\n((The Next part of this book is OOC. Its mainly for ones who already know Magic but want to Emote it. It is OOC. Remember that...\n\nGarsto is focused on a spot on a hay bale in front of him.\n\nis twitching a tad with his eyes locked on the position of the hay bale. He is muttering something. \n\nA tiny faint orange flame appears on the hay bale.\n\nThe flame gets smaller and blows itself out.\n\nGarsto falls back into a chair in fatigue.\n\nThat was an example of a Tier 1.\n\nThe next is Tier two.\n\nGarsto is concentrating on an unlit torch in front of him.\n\nHis forehead is sweating and he is breathing heavily.\n\nA Spark flickers on the coal in the torch.\n\nThe Spark lights the coal which results in the torch being lighted.\n\nGarsto's kitchen is now lit by the torch.\n\nStumbles in Fatigue.\n\nThat was a Tier Two. Next is a Tier Four.\n\nGarsto is concentrating with his hand outstretched towards the cow.\n\nHe begins to breath heavily and his forehead is sweating.\n\nHe mutters something. Flames erupt from his hand spinning into a shape.\n\nThe Shape looks something like a small dagger. Its not clear though.\n\nOpens his eyes and mutters something sending the fiery dagger towards the cow. \n\nThat was all OOC remember.))\n\nIts suggest that you meditate to help your self connect to the void. This can help you improve and go through tier 1.  At Tier two the user is able to hit a target most of the time. At the end of tier two the mage will be experimenting with conjuring Fire\n\nballs. Its said by Tier three the mage can conjure small fire balls successfully which they can conjure as Explosive or just normal. They can also vary the temperature of their fire. \n\n((Ooc: If you dont emote Fatigue you aren't doing it right.))\n\nUnless you are tier five and you are conjuring a small Spark. Even if you are Tier five you can still have Fatigue depending on how big the flame. Attempting to summon white hot flames. eah, not a great idea. You would die of heat. Or lack of Mana.\n\nSummoning fire from a distance takes time. ((Ooc: Summoning fire ON or BENEATH someone isn't allowed.)) You cant mold flames until you start to reach the end of Tier two. Even then you can only just about mold it into a ball of fire. With difficulty.\n\n((Ooc: Controlling a flame that is in the Physical world and was made in the Physical world, not drawn from the void would be considered power gaming.)) You will have increased heart rate, breathing heavily and will still get sweaty at levels above tier 3\n\nFrom personal experiences I learnt that when pulling from the void it feels like 'tugging a rope to pull something from nothingness.'\n\nI hope this book helped you with what you were looking for about Fire Evocation.\n/Signed Garsto Leaf./\n\n((Ooc: If I find you use this for some description wether its for the lore of your magic application or anything else. I will make a comment on your post saying its a copy of what I wrote origionally for when I tried to apply for magic.))", ""),
        StaticBook("Evocation: Basic", "SerahLove", "In order for Sorcerers to put into use their craft, they must have a deep attunement with and knowledge of their element. A caster must know that fire begins with friction and the many stones that make up the earth. A common rule of thumb is:\n\n\"If the Sorcerer does not know how something functions, he cannot make it work himself.\" This simply dictates that a mage MUST know how water flows and how wind gusts, just as much as he must know how fire feels to the touch. Due to this, sorcerers often\n\nspend a great deal of time in study, on a deep spiritual level, of their respective element(s). Despite this rigorous training, their spells may still deal them harm, just as much as it may harm their opponent.\n\nEvocation is often quite physically and\n\nmentally taxing on the Sorcerer- as it takes a great deal of mana to cast. The larger the object one wishes to bring forth, the more mana is required of him to cast the spell. And just as any other magical branch of the arcane tree, the mage must be\n\ncapable of envisioning where he is casting, and at what- that he must first visualize the spell itself, in his own mind, in order to bring forth its full fury. Therefore, a mage cannot simply conjure a fireball behind a wall, nor can he cause a wave of\n\nwater to appear in an area so distant- so beyond the caster's field of vision. Due to this law, Sorcerers are limited in some regard- hindered by the reaches of their sight.\n\nDespite the seemingly passive nature that any one mage may seem to possess, they\n\nhowever remain filthy adominations- dangerous and unholy grotesqueries- who must be promptly taught that such profane powers can bring no good, and must be wholly forgotten, for the sake of every man, woman, and child- for all who walk upon God's blessed\n\nearth.\n\n\n    The Basics of Evocation\n\n\n\n\n\n              - Arthur Versluis\n\n   Revisions by E.S. and A.F.", ""),
        StaticBook("Origins of Magic", "§bValmuel Elibar'acal", "In a normal perspective, energy is taken from the void and projected into anything the caster may like. Take it be a Fireball or a wall of solid ice! Of course, there are many ways one could direct this energy from the void. However, we will be delving on\n\nhow exactly it works! Now, to begin, we must remember that all magic is of void origin, and it all must return to the void. \n\nThere are a few exceptions to this rule, such as the Druids, of which they're magic leaves\na lasting effect\n\non the world. This can be done by using the energy granted by such forces to move the objects appropiate to the magic they are using. This would pertain to nature, obviously. So it is not the magic that stays in the world, but rather the object\n\ninfluenced by the magic. With this being said, after a spell is cast and the connection to the spell is lost, it will return to the void. This is a quick and natural practice, however those who have trained to utilize this process can be\n\nknown as users of Fi\nhiiran'tanya. The entire idea of this, is that one would open up an immediate \"portal\" of which the mana returns instantly, to the caster's will or not. Only mana can pass through this portal.\nThe entire explanation of this shall be\n\ncovered shortly. THe reason I am bringing this up is because I wish to explain a large concept, and perhaps, truth. All magic comes from the void, even Deity/Source magic. The reason this is, is because we as mortals have difficulty drawing from the void.\n\nAlthough as powerful as we may conceive ourselves to be, our access over the void is somewhat limited. That is why some turn to Deities/Source Users, who have much more power.\n\nPower brings ability. These have the ability to draw upon\n\nthe void in ways we cannot imagine. That is where holy magic, and perhaps dark magic, may originate. In a basic sense, it is casted in a sense to \"smite\" the mana, to return it to the void swiftly.\n\nOne could argue that is exactly the conflict\n\nof which these users face. Whether it is holy or dark is not the question, but perhaps different ways of expelling the magic to the void? \n\nAs I was saying, Deities/Source Users can cast in ways we cannot do. It brings in possibilities\n\nof Druid Magic, Holy Magic, Dark Magic, Kha Magic, and other forms. However, all of it is drawn from the void. With the immense power of these beings, one could mold and shape the mana in different ways we could never do.\n\nIn recent events,\n\nwe have witnessed some interesting things happen to the progression of magic. The Dark Lord Setherien, the Leader of the Scourge, fuels his servants with his power. Now, this may seem normal as other Aenguls or Daemons might do. However, there is one key\n\npoint. Setherien is mortal! Atleast, he is a physical form. The potential knowledge from this is unlimited. If one could give another magic, if powerful enough, in the physical plane, why can't others do it?\n\nThis is a pure concept, and not to be taken\n\nentirely serious just yet. However, the possibilities are endless, if there is no restriction in the physical plane. Could one possibly amass enough power to give another his/her abilties? Again, I do not know the answer to this. It is all speculation.\n\nIn conclusion to this small essay, magic in of itself is highly peculiar, and much of it we still do not know about. However, I hope you have had a stronger grasp of it's origins and capabilities, even though I personally do not recommend it.\n\n- Valmuel Elibar'acal", ""),
        StaticBook("Light Illusion", "§bCorvo", "Illusion from what we know is the use of the void to create images into one’s mind. How this is done is simple infact, First one must connect to the endless realm that is the void, A realm of nothingness and at the same time the Void is everything\n\nwe so wish it to be. Now let us continue, With the use of the Void we as Illusionists are able to draw an image within it, Whether it be a small petal or that of a gigantic demon.  Once an Illusionist has done so they must simply connect to the mind\n\nof their opponent and place the image that they have made into their mind. Allowing them to see what you infact have seen.  However one must note that whatever you are to create you must have seen or experienced in your life,\n\nYou can simply create that of a demon however what is the point of you do not know how it sounds or feels.  One is of course able to make simple images from what they can think of in their own mind\n\nas well it is possible to simply add a feeling or sense to said image. Now in order to start learning such a power that is Illusion people tend to start the same as most Mages, Connection to the Void.\n\nTo do this one must simply empty their mind of all thoughts thus creating a link to the Void, this is mainly done through the simple means of mediation. However some are unlucky in their attempts to connect as some might be rejected from it.\n\nWhen one is rejected by the Void they may feel a small headache occur or even be knocked out from intense pain. Now once one has connected to the Void they simply draw an image in it and thus try to place that image into another entity's mind.\n\nSome at this stage tend to try and place the image into the mind’s of animals as an animal's mind is weak and far too stupid to even figure out that what they are seeing or feeling is an Illusion.\n\n Once the simple sense of images has been completed Illusionists begin to head towards the Illusions of senses, Pain and touch. This is where some Illusionist find things to be tricky, As an Illusionist must feel and how what they are creating feels,\n\n that intends one must be able to survive intense pain if they are so wishing to create it. To perform these sensations. it is simple they must simply draw the image of pain inside the Void and place it into that of their target.\n\nSimilar to how creating images of Light work. After the Illusionist has played with senses they may now head towards their end, The Illusion of emotions. This is quite hard to do in fact. To perform it they do the same as before,\n\n however it is much more difficult to perform as if one feels happy than angry the next moment it won’t make logical sense as they will know it is an Illusion and it shall break.  The simple way to do this is to ease the Illusion in silently,\n\nHow this works is if one was happy one second, it is quite easy to cause them to feel the emotion of love the next. This works quite well however from what we know emotions can not be forced upon the target only eased in.\n\n As such we come to an end and now I leave the rest to you, The holder of this book. \n\n((For the Light Illusion Subtype))", ""),
        StaticBook("Mana Gem S/R-1", "§bTyrell Blavier", "Mana Gem Research:\n\n-A mana gem is part of enchanting, and supplies mana to an enchantment. It must be charged by an enchanter. \n-After awhile it loses it's charge, and needs to be charged with mana again.\n\nI talked to Mark Volaire xXMark123Xx about these gems and the theory of mana gems and being used for energy distribution back into us. He suspected this would not be possible, however suggested the idea of using them on Golems.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Elements", "§bJerry_Man", "PROLOGUE\nThis book was recovered from Aegis and is dated around 1378 of the last age of Aegis. The author has since dissappeared from existence.\n\n          WATER\nThe neccesity of life. All animals, plants, and even we, the races of Aegis, need it to survive. Water can often be associated with emotion, art, time, and especially healing.\n\n         EARTH\nIt is the first thing you need for almost everything, to ensure stability and endurance. Earth is a reliable element, peaceful and tranquil. Earth is the most passive and strong element, although it can be quite destructive.\n\n           AIR\nIt is invisible, unpredictable, unreliable, soothing, inspiring, and destructive. Air is the representation of everything we cannot see; our spirits, our souls, our minds, and our hearts.Of that what lies beneath, or beyond some\n\nscholars say. The unpredictable and changing wind can become a devestating Hurricane, or a playful breeze.\n\n            FIRE\nIt has been an important part of all cultures and religions in the past to modern day Aegis. Fire is bright, strong, takes charge, and wants control. Fire is never subtle, it proudly shows itself. Fire never stops unless acted upon by\n\n another element or force, and it consumes everything it touches.\n\n  To Become A Mage\n There is much an apprentice must learn before becoming a Mage. Your Guild Master may send you on quests of sorts, but I prefer to study in the Library, the source of much information. Here, you can learn everything you need: ...\n\nThe Elements, Self-Control, Mage Powers, and much more. This is the first part in become a Mage, studying. You need to learn all you can from your experiences and readings. Once an Elven Week has passed, possible longer, you will be asked to gather\n\n materials. These will be used to create your staff. The particular ingredients in your staff will cause a different outcome,  than if you were to use different materials. This Staff you create will be specially connected with you, your spirit\n\nenergy will be infused with the energy of the staff, thus the connection. Once you have earned your staff, you will become a Mage, and be given a job within the guild.", ""),
        StaticBook("Wisdom Theorem", "§bAevalyn", "§0  Theorem of Wisdom§0\n§0\n§0       Haelun'or,§0\n§0          Axios§0\n§0\n§0    Published 1593§0\n§0\n§0\n§0  by Godwein Stafyr§0\n§0   Transcribed by§0\n§0  Aevalyn Bethiros\n\nWhat is Wisdom?§0\n§0\n§0Wisdom is not easy to define, likely similar to eternity. Every person has its own views of wisdom.\n\nTo elaborate this further, a row of examples.§0\n§0Is knowledge wisdom? Perhaps.§0\n§0Is knowing the right thing to do wisdom? Perhaps.§0\n§0Is doing something good wisdom? Perhaps.§0\n§0Is learning wisdom? Perhaps.\n\nAs aforementioned you cannot simply define wisdom. Wisdom is a multitude, colorful, differently shaped. Wisdom can and does exist in many of us. To different degrees, in different shapes.\n\nA forester can have the wisdom of knowing how to treat his plants and trees properly, when to grow what to reach the best result. As can the farmer.\n\nA sorcerer or wizard can know a lot about the divine art of magic and has through this his very own wisdom.\n\nStill, one would say, that in these example wisdom turns out to be rather knowledge, which is only partially right, if it’s right at all.\n\nWisdom requires more than simply having knowledge. Wisdom requires understanding the spirit, the being, the living and many things more.\n\nThe inner peace and silence may bring wisdom. On the other side, a warrior may achieve a new level of combat by finding his inner stability in combat. To be calm in the face of the storm and evil.§0\n\nAs we now scratch the evil, the darkness, I wish to bring in a small part of my very own opinion. Wisdom doesn’t exist in people who are simply evil and work for their very own profit. Wisdom requires selflessness as well.\n\nYou may now think I am taking the side of the good here, solely. It is right. I do align myself to the good and the light, but must say, that I may be completely wrong with my statement about wisdom and the evilness here.\n\nPerhaps the other side may have their own kind of wisdom, being evil as it is.§0\n§0\n§0Is it wisdom to write a book about wisdom? No.§0\n§0Is it wisdom to explain what wisdom can be? Perhaps.\n\nWhy perhaps? Because wisdom is wisdom. Colorful and different. Not definable. That is wisdom.", ""),
        StaticBook("Illusion", "§bLyu Elibar'acal", "Illusion\n                  ~@~\n         A study of magic\n        by Lyu Elibar'acal\n                  ~@~\n\nMany non-mages wonder over the spectacular spectacles that an Illusionist can create, I did so myself, at one point. So I set my mind unto finding out the mysteries of Illusion magic. Now, as with all arcane arts, a user of Sensory, or as some call it-\n\n- Light, Illusion users must have and maintain a connection to the void. I will not go over the process in which one does this, as this is a study of Illusion, not the void. Now, Illusion allows a mage to form a sensory stigma in the void and pull it\n\nthrough into the real world. As with all magic though, it has its limits. As my source of Illusion knowledge has explained to me, it must follow three basic guidelines or rules. The first being that it must be logical. This means that it must make sense,\n\nfor example, making a giant monster appear from nothing would cause the illusion to break more easily. The second rule, is that an illusion is just a stigma placed in the mind, and thus is not physical. At last the third and\n\nfinal basic concept is that you must have experienced the thing that you are illusioning. For example, you could not illusion a rabbit if you have never seen a rabbit. \n\nNow, that is enough about the basic rules, let us enter into the interesting realm of\n\nillusion and discuss the types of illusion that a mage can make. \nFirstly, there are glamours, when in one alters an already existing object with illusion, an example being changing one's eye or hair color, or creating a disguise.\nNext there are\n\nfigments, an illusion made entirely new, not altering somethig existing. An example of such would be illusioning a fog rolling in, or an animal running out of a bush.\nFinally, we have phantasms, the most comples and hardest to execute illusions, this is\n\nbecause they do not alter your external senses, but instead, they affect your emotions. The reason phantasms are so hard to do correctly is due to the fact that they must be logical, as all illusions must be, and thus must mesh well with the target's\n\npersonality, lest it break. Now, Phantasms are performed as such: An illusionist forms an emotion or emotional stigma and offers it to the target's mind, usually to exact a certain behavior. A phantasm will not be able to\n\neasily cause an Uruk to feel compassion, or a pacifist, rage. \n\nAnd with such, this tome comes to a close, but first, I shall like to thank my source for all such knowledge, Riondil Seni, he constanly poured information over me for this book,\n\nso I give credit where credit is due.\n\n\n\n                   ~@~\n                    Fin\n                   ~@~", ""),
        StaticBook("Conjuration", "§bHaadi Mubdee", "=--=--=--=--=--=--=           Conjuration\n=--=--=--=--=--=--=\n\nAh, Conjuration; the rarest Arcane Art known… well, it won’t be rare anytime soon. Of course, once you begin to learn the art, you will be referred to as a “Conjurer”. A conjurer is an individual who access the Void and construct living organisms which\n\nare fueled wholly by the mana of their creator. Through the depths of the void, a Conjuration user will be able to summon a mighty creatures of placid flowers into reality. Alas, as a conjurer you will be constantly delivering your mana for virtually\n\nvery second your ersatz summon exists. You will soon come to a realisation that Conjuration uses the most of your mana unlike any other Arcane Science. In order to uphold the existence of your faux creature in reality, you must feed it prescisely a great\n\nportion of mana to flow within it. As the creature you summon will be downright complex, the process of building it within the Void will be rather tiresome and there is a good chance you may be drained completely of your mana until you reach\n\nunconsciousness, as that happens, your beast will be removed from the physical realm and be sent back to the Void. Yes, you can decide to desist the flow of mana beforehand, in which case the fauna will vanish to no consequence. Now, for you to construct\n\nan organism within the Void, you must first have a sharp and great understanding of both the interior and exterior of the living you wish to formulate. This can be achieved by oftenly killing and dissecting the creature you wish to summon. However, it is\n\nalso best to keep very detailed anatomical diagrams of the beast you wish to conjure. Remember to have note son the animal’s exterior, interior and bone structure if you want to have an accurate understanding of it. In the opaque Void, the fabrics which\n\ngenerate the entity are summoned bone by bone, layer by layer, muscle by muscle and organ by organ the Conjurer crafts and sculpts their being into an exact copy of their previously existing self. As a result of poor research, the Conjurer may fail at\n\nthis point, producing a being unlike that of its true and real form will result in an uneven and revolting entity which would certainly turn to a sludge once conjured. After a while of practice, the Conjurer would be able to summon the creature exactly\n\nhow it was before it was slain and have it formed with sheer stability. As it will behave akin to the life form it represents, it will follow its creator’s commands. As a Conjurer myself, I would highly recommend you begin to study roses, then petite\n\ninsects until you grow stronger and confident enough to move on to study and conjure anything larger. Think of Conjuration as art. You are the painter and the Void is the frame. What you paint in that frame will need to look beautiful and make sense in\n\nthe judge’s eyes. Bear in mind that the “judge” is the physical realm. If the judge thinks your work of  art is good, it will be accepted and be brought into reality. However, if it’s horrible and ugly, it will be stamped on and crushed and it will appear\n\nas an abomination in the mortal plane, you will be smacked in the face for your poor efforts, that smack will tire you. Keep practicing and your artistic skills will improve and the judge will begin and continue to accept your art. Overtime, your frame\n\nwill expand and leave you open to produce which the judge can view. Be warned though, the larger the harder. And yes, you can fabricate hybrid fauna. I’ll leave that for you to grasp the concept of that alone.\n\nAuthor: Unknown\nCopied by Guildmaster Haadi Mubdee of the Mages Guild, 1461.", ""),
        StaticBook("Game of Minds C3", "§bSofetios Jayamen", "A Game of Minds\n\nA fictional adventure series.\n\n          ~By Sofetios\n               Jayamen\n\n      Chapter Three\n        The Arrow\n\nAfter about five minutes of riding, I began to feel cold and weak. I looked down to the arrow in my leg, for some reason it was already clotted over with the blade of it still in my leg.\n\nThe man in front of me noticed my shift in weight and looked at the arrow to for a second. Then he cursed, looked over to Jed who was riding beside us and said, “Stop the horse!” Both Jed’s horse and the horse I was on came to a stop.\n\n The man got off and motioned me to get off. I got off as he helped me down and Jed got off his as well.\n\n“What are we stopping for?” Said Jed impatiently.\n\n“The arrow.” Said the man.\n\n“The arrow can wait!” Said Jed. “It’s already clotted over anyways.”\n\n“Exactly why we need to stop.” Said the man. He took the horses rein with one hand and helped me walk with the other.\n\n Jed followed behind us with his horse as we went into the thick woods on the side of the path we were on.  We were walking in the woods for a bit when the man said, “Do you feel cold? Weak as well?”\n\n“Actually yes.” I said a bit worried. “Isn’t that normal for an arrow wound?” The man cursed under his breath then said,\n\n“No, not this fast at least.”  I looked at him and said,\n\n“What do you mean?” The man sighed and said,\n\n“The blood already clotting on your leg isn’t a good thing, It also isn’t just a coincidence. The clotting allows the blood to circulate unhindered.”\n\nI put the pieces of information together, then said,\n\n“Are you saying I’ve been…” \n\n“Poisoned, yes.” Said the man seriously.\n\n I panicked and reached down to the arrow to grasp it and pull it out, but the man grabbed my hand before I could do it.\n\n “It’s best to leave it in for now, trust me.”\n\n“What are we going to do!?” I half said half yelled. The man didn’t say anything for a few seconds then said,\n\n“We are going to see an old friend of mine.” I nodded slightly, regaining my wits as we walked on into the woods.\n\nWe were still walking in the woods until I spotted a wooden hut with a straw roof. By now I was freezing and stumbling even though it was sunny out and I had the mans support.\n\nThe man tied both horses to a tree then we went toward the hut. \n\n We came to its entrance and the man peeked inside. There was no one there.  “Era?” The man said. “You in here?”\n\n We walked a bit into the hut on the wooden floor until there was a crunch of paper under the man’s foot. \n\nThe man frowned and picked up the paper, but as he did, there was a quiet ‘snap’ and a ‘rip’.\n\nA piece of the paper was torn off by something.  Then I saw it.  It was being dragged fast by a small piece of string. \n\n“MOVE!” The man yelled. He basically ran and dragged me out at the same time.\n\nJed was right behind us. We were about 30 feet from the hut when I felt heat from behind me. We slowed down, stopped, and turned around. The hut was in flames.\n\n I turned to the man and saw he had fear and anger in his eyes.\n\n He looked quickly at the paper. Then he jammed it into a ball, yelled, and threw it at the fire. The man was breathing heavily in anger, Then as he started to calm down,  he said in a lower voice, “They have her…”  Neither Jed or I said anything,\n\n Until the man turned his head a bit to me and said, “I’m sorry, but you are most likely going to pass out with the poison.”\n\n“You mean I’m not going to die?” I said.\n\n“No, you’re not.” Said the man, “He, wants you alive.” For some reason I felt that passing out would be worse than dying. \n\n“There’s got to be another way to get something to cure the poison.” The man shook his head and said,\n\n“The only other place that would have the poison’s cure is Era’s Stash. Even I’ve never found it.” The hut was nearly burnt to the ground now.\n\n“Then lets go!” Said Jed, “We can put James on the horse, get out of here, and find someone else to give the cure!”\n\n“Don’t you understand.” Said the man sadly.\n\n“We’re most likely surrounded, or soon to be. That trap wasn’t meant to kill us, it was meant as a signal.” These words made me feel more chilled then the poison did. I turned to the hut once again. Then, I saw it.\n\nThe hut had burnt to the ground, but also the wooden floor that was there, what was left was the outline of a square on stone. \n\n“Look!” I said pointing to the remains. The man looked and his eyes got wider.\n\n“Could it…” He started saying but never finished. “Let’s move!” We went as fast as we could toward the remains but I could barely walk. When we got there, the man pulled out his long dagger and fit it between the crack where the outline was.\n\nThen used it like a lever lifting the square indent. As it rose I got a strong smell of herbs coming from underneath. The man held the stone square up and told Jed to help me down. There was a steep stepping ladder going down.\n\nAs I descended with Jed’s help I noticed bottles and beakers in the faint lighting as well as their shadows against the wooden plank walls. There were also cabinets against the wall as well as a large wooden bookcase, except there were beakers on it.\n\nWhen we got to the floor, the man came after us. Jed sat me down on an old wooden chair that was there as the man went though cabinets and bottle looking for the right one.\n\n The man ran to me carrying a small bottle, a piece of cloth, a leaf, and a piece of leather. He opened the bottle and whispered, “Brace yourself, this will hurt. It is very important you don’t make noise.” He gave me the piece of leather and said,\n\n“Bite on this.” I did so. He grasped the arrow and carefully but quickly took it out, along with a bit of the clotted blood. I grunted in pain and bit down hard on the leather as it was removed and the blood started rushing out.\n\nHe then quickly poured some of the liquid in the bottle on the wound. It felt like lava as it fizzed and bubbled. I felt like I was getting stronger and warmer at the same time but it was still painful.\n\n Then the man after pouring more on quickly put the leaf on the wound and wrapped the cloth around it, tying it in place. As soon as the leaf made contact with the wound I felt a rushing feeling of relief, as if the leaf was absorbing the pain.\n\n  After the man was done, he whispered,  “That should do it. Now we better get out of here and fast. Can you stand?” I tried standing up, I was a bit wobbly but much better than before. “Good.” whispered the man to Jed and I.\n\n“They are probably waiting above us as we speak. Now our only chance is to find an alternative exit. Era would most likely have made a side tunnel down here. Let’s get to looking.”  We then dispersed around the room quietly. \n\nEnd of Chapter Three\n\nThank you for taking the time to read this. I'll be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing, send me a bird, or tell me in person!\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen\n((Sofetios))", ""),
        StaticBook("Lady Kyra Leigh", "§bKyra Leigh", "Warmth, tranquility. \nI am surrounded by the spirits of the forest.\nThese magnificent creatures, tower over me, as if they were protecting me, as I do for them.\nI am in darkness, sitting in the folds of the roots of the trees. Everything is moving.\n\nThe moving water off the cliff, the wind blowing through the trees, rustling the leaves. \nAs I sit in the meadow, I see a beautiful old Elk, resting next to a large oak tree. The image is unbelievable. He lays there still, slowly breathing, in and out.\n\nI am now lost. It is dark. The sounds of the waterfall have since faded away. All is quiet now. All I hear are the chirpings of the small creatures in the flora.\n\nI have discovered...\n\nPerfection.", ""),
        StaticBook("Sutica", "§bErolas Ba'ikana", "very huge sewer system underground, good for moving through undetected\n\ntowards the middle of the city there are alot of shops\n\na real big church lies to the back of the town,\n\nArmy command sits to the rear end of the city, with sutica prison and a bunch of jail cells built into it\n\nThe ministry of interior seems to be right next door\n\nMinister William Corvinus' office right across the street", ""),
        StaticBook("Song Anthology 3", "§bAeran", "Song Anthology: Volume 3\n\nThe Maiden of Alrash\n\nThe Great Pumpkiness\n\nThe Money-Grubbing Dwarf\n\nThe Maiden of Alrash\n\nA maiden from Alrash/\nThe subject of my wonder/\nAnd also the rash/\nThat is down under/\n\nFatbuttum Silverblade/\nSecond of her name/\nAlthough I'm not afraid to admit I paid/\nI still felt flayed/\n\nAnd on the morrow/\nMy wallet empty, my needs fulfilled/\nTo my horror, I gaze upon her/\nMy eyes were unskilled/\n\nThe ale must have gotten to me/\nFor that was no wench/\nIt was King Syrio, pretty as a flea/\nBut oh, his stench!\n\nThe Great Pumpkiness\n\nMy father came up to me one day/\nHe said 'Son, I hope you won't have to pay/\nFor in my life I've sinned, true to God/\nBecause one day, I messed with a pumpkin broad/\n\nI can remember that night so clearly/\nBetter than the night I met your mother, although I do love her dearly/\nTwo strangers came by/\nSo frightful, nearly made me cry/\n\nA pumpkin on each other their heads/\nI had to make sure I was not on meds/\n\nWhere are those, anyways?/\nI talk in rhymes without them... Are they in the entry way?/\n\nBut I dress/\nOne wore a pretty dress/\nDarker than your uncles soul/\nEven if it was dipped in coal/\n\nThe other was a man, so I did not care/\nThe nicest woman I've ever met, I swear/\nMy heart flutter, my knees quiver/\nBut I could not look at her without a shiver/\n\nSo I knew what must be done/\nI knew it was not a good idea, in the long run/\n\nBut I gave that pumpkin a yank!/\nAnd she pulled out a shank!/\n\nThat's why they call me One-Ball Jon/\nFor one of my eyeballs, is gone/\nSo if you see the Pumpkiness/\nJust stare at the dress, not her breats!\n\nThe Money-Grubbing Dwarf\n\nThere was a dwarf, his name unknown/\nHis beard would morph, when the wind had blown/\nHe came across, one fateful eve/\nA she-dwarf skillled at basket-weave/\n\nThey wed that week, little did her know/\n\nShe was antique, and very old./\nShe was however weel-endowed,/\nIn more ways than one, so when she bowed/\n\nMore was seen, than was decent/\nLet's keep it clean, her wealth came recent/\nHe father'd died the night before/\n\nBeen murdered by a pretty whore./\n\nTo him she left all that she owned,/\nSo when she'd die, her love'd not groan/\nFor he'd inherit all she had,/\nHer money'd rid him of the sad.", ""),
