        StaticBook("Coven Inspection", "§bUlaroth", "Assessment of           the Iblees Coven\n\n           Ularoth\n\nOur trek begins within an abandoned complex carved beneath the outskirts of Alras. Upon our entry into the quaint cavern, we heard distinctly nothing; The dwelling has been long abandoned. Left standing in the strange pit of offal and rock appears to\n\nbe a heavyset table of lackluster quality. It sounds of splintering wood, its innards eaten away by woodworms and their ilk. The chairs that accompany the furnishing are in just as awful a shape. The thought of a gathering of naive cultists squirming\n\nuncomfortably in their seats amuses us. Upon the table rests a tattered board that sounds and tastes of driftwood, no doubt fished from a river. Its edges seem caked with a thin grime of taint, once carved intricately in beguiling sigils now rests on the\n\noutskirts of the plank in unsightly blobs of vile. Letters are etched in the board's surface, scrawled lazily in three strokes of a dull pike, at most. Wedged in a hastily dug pocket of stone lies a shape of wood, cut from the same as the board, with a\n\nglassen port wedged center-most.\n\nMoving away from the table, We've noticed a hallway-esque expanse to our right, whereupon a mound of soot rests. It smells of bone, the intense heat that surely singed a mound of bodies that once resided where\n\nthis dust dwells seemed hot enough to dissapate fetid flesh into vapor. The numerous rats that have since taken refuge in this place seem to avoid this mound of dead. We too are vaguely offput by it.\n\nFurther down the hallway resides nothing but a sturdy end, accompanied by a stoney pillar which bares no intrigue.\n\nResiding in the midst of this place are several chests which reek of rot. Further inspection has rendered the bins\n\nvastly unintriguing, as only decomposing plantlife dwells within.\n\nThe cavern-like expansive to our left has been bored into thrice, all along the right walls. We may dwell within this place after our inspection is completed. The three hovel-sized\n\nrooms contain a stark nothing. Further meandering has caused us to come across a single room dug into the left of the wall; our apologies.\n\n\nDeeper in the cavern is a strange chamber, slathered hastily with sigils drawn of offal.\n\nIts centre seems to hold a forge of strange architecture, with a shallow indent carved in the floor just about a beam of glass. One would best compare this to something of Urukai construction, albeit of rock. The small chamber remains barren apart from\n\nthe strangness previously mentioned. We shall scribe the glyphs that adorn the walls after the entirety of our assessment.\n\nSo to will we scrawl the sigils drawn into the floor with the same gore found amoungst the 'ritual' chamber.\n\n-A strange sigil comprised of a circle trapping many a piece of odd geometry is drawn here in a thick, piney sludge. The entirety of the glyph seems to resemble some sort of crucifix turned on its head-\n\nThe most daunting of the subterranian abode's sigils. Its relative size dwarfs the rest of its siblings. It resides on the leftmost wall of the assumed ritual chamber. It evokes a calm within us; something reassuring.\n\n-Another glyph is drawn, in the same ichor no less. This one vaguely depicts a conjuring fire, which a primative body lurches over menacingly. Four lines impale the otherwise lackluster rune, adorn at their ends with what appear to be eyes-\n\nThis dwells the backmost wall of the ritual chamber. Its paint-thick fluid seems to grasp more particulate than most. We believe it to be soot, or fine sand. Its texture is bone dry, and has chipped to our louder inspections.\n\n-Yet another sigil is scribed in piney ichor, accompanied by occasional streaks of ash. Its relative shape is all but a mystery, wavering this way and that with bizzare connections linking the irredeemably baffling structure together-\n\nWe're not sure what to make of this, its quite oddly placed. It lies near the central support beam of the cave. We can't stand to hear it much, it prods at us with a sense of unease.\n\nThe remainder of the scrawlings we've deemed uninteresting. They are far too simple to rival the three we've already found. If someone wishes, you may contact ourselves to delve into a further study of this place.", ""),
        StaticBook("Game of Minds C1", "§bSofetios Jayamen", "A Game of Minds\n\nA fictional adventure series.\n\n           ~By Sofetios\n                 Jayamen\n\n      Chapter One\n         Awakening\n\nPulsing. That is all I felt as I started to see again. Everything was a hazy black, even though my eyes were open. Another pulse happened inside my head. Things were beginning to become clearer.\n\nI could make out that I was in a small stone room… and there was something else in here with me. \n\n“Finally awake are we?” said a gruff voice.\n\n I could now see moderately well and saw a person sitting across from me looking straight at me. I coughed and said slowly, \n\n“Where am I?” My head still throbbing though getting better. The person then said,\n\n“You’re in the holding chamber, along with me.”\n\n“Holding chamber?” I said. “Holding chamber for what?”\n\nAt that moment there was a slight creak to the right, as a part of the wall opened up like a door.\n\nThe person got up and came to me, holding out a hand. He said,\n\n“We had better get going. They don’t like it when we stall.”\n\n I grasped his hand and he hoisted me to my feet. I nearly fell over but he helped me catch myself.\n\n I looked upwards and far above saw there was an opening to the sky.   I said,\n\n“Who are they?” The Person helped walk me toward the door.\n\n“They call themselves…The Followers of Truth.” He said.\n\nWe got to the door and opened it. A burst of cold air came in.  In the room was only a table that had frost on it, A fireplace to the right that had a metal pan on the fire with what appeared to be a steak on it,\n\na small vase with a small top for an opening was on the floor with a lid next to it, a poufy cushioned chair was next to the fireplace, and there was also another  fireplace at the far back of the room.\n\n“Great…”  Said the person. “ A Puzzle.” He walked with me into the room. As we got in the door slammed behind us. “Can you stand now?” \n\nI nodded slightly as I took my own weight back.\n\n “Good. My name is Jed. And you are?”\n\n“James.” I said.  Suddenly  I felt an overwhelming hunger as I smelled the smell of cooked steak.\n\nI looked to the steak and started to walk over, but as I did Jed caught my arm. “What?” I said.\n\n“The thing you should know about this place, is that it’s a death trap.” Said Jed with a serious face.\n\nHe lifted his sleeve and there was a severe gash about a foot long. “That.” He said pointing to it, “Is what happens when you aren’t careful.”  I nodded, swallowing. “We had best get to solving this. Before the cold kills us.”\n\n I hadn’t noticed but my toes were starting to get numb. \n\n“What should we be looking for?” I said. \n\n“A key to get back into the room we came in.” Said Jed.\n\n“What!?” I said, “We were just in there?” \n\n“Trust me James.” Said the man. “We are better off in here for the moment. What you need to understand is that The Followers of Truth are ruthless.\n\nIf you don’t do what they want you to, they will make you do it, one way or another. Put your ear to the door.”\n\nI went to the door and put my ear on it like he said to, There were clicking sounds in the room. Like a giant insect walking in there.\n\n“Hear that?” Said Jed. “That’s the Grimmer.”  I got my head off the door and said. “The Grimmer?”\n\n“Yes.” Said Jed. “A fearsome thing, they release it through the ceiling in the room to make sure we are out.” Then a thought occurred to me. I said,\n\n“How do they know we are still alive?” Jed Shrugged the said,\n\n“Well If the Grimmer doesn’t come back early I guess that means we are still alive. Either way we better find the key to the door. Before they push us to find it. Now where is that key…”\n\n“Maybe it’s in the chair?” I said. “Seems poufy enough to hide it.”\n\n“Or to hide something else.” Said Jed. “Always test things.”  Jed took a glove off and went to the couch. I followed but at a distance.\n\n Jed, a few feet from the couch tossed it on the Seat. \n\nSuddenly there was a click. The cushion rose at an incredible speed to the ceiling, crushing the glove, then lowered back to position.\n\n The cushion didn’t even flatten, like it was rock hard.  The glove fell on the floor.  Jed looked to me, nodded, and said.\n\n“See?”  As he picked up and put his glove back on. “Never touch things directly.”\n\nI took off my own glove went to the jar on the floor. \n\n I kind of whacked it with my glove. It just knocked over… and something clanked inside it. Jed looked to me and said “Be careful.”\n\nI put my glove back on, picked up the jar and looked in the opening. The key was inside it. \n\n“The key is in here!” I said. I turned the jar upside-down and the key came out… a little. It was stuck.\n\nJed let out a small grunt. “They never make it that easy.”   Suddenly there was a trickling and hissing sound coming from the right fireplace.  There was a small amount of water coming above the fireplace.  Jed cursed under his breath.\n\n I then panicked. I tried to smash the vase against the floor.  “Stop!” Said Jed. The vase landed on the ground in my hand with a large thump, but didn’t break.\n\n“Careful you idiot!” That could have been filled with glass as well as the key.\n\n “Well what are we supposed to do?” I said in a panicked voice. Jed then said, “Not panic. That’s what they want us to do, we need to think.”\n\nI forced myself to relax.  Jed then said after a moment,\n\n“I’ve got it! Put it on the cushion.”  I rushed over and tossed it on the cushion. The Cushion rose again with great speed and there was a loud clunk.\n\n The cushion went back down and the vase, still whole, rolled off it. Jed cursed again. “So it’s unbreakable.” Jed then angrily threw his glove at the frosted table. As it hit the side, it stuck and turned frosted too.  I came to a sudden realization.\n\n“The water!” I said.\n\n“Yes I know.” Said Jed looking down at the ground, which had water rising a half inch above it.\n\n“No I mean-“ I didn’t explain. I ran to the jar, picked it up and the lid.\n\n I ran to the fireplace that was now extinguished, and filled it with water, then screwed on the lid. I turned to Jed who looked at me and looked like he just realized what I was doing and nodded. I Hurried to the table and tossed the jar at the side.\n\n As it made impact it stuck, frosted, then in a few short seconds the jar exploded. Shards went everywhere. Startled, I yelled and was knocked back on my backside.  A few cut me on my arms and legs from the jar shards.   “Jed!” are you alright?”\n\n I heard a few curses from the back of the room.\n\n“I’m fine.” Said Jed. ”I should have seen that coming.”  I saw that there was a solid bit of ice in the shape of a vase on the floor near the table, and inside was the key.\n\n I picked it up and headed to the extinguished fireplace, picking up the pan.  I didn’t head to the chair to smash the ice because I didn’t want to risk breaking the key.\n\nBy now the water was an inch and a half deep and I couldn’t feel from my ankle down. I ran carefully to the other fireplace, which was luckily a bit raised off the ground, but not much.\n\n I put the pan over the fire and the ice on top of it. The pan was about 2 inches deep. Jed came behind me. After about a minute, the pan was full of water, but the ice wasn’t melted all the way.\n\nI took the ice in one hand and tossed the water in the pan on the ground with the other, then put the ice back on the pan and started melting it again.\n\nAfter another minute the key was free. I took it and went to the door where Jed was now with his ear on the door. He took his head off the door and said,\n\n“The Grimmer isn’t there anymore.”\n\n  I unlocked it and opened it. The Grimmer wasn’t in there. I went into the room and Jed followed me, but suddenly the door closed, there was a hissing sound, like air through a tight space. I started coughing, as if the air was bad. So did Jed.\n\nThen everything went black.\n\nEnd of Chapter One\n\nThank you for taking the time to read this!\nI'll be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing, let me know by bird or in person!\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen\n((Sofetios))", ""),
        StaticBook("Progress-Health", "§bAthedil Haler'thilln", "----------------Maehr’sae Hiylun’ehya§0\n§0----------------Chapter 3§0\n§0As interpreted by Lucion Sullas, former Okarir, Tilruir, Sohaer, and Maheral of Haelun’or.§0\n\nAs with any ideology, there are misconceptions amongst the uninitiated. Mainly upon the desires of its adherents, the reasons behind their actions, or perhaps even mere differences in personality. It is difficult\n\nto write a truly concise list, but one hopes the ones he lists are relevant to the reader.\n\n----------------1.§0\n§0On Racism and Seclusion.§0\n§0----------------The Maehr’sae hiylun’ehya does not support illogical hatreds of other peoples. Rather, it supports rational\n\nwariness of dangerous outsiders--such as urukan, tal’urukan and suspicious strangers. The urukan and tal’urukan are beings with a natural aversion to elven thought and philosophy. Though there can be\n\nexceptions, they are often ignored or destroyed, should they be in the presence of the Pure. They cannot be trusted under any circumstances. In regardes to the other races, it is most certainly dependant on the\n\nMali’aheral in question. I am yet to meet a Mali’aheral aside gtom Uradir who truly despised all peoples equally. It is for these reasons adherents separate themselves from contemporary society.\n\n----------------2.§0\n§0On Insults and the Insulted.§0\n§0----------------The Maehr’sae hiylun’ehya does not support arbitrary personal attacks. It does however, support learning,\n\nadvising, and truth. Should an individual speak without merit or evidence upon an individual with the purpose of cruel amusement, then they are a poor adherent, and have provided nothing to anyone.\n\nHowever, advise can often be misinterpreted as insult. Adherents are taught to despise falsehood, therefore should something be perceived as incorrect they rightly feel the need to amend it. If an individual\n\nhas their feelings hurt during the process, it is their issue, and not the adherent’s.\n\n----------------3.§0\n§0On Violence and Aggression.§0\n§0----------------The Maehr’sae hiylun’ehya does not support physical violence of any kind. It does support safeguarding the\n\npurity of its people. Often, death to those who would sully the purity of the adherents is the first and foremost answer to such crimes. However, violent actions belong to beasts, and are beneath us. Ours is an\n\norder built upon the mind, not the passions of raised fists.\n\n----------------4.§0\n§0On ‘Purges’ and Murder.§0\n§0----------------Maehr’sae hiylun’ehya does not support ‘purges’, or murder. It does, however, support the exaction of\n\njustice to craven monsters. Some individuals are so profane in their existence that they insult and endanger our way. Such people are more often-than-not loathed throughout all civilised lands. Such individuals are\n\nfew and far between, but if by the act of living they injure us, then they must be destroyed for the good of all. Exile ought to be the first and foremost method of drastic punishment in any case.\n\n----------------5.§0\n§0On Experimentation and Learning.§0\n§0----------------The Maehr’sae hiylun’ehya does not support unethical and cruel experiments. It does support the vigorous\n\nacquisition of knowledge. Those that would perform any experiment on a living, sentient, non-consenting induviduals are preforming a disgusting act that harms their own purity through reckless and untethered\n\ncuriosity, as well as harming the reputation of their own people. It is bizarre, cruel, unethical, and the findings never outweigh the cost.\n\n----------------6.§0\n§0On Emotion and Aversion to it.§0\n§0----------------The Maehr’sae hiylun’ehya does not support and emotionless state in the individual. Rather, it encourages\n\nlogic, foresight, and wisdom over passionate outbursts. It is wiser to think before feeling. In public, it is beneath an adherent to act by the whims of emotion--it shows a lack of self-control and would likely offend\n\nthose around yourself.\n\n----------------7.§0\n§0On Superiority and Arrogance.§0\n§0----------------The Maehr’sae hiylun’ehya does not declare Mali’aheral to be superior to other races. It does, however,\n\nsupport that those who follow its path closely are greater than others, as they seek enlightenment through the unravelling of truth, rather than the mundanities of life. Identifying adherents as ‘arrogant’ is simply an\n\ninnocent misinterpretation on the side of the uninitiated. Should one encounter an adherent that claims themselves to be ‘superior’ generally speaking, one would advise you to ask them their current academic\n\nprojects. Should they refuse to answer, it is safe to say their ego speaks, and not the Maehr’sae hiylun’ehya.\n\n----------------8.§0\n§0On adherents and Their Qualities.§0\n§0----------------The Maehr’sae hiylun’ehya supports that only the Mali’aheral, with their inherent blessings, can\n\nsuccessfully follow the Pure Way. But this is only true in theory. The Maehr’sae hiylun’ehya encourages community cohesion and the search for truth. Theoretically, any race (with the exception of perhaps\n\nlye’urukan) may adopt and follow the path to enlightenment. No other race has attempted to adhere to it, thus perhaps it will never be known for certain.\n\nOne hopes that this series has been illuminating. Seek out L. Sullas, if you can, should questions be sought. Otherwise, good luck on your own twisting path.\n\nMaehr’sae hiylun’ehya.", ""),
        StaticBook("Dwarf report", "§bBalduul Exrith", "*Broken front gate§0\n§0*Secondary gates open§0\n§0*No security or populace§0\n§0*Bank was closed off§0\n§0*Empty buildings§0\n§0*Kevay Ireheart lives on 1 Ireheart Way§0\n§0*Unprotected smeltery§0\n§0*No security in in Great Hall§0\n§0*Wedding many moons ago\n\n*Tenshar Karkun lives in deluxe quarters§0\n§0*Balek Irongut owns security bear§0\n§0*Jorik Grandaxe has reserved deluxe residence§0\n§0*All residence taken except one building§0\n§0*practicly all doors locked§0\n§0*Borug Te Shurt building is open\n\n*Flimlin's Square locked§0\n§0*Grandaxe family§0\n§0deeply present§0\n§0*Some buildings under construction§0\n§0*Smithing business open§0\n§0*Karina Gotthold talks with Mafraedon about architecture§0\n§0*Mafraedon possible city architect\n\n*Mafraedon character works at forge§0\n§0*Asked Mafraedon about city's state, would not answer§0\n§0*Followed me out of city§0\n§0*Very suspicous", ""),
        StaticBook("Stone and Claw", "§bDedicant Gileandra", "Of Stone and Claw\n---------------\n\nI have walked the mountains in my anger.\n\nI saw nothing, and everything, and became aware.\n\nI have walked the forests in my sorrow.\n\nThe sounds gave me peace, and a path to follow.\n\nI have walked the rivers in my confusion.\n\nThe babbling streams washed away all my illusion.\n\nI have walked the plains in my grief.\n\nThe animals accepted me, in my respect and belief.\n\nI have walked the caves, deep and cold.\n\nI found things wonderous, ancient, and old.\n\nI have walked the valleys with my kin.\n\nI will not forgive the deep ones for their sin.\n\nI have walked the hunter's path, the grizzly found me.\n\nWe fought, we fell, I used it to survive, showed respect and humility.\n\nI have walked these lands and found myself and my meaning.\n\nI will walk with my new brethren, through nature vast and teeming.\n\n\n~Gileandra", ""),
        StaticBook("Breaking Iron", "§bSaradunan Steelfist", "Breaking iron, cracking ice.\nDoing to much will give you lice.\nSmash a rip and kill a boar.\nNothing can make me roar.", ""),
        StaticBook("Task Complete", "§bZerix Fury", "East From The Gate: No Mediate Threats, Just trees.§0\n§0South Of Gate: No Forest Found, Nor Threat.§0\n§0South West: Large Canopy, Rock Slide capable of hitting the camp§0\n§0West: Canopy continues, No immediate threat, Regular woodland\n\ncreatures. Alot of mushrooms. Conclusion, Its quite dim lit and some nocturnal creature could possibly move in. A large large tree wuth roots jutting out of the mountain side, This may cause a small forest collapse. §0\n§0\n§0\n§0North: Ashguard\n\n~Zerix Fury§0\n§0\n§0Task Complete: Over The Wall", ""),
        StaticBook("Lewdness", "§bLucion Sullas", "The Dichotomy of\n       Lewdness\n\n\n\n\n\nby L. Sullas\n\n-Physical fraternization outside of marriage.\n\nThis despicable crime disrespects the very nature of the matrimonial union. Unmarried elves who physically fraternize disregard the purpose of intercourse, and cause of unruly\n\ndistraction, emotional disruption, and in the worst case scenario--consequences of bastardization, which is the most deplorable crime of all, considering it taints the reputation of an otherwise innocent Mali’aheral child.\n\n-Physical fraternization with a minor (a minor being any Mali below the age of 50 years old).\n\nChildren are indeed the most precious safeguard of our illustrious city. Subjecting the emotionally immature mind to such matters\n\nis utterly unacceptable, and it disgusts this writer that it need even be listed. Let it be known that Mali under the age of 50 are -unable- to give consent, as their uninformed opinions are too juvenile to be capable of discernment.\n\n-Fraternization of any intimate sort with impures, or other races\n\nPreservation of life itself resides in the idea of purity. To lay waste to one’s untainted Mali’aheral blood is a crime no less potent than murder of another\n\ncitizen.\n\n-Non-consensual physical fraternization\n\nLarihei is blind to you, desecrators of this law. It is beyond reproach. The city shall expunge you as a sick stomach does its vile illness.\n\n-Intimate fraternization of a homosexual nature\n\nAll who express intimacy in a public space with a member of the same gender should be treated as unclean, dirtying the sights of their fellows with such a display of uncivilized,\n\nunproductive lust.\n\n-Incestuous intimate fraternization of any kind\n\nNo self-respecting Mali will need explanation as to why this type of behavior is both obscene and illegal.\n\nby L. Sullas\n\nCopied from the Mali'aheral Law\n\n*a pile of paper is neatly stacked*", ""),
        StaticBook("Settlement state", "§bBalduul Exrith", "*No security gates, security guards or seen populace§0\n§0*All structures made of quartz§0\n§0*Supply bins everywhere§0\n§0*'Alreis Faereon' character owns residence§0\n§0*'Destria' also owns residence§0\n§0*Tax chests unlocked\n\n*Many towers§0\n§0*Most are incomplete§0\n§0*Main tower is to be library§0\n§0*Snow-capped§0\n§0*Holes throughout place§0\n§0*Lots of ice§0\n§0*Only 1 gate§0\n§0*Would not be good when attacked§0\n§0*Only one way to get in and out§0\n\n*Focuses on education and magics§0\n§0*Lots of public buildings§0\n§0*Not so many residences", ""),
        StaticBook("Poem", "§b[Dedicant] Anthony Hearthal", "In the meadows of asulon\nThere are many plants and trees\nAnd most of the grass grows up, passed your knees\nIn the deepest sea of the world\na massive fish did wonder\nwhy men would dive so far down under\n\nWell the truth is simple they just wanted to plunder\n\nLike magic trees and humming bees the world also has many secrets, where it hides treasure, the beauty of nature and soulmates that one desires.", ""),
        StaticBook("Little Gifts", "§bLelien", "Little Gifts\n\n    by Acaele Lazul\n\nLittle gifts that bring good cheer\nTo ease your mind when I'm not near\nFor all the good you've done for me\nThat I'm a little too blind to see\n\nAlthough my thoughts are kept inside\nI always walk upright with pride\n\nTo know that you will always be\nThe only one always for me.", ""),
        StaticBook("Bundle of poems", "§bthomas20101997", "In Ithilien \n----------------The leaves of the beeches breathe the sparkling air of day's awakening.\n\nThe birches spread the buds of leaves for small singing birds to linger there. \n---------------\n\n\n----------------In Ithilien, land of the tunefull waterfalls wandering together heals my beloved.\n\n\n\n\n\n---------------\n\n      Galadriel\n----------------In joy thou hast lived. Beware of the sea!\n\nIf thou hearest the cry of the gull on the shore Thy heart shall rest in the forest no more\n\n---------------\n\n    Vi Dyr Ennui\n----------------In western lands beneath the sun. \nIn spring, flowers rise, the trees bud, waters run, and the merry little birds sing. \n\nThere it is cloudles night and-\n---------------\n\n\n----------------shuddering beechs hold the starry host, the white jewels, on their branching hair.\n\nHere at my path's end I am lingering in deep darkness buried. Beyond-\n---------------\n\n----------------towers strong and high. Beyond all mountains steep.\n\nAbove all shadows rides the sun. And stars always dwell. I will not say \n'The day is done'\nOr to the stars \n'Farewell'\n---------------\n\n     Thinnuel\n----------------At starfade a time comes. When you see one brilliant star left behind. When the starry host ha departed. The star fades, the world does not wait.\n\nWhy do I linger-\n---------------\n\n\n----------------and sing under this fading light.\n\nThere is a daisy among the elanor blossems. \nTo me it is fair.\n\nThere is a birch tree under the-\n---------------\n\n\n----------------mallorn trees.\nTo me it is fair.\n\nThere is a butterfly above the swan.\nTo me it is fair.\n\nA small star follows sirius. \n---------------\n\n\n----------------\nTo me it is fair.\n\nThe leaves of the mallorn are numberless. One tiny leaf, one fading leaf holds my eye. When will it fall?\n\nThe brilliant star-\n---------------\n\n\n----------------iis fading. Now it departs to the heavens. \n\nNow I will depart the world holding a leaf in my hand.\n\n\n\n---------------", ""),
        StaticBook("The Divine", "§bSage Kalen", "What's one is five,\nbut five is nothing.\n\nNothing shall be everything,\nWhilst everything shall be the end.\n\nOnly one knows the way, but the five hold the key.\nThe key unlocks everything.", ""),
        StaticBook("Poem Of Death", "§bVirgo the Venalicius", "It does not matter wether your the happiest person... Or the saddest beggar.\n\nThe biggest giant or the most terrible creature. \n\nWe are all the same in the eyes of The Reaper.\n- Virgo", ""),
        StaticBook("Ink Sacks & Bubb", "§bThe Fable Maker", "================       Ink Sacks\n    & Bubbles\n================\n\n\nScribed by\n \n     The Fable Maker\n\n\n\n==================\n\nThey swim as fish do.\nGlide like birds do.\nSquirm as bugs do.\nGulp as men do.\n\nAll sizes and shapes.\nMaking little sound.\nAbsent of capes.\nIn seas they are found.\n\nSquids of battle,\nHardly cattle.\nTis loudly said,\nThat they fought the undead.\n\n\n*A painting of a battle squid is here*\n\nSquids of the air,\nCan it be true?\nDo they really fly?\nWell that's up to you.\n\n*An ancient painting of an Aegian river is here, a squid elaborately envisioned in the sky*\n\nSquids of great size,\nTis said a great prize,\nLays hidden within,\nCreating a din.\n\n*A hurried painting of a gigantic squid, surrounded by musical notes is here*\n\nThough passive most times,\nBe mindful of squid.\nFor in that which chimes,\nGreat treasure is hid...\n\n*The final page reeks with the ancient scent of fish...*", ""),
        StaticBook("Silent Thanks", "§bThe Eternal Library", "Silent Thanks\nBy Unknown\n\nFor even as our mouths stay shut\nThank her\nEven as our words are silent\nThank her\nThrough ways voices cannot express\nThank her\nGive thanks in actions, silently gracefully but\nThank her", ""),
        StaticBook("For The Bold", "§bAncient Poet", "So told you were\nOf rumours\npertaining to my advance\n\nOh could you were\nwith ill-humor:\nStood I not a chance\n\nToo bold I where\nwhen with such fervor I asked: Would you care for this dance?\n\nTook a better stance:\nFold, you did\nWith that glance you give\n\nAs a rose in bloom\nPosed you did\nswooning to and fro\n\nLo, behold how poorly\nThat I hid\nAnd gave to me\n\nYour scorn\nMy Love Forlorn\nBut still:\n\n\"I'll take your thorn\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Love Poem", "§bCeleion", "Shall I compare thee to a summer’s day?\nThou art more lovely and more temperate.\nRough winds do shake the darling buds of May,\nAnd summer’s lease hath all too short a date.\n\nSometime too hot the eye of heaven shines,\nAnd often is his gold complexion dimmed; \nAnd every fair from fair sometime declines,\nBy chance, or nature’s changing course, untrimmed;\n\nBut thy eternal summer shall not fade,\nNor lose possession of that fair thou ow’st,\nNor shall death brag thou wand’rest in his shade,\nWhen in eternal lines to Time thou grow’st.\n\nSo long as men can breathe, or eyes can see,\nSo long lives this, and this gives life to thee.", ""),
        StaticBook("Pick Up Line", "§bElayne", "I wake up to your warmth of breath,\n\nRealising you're with me.\n\nI feel your arms tighten around,\n\nAnd draw a smile onto me.\n\nI turn around to face your front,\n\nFeeling blessed a millionth time.\n\nI stare at your sleeping self,\n\nAs I murmur a thankful pray.\n\nWhen I needed, you were there,\n\nTo help me up and walk me through.\n\nI held on tight to your hands,\n\nAs you pull me closer to you.\n\nI found something that I had lost,\n\nA love so pure and so serene.\n\nI kiss your brow slow and soft,\n\nIn my arms I hold you close.\n\nTears would form when you're close,\n\nA clear smile to change the means.\n\nI see your eyes open up,\n\nAnd gaze into mine.\n\nI feel your hands brush my cheeks,\n\nAnd pull me closer under the sheets.\n\nWe both grin at a sync,\n\nToss this poem and let's pass time.\n\n-=Lain=- <3 <3 <3", ""),
        StaticBook("Poems and That", "§bPosco Bramble", "Lenfarthing\n             ~\nI've heard the tale of wheat and ales\nI'm heading off to the Dales\n\nAmber waves of wheat, so lush\nFellah singing like a thrush\n\nFlaxen crops and flaxen hair\nOh that land so fair\n\nOnce a fest\nEver blest\n\nPeppy, thrill, and glowing\nTavern always crowing\n\nSun strectching over dawn\nAll gone\n\nBurning up to the sky\nAll 'cause Gamle baked a pie.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n             ~\n\n          Fellow\n             ~\nI sat with a fellah at tea\nAnd he asked: \"D'ye fart 'en ye pee?\"\nI said with some wit:\n\"D'ye belch en ye shit?\"\nAnd thought that was quite good fer me.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n             ~\n\n           Lass\n             ~\nThere's a right fine lass.\nHas a truly magnificent ass.\nIt is not round and pink,\nAs you probably think,\nBut is grey, has long ears, and eats grass.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n             ~\n\n           Owly\n             ~\nI know an old owl named Boo,\nEvery night he yelled \"Hoo,\"\nOnce a kid walked by,\nAnd started to cry,\nAnd yelled \"I dun' 'ave a clue!\"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n             ~\n\n           Nude\n             ~\nA bather whose clothing was srewed\nBy winds that left her quite nude\nSaw a man come along\nAnd unless we are wrong\nYou expected this line to be lewd.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n             ~\n\n          Moo Moo\n              ~\nOn the horse, a fellah named Jack\nSaid good-bye, and leaned out to smack\nThe lips of his chick\nBut the horse took-off quick\nAnd he kissed a cow's ass down the crack.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n             ~\n\n              ~\nBy Posco, can't ye tell.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n              ~", ""),
        StaticBook("Poetry", "§bSofetios Jayamen", "Poetry\n\n             ~By Sofetios\n                  Jayamen\n\n        The Lake\n\nI wander upon a path by a lake,\nWondering where it’s all gone.\nEvery day I used to come here,\nFrom the morning to the evening's yawn.\n\nEvery now and again I wonder,\nWhy the days have passed.\nAnd think very deeply,\nWhy they don't seem to last.\n\nDay by day, night by night,\nThe Lake becomes smaller still.\nThe thoughts of it become thinner,\nEven to my strongest will.\n\nIt has become ten times smaller and ten times shallower,\nEven thought it has not changed.\nTo other people it might be normal,\nbut to me it is considered strange.\n\n      The Dreamer\n\nThe Dreamer,\nThe one who dares not be the same,\nFor in dreams mysteries unravel,\nAnd endless space you do travel,\nAs your dreams you try to tame.\n\nAs the Dreamer,\nDreams above the sky,\nAnd looks down below,\nTo the people that show,\nWho know not what lies that high.\n\nUnawakened, the Dreamer,\nIn a maze of thoughts,\nWandering the mists,\nWith endless twists,\nAs the path on minds paper he jots.\n\nThough be wary dreamer,\nAs thoughts tend to run away,\nOnly the ones you really need,\nAnd the ones you often feed,\nAre the ones that often stay.\n\n      The Cobbler\n           and\n        The Crow\n\nI was working in the night,\nAs I bent over a shoe,\nI was working very hard,\nto finish before the morning dew.\n\nI was just about finished,\nWhen what was this I hear?\nA tapping, slight tapping,\nAnd it seemed to be so near.\n\nI turned to the window,\nAnd what did I see?\nA crow tapping at my glass,\nStaring at me.\n\n\"Go away.\" I said,\nSternly as I did stand.\n\"Don't you know I have work to do?\"\nThough I know it didn't understand.\n\nBut the crow kept tapping at the glass,\nAnd I began to get mad,\nThis crow just would not stop,\nNot even a tad.\n\nI turned to my unfinished shoe,\nTo work as long as I could bear,\nBut then I noticed something missing,\nThat would stop me from completing the pair.\n\nThe golden lace that I needed,\nFor this order to be complete,\nI couldn't find it anywhere,\nAs the glass the crow did beat.\n\nI checked my table, checked my pockets,\nAnd then to my surprise,\nThere was a hole in one,\nThat was just the right size.\n\nI would never finish this shoe,\nBy the morning light,\nNow things were dire,\nand my situation was quite tight.\n\n\"I must have dropped it,\nOn the way back from the store!\"\nBut it was too far I thought,\nAs my confidence tore.\n\nBut then I realized \"What's this?\"\n\"Where was the tapping I couldn't bare?\"\nI looked to the window,\nAnd the crow wasn't there.\n\nI went to the window,\nAnd what was in its place?\nIt was simple and divine,\nThe golden lace.\n\nI opened the window,\nAnd took it with care,\nBecause of who gave this gift,\nI was certainly aware.\n\nI went to my table,\nAnd finished the shoe.\nBut then thought of the crow,\nIf I only knew!\n\nI then went to bed,\nWith not much I could say,\nI only did hope,\nThe crow would come back some day.\n\n         Imprint\n\nI stand in a forest,\nOne that I know quite well,\nWhere the trees are tall,\nAnd they never do fall,\nAt least that's what I like to tell.\n\nI came here,\nWhen I was a boy,\nUsually in the day,\nBut any time I would say,\nThe forest was my favorite toy.\n\nI came here again,\nA few years ago,\nIt was the same place I knew well,\nAs far as I could tell,\nThere was nothing different to show.\n\nI open my eyes now,\nTo see the trees,\nMy eyes see an empty place,\nAs if leaving no trace,\nThough this is not what my mind sees.\n\n         Friend\n            or\n          Enemy?\n\nEverywhere I am\nI hide from your sight,\nIf it's in the day,\nAnd especially at night.\n\nI am one of sleep's worst enemies,\nAnd one of fear's best friends,\nI grow ever more larger,\nAs your will begins to bend.\n\nIn the day I am more friendly,\nEspecially when its hot and I'm in sight,\nBut when it turns to night time,\nI'm sure to give you a fright.\n\nI am a mimic of sorts,\nI try to copy you while you walk,\nI even mimic other people,\nAlthough I cannot talk.\n\nEven when you go about the day,\nI skulk and kind of stalk,\nFear or fear not, I am always here,\nI am the shadow on which you walk.\n\n   An Uncommon Sight\n\nI go about my day,\nDoing the same things as before,\nThe same old road,\nFrom my humble abode,\nNot realizing it is a bore.\n\nI walk ever onward,\nKnowing all my tasks by heart,\nA straight narrow trail,\nWith nothing new to avail,\nAll I do is my part.\n\nThen one day as I'm going past,\nA park once again,\nI see a boy,\nwithout even a toy,\nwho couldn't be past ten.\n\nThe boy was doing something abnormal,\nsomething I hadn't seen before,\nHe was looking in the sky,\nNot even blinking an eye,\nthey didn't even seem sore.\n\nI wondered if there was a hawk,\nOr something in the sky,\nI didn't hear a thing,\nOnly the birds that always sing,\nSo I did wonder why.\n\nI looked over,\nto the boy sitting by,\nHe was being bullied by his peers,\nAnd was starting to go in tears,\nJust for looking in the sky.\n\nI was about to go over,\nTo try to help if I could,\nbut the parents went first,\nBefore I feared the worst,\nTo help as they should.\n\nI walked on my way home,\nWondering why what I saw came to pass,\nI had a thought why,\nso I looked to the sky,\nand saw it in it's great mass,\n\nAs I looked to the sky,\nI was transfixed,\nI hadn't done this in a while,\nAnd it did make me smile,\nAs past and current memories mixed.\n\nI saw the vast expanse,\nIn all its mystery,\nI watched the clouds in the sky,\nAs they changed shape and passed by,\nAs they had for all of history.\n\nSuddenly someone laughed to my left,\nEven thought it was mild,\nSomeone looking at me,\nLaughing at what I appeared to be,\nJust as the others had done to the child.\n\n         Courage\n\nIt is a thing you use\nWhen you can't go to the end,\nWhen all seems lost\nAnd you can't defend.\n\nYou use it as\nIt combines with your will,\nAll you need to do is\nLet it give its fill.\n\nThe choice is yours,\nGive in or go,\nThough in the end\nOnly you will know.\n\nSee with your spirit\nAnd not with fear,\nfor then you will know\nThat courage is here.\n\nThe End\n\nThank you for taking the time to read this book! I will be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing, please send me a bird or tell me in person!\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen", ""),
        StaticBook("Poëm of Calethel", "§b[Dedicant] Calethel Faelith", "Task one: Poëm\nThe roots starts from below, as it the roots are starting to get thicker when they go up, to a stem, going up and up, from thick to thin, as it is starting to show leaves, with branches, the wood color of dark brown, leaves of dark green,\n\nThe wind blowing in the eastern horizon, as it blows the leaves with it, hearing the birds chirping and chirping, flying around the tree, as they nest in the branches, and little baby birds being born, chirping for their parents, as they are hungry.\n\nThe grass around it growing and growin, with the flowers, as the insects crawl around the ground, looking for food, the grass starts to spread at soil, growing more and more, as fallen branches on the ground starting to grow to trees.\n\nThe animals walking around happily, the cow eating the overgrown grass, chickens eating the seeds, and the sheep eating the grass also, as further into the grassland a thick forest starts to appear, starting from thin, to thick, the with growing bigger\n\nas the lenght doesn't stop, starting from oak trees, to birch trees, then to pine trees and then to acacia trees, and then to dark oak, as thick as they can get, as huge as they can, the leaves growing on the branches, as they grow more up, higher in the\n\nsky, reaching the clouds, the sun shining on the leaves, the wind quietly humming around the forest, as it is silent, with only the chirping songbirds to hear.", ""),
        StaticBook("No Songs", "§bDio Astóre", "Fathers. Brothers. To here have come.\nVoice of creation whispers. Silence - a brittle glass in shattering - undone; tears away at flesh as reflection scattered 'fore feet and eye.\n\nStifled song for children longed and distant the hand that caresses so fondly the growing thing; sing for the green few.\nLonely star amidst far pallid heavens; mournful silver, dreaming of golden waves; dream itself of days golden gone.\n\nFathers and Mothers here are none.\nNot abyssal fire relieve the ear from the howling, unsettled, fretful and frightful; lurks in changing darks that not light alters.\n\nRemoved earth; chasms ravinous, wide and gaping maw stolen from sanctuary's prideful estate; precious the nothing-color wistful; falling crystal.\n\nSwooning, frays cannot delay. Bark cease! Delay. Embers for sand show no clear shapes in rejuvenation, exhultation: lame you our Father's shame!\n\nSlow wavering hope, fog shifting; alike twisting hidden-water shan't be red-stained - blossoming steel rose; foes begot by another.\n\nOh Mother! From hence forth lead us away, not astray to escape belated those doom fated in hatred grasped; abated for neither Son nor sun, or daughter's laugh.\n\nNo error in our unblighted spirit; merit with it go, we know and so under storm a sail we had set. Forget not these drums light.\nHum. Hum. Old life undone. Fathers and Brothers, Heroes and Mothers to here have come.\n\nBut no songs by children sung.\n\nNo songs.", ""),
        StaticBook("Various Works I", "§bFenris Cross", "~The Brook Song\nBabble goes the brook in the forest deep, Splash and sputter does the water through the trees!\n\nBabble goes the brook on the mountain steep, That sash that flutters on a mountain breeze!\n\nListen to her song either here or there, Listen to her melody with not a care.\n\nFor brook of deepest forest glen, Is no different from mountain kin.\n\nBabble goes the brook in the harvest field,\n\nFlash and flicker does she through golden yield!\n\nBabble goes the brook in the cave below, A cache of life in the darkness sewn!\n\nListen to her song either here or there. Listen to her melody with not a care.\n\nFor brook of golden, harvest field, Is no different from kin concealed.\n\nBabble goes the brook in the desert sand, Where her gift is so much more than song.\n\nBabble goes the brook through the hills so grand\n\nWhere she makes her way winding and long.\n\nListen to her song either here or there, Listen to her melody with not a care.\n\nFor brook of hill or desert sands\nIs no different from her kin in stranger lands.\n\n~Completely Yours\nThe sun that shines over my head, Has slowly made her way to bed.\n\nAnd evening sky is darkening, To night with dreams to bring\n\nThe moon that shines within the night,\n\nReflecting here the sun’s warm light.\n\nAnd stars like diamonds high above, Do remind us what we’re dreaming of.\n\nThe wind touches the grass so green, And water flows within the stream.\n\nMy fingers trace along the seam, Of something I can’t quite see.\n\nThe thud of hooves against the ground, Is in my ears the only sound.\n\nA trail of dust I’ve yet to find, In this landscape of my mind.\n\nOh see how the stars dancing there, Shine down on the land below.\n\nAnd see how the stars, bright and fair, Show us where we’re meant to go.\n\nAnd rain starts to fall upon me, As I begin my journey.\n\nInto the dreams that night has brought, To see just what this life has wrought.\n\nTake hope into your dreamland, And love so you may understand.\n\nJust what it is you long for, From this distant and untouched shore.\n\nYou’ll find what it is you’re looking for, Below the stars with all their hidden lore.\n\nAnd see what it is you need to be, Completely yours.\n\nAnd know that you are upon the end, Of the journey through which you wend.\n\nWhen the dream at last comes to be..\n\nCompletely yours\n\n~The Dance\nA howl on the wind, Shivers down my spine.\n\nAnd leaves dance again, To the song of the night.\n\nClose your eyes and listen, Hear the melody.\n\nSweet, sweet harmony.\n\nSee the moonlight glisten, Oh that lovely silver fantasy.\n\nDance with me as the wind blows, Twirl beneath the cloud that glows.\n\nLike moonlight caught inside a mist, And feel the cool night’s kiss.\n\nA nightingale sings for we, Who stay awake to hear.\n\nHer notes pierce the darkness so readily, To offer a shield against fear.\n\nClose your eyes and listen, Hear the melody.\n\nSweet, sweet harmony.\n\nSee the moonlight glisten, Oh that lovely silver fantasy.\n\nDance with me as the bird sings, Twirl with me through fields and rings.\n\nOf flowers and produce all in their rows, And leap and tumble\n\nwhere e’re your heart goes.\n\nThe crickets chirp, A chorus arises. Ehcoing o’er the waving grass.\n\nTheir song will stir, So many surprises not seen before.\n\nClose your eyes and listen, Hear the\n\nmelody. Sweet, sweet harmony.\n\nSee the moonlight glisten, Oh that lovely silver fantasy.\n\nButterflies wake in the night, With painted wings they take flight.\n\nColoring images outlined by stars,\n\nThat we may see them anew from afar.\n\nDance with me as they do fly, Twirling and spinning while they flutter by.\n\nOur eyes will turn to the blanket above, Where winged, colored patterns meet white of the dove.\n\nA howl on the wind, Shivers down my spine.\n\nAnd leaves dance again, To the song of the night.\n\nLet us be no different, From this melody.\n\nHonoring life’s sweet harmony.\n\nAnd let us be reverent, Of all we have seen ‘Neath the silver fantasy.\n\nDance with me as the day comes, Twirl and spin ere the music is done.\n\nLike just so much sand, Captured in a hand.\n\nThere’s only so long\nWe can sing this song.", ""),
        StaticBook("Badger Apples", "Valmir_", "Apples for a Badger\nBy Callax of the Druidic Order (12werbles12)\n\nAs I travel through the wood,\nI hear a faint rustle of leaves.\nThe sound seems to come from a nearby tree.\nI look around myself, wondering…\nThen I spot her.\n\nA small badger,\nHer belly petite and thin,\nTrying to reach an apple stem,\nI stand back not to bother,\nHoping to see her mission succeeded\n\nShe can’t help but fail, and fail again.\n‘Tis now my time to assist,\nFor she won’t do well by going at this.\nI pull down for her a few apples,\nSetting them at her feet.\n\nShe takes eat,\nHer hunger now satisfied.\nAs I then take my leave,\nShe gives a small yip,\nShe is greatly thankful,\nFor my time to her spent.", ""),
        StaticBook("Seasons", "§b[Dedicant] Sevisa", "Seasons\n         by\n\n       \n     Dedicant\n      Sevisa\n\nHere I stand among the blooming meadows\nYour touch is fleeting,\nlight and playful.\nIt runs through my hair \nand across my cheeks.\nI can barely feel your touch, love.\n Looking skyward towards your pale blue hues\n\nI reach giving, longing to feel your touch.\n\nMoments past and I continue to implore\nand eventually you comply your touch\nbecomes almost tangible\nleaving me breathless and in awe.\nI look to your ever deep blue hues\n\nand watch as they darken,\nflashing dangerously and accompanied\nby a deep thundering growl.\n\nTaking shelter from the sudden flurry of emotion, I watch as your tears water the earth, nourishing your brothers and sisters.\n\nAnd I think to myself, love.\nHow much you give and never ask.\nI revel in the strength of your emotions, dear.\nYour power never ceases to amaze me.\n\nIt bothers me not to stay by your side.\nTo comfort you until the very last deluge.\n\nYour eyes change now, I can see it. From dark blue to hazy grey. Your touch still remains as always. Though now it is tinged with a sadness. A cooler touch, and I know, soon something is coming.\n\nYour hair begins to change, now.\n\nFrom the deepest of greens, to fiery reds, oranges, and yellow. I watch in reverence, as these tones shower down around me, turning brown and withering away to dust. This change does not scare me, darling. I’ll stay here forever more.\n\nA shiver runs through my being, as your touch turns frigid, your hair now blanketed in white. Holding my arms out for that last embrace, I watch as that light goes out in your eyes, and pledge that I will be there, evermore. Among the blooming meadows,\n\nwaiting for your light hearted touch, and the warmth to return to your eyes.", ""),
        StaticBook("du Mont Poems", "§bAevalyn", "§0A collection of Poems§0\n§0 by Griffith du Mont§0\n§0\n§0       Haelun'or,§0\n§0          Axios§0\n§0\n§0    Published 1592§0\n§0\n§0  by Griffith du Mont§0\n§0    Transcribed by§0\n§0   Aevalyn Bethiros\n\nVoyage§0\n§0\n§0Unfurl the sails and off we go,§0\n§0Divine wind will gently blow,§0\n§0Stirring waves t'rock us to and fro,§0\n§0Guiding us the stars that glow.\n\nShining brightly with the moon in tow,§0\n§0Towards the horizon, they'll come to show,§0\n§0A land where times ever slow,§0\n§0That eases your heart and quells your woes,\n\nBut for now we rest in the deck below,§0\n§0We'll reach paradise before you know!§0\n§0\n§0\n§0Written by Griffith du Mont, Grand Maestro.\n\nThese Words, My Love§0\n§0\n§0This song was written for my beloved wife, Kyra. Though we will never have children... It matters not. We are rich in happiness. Words are my children and you my inspiration.\n\nMy pen pierces the page and§0\n§0Impregnates her with my thoughts.§0\n§0She nurtures my mind in§0\n§0A womb of poetry,§0\n§0Kept deep inside her,§0\n§0Those memories I'd long forgot.\n\nA preservation of distant,§0\n§0Yet vivid imagery,§0\n§0Nested within the warmth§0\n§0Of her embrace,§0\n§0Are the comforts of§0\n§0Melancholic, younger days\n\nThe wind on my back,§0\n§0The sun in my face,§0\n§0Rereading my past in§0\n§0New yet nostalgic ways,§0\n§0The precious moments that§0\n§0I can't replace,\n\nThese words, my children,§0\n§0Bring me back, to that special place.§0\n§0\n§0\n§0Griffith du Mont, Grand Maestro and Husband", ""),
        StaticBook("Slight bleeding.", "§b[Ghost hiding] Irelia Fenn'asul", "Alternative ways to stop bleeding.\n\nWhen treating a victim, the primary sources which help stop bleeding may not be available. However, there are alternative sources. \n\nSugar, for example, when poured onto a\n\nwound will help with the clotting process and reduce the risk on infection, but only slightly, and is a good secondary method.\n\nIce can also slow blood flow and provide pain assists due to numbing.\n\nMixing salt with water\n\nand poured over an open wound can aid in disinfecting and bleeding cessation.\n\nThese methods work best for small cuts and wounds. Do not waste time finding these things if proper equiptment is available!", ""),
        StaticBook("Refreshments", "§bLilliana", "-=+=-Cool Milk-=+=-\nFreshly cooled milk that stays frosty in its glass. This milk will throw you back into the days of your youth and fill you with soothing joy.\n\nPrice :: 2 minas per glass\n\n-=+=-Calming Tea-=+=-\nMade with fresh Chamomile leaves and set to relax you. This tea will help sooth the nerves and make you feel calm. Perfect before meditation or a test.\n\nPrice :: 2 minas per glass\n\n-=+=-Nightly Tea-=+=-\nTea made with Jasmine and small amounts of Nightshade, this safe tea is tested with our youngest patrons to give you the perfect drink to help sooth you to sleep.\n\nPrice :: 2 minas per glass\n\n-=+=-Sweet Tea-=+=-\nA burst of sugar and tea leaves, this tea is meant to give a short burst of energy and sooth that sweet tooth.\n\nPrice :: 2 minas per glass\n\n-=+=-Honey Tea-=+=-\nA honey based tea meant to help sooth muscles and sooth damaged throats. This is perfect to drink after a cold or illness.\n\nPrice :: 2 minas per glass\n\n-=+=-Black Tea-=+=-\nFor a stronger and more intense burst of energy, this tea is made with you in mind. This will help give you the necessary burst of engery to fill your day up.\n\nPrice :: 2 minas per glass\n\n-=+=-Ice Cool Water-=+=-\nA tall soothing glass of water, this will help quench any thirst. Fetched directly from a deep spring, its guranteed to be cool and refreshing.\n\nPrice :: Free\n\n-=+=-Apple Juice-=+=-\nFresh squeezed apples mixed in with the cool spring water makes this sweet juice all the more desireable to drink.\n\nPrice :: 2 minas per glass\n\n-=+=-Berry Juice-=+=-\nMade with a variety of berries, this cool juice will make you content to sit and sip away like a carefree child.\n\nPrice :: 2 minas per glass", ""),
        StaticBook("Common Knowledge", "§bRinaline Sirius", "Preface:\nIf you're new to this world or just completely oblivious to everything, this book is for you.\n\nChapter 1: Conversation\n\nConversation must always be prim and proper. Cussing is an absolute no-no.\n\nPersonally, I have accidently done this and I still feel impure to this day because of it.\n\nRemember, arguing and violence solve nothing. So don't get into a fight with another 'aheral.\n\nYou are no more superior than the common mali'aheral, unless you are of higher status or speaking to a mali'ata.\n\nMali'ata are not posionous but it certainly is plausible to stay away from them. If they try to seduce you, don't fall for it.\n\nChapter 2: Love\n\nAs tempting as love sounds, it is forbidden until you come of age. It is fairly disgusting to see two people showing affection in public is degrading and immoral. You will get judged and perhaps even put on trial.\n\nIf you really wish to pursue someone you MUST be more than 50 years of age.\n\nIf a mali'thill is seen trying to court another mali'thill and this mali'thill is younger than 50. This mali'thill is too child-ish to understand what \"love\" is.\n\nSo if the other mali'thill agrees. . . this mali'thill is revolting.\nIt is NOT okay to pursue sexual relations with mali'thill of under 50 years of age.\n\nSexual relations with a race that is not mali'aheral is considered impure. You will be labeled as a\n\nmali'ata and your 'lover' will be dunked in acid. It is disgraceful to have a child with another race. Even another race of Mali. (eg:Mali'ker, Mali'ame.)\n\nHowever, if you do not get caught, have fun living a wretched life of impurity.\n\nChapter 3: Knowledge\n\nKnowledge is power, and only the mali'aheral can achieve this state of being pure.\n\nOther races make too many mistakes, lack the common sense that is necessary to survive, or are just incompetent.\n\nThis is not 'racist' , it is a known fact by all except those who truly want to be ignorant.\n\n\n------------------\nThat was the end of this book, if you have any corrections or need any tips, be sure to talk to me.\n\nThis was written by Rinaline Sirius, on the 16th of First Seed, 1589.\n\n\nThank you for reading.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Abjurist", "§bGallidron Elindrow", "Wards & Abjuration\n^v^v^v^v^v^v^v^v^v^              a\n         study by\n\n  Gallidron Elindrow\n\nAbjuration is a facinating branch of voidal magic, and perhaps the most difficult. I say this because the most knowledge is required to be a succesfull abjurist or warder.\nTo cast a mighty evocation, one need only understand the element that will be their\n\nmedium and the art of evocating. To transmute water into ice one needs to understand the intricasies of the freezing process, to conjur one must know what they wish to make in exact detail before they can begin to even form the creature or item in the\n\nvoid. But to ward against these things, to abjure them into non-exsistance, one must not only understand how to cast protective magic, but they must also understand in detail the magic they wish stop.\n\nIt is for this reason that I say, the successful abjurist should be lauded above all other mage kind for they are at once massively powerfull and a dutiful student of all forms of magic. I would admonish any who seek to ward or abjure to learn all they\n\ncould of magic that they might not be caught unprepared. It would even be prudent to learn what one could of the dark arts, within the bounds of reason and safety, that they are not caught off guard by these most sinister magics.\n\nAn Abjuration is a more offensive style of protecting one's self or others from magic. Suppose that an enemy mage has cast a fire spell at you and a veritable flood of flame is headed in your direction. Now, you are somewhat knowledgable in the department\n\nof evocation and so you realize that the spell itself is functioning on the premise of converted energy. Fire is after all just energy moving from one form into another. In this case that energy is the mana of the mage and the form it has taken is shaped\n\nin the void. There are several ways to go about this, as is the beauty of abjuration (one is only limited by their resevoir of power and their imagination). You could try to disrupt the flow of mana from the mage to the spell, or you could break its\n\nconnection to the void. All in all, the most effective, fool proof, and easiest way is likely to break the connection to the void. This is called disrupting the mana anchor. The Mana Anchor is the metaphorical place where the spell and the energy for it\n\nmeet and create its effect. It is also what keeps the spell active in our world and not just a figment of a thought in the void. Knowing these things you craft your abjuration and destory the spell before it gets to you. Assuming of course that you are\n\nas powerful or more so than the mage who is trying to fry your skin off.\n\nA ward is more deffensive and takes more time to make. They are also potentially more versitile and usefull, this is the cause for the increased time of casting.\n\nA ward is stationary and is used like a shield against one or various forms of magic. Where as an Abjuration can be cast in the moment, it can only defend from one kind of magic. A ward can be crafted with care and forethought and thus be made to\n\ncounteract various kinds of magic at once. They can last for a long time, given that they have a steady supply of mana to draw upon. The larger and more intricate the ward, the more energy will be required. A ward that is crafted and placed on an object\n\nor in a place can be thought of much like an enchantment. In fact many enchantments are wards of one kind or another. These spells require the use of a mana gem, a kind of magical battery, to continue to function after the mage has left, and even then\n\nthe mana gem will only last for so long.\n\nA working knowledge of the magic to be blocked by the ward is still required to create the spell.\n\nPower is of tantamount importance. If you are a beginning abjurist, you cannot block the fire ball of a master evocationist. They will simply overwhelm you with the might of their spell. One must be at least equal to their opponent to bring their casting\n\nto a stand still.\n\nOne does not need to be a master evocationist, or even be able to cast evocation, to create wards or abjurations against it. But you will need at least a working knowledge of the element used.  Of course the more experience you have\n\nthe better you will fare as an abjurist.\n\nI hope that this book has been enlightening to you as a reader and that you will put this knowledge to good use.\n\n-Gallidron Elindrow\npublishing date 6th of\nGrand Harvest, 1519\n\nPlease look for my next study on the Arcane!\n\nLightning and fire and frost, oh my!\n\na study on Evocation", ""),
        StaticBook("JayasJourney C2", "§bSofetios", "Jaya's\n        Journey\n\n            ~By Sofetios\n                  Jayamen\n\n      Chapter Two\n\n         Linink\n\nAs I woke up the next morning I couldn’t help but feel a bit afraid. Linink was one of the most important people in town and he was never really free.\n\nAlso, why was my Mom so worried last night? I stepped out of bed. Instead of the usual hardwood floor I was used to I was stepped on something lumpy and cold. I looked down and jumped back on my bed.\n\nThe weapons from last night where on the floor right next to my bed. \n\n“Jaya what’s all the racket?” Said my mom’s voice. It sounded like she was coming to my room. Quickly, I covered the weapons with my blanket.\n\nLike that would do anything, but it was worth a shot. My mom came in the door and said,\n\n“Jaya why is your blanket on the floor?” \n\n“Bad dream.” I Lied.\n\nMy mom frowned, bent over, and was about to pick up the blanket. I closed my eyes and cringed from what was about to happen. \n\n“Jaya are you ok?” Said my Mom concerned.\n\nOpening my eyes I saw my mom had folded the blanket and on the floor was nothing.\n\n“Uh, yea.” I said scratching my head. “I think I just must have hit my head.”\n\n“Well it’s a good thing your going to see Linink today.” My mom said. “Come on, breakfast is ready.”\n\nI ate very little breakfast this morning. The taste of it as usual was great, but the stress of what I thought was to come overcame my hunger. After breakfast my mom took me straight to The Adventurers Guild.\n\nAs I approached the massive building I started to get nervous. My mom opened the doors, led me past staring students, rooms that where full of weapons, and many more things.\n\n Finally we reached Linink’s Office. The large green door looked inviting enough but as to what was to happen behind it still worried me.\n\nWithout knocking a voice said, “Come in.” My mom and I entered the triangle shaped room. I saw Linink sitting a table in the middle, but beside the table on both sides where my friends Sofetios and Sofia.\n\nI relaxed a little. At least they would be here with me. “Jaya, would you please take a seat.” Said Linink coolly.  I took a seat, looked back at my mom, and Linink then said,  “I need to speak to Jaya and his friends alone Madam.”\n\nMy mom nodded and left the room quietly. Everyone was quite for a bit until I said, \n\n“What am I here for?” \n\n“I’d like to know that too.” Sofetios added.\n\n“Same here.” Said Sofia. After a moment Linink said,\n\n“We are here to discuss a recent series of events.”\n\n“Like what?” said Sofia. “Other then the Melonko Festival being today all that’s really happened was that Rainstorm last night.\n\n“Exactly my point” said Linink. “A rainstorm now? In the middle of summer? I just don’t really see that as natural. There is also the Prophecy.”\n\n“You mean that old tale everyone listens to at the festival?” Said Sofetios inquiringly.\n\n“Just that.” Said Linink seriously. “From what I’ve seen happening in the last two days I’m inferring that it may be coming true.” At this Sofia laughed and said,\n\n“Like that would happen now. I mean the rain was probably coincidence and it’s not like the weapons have been found.” At this I felt the blood drain from my face.\n\n“Well from what I heard from Jaya’s Mother last night, I think they may have been found.” Said Linink. Sofia and Sofetios look right at me and Sofetios said, “You didn’t really… I mean you couldn’t have…”\n\n“He probably didn’t” Said Sofia, Otherwise he would be wearing them right now. It’s not like he would leave them at his house or something if he found them. Right?” I looked slightly down at this comment.\n\n“The weapons only appear if the owner wants them to.” Said Linink. Sofetios turns to me and said. “Well Jaya? Did you actually find the weapons?” I tell them the whole story of what happened last night and this morning.\n\nWhen I finish, Sofia is staring at me in disbelief, Sofetios is too, and Linink looks the same as he did before. No one said anything for a while until Linink said,\n\n“Well I guess the prophecy is coming true.”\n\n“Should we tell everyone?” Said Sofia.\n\n“No.” said Linink. “That would only raise panic. We should just tell whoever really needs to be told then you three set off after the festival.”\n\n“Us three?” I said.\n\n“Yes.” Said Linink “You know both Sofia and Sofetios pretty well. Except for what each they specialize in.” I didn’t actually know what Linink taught Sofetios and Sofia in the Guild and they had never shown me.\n\nI gave an odd face to all three of them then Linink said, “To put it bluntly Sofetios is a magician and Sofia is a fighter, in class terms that is.” \n\n“A magician?” I repeated while looking at Sofetios.\n\n“Intermediate magician.” Said Sofetios. “Sorry but Linink said we couldn’t tell anyone outside the guild.” \n\n“Wait so what can you do?” I said. “Levitate stuff or something?” Sofetios looked at Linink and Linink nodded.\n\nAll of the sudden everything small in the room started to slowly floated upwards.  I froze and looked around.  Then the things in the air floated down to their original places.\n\n“You see Jaya, Everything we teach at this guild more or less has to do with Lin.”  Said Linink.\n\n“Lin?” I said curiously.\n\n“Yes, it is something that is in everything around us.” Said Linink. “Kind of like a fifth element so to speak. Sofetios will be teaching you about this on your travels.”\n\n“Wait.” I said. “There’s a whole Fifth element?”\n\n“Yes.” Said Linink simply. “Sofetios, Sofia, please get ready for your departure.”\n\n“But sir.” Said Sofetios. “We still-“\n\n“Come on Sofetios.” Said Sofia as he grabbed Sofetios on the shoulder and had him follow her. When they closed the door, Linink turned to me and Said. \n\n“You have a long journey ahead of you.”\n\n“Yes sir…” I said tentatively.\n\n“I just need to make sure you really have the weapons.” Said Linink concerned.\n\n“I would sir but I don’t know how to get them to appear.” I said.\n\n“Just hold your hand as you would your sword and try to will them there.” Said Linink. I did so and in a second a white sword materialized there. It felt warm in my hand. Linink seemed was taken aback a little, so I asked,\n\n“Sir, why me?” Linink seemed back as himself and said after a moment,\n\n“Because He chose you. At least that’s what the Prophecy sais.” Linink got up and went to his bookshelf.\n\nHe picked up two books and put them both on the desk. He opened one and the inside of it was hollowed out. All there was, was a semi-clear sphere about an inch long.\n\nHe handed it to me and said. “This is something I’ve been working on for a long time. Use it well.”  I sheathed the sword and took the sphere and said,\n\n“Sir, what is this?”\n\n“Don’t you recognize it?” Linink said surprised. “It is object from part of the lore they tell at the Melonko festival. Well, my version of it anyway.”\n\n“I’m sorry sir, I don’t really listen to the tales they tell.” I said a little embaraced.\n\n“Well then I should probably tell you.” Said Linink. “It is my version of a water sphere. This one I would say holds about 1 week worth of water for 3 people.” Unbelieving, I stared at the sphere.\n\n“All you need to do is heat it in something and water will come out.”\n\n“Sir?” I said. “What did you mean by ‘your version’?”\n\n“You’ll find out soon enough.” Said Linink. Suddenly there was music outside. “The festival is starting.”\n\n“Sir, what now?” I said.\n\n“Now, you go enjoy the festival.” Said Linink. “But before you go,” Linink picked up the other book and handed it to me. “This is a book on lore of Edrot. You may want to study it.”\n\n I turned to leave when Linink said, “and Jaya, two words of advice. First, with the powers you will soon possess, Make sure you use them the right way.\n\nSecondly, I need you to will away that sword for now. The people in town don’t need to see it.”\n\n“Is that all sir?” I said.\n\n“That’s all” Said Linink.\n\nEnd of Chapter Two\n\nThank you for taking the time to read this book! I will be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing, please let me know by bird or in person!\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen", ""),
        StaticBook("History of Magic", "§bThe Eternal Library", "Transcribed on the 9th of The First Seed, 1548. Scribed by Archmage Bell, from the words of the Arcane Mage, Slayer.\n\nBefore the beginning of time there was nothing, no planes of existence, only the Void. But within the Void was a light, we know this light as god, the one deity. But this is not the lore we are interested in. This deity created in the void the world of\n\nAegis, wherefore you live out your lives. It came to pass that the deity moulded on the world of Aegis Man, whom he blew his breath of existence into; giving life. After breathing his existence into man, he entwined some of his divine power, raw and\n\nunformed into the world of Aegi itself. The power was to be called magic by man, and just as man was placed into the world to be tested, the power was to be utilized by man to be a test, a test of greed and power.\n\nThe power was entwined into the very fabric of both worlds; it ensured the worlds were linked ever connected. So it was that all the races of Aegis could access this power, but none were the same. As the power channeled through the individual, it scanned\n\ntheir soul, and was therefore manifested as an extension of their natural enviroment. So it was that the Humans had a diverse spread of magic, as they were the most versatile. The Elves came from their woodland homes, of nature and healing. Dwarves came\n\nfrom their mighty halls of stone hardened and sturdy. Then the orcs came from their harsh deserts, strong and vicious. The power of the Noble races came from their allegiance; the Ascended from their hallowed halls, pure and righteous. The undead from\n\ntheir fiery hells, tainted and evil. But the magic was not forever present in the world of Aegis. It was lost in times long past, forgotten to all but a few, when the evil Iblees was banished from this world. In his banishment he was sent to the void by\n\nthe ancients. This sent his minions back whence they came, and a barrier was erected between the worlds to stop their return. So it was that the worlds were no longer entwined, in seperation from the power no longer held, and magic all but disappeared\n\nfrom the world. But it was not to last. The power of Iblees was great, even in the void, and he worked at unraveling the barrier between the worlds. Soon the barrier began to break down, the undead taint spread once more across the face of Aegis. Although\n\nthis time there was littl hope for salvation. With the failure of the barrier protecting the world of the living from the dead, the magic once lost to the descendants, all those years ago, once again seeped into the fabric of the world. The people of\n\nAegis once more felt a somewhat unfamiliar tingle in their blood, unfamiliar but as though it was always there. So it came to pass that the ancient power of magic was awakened in Man. Tose with the power of foresight could see ahead a fork in the path.\n\nOne to light and the other to darkness. They could only hope that with this newly found power the right decision would be made.\n\nThe History of Magic\nWritten by Leyu'Maehr\nCopied by Rosso\nVaerhaven", ""),
        StaticBook("Double Dragon 4", "§bFenexo Freely", "\"Double Dragon!\"\nBy Fenexo Freely\nForward: The action begins in part four of \"Double Dragon!\" I hope you enjoy, and as always, Onto the story!\n\nChapter Eleven: Moonlit Melee\nAfter helping to clean up the large meal, I asked if it would be any trouble if I slept somewhere in their home. \"Follow me! I gotcha covered!” Natalie said, then led me to her room. She rubbed the back of her head\n\nSheepishly when we arrived \"Eheh, we don't really have a guest room, but I have two beds in mine so uh, guess where you're sleeping tonight!\" She said with a sly smile. I looked in the room with a grin on my face, \"Thank you so much for everything\n\nNatalie; I don't know how I could repay you.\" I said. Natalie waved her hand at me \"Forget about it! We're friends now right?\" She replied,walking into her room and throwing open a chest in front of the bed to the right. She rummaged around in it before\n\nPulling out a big net, exactly like the one that I was trapped in previously. She began to head back out of the room \"I just gotta go put this up, be back in a second!\" She said as she dashed down the hallway and out the front door. I giggled softly,\n\nLooking out the window to watch. She glowed slightly in the light of the full moon, giving a peaceful aura about her. She unhooked the cut net, before attaching the new one, standing back and resetting her trap. I saw some movement out of the corner of my\n\nEye, and when I looked over to the source, I saw the glint of a metal blade cut through the darkness. Bandits! I pounded on the window, desperately trying to get Natalie’s attention, but my racket making must not have been loud enough. The bandit was\n\nGetting closer, and now there were two! I sprinted out of the room, throwing my hat off “Shamone wake up! We need to save Natalie!\" I shouted at the parasite. He startled awake,before my hair grew out in thick cords, now reaching my lower back. Long hair\n\nHovered up and down my back, as I burst out the front door, \"Natalie! Watch out!\" I called out, warning her of the bandits. She looked to me, then around her, jumping back as she spotted the bigger of the two bandits already bringing his arming sword\n\nForward diagonally, aiming for her neck. She wasn't fast enough. The blade cut straight through her, cleanly decapitating her head from her body. I watched as her head flew through the air, landing on the ground, her body collapsing beside it. The clunky\n\nMan laughed \"I gotcha bitch!\" He shouted. I stopped dead in my tracks, holding my hand to my mouth to stop me from screaming. Sudden anger and sadness filled me to the brim, fueling my drive to take these guys out. I dropped my hand into a fist, stomping\n\nTowards him loudly as he laughed \"What are you gonna do little girl, kill me?\" He said amused, raising his bloody sword. I grimaced and bared my teeth, \"I'm not gonna kill you... I'm just gonna beat the shit out of you!\" I yelled, before charging at the\n\nMan, reaching into my hair and grabbing Shamones jaw, flinging it forward. His mouth shot outwards in my right hand, covering my arm in hair, forming the gaping maw of an alligator's mouth at my hand, outwards. The action took both of the men by suprise\n\nAnd the one that struck down Natalie swung at the hair, only to have his sword bounce off the thick cord back towards him, smacking his muscled chest but not cutting into him. Shamone bit the bandits left arm fiercely, piercing through his clothing and\n\nTearing at his flesh with his gruesome teeth. He cried out in pain, twisting his body, trying to escape the wicked bite. I swung my left fist as hard as I could, lengths of hair covering it up and spinning rapidly as tiny blades of hair grew on the now\n\nSpinning drill of a punch. He brought up his sword with his right arm, his left being disabled by Shamones teeth, attempting to block the powered up punch. My fist slammed into the flat of the blade, pressing it hard against his chest. I roared in pure\n\nUnadulterated rage at the man and the drill punch sped up while the sword starting glowing orange with heat from the intense friction acting against it. The man grunted and struggled with the blade, barely staying in place as his feet began to drag\n\nAgainst the dirt. \"Behind us!\" Warned Shamone. I shouted in fury, retracting the drilling arm and alligators bite, swinging my head forward, while a loud buzzing played in my ears. \"Gotcha!\" Shamone said as he responded to my actions, and thick cords of\n\nHair shot out with the headbutt like action. The hair ensnared the bandit in front of me as I lifted my head along with the bandit, and flung him behind me. The bigger bandit landed on the small frail one behind me, being crushed by his comrade. I turned\n\nTo face them, my hair flowing in thick swaths behind me “This is for Natalie you bastards!” I screamed and proceeded to kick the living hell out of both of them until they were poor, sniveling excuses of men. They moaned in pain and agony, as I looked at\n\nThem to make sure the bandits were down for the count. \"Don't you dare try to escape.\" I said in the most threatening voice I could muster. They coughed in response, writhing on the ground. I looked back to where I thought Natalie's body was and... It\n\nWasn't there. I looked around frantically, a panicked look on my face. Who would steal a body?! Then I heard something that made me believe I was going insane. I heard Natalie’s laughter. I looked to the source of the laughter and very nearly fainted.\n\nThere stood Natalie’s headless body, but she held her head at her side like a helmet. \"I didn't know you had a parasite! That's awesome!\" Her head said with a smile. Tears brimmed at my eyes and my knees grew weak \"This can't be real, you were killed!\" I\n\nSaid, not believing what I was seeing. She laughed again \"Haven't you heard that cats have nine lives? Well, I guess you could say this cat has quite a few more lives than nine, but the puns still there.\" She said, raising her head up over her exposed\n\nNeck. She plunked her head back down where it belonged, twisting it left then right. She smiled and spread her arms wide, as if it was no big deal that her head had come off. It was at that moment that the night had become too much for me to handle.\n\nI fell onto my knees, as my vision quickly went dark, and I blacked out.\n\nChapter Twelve: So That Really Did Happen?\nI woke up in a bed, still fully clothed from the feel of it. I was laying on my right side, with Shamone facing the rest of the room. The bed smelled of fresh laundry and, not surprisingly, cat hair. The\n\nEvents of last night came crashing down on me, as I sat up quickly and threw the covers off myself. I looked around the room to find that Natalie was nowhere to be found. I got out of the bed and grabbed my hat which was sitting on the bedside table,\n\nSlamming it over Shamone, right as he was greeting me with a \"Morn-\" Poomf. Covered. I heard his teeth clicking together in annoyance. \"Kid, take it easy, your friends fine. She said she was gonna get a small catnap in then go make breakfast... Speaking\n\nOf which! It's food time!\" Shamone said greedily. Even after hearing this reassuring news from Shamone, I rushed out the bedroom and to the kitchen. There I saw Sixis and Roger, weeping over dramatically \"Oh whatever shall we do! Our one and only\n\nDaughter was slain last night! Oh woe is we!\" Roger said, trying to hide a grin, while Sixis just started laughing. \"You-you’re laughing about your daughter dying!?\" I exclaimed in disbelief. Roger waved his hand \"She didn't die Fuqua! She has a parasite\n\nTo. Although from what we know from our chat with Shamone (\"He was still awake when you passed out by the way\" added Sixis.), Fishtail is a much different type of parasite.\" Robert said, as Sixis picked up where he left off \"Shamone helps you in combat a\n\nLot more than Fishtail, and can talk, but he can't grant you the ability to remain alive even after having your limbs cut off!\" Sixis explains. I eventually caught on to what they were saying \"So you mean to say that she's-\" I asked, before being\n\nInterrupted by Natalie herself \"Indestructible! Pretty much!\" She said, bursting into the dining room with huge stacks of pancakes on platters in both hands, balancing a bottle of syrup on her head between her long cat ears. She set the platters of\n\nPancakes on the table, then snatching the syrup off her head and placing it in between the fat stacks. I ran forward and grabbed Natalie in a hug \"You scared me half to death!\" I thought when your head got cut off, you were done for!\" I exlaimed, being\n\nVery upset. \"Eheh, sorry about that, probably should have mentioned that sooner. But for now, why don't you sit down and we can have an eat off again. I won't let you beat me this time!\" Natalie replied with grin, hugging me back briefly, before gently\n\nPrying me off. I nodded my head, “What about the bandits? What happened to them?” I asked. “Oh, them, I took care of it, told them that if they came back, their head would be the one on the ground. Then they just ran off.\" Natalie responded, sitting down\n\nAt the table.  \"Stop messing around babe and get ta eatin! I'm starvin up here!\" Shamone yelled, vibrating my skull. I took the hat off my head, letting my short hair free. Now that the family knew of Shamone there was no real reason to hide him \"Fine you\n\nGreedy hog!\" I shouted back, then sliding into the seat next to Natalie. The rest of the family chuckled at our bickering, as we all began to eat. It looked like a return to normalcy for me... Wait, wasn't I supposed to train for something?\n\n\n\n\n================End Of Part Four\n================\n\n\n\n\n\n\nCharacter Info-->\n\nNatalie FoRune: Age: 17 1/3. Height: 5'8\". Weight: 127 lbs. Likes: Making friends, Sunlit naps, Bowling, Golf, Parkour, Subjecting others to terrible puns, Robbing from the rich, Giving to the poor, Potatoes.\nDislikes: The mob, Feliz, Sushi, Guards,\n\nBullies, Marsupials, Loud noises, Lemonade, Getting caught.\nA feline feral, Natalie FoRune is an excelent thief and swordswoman. She makes use of her ability to not die of mortal wounds to her advantage, often stopping large groups of bandits from robbing\n\nA singular person. She is the mix between a human and a kharajyr, and has the traits of both. Her parasite, Fishtail, is her primary weapon of choice, her second being her claws. Her playfulness while fighting is a key signature of hers, often throwing\n\nOff enemys at how she laughs in the face of certain doom and death. She has been known for taking off her own head and placing it in random places, either to spy on people, or scare them.", ""),
        StaticBook("The little golem", "§bYden", "The little golem that could\nBy Yden\n\nJames was one of many golems in golem school, he was taught lots of subjects but he was best at following instructions class because he studied hard and practiced, when James got his degree in following orders he\n\napplied for a job, he would serve the common folk, James demonstrated his ability but he was much too cumbersome, he kept bumping into everything and his feet were too big, they broke floor boards and cracked the stone beneath his feet, his smaller friend\n\nTimmothy got the job instead so instead he applied to serve a knight, again he showed the knight what he could do, he shined the armour but his hands were too big and he was too strong, he simply crushed the armour like paper when he handled it so his\n\nmedium sized friend Jakob got the job instead. James was feeling very sad at this point, he felt like nobody wanted him, he was too wide, his feet were too big, he was too strong and his hands were too big but in one final attempt to serve a master he\n\napplied to serve the king, the king allowed him to perform and he excelled, the king had marble floors, the strongest floors money could buy, every room was huge so he would never bump into anything and the kings armour was made of mythril, not even James\n\ncould bend mythril so James was given the status of kings golem and served the king with honor until the king fell ill, James was very sad but he served the prince instead to carry on his great work!\n\n-Yden \nAlso see; The golem who learnt to love\n\nAlso see; The musical golem", ""),
        StaticBook("Jayas Journey C1", "§bSofetios Jayamen", "Jaya's Journey\n\n             ~By Sofetios\n                  Jayamen\n\n((This is a book I'm actually writing in real life. I really need motivation to continue, so please let me know what you think.))\n\n      Chapter One\n\n  A Walk in the Woods\n\n“Mom!” I yelled as I went out the back door. “I’m going out to the woods!”\n\n“Fine Honey!” My mom yelled back. “Just be back before dusk ok?”\n\n“Sure Mom!” I answered. I put on my sword, closed the back door, and headed out. Knowing exactly where I was going as I ventured more into the woods. As I walked I couldn’t help but notice how nice everything was today.\n\nFrom the glistening emerald green leaves leaving a slight greenish tint on the ground, to the random scuttles and noises coming from the woods, it just seemed like it was going to be a good day. I approached the usual spot I hung out at near dusk.\n\nA small opening with a flat rock and a small clear pool in the middle. It wasn’t much but I always thought better here. I laid down on the rock looking up into the trees and begin to wander off in my head.\n\nI was about to dose off when I heard something. I sat up quickly. It wasn’t a normal sound. It sounded like the song that Linink played on the piano at the Festival, only it was on a flute. Cautiously, I got up and went in the direction of the sound.\n\nIt got louder and louder as I went along through the trees and bushes until I reached another larger clearing. I looked amazingly at what I saw. There was a Statue in the middle of the clearing with multiple statues around the clearing.\n\nThey all looked just like the one in the middle of town, except in different poses. The one in the middle had his arms outstretched in front of him.\n\nI went closer and saw that in it’s his hands outstretched was a Bow, Arrows, A sword, a dagger, and a scroll. Cautiously I went up closer to the statue and was about to take the scroll when suddenly the music stopped and a voice said,\n\n“Hey! Boy! What are you doing here” Startled I jumped back and turned to face an old man.\n\n“I’m sorry!” I said shocked. “I was just going to look at it! I swear!” The Old man had a frown on but then smiled and said.\n\n“Well why didn’t you say so? Go ahead and take a look.”\n\n“Wait… what?” I said surprised.\n\n“Go on, take a look.” Said the old man again still smiling.\n\n“Um… ok…” I said confused, turning to face the statue. I slowly took the scroll and opened it. Written on it was… nothing.  I frowned and looked back but the old man was gone. Only in his place was a flute.\n\nI scratched my head, went to it, and picked it up. As I touched it I heard the music start again but this time, it was in my head. Shocked again I dropped the flute, the scroll, and the music stopped.\n\nCuriously I picked up the flute and tried to play the notes I had heard. I had played the flute many times before but this time felt different somehow. As I finished playing the notes nothing seemed to happen.\n\nI wondered what I was expecting to happen. Once I waited about a minute I bent down and picked up the scroll. Only this time there was something written on it. Curiously I examined the scroll.\n\n It had a few lines of music notes on it and the title of it was called “Song of the Rain”. The music notes where different from the ones I had just played, so I began to play these ones.\n\nAs I played it sounded like a stormy night at sea. When I completed it, nothing seemed to happen. After a minute I walked to the statue, wondering if it would be a good idea to take the sword. Suddenly a Voice behind me said,\n\n“Would you look at that! You’re a natural at the flute.”\n\nShocked, I turned to see the old man again, this time he had a bird on his shoulder. He had the same smile on his face. \n“Where do you keep coming from?” I said.\n\nSeeming to ignore the question the old man looked at the weapons the statue had.\n\n“I see you like those weapons?” Said the old man. \n\n“Yea. They look amazing.” I said turning to the statue.\n\nThen looked at the face and said,” But… where they his?”\n\n“What do you mean?” Said the old man. “You’re the one wearing them.”\n\n“What do you mean?” I said turning to the old man again.\n\nBut he was gone. I turned back to the statue that had the weapons but they were gone. Suddenly I saw something on myself. I looked down and there was a sword on my belt, but it wasn’t my sword, it was the statues.\n\nI looked to the other side of my belt and there was the dagger. I slowly reached up and touched my shoulder. As I did I felt the feathers of an arrow and the string of a bow.\n\n I then realized it was dusk, my mom would be annoyed if I wasn’t home soon. I took off the weapons, put them back on the statues hands, and began to walk home.\n\n As I walked home I noticed the air began to feel denser and the wind began to pick up a little bit. Suddenly. I felt a raindrop. I looked up and one hit me on my forehead. I started running as fast as I could toward the house.\n\nAs I got in the back door my mom was standing there with her arms crossed. \n\n“Where have you been?” She said sternly. “You’re late and your soaked head to toe!”  All I could say was,\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“Jaya.” Said my mom in a warning voice. \n\nI sighed and recounted the whole story about the old man, the weapons, the flute and the scroll. As I finished my mom looked pale.\n\n“Are you alright?” I said. She walked slowly to a kitchen chair and sat down.\n\n“Jaya.” My mom began. “I’m going to have you see Linink.”\n\nLinink was the best doctor around and also the leader of the adventurer’s guild, a guild that helps people and goes on quests. My friends Sofetios and Sofia go there.\n\n“Wait, why?” I said concerned. “Do you think I’m sick?”\n\n“No Jaya.” My mom said. “It’s about something else. I think it’s time you went to bed.” Confused and concerned, I went to bed.\n\nEnd of Chapter One\n\nThank you for taking the time to read this book! I will be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing, please let me know by bird or in person!\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen\n((Sofetios))", ""),
        StaticBook("Azdrazi", "§bMichael Corvin", "The\n         Azdrazi\n       -=======-\n       \n\n\n\n     \n\n\n\n       Written By \n    Michael  Corvin\n\nThe Azdrazi are creatures created from trials and a ritual called 'The Transference' within this ritual, the subject's soul is believed to be reformed from that of a human soul, into the soul of a dragon.\n\nThis is what causes the creatures to have a dragonic look to them, such as the horns and glowing orange eyes. The Azdrazi can also hide away some of their physical traits, such as scales and slitted pupils.\n\nThese creatures also hold something within them they refer to as their inner flame. In many senses it acts as their mana pool, this so called inner flame allows these creatures to breath fire, and alight their weapons with brillant flames.\n\nAzdrazi are warmer to the touch then most, due to the inner flame within them. Although they may be warm to the touch when in cold temperature Azdrazi will find their stregnth fades and they'll become weak until they eventually die due to the cold.\n\nContrary to popular\nbeliefs Azdrazi are in fact creatures created for a positive cause. They exist to fight the darkness within our world, to protect mortals from any threats that may reveal themselves. Keep this in mind when meeting any Azdrazi.", ""),
        StaticBook("Healing Potions", "§bIatrilemar", "= Alchemical Healing =\n\n\nAlchemy is one subject I believe is better left untouched by most. It often results in deadly explosions and vile abominations brought on by unnatural means. However Alchemy covers one\n\narea that is quite useful in many survival situations. That is alchemical healing. While it seems mundane with the existence of the monks and, as I like to call them, void healers, knowing how to prepare simple potions is akin to knowing how to light a\n\nstove in order to eat or learn to write in order to document knowledge.\n\n= =  The Basics  = =\n\nThere are three basic ingredients that will be useful in most potions. Distilled water, Tippen's root and what I call a basic\n\nbandage potion.\n\nTippen's root can be found in oak trees and by crushing together two oak saplings.\n\nDistilled water is made via the use of a alchemy stand and coal. Run the water through the alchemy stand using\n\nthe coal as a filtration device. The end product will be distilled water. \n\nNow, a bandage potion is made by the same process as distilled water but you simply use Tippen's root instead of coal. \n\nWhile making\n\ndistilled water and a bandage potion should be common alchemy knowledge we will only be using Tippen's root for healing potions. \n\n=  Healing Potions  =\n\nI have found five different ways of creating what I call a minor healing potion\n\nwhich can hasten the healing process of a wound. I shall list them and the ingredients used in making them. I hope it is obvious that to make these you will need an alchemy stand. This isn't some mundane void healing based on false science.\n\nPotion #1\n\nIngredients:\n\n-Tippen's root\n-One rotten potatoe\n-Sugarcane\n\nEffects:\nMundane healing. May experience slight weakness.\n\nPotion #2\n\nIngredients:\n\n-Tippen's root\n-One rotten potatoe\n-A bundle of vines\n\nEffects:\nMundane healing. May experience tightness and inability to sprint.\n\nPotion #3\n\nIngredients:\n\n-Tippen's root\n-A rotten potatoe\n-A lily pad\n\nEffects:\nMundane Healing. May cause slight blindness for limited period of time.\n\nPotion #4\n\nIngredients:\n\n-Tippen's root\n-A rotten potatoe\n-Ice\n\nEffects:\nMundane healing. Muscle tightness and temporary weakness.\n\nPotion #5\n\nIngredients:\n\n-Tippen's root\n-A rotten potatoe\n-Shiny Melon slice\n\nEffects: \nMundane healing. Temporary blindness and muscle tightness.\n\n~= Rejuvenation =~\n  ~=   Potion    =~\n\nRejuvenation potions are similar to mundane healing potions but work in a slightly different way. Instead of applying a direct healing effect to the body the user will experience a sensation of\n\nhealing over a period of time. This potion is useful for helping with the gradual healing process and often provides a more wholesome healing effect. I have found five seperate ways to construct a rejuvenation potion.\n\nPotion #1\n\nIngredients;\n\n-Tippen's Root\n-Sugarcane\n\nEffects:\nMundane rejuvenation. Slight weakness.\n\nPotion #2\n\nIngredients:\n\n-Tippen's root\n-A bundle of vine\n\nEffects:\nMundane rejuvenation. Muscle tightness.\n\nPotion #3\n\nIngredients:\n\n-Tippen's root\n-A lily pad\n\nEffects:\nMundane rejuvenation. Temporary blindness.\n\nPotion #4\n\nIngredients:\n\n-Tippen's root\n-Ice\n\nEffects:\nMundane rejuvenation. Muscle tightness and slight weakness.\n\nPotion #5\n\nIngredients:\n\n-Tippen's root\n-A shiny melon slice\n\nEffects:\nMundane rejuvenation. Muscle tightness and temporary blindness.\n\nI have also documented a more volatile version of the rejuvenation potion that has increased healing properties but with worse side effects. It is important to note that all potions will have side effects. It is incredibly difficult to purify a potion and\n\ncreate a potion with no negative side effects.\n\n~Greater Rejuvenation potion~\n\nIngredients:\n\n-Tippen's root\n-A rotten potatoe\n-Ice\n-A lily pad\n\nEffects:\nLasting rejuvenation. Lasting muscle weakness. Lasting blindness. Temporary muscle tightness.\n\nWith this knowledge I hope you may learn to better serve yourself in times of need.\n\n*A couple ink spots dot the page*\n\n\n~Iatrilemar \n      Elervathar", ""),
        StaticBook("Unknown", "§bUnknown", "Ellir\nAsul\nBarbu\nEloem\nTahorran\nCelia'onn\nLeyulin\nFaesu\nSan'thill\nLuci'onn\nSirame", ""),
        StaticBook("Mundane Healing", "§bValmir", "Mundane Healing\nBy Unknown\n\nMatron’s Foreword:\nWhen I was studying medicine in Oren, a strange Dwarf brought me with a book of medical recipes that used very common ingredients for the treatment of various medical casualties, by an Aleya Darolien.\n\nOne or two of them sounded interesting, though a large amount of them were, largely ridiculous, like one that claimed it possible to heal a major burst of an artery or internal organs with nothing but rose thorns and petals.\n\nIn the interest of not wasting precious paper, I’ve recorded some of the more practical recipes in the book, and added one of two of my own, to make up for lost space.\n\nMinor Cut (Matron)\nSimply apply a dose of deceased sentient slime to the affected area, and wrap a light bandage around it. The slime helps stop the bleeding, and the mildly acidic nature of it is known to remove the taint from a body.\n\nHeadache (Aleya)\nBoil milk, Add honey while the milk boils. Take Dandelion stems and Rose thorns and put them in a thin satchel. Dip the satchel in the milk and let it infuse with the mixture for about 2 minutes. Slowly drink the milk.\n\nStomach Ache (Matron)\nBoil water. Once it hits a full boil, add honey until the liquid turns an amber colour. Add tea leaves to a small bag, and let it soak in the mixture for about five minutes. Drink the tea slowly.\n\nSoreness (Aleya)\nSimply apply rags with hot water to relax he muscles, and rest a lot.\n\nBurns (Matron)\nSet your hands on some vinegar, and drench a clean bandage with it. Apply this bandage to the affected area, and wrap a dry bandage around this to keep the vinegar from getting absolutely everywhere. The important thing about burns is\n\nto keep the affected area moist while it mends, as burned areas do not produce sweat properly. Vinegar is ideal for this, as it will keep the area moist and not evaporate as water would. Be sure to check the burn wound daily until\n\nthe skin can finally reform.\n\nFever (Aleya)\nKeep the victim’s body cool using cold water. Give him hot milk steeped with brown mushroom stems and ground rose thorns. It will fight off disease. Keep the victim rested and feed him mushroom soup.", ""),
        StaticBook("Black Moon Fest", "§bThe Eternal Library", "Black Moon Festival\n\nBy Unknown\n\nThe day of a lunar eclipse in Anthos. Once night falls, you won’t feel the warmth of the sun for many hours to come. Although a rare event, it occurred a few times in Asulon.\n\nSome denizens of the word took it as a sign of the end of everything while\n\n others celebrated the strange event. During this time the rates of crime increased dramatically as criminals took advantage of the unexpected darkness.", ""),
        StaticBook("The Great North", "§bAptrotta", "The birthplace of the scourge… The final resting place of an ancient civilization… A land without hope… A land filled with beauty… Death hides everywhere… Life reveals itself everywhere… This land.. This ancient land… This land is The North.\n\nI, Cyrenn Yuln’Taynei, ventured into this dangerous territory with no respect to my own life all for one purpose. To discover. Discover what was lost. Discover what was hidden. Discover the unknown. The following pages you are about to read are written\n\nfrom using the notes I took on my expedition into The North.\n\nThe taint. The corruption. Its everywhere it seems. Even the entrance to The North is blocked by a large gate erected by the corrupt hands of the scourge. The air is foul with the stench of\n\ndeath. The snow stained crimson. Blood has been shed here recently. But, I must continue…\n\nJust after passing the gates I am greeted by an ancient ruin and the icy face of a cliff towards my front and to my left. With a quick glance to my right I notice a\n\na path revealed in the snow. The paw prints of some native animal leading me towards an unknown world.\n\nAfter about an hour of following the tracks I finally reach what I was looking for.  The path which shall lead me into the secret region of The North.\n\nA chasm lays before me. It twists and turns as my eyes follow it.Large stone tendrils reach out from the sides of the cliffs and the bottom like arms trying to reach for the sky. Luckily, these stone formations form perfect land bridges for I to walk\n\nupon. Without a thought of giving up, I continue forth.\n\nMy first night I spent still inside the chasm. I pitched my tent on a flat spot near the edge of a cliff. It was impossible to keep a fire going. The harsh wind that blew throuh the chasm smothered\n\nmy fire, stung my skin, and tried to blast my tent away from the cliff. Every hour the temptature dropped. This night was becoming a nightmare. Finally, my eyes gave into sleep.\n\nI awoke to a scream at the crack of dawn. A scream of terror. A scream of\n\npain. A scream signifying their final moments in life. The scourge was near. I had to go. With great haste I packed my tent and left.\n\nNothingness… All I encountered for miles was nothingness… Bare land stretched out before me for miles covered in snow\n\nand nothingness… This can’t be it… This wasteland can’t be all that The North is. My journey must go on.\n\nThere it is. The first sign of a past civilization. Standing before me was a towering ruin of a kingdom lost from time. Thewalls have fallen, stone\n\nlaying around the snow, while the supports stand tall holding up this ancient structure. A closer work would reveal the history of the ancient people who lived here before us. We could finally see how life really was for the previous inhabitants of our\n\nworld. This kingdom. This ancient Kingdom held secrets that could change our view on life forever. It was my duty to take a peek into the past.\n\nI stomped forward through the snow over fallen structures, over relics, over bones. The bones...\n\nThey were everywhere. Perfect skeletons resting against stone walls, skeletons laying down in the middle of the path, skeletons sitting in chairs, skeletons hunched over counters. The farther I went into the city the more I saw. Each skeletal corpse\n\nresting in a position one would do during life. Something hit this city… Something purged the city of its population. But what?\n\nWith that I leave this forgotten kingdom. I leave its people behind. The mystery to what wiped away its people will stay that,\n\na mystery.  I don’t dare to take anything with me in worry that whatever killed these poor citizens may still be lurking the ruined streets.\n\nThe day is still young when I came across a small white tent and a fire pit. Excited, I set of in a run towards\n\nit. Laid out before me was a horrific scene. A decaying body was left in the snow, the campground covered in blood leading towards a small tunnel. I decided to follow the trail, my curiosity getting the best of me.\n\n\nThe tunnel was small and cramped. Sunlight shone down through cracks in the ceiling. When I reached the end of the blood trail, a skeletal women stood hunched over a small lever as if she was guarding it from someone. Next to her sat a large basket\n\nconnected by rope. It hung over a large hole. The rope stretched upwards curled around a wooden beam. It hit me. A lift.\n\nThe lift shook slightly as I stepped into the basket. I reached out towards the women, my fingers splayed. Carefully, I knocked the\n\nlever the other direction, the bones falling over into a heap. With a creak, the basket started lowering itself downward. I was plunged into a darkness as the basket passed through the stick thone.\n\nMinutes passed before I was able to see light. The stone\n\ntunnel finally opened up and it came to my attention that I was placed inside a large cavern, the floor flooded with molten lava except for few places.  A bridge made of dark stone, the color of blood, stretched across the lake of lava. Blood shards\n\nfloated over the molten liquid. Across the lake sat a strange looking ruin.\n\nThe basket, only five feet away from the floor, plummeted towards the ground as the rope snapped above. I hit the ground hard, the basket landing on its side. Slowly I crawled\n\nout from the basket unharmed. I swiveled my head to the left, already making my way towards the bridge. Bubbles formed on the surface of the lake, popping every few seconds sending droplets of lava upwards at an alarming speed while I crossed the bridge.\n\nThe ruin was a mess. It appeared as if someone tried to get out quickly. From what it looked like, the place had once been a workshop for some sort of engineer.  There was faded blueprints laying on a desk to the front of me, a tool table to my left, and\n\nan oddly placed track to my left which rounded a corner. The tunnel in which it disappeared gave off a dim glow. My interest having peaked, I set off to follow the track.\n\nThe tunnel twists and turns as I walk through, a faint clicking sound audible. The\n\n room suddenly opens up to a large cavern, a weird iron device hanging from the ceiling nearing the floor. A few feet in front of it rests a mound of stone with a keyhole in the center. Soon I discovered I am sitting atop a gaint cage. Could whatever it\n\nheld be what killed the population? Scared, I dart back towards the lava lake quickly crossing the bridge only to remember the basket had fallen. \n\nMy eyes dart around the cavern desperately searching for a way out. A stroke of luck hit me as I noticed\n\nvines hanging down from a rock edge above, daylight shining down. My feet moved towards the vines before my brain could think. I curled my fingers up around one of them and slowly pulled myself up, panting by the time I reached the surface.\n\nThe endless\n\nwalk began again. Tall pine trees have begun to appear more frequently. Every once in a while I would come across a deer or some other animal…\n\nAfter a few days of nothingness, I finally gave up on the expedition... Food supplies were running low and so\n\nwas my patience... I knew The North had to hold more, but my life sources were dwindling...\n\nThe Northern Expedition was a great intellectual experience for myself. I hope my efforts to discover have affected some of you to venture out into this vast land by yourself. As usual, I wish you farewell.\n\n*A large, fancy signature rests on the bottom of the page*\n\n\n<()>Cyrenn Yuln’taynei<()>", ""),
        StaticBook("I.E's Journal", "§bIaria Elervathar", "*The cover of this journal seems to be cladded with blue and purple water paint, along with vague drawings that got eventually ruined. On the first page, there's a beautiful night sky drawn. The owner's initials are written in the middle.*\n\n-I. Elervathar\n\n[!] The pages are filled with massive walls of text. At times, a small drawing that relates to the topic is seen at the side.\n\n(PM Halsi if you're interested to see the entries!)", ""),
