        StaticBook("Odd Events C1", "§bJaya Jayamen", "Odd Events§0\n§0\n§0       ~Originally by§0\n§0     Sofetios Jayamen§0\n\n         Chapter One§0\n§0    §0\n§0      The Light§0\n§0\n§0My name is James Geedin. My family recently settled in a new land without a name, so I call it Genway.  We were lucky enough to stumble upon an old house.\n\nWhoever built it, I don’t know. Some odd stuff seems to happen here, but I’m determined to stay at my new home. This is my story.\n\nI woke up in the middle of the night with chills running down my spine. I was around 17 at the time. I went downstairs to get something to drink.\n\n That’s when I noticed it. I looked out the window and noticed a light in the distance at the woods. Though it wasn’t yellow like a fire, it was white. I thought about checking it out, but decided I should go to bed instead.\n\nThe next day in the morning, I went downstairs to have breakfast, but the thing was, my parents weren’t awake. That was unusual, so I went upstairs to their room and knocked on the door. §0\n§0\n\nWhen I knocked on the door it simply opened slowly from the force of my knock. I looked in, but no one was there. I spent the rest of the day looking for them, but to no avail. §0\n§0\n\nSoon it became nighttime and I was in the kitchen. That’s when I noticed it again, the same light as before. This time I went out towards the light. I came to the forest clearing where the light was coming from.\n\n There was a small mirror in the center, angled towards my house. Then I noticed something, the white light wasn’t coming from the mirror, It was coming from the attic window of my house being reflected by the mirror.\n\nI saw a word carved into the mirror. It said “Truth”. I looked back towards the house. Could my parents be were that light is? I took the mirror and made my way back the house.\n\nI got to the house and made my way upstairs to look for a way up into the attic. I looked different places until I got to my parents room. I walked in and there was a small white light coming from the bookcase in there, going around the books.\n\nI walked up to the bookcase and looked through the cracks between the books. There was a white stairway that lead upwards.  I shifted the bookcase over and started walking up the white stairs. Until I got to a white trapdoor above me.\n\n cautiously, I lifted it up a crack and looked in the attic room.  There were two statues that looked just like…  I then opened the trapdoor fully and rushed into the white room towards the two statues.\n\n They were exact replicas of my parents, but they had faces that looked like they were scared of something. Suddenly there was the sound of the trapdoor shutting behind me.\n\nI looked back to see something in white robes that covered its whole body and a light coming from under its hood.\n\n“Who are you?” I said scared. It simply stood there. I looked back towards my parents for a second and back again. §0\n§0\n\n Then it was right in front of me. Startled I fell back and the mirror dropped on the floor face up but didn’t break. I got up and backed up. It didn’t move.\n\n Then I noticed something. In the reflection on the mirror on the ground, where the thing was supposed to be was a person. The person in the reflection had light coming from its eyes.\n\nI took out my short sword. Getting ready to fight whatever this was. I lunged at its chest, but as it made contact with the light coming from it, the blade turned white. I dropped my sword from it happening.\n\nI was about to panic when I remembered the mirror, then got an idea. Keeping my eyes on the person, I picked up the mirror, careful not to get in the light.\n\nThen with one hand, I slid it under the light coming from the person’s hood, trying to aim it back at it. Suddenly the light coming from under the hood disappeared. Then the person in the robes simply fell over, solid as a stone. §0\n§0\n\nI turned to the statues of my parents. I wasn’t sure what to do. I talked, I yelled, but there was no response. I had never felt so alone before. My parents turned into stone, not knowing how to turn them back to normal.\n\nBut then that made me realize something. Something I should have thought from the start. §0\n§0\n§0Who had left the mirror in the woods?§0\n§0\n§0End of Chapter One§0", ""),
        StaticBook("Melunis the Mad", "§bJaya Jayamen", "Melunis the Mad§0\n§0\n§0          ~Originally by§0\n§0       Sofetios Jayamen§0\n\nI was travelling to a place called Geedin in search of a philosopher called Melunis. This man was said to be the wisest in the land, according to my friend who went there a while back. So I figured I would see what wisdom he could offer me.\n\nI arrived in the town and walked up to a guard. I said,§0\n§0\n§0“Excuse me sir, could you direct me to the man named Melunis?” The guard just laughed and said,§0\n§0\n\n“The only Melunis I know is a madman, you can find him over by the alley.” I thought to myself, this madman couldn’t be the one that my friend was talking about. My friend was a very smart person and wouldn’t confuse a wise man with a madman.\n\nSo to satisfy my knowing of whether or not this was the correct man, I went to the alleyway and saw an old man in rags. I said to the man.§0\n§0\n§0“Are you the man known as Melunis?” to which he looked at me, smiled, and said,§0\n\n“Indeed so! How can I help you this fine day!” The day was overcast, and cold. I couldn’t see how it was fine.§0\n§0\n§0“Would you happen to know my friend, James Linson?”  The man looked overjoyed at the name and said,§0\n\n“Why yes! I remember the lad. I do hope he is doing well.”§0\n§0\n§0“Well he told me you were the wisest in the land…” I said, to which Melunis replied with a frown,§0\n\n“Wise? No I’m not wise. I am just a ragged old man in an alleyway. Do I look like I’m Wise?”§0\n§0\n§0“No you don’t.” I said. “You don’t look wise at all.” The man then got a sparkle in his eyes.§0\n§0\n\n”Not wise at all?” said the man. “Well I must agree. You being very well off must know what it means to be wise, as you are in a far better place then I.” The man thought for a second, then said, “Tell me, what does it mean to be wise.”\n\n“Well it means that you know a lot about things.” I said, “Like a smith knows how to smith well, he is wise in smithing.”§0\n§0\n§0“Ah..” said the man. “ So in a sense, being wise is having knowledge.”§0\n§0\n§0“Yes.” I said.§0\n\n“ Alright, well what is knowledge.” Said Melunis. “As in how would I be able to obtain it to become more wise.” I thought for a second, then said,§0\n§0\n§0“Knowledge is knowing something, and obtained by learning.”§0\n\n“I see!” Said the man. “But… how do I know I know something.” At this I was a bit perplexed. I said,§0\n§0\n§0“Could you explain what you mean?” The man nodded and said,§0\n\n“In other words, How do you know what you know is true.”§0\n§0\n§0“Because you know it works when applied to things.” I said.§0\n\n“Alright.” Said the man. “So knowledge is what you know works when applied to things, but how does that make it true?”§0\n§0\n§0“What do you mean?” I said.§0\n\n\n§0“I mean for example, we could see an abstract painting.” Said Melunis. “You could think it’s one thing, I could think its another. Both works when applied, at least to the individual, but who is right?”§0\n\n“I guess we would both be right.” I said. “It would be up to the individual.”§0\n§0\n§0“Ah I see now!” Said the man. “So knowledge is what works with the individual. But still, how do we know it is true? Because isn’t the truth universal?”§0\n\n I began to get frustrated. But… Then I came to a realization.§0\n§0\n§0“I guess the truth is up to the person.” The man looked at me and said,§0\n\n“So there is no real truth? But what about common knowledge?” I thought for a second and said,§0\n§0\n§0“The common knowledge is what people have a common belief in.” Melunis nodded, seeming to understand.§0\n\n“But then what is the real truth?” The man said.\n\n“The real truth…” I began, “The real truth is… unknowable. Even if we got to it, people still would have different opinions. And those opinions might have more people believing in it than the real truth.”\n\nMelunis smiled at me and said,§0\n§0\n§0“Thank you, I think I understand now what it means to have knowledge.”§0\n§0\n§0Soon after this conversation, I left. Slightly confused, but more understanding.§0\n\n Though now I realized what my friend meant by him being the wisest in the land.§0\n§0\n§0The End§0", ""),
        StaticBook("Poetry V1", "§bJaya Jayamen", "Poetry§0\n§0\n§0          ~Originally by§0\n§0       Sofetios Jayamen§0\n\nCOURAGE§0\n§0\n§0It is a thing you use§0\n§0When you can't go to the end,§0\n§0When all seems lost§0\n§0And you can't defend.§0\n§0\n§0You use it as§0\n§0It combines with your will,§0\n§0All you need to do is§0\n§0Let it give its fill.§0\n\nThe choice is yours,§0\n§0Give in or go,§0\n§0Though in the end§0\n§0Only you will know.§0\n§0\n§0See with your spirit§0\n§0And not with fear,§0\n§0for then you will know§0\n§0That courage is here.§0\n§0\n\nTHE DREAMER§0\n§0\n§0The Dreamer,§0\n§0The one who dares not be the same,§0\n§0For in dreams mysteries unravel,§0\n§0And endless space you do travel,§0\n§0As your dreams you try to tame.§0\n\nAs the Dreamer,§0\n§0Dreams above the sky,§0\n§0And looks down below,§0\n§0To the people that show,§0\n§0Who know not what lies that high.§0\n§0\n\nUnawakened, the Dreamer,§0\n§0In a maze of thoughts,§0\n§0Wandering the mists,§0\n§0With endless twists,§0\n§0As the path on minds paper he jots.§0\n\nThough be wary dreamer,§0\n§0As thoughts tend to run away,§0\n§0Only the ones you really need,§0\n§0And the ones you often feed,§0\n§0Are the ones that often stay.§0\n\nTHE LAKE§0\n§0\n§0I wander upon a path by a lake,§0\n§0Wondering where it’s all gone.§0\n§0Every day I used to come here,§0\n§0From the morning to the evening's yawn.§0\n\nEvery now and again I wonder,§0\n§0Why the days have passed.§0\n§0And think very deeply,§0\n§0Why they don't seem to last.§0\n\nDay by day, night by night,§0\n§0The Lake becomes smaller still.§0\n§0The thoughts of it become thinner,§0\n§0Even to my strongest will.§0\n\nIt has become ten times smaller and ten times shallower,§0\n§0Even thought it has not changed.§0\n§0To other people it might be normal,§0\n§0but to me it is considered strange.§0\n\n\n§0FRIEND OR ENEMY?§0\n§0\n§0Everywhere I am§0\n§0I hide from your sight,§0\n§0If it's in the day,§0\n§0And especially at night.§0\n\nI am one of sleep's worst enemies,§0\n§0And one of fear's best friends,§0\n§0I grow ever more larger,§0\n§0As your will begins to bend.§0\n\nIn the day I am more friendly,§0\n§0Especially when its hot and I'm in sight,§0\n§0But when it turns to night time,§0\n§0I'm sure to give you a fright.§0\n\nI am a mimic of sorts,§0\n§0I try to copy you while you walk,§0\n§0I even mimic other people,§0\n§0Although I cannot talk.§0\n\nEven when you go about the day,§0\n§0I skulk and kind of stalk,§0\n§0Fear or fear not, I am always here,§0\n§0I am the shadow on which you walk.§0\n\nTHE COBBLER AND THE CROW§0\n§0\n§0I was working in the night,§0\n§0As I bent over a shoe,§0\n§0I was working very hard,§0\n§0to finish before the morning dew.§0\n§0\n\nI was just about finished,§0\n§0When what was this I hear?§0\n§0A tapping, slight tapping,§0\n§0And it seemed to be so near.§0\n\nI turned to the window,§0\n§0And what did I see?§0\n§0A crow tapping at my glass,§0\n§0Staring at me.§0\n§0\n§0\"Go away.\" I said,§0\n§0Sternly as I did stand.§0\n§0\"Don't you know I have work to do?\"§0\n§0Though I know it didn't understand.§0\n\nBut the crow kept tapping at the glass,§0\n§0And I began to get mad,§0\n§0This crow just would not stop,§0\n§0Not even a tad.§0\n\nI turned to my unfinished shoe,§0\n§0To work as long as I could bear,§0\n§0But then I noticed something missing,§0\n§0That would stop me from completing the pair.§0\n§0\n\nThe golden lace that I needed,§0\n§0For this order to be complete,§0\n§0I couldn't find it anywhere,§0\n§0As the glass the crow did beat.§0\n\nI checked my table, checked my pockets,§0\n§0And then to my surprise,§0\n§0There was a hole in one,§0\n§0That was just the right size.§0\n\nI would never finish this shoe,§0\n§0By the morning light,§0\n§0Now things were dire,§0\n§0and my situation was quite tight.§0\n§0\n\n\"I must have dropped it,§0\n§0On the way back from the store!\"§0\n§0But it was too far I thought,§0\n§0As my confidence tore.§0\n\nBut then I realized \"What's this?\"§0\n§0\"Where was the tapping I couldn't bare?\"§0\n§0I looked to the window,§0\n§0And the crow wasn't there.§0\n\nI went to the window,§0\n§0And what was in its place?§0\n§0It was simple and divine,§0\n§0The golden lace.§0\n\nI opened the window,§0\n§0And took it with care,§0\n§0Because of who gave this gift,§0\n§0I was certainly aware.§0\n\nI went to my table,§0\n§0And finished the shoe.§0\n§0But then thought of the crow,§0\n§0If I only knew!§0\n§0\n§0I then went to bed,§0\n§0With not much I could say,§0\n§0I only did hope,§0\n§0The crow would come back some day.§0\n\nIMPRINT§0\n§0\n§0\n§0I stand in a forest,§0\n§0One that I know quite well,§0\n§0Where the trees are tall,§0\n§0And they never do fall,§0\n§0At least that's what I like to tell.§0\n\nI came here,§0\n§0When I was a boy,§0\n§0Usually in the day,§0\n§0But any time I would say,§0\n§0The forest was my favorite toy.§0\n\nI came here again,§0\n§0A few years ago,§0\n§0It was the same place I knew well,§0\n§0As far as I could tell,§0\n§0There was nothing different to show.§0\n\nI open my eyes now,§0\n§0To see the trees,§0\n§0My eyes see an empty place,§0\n§0As if leaving no trace,§0\n§0Though this is not what my mind sees.§0\n§0", ""),
        StaticBook("Level 2 Code 1", "§bJaya Jayamen", "Level 2 Code 1§0\n§0\n§0~By Jaya Jayamen§0\n§0\n§0Tell me the answer for a prize! First to come gets the prize!\n\nIf you want easy try level 1.§0\n§0\n§0So you read this and think what?§0\n§0\n§0It helps if you please say a good greeting.§0\n\nI know well you can say the word cook.§0\n§0\n§0If I knew you well you are a breaker.§0", ""),
        StaticBook("Theory Infinity", "§bJaya Jayamen", "A Theory of Infinity§0\n§0\n§0          Originally by§0\n§0      Geedin Jayamen§0\n§0\n\nLately I have begun to think about how things are connected. Take a rug for example. We see it as just one rug, but that one rug is made up of smaller things. Wool or string, which in turn is made of smaller fibers.\n\n I think that things get smaller, maybe even §0infinitely.Just§0 as things can be made of smaller things, so can smaller things make up one big thing. Again with the rug example.\n\nAll of those fibers make up wool or string, which make the rug. You may think it stops there, but what is the difference between fibers to a rug then to a rug being a part of a house?\n\nThe rug isn’t physically sewn into the house, yet it is part of it, the same as the fibers not being part of a rug until it is sewn into §0one.This§0 begins a theory of Infinity. Let me give another example. Apples and oranges.\n\nThey are different, right? Well that is all a matter of perspective. Apples may be different then oranges, but they can still be grouped into what we call fruits. Even everything in the world,\n\nwith its infinite different parts and pieces, can be grouped into what we call, well, the world. We may seem to think of the smaller things as making more sense when we should really be looking towards the bigger.\n\nNow let’s get a bit deeper. What is the difference between yourself and someone else? Well, there are differences, but there are also similarities. Well let’s think, what would make you two the same?\n\nWell the thing is, you are part of something that doesn’t make you the same, but groups you into one thing. Now for the trick. Say we are seeking all knowledge of things. We learn more and more, yet never are satisfied with what we know,\n\nbecause there is always more to know. Well we are probably looking for some way or some knowledge that can make us content. I think I may have realized what that something is. It is something complex, yet simple at the same time.\n\nIt is everything, and it is also one thing. Imagine everything, every thought, every physical and non-physical thing connected into one, single, thing. That is Infinity.\n\nYou may be asking, how are we connected? I may ask you the opposite, how are we disconnected? As a rug is made up of fibers, so are you made up of flesh and bone.\n\n. If everything can be made up of infinitely smaller things and infinitely bigger things can be made of those smaller things, what does that leave, but the fact of infinity?§0", ""),
        StaticBook("Game of Mind C1", "§bJaya Jayamen", "A Game of Minds§0\n§0\n§0     ~Originally by§0\n§0    Sofetios Jayamen§0\n§0\n§0\n\n      Chapter One§0\n§0\n§0         Awakening§0\n§0\n§0Pulsing. That is all I felt as I started to see again. Everything was a hazy black, even though my eyes were open. Another pulse happened inside my head. Things were beginning to become clearer.\n\nI could make out that I was in a small stone room… and there was something else in here with me. §0\n§0\n§0“Finally awake are we?” said a gruff voice.§0\n\n I could now see moderately well and saw a person sitting across from me looking straight at me. I coughed and said slowly, §0\n§0\n§0“Where am I?” My head still throbbing though getting better. The person then said,§0\n\n“You’re in the holding chamber, along with me.”§0\n§0\n§0“Holding chamber?” I said. “Holding chamber for what?” §0\n§0\n§0At that moment there was a slight creak to the right, as a part of the wall opened up like a door.§0\n\nThe person got up and came to me, holding out a hand. He said,§0\n§0\n§0“We had better get going. They don’t like it when we stall.”§0\n\n I grasped his hand and he hoisted me to my feet. I nearly fell over but he helped me catch myself.§0\n§0\n§0 I looked upwards and far above saw there was an opening to the sky.   I said,§0\n§0\n\n“Who are they?” The Person helped walk me toward the door.§0\n§0\n§0“They call themselves…The Followers of Truth.” He said. We got to the door and opened it. A burst of cold air came in.\n\n In the room was only a table that had frost on it, A fireplace to the right that had a metal pan on the fire with what appeared to be a steak on it, a small vase with a small top for an opening was on the floor with a lid next to it,\n\n, a poufy cushioned chair was next to the fireplace, and there was also another  fireplace at the far back of the room.§0\n§0\n§0“Great…”  Said the person. “ A Puzzle.”\n\nHe walked with me into the room. As we got in the door slammed behind us. “Can you stand now?” §0\n§0\n§0I nodded slightly as I took my own weight back.§0\n§0\n§0 “Good. My name is Jed. And you are?”§0\n\n“James.” I said.  Suddenly  I felt an overwhelming hunger as I smelled the smell of cooked steak. §0\n§0\n§0I looked to the steak and started to walk over, but as I did Jed caught my arm. “What?” I said.§0\n\n“The thing you should know about this place, is that it’s a death trap.” Said Jed with a serious face. §0\n§0\n§0He lifted his sleeve and there was a severe gash about a foot long. “That.” He said pointing to it, “Is what happens when you aren’t careful.”\n\n.”  I nodded, swallowing. “We had best get to solving this. Before the cold kills us.” §0\n§0\n§0 I hadn’t noticed but my toes were starting to get numb. §0\n§0\n§0“What should we be looking for?” I said. §0\n\n“A key to get back into the room we came in.” Said Jed.§0\n§0\n§0“What!?” I said, “We were just in there?” §0\n§0\n§0“Trust me James.” Said the man. “We are better off in here for the moment. What you need to understand is that The Followers of Truth are ruthless.\n\nIf you don’t do what they want you to, they will make you do it, one way or another. Put your ear to the door.”§0\n§0\n§0I went to the door and put my ear on it like he said to, There were clicking sounds in the room. Like a giant insect walking in there.\n\n“Hear that?” Said Jed. “That’s the Grimmer.”  I got my head off the door and said. “The Grimmer?”§0\n§0\n§0“Yes.” Said Jed. “A fearsome thing, they release it through the ceiling in the room to make sure we are out.” Then a thought occurred to me. I said,§0\n\n“How do they know we are still alive?” Jed Shrugged the said,§0\n§0\n§0“Well If the Grimmer doesn’t come back early I guess that means we are still alive.\n\n. Either way we better find the key to the door. Before they push us to find it. Now where is that key…”§0\n§0\n§0“Maybe it’s in the chair?” I said. “Seems poufy enough to hide it.”§0\n\n“Or to hide something else.” Said Jed. “Always test things.”  Jed took a glove off and went to the couch. I followed but at a distance. Jed, a few feet from the couch tossed it on the Seat.\n\nSuddenly there was a click. The cushion rose at an incredible speed to the ceiling, crushing the glove, then lowered back to position. The cushion didn’t even flatten, like it was rock hard. The glove fell on the floor.\n\nJed looked to me, nodded, and said.§0\n§0\n§0“See?”  As he picked up and put his glove back on. “Never touch things directly.”§0\n§0\n§0I took off my own glove went to the jar on the floor. §0\n\n I kind of whacked it with my glove. It just knocked over… and something clanked inside it. Jed looked to me and said “Be careful.” I put my glove back on, picked up the jar and looked in the opening. The key was inside it.\n\n“The key is in here!” I said. I turned the jar upside-down and the key came out… a little. It was stuck. §0\n§0\n§0Jed let out a small grunt. “They never make it that easy.”§0\n\n Suddenly there was a trickling and hissing sound coming from the right fireplace.  There was a small amount of water coming above the fireplace.  Jed cursed under his breath. I then panicked. I tried to smash the vase against the floor.\n\n“Stop!” Said Jed. The vase landed on the ground in my hand with a large thump, but didn’t break. §0\n§0\n§0“Careful you idiot!” That could have been filled with glass as well as the key.§0\n\n “Well what are we supposed to do?” I said in a panicked voice. Jed then said, “Not panic. That’s what they want us to do, we need to think.” I forced myself to relax.§0\n§0\n§0 Jed then said after a moment,§0\n\n“I’ve got it! Put it on the cushion.”  I rushed over and tossed it on the cushion. The Cushion rose again with great speed and there was a loud clunk.\n\n The cushion went back down and the vase, still whole, rolled off it. Jed cursed again. “So it’s unbreakable.” Jed then angrily threw his glove at the frosted table. As it hit the side, it stuck and turned frosted too.  I came to a sudden realization.\n\n“The water!” I said.§0\n§0\n§0“Yes I know.” Said Jed looking down at the ground, which had water rising a half inch above it.§0\n§0\n§0“No I mean-“ I didn’t explain. I ran to the jar, picked it up and the lid. §0\n\n I ran to the fireplace that was now extinguished, and filled it with water, then screwed on the lid. I turned to Jed who looked at me and looked like he just realized what I was doing and nodded. I Hurried to the table and tossed the jar at the side.\n\n As it made impact it stuck, frosted, then in a few short seconds the jar exploded. Shards went everywhere. Startled, I yelled and was knocked back on my backside.  A few cut me on my arms and legs from the jar shards.   “Jed!” are you alright?”\n\n I heard a few curses from the back of the room.§0\n§0\n§0“I’m fine.” Said Jed. ”I should have seen that coming.”  I saw that there was a solid bit of ice in the shape of a vase on the floor near the table, and inside was the key.§0\n\n I picked it up and headed to the extinguished fireplace, picking up the pan.  I didn’t head to the chair to smash the ice because I didn’t want to risk breaking the key.\n\nBy now the water was an inch and a half deep and I couldn’t feel from my ankle down. I ran carefully to the other fireplace, which was luckily a bit raised off the ground, but not much.\n\n I put the pan over the fire and the ice on top of it. The pan was about 2 inches deep. Jed came behind me. After about a minute, the pan was full of water, but the ice wasn’t melted all the way.\n\nI took the ice in one hand and tossed the water in the pan on the ground with the other, then put the ice back on the pan and started melting it again.\n\nAfter another minute the key was free. I took it and went to the door where Jed was now with his ear on the door. He took his head off the door and said,§0\n§0\n§0“The Grimmer isn’t there anymore.”§0\n§0\n\n  I unlocked it and opened it. The Grimmer wasn’t in there. I went into the room and Jed followed me, but suddenly the door closed, there was a hissing sound, like air through a tight space. I started coughing, as if the air was bad. So did Jed.\n\nThen everything went black.\n\nEnd of Chapter One", ""),
